export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","name":"CadCraft","common":"basics","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"variants":{"os":{"mac":"Mac","windows":"Windows","linux":"Linux"},"browser":{"chromium":"Chrome or Edge","firefox":"Firefox","safari":"Safari"},"shell":{"web":"In the browser","desktop":"Desktop app"},"net":{"online":"Online","offline":"Offline"},"input":{"mouse":"Mouse","touch":"Touch"}},"topics":[{"slug":"whats-new","scope":"cadcraft","title":"What's new in 0.4","summary":"DWG saving in six versions with sheets kept, sheet sets, a rebuilt Properties panel, trim by dragging, lasso selection, split screen and several drawings at once, LISP routines, the Electrical add-on, and many new drafting tools.","section":"start","order":5,"keywords":["whats new","what's new","release notes","changelog","changes","0.4","version","new features","dwg versions","sheet sets","properties panel","trim","lasso","split screen","tabs","lisp","appload","aliases","electrical","add-ons","hatch","mtext","linetypes","spanish"],"headings":[{"id":"new","text":"New","level":2},{"id":"saving-dwg-and-dxf","text":"Saving DWG and DXF","level":3},{"id":"drawing-and-editing","text":"Drawing and editing","level":3},{"id":"selecting","text":"Selecting","level":3},{"id":"text-and-notes","text":"Text and notes","level":3},{"id":"the-properties-panel","text":"The Properties panel","level":3},{"id":"sheets-sheet-sets-and-plotting","text":"Sheets, sheet sets and plotting","level":3},{"id":"several-drawings-and-split-screen","text":"Several drawings and split screen","level":3},{"id":"layers-blocks-and-linked-drawings","text":"Layers, blocks and linked drawings","level":3},{"id":"the-3d-tab","text":"The 3D tab","level":3},{"id":"lisp-and-your-own-aliases","text":"LISP and your own aliases","level":3},{"id":"add-ons-and-electrical","text":"Add-ons and Electrical","level":3},{"id":"in-every-app","text":"In every app","level":3},{"id":"improved","text":"Improved","level":2},{"id":"fixed","text":"Fixed","level":2},{"id":"not-in-this-version-yet","text":"Not in this version yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThis is what changed in CadCraft 0.4, compared with 0.3, the version Try ran before. It is the biggest update so far, so the list is grouped by area, with the most important first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"new\"\u003ENew\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"saving-dwg-and-dxf\"\u003ESaving DWG and DXF\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChoose the DWG version.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E offers DWG in the AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018 format. A DWG you open saves in its own version. From 2004 on, true colors are kept. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/open-dwg-save-dxf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/open-dwg-save-dxf\"\u003EOpen a DWG, edit it and save a copy\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheets are kept.\u003C/strong\u003E A saved DWG now keeps each sheet with its paper, what is drawn on it and its viewports.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore survives a save.\u003C/strong\u003E Dimensions save as real dimensions that other programs re-measure. Patterned hatches keep their pattern, scale and angle. Named views, the length format, multileaders, linetypes, lineweights and everything the Properties panel sets are kept too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF 2000.\u003C/strong\u003E A third choice in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E: a text DXF that keeps sheets, real dimensions, hatch patterns, true colors, lineweights and linetypes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#saving\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"saving\"\u003EFiles: what opens, what saves\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore comes in.\u003C/strong\u003E Multileaders in opened files are editable. Pictures show as a frame with the file name, and wipeouts as their frame, instead of being left out. Big DWGs open in the background, so the page keeps responding.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"drawing-and-editing\"\u003EDrawing and editing\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETrim by dragging.\u003C/strong\u003E In \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E, drag through the pieces you want gone: every piece the path crosses goes at once, and one Undo puts them back. Rest the pointer on a piece to see it greyed out first. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to extend instead. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELasso selection.\u003C/strong\u003E Press and drag on an empty spot to draw round objects: move right first for a window, left first for a crossing. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging for a fence. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChamfer, and a fuller Fillet.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E bevels a corner. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E now works on arcs, circles and polylines too, and joins parallel lines with a half circle. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/offset-trim-extend-fillet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/offset-trim-extend-fillet\"\u003EOffset, trim, extend, fillet and chamfer walls\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHatch.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EHATCH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E) fills an area with a solid fill or a standard pattern. Click inside the area, and closed shapes inside it stay empty. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/hatch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/hatch\"\u003EHatch and fill areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStretch, array, join, break and lengthen.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E moves what lies inside a crossing window. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAY\u003C/code\u003E copies in rows and columns or round a center. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/code\u003E join, cut and resize lines, arcs and polylines. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMATCHPROP\u003C/code\u003E copies one object&#039;s properties onto others. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/modify-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/modify-objects\"\u003EMove, copy, rotate, scale, mirror, stretch and array\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFeet and inches.\u003C/strong\u003E Choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E in Current settings, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E: Architectural, Engineering, Fractional or Scientific. Type lengths as \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\"\u003EUnits, paper sizes and scales\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinetypes and lineweights.\u003C/strong\u003E Dashed, hidden, center and the other standard linetypes, and lineweights, for layers and objects. They show in plots. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar to see lineweights on screen too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArea and measuring.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E measures an outline, a circle, a polyline or a hatch, with a running total. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMEASUREGEOM\u003C/code\u003E measures a distance, radius, angle or area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPolar tracking, object snap tracking and dynamic input.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) in the status bar. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/object-snaps\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/object-snaps\"\u003ESnap to points on objects\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom previous and named views.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E goes back to the view before. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ENV\u003C/code\u003E) saves the view under a name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConstraints survive mirror and trim.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E keep the constraints on what is left. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003EConstrain a drawing\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"selecting\"\u003ESelecting\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQuick select and select similar.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E) selects every object that matches a rule, with a live count. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESELECTSIMILAR\u003C/code\u003E selects everything like what you picked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore ways at \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E for a fence, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E for the previous selection, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E to take objects out. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E steps through objects on top of each other. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/select-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/select-objects\"\u003ESelect objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"text-and-notes\"\u003EText and notes\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParagraph text.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParagraph\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E) draws a box for the width, then you type straight onto the drawing. Double-click any text to edit it in place. Single-line text is now \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMultileaders.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMLEADER\u003C/code\u003E) draws an arrow, a leader and a note as one object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERevision clouds.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREVCLOUD\u003C/code\u003E draws a cloud round a rectangle, a polygon, a path you trace, or an object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind and replace.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E finds words in text, attribute values and dimension text. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERebuilt.\u003C/strong\u003E Sections you can fold, a divider you can drag, auto-hide, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 1\"\u003E⌘1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to open it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/properties-panel\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMany objects at once.\u003C/strong\u003E A list at the top picks one kind of object in a mixed selection. Values that differ read Varies, and a new value goes to every object in one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery point with X, Y and Z,\u003C/strong\u003E with a pick button beside each one. Every number field takes expressions and units, such as \u003Ccode\u003E=2*45\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E900mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEverything for dimensions,\u003C/strong\u003E and transparency, thickness, hyperlinks, text styles, polyline widths and hatch backgrounds. Transparency, widths and backgrounds show on the drawing and in plots.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EQuick Properties.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQP\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar shows the main properties by the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"sheets-sheet-sets-and-plotting\"\u003ESheets, sheet sets and plotting\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESheet sets.\u003C/strong\u003E Gather sheets from many drawings in one numbered list. Fill title blocks from the set, publish the whole set as one PDF, keep a sheet list on the cover sheet, and archive everything as a zip. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESHEETSET\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheet-sets\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheet-sets\"\u003ESheet sets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery sheet in one PDF.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets in one PDF\u003C/span\u003E in the Plot dialog, makes one PDF with a page and a bookmark per sheet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlat views of solids.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E places front, top, side or isometric views of the 3D solids on a sheet, with hidden lines dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"several-drawings-and-split-screen\"\u003ESeveral drawings and split screen\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeveral drawings at once.\u003C/strong\u003E Opening a drawing, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, adds a tab over the drawing area instead of closing what is open. Copy and paste between drawings, tile them side by side, and link their views to compare two revisions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESplit the screen.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit side by side\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E) shows the drawing in two, three or four panes, each with its own zoom. Keep a sheet in one pane beside the model in another. The split is saved with the drawing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETouch screens.\u003C/strong\u003E Pinch to zoom, two fingers to pan, and hold a finger still for Enter. A key row under the command line gives Esc, Undo, Enter and the characters a tablet keyboard hides. On the 3D tab, three fingers orbit. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layers-blocks-and-linked-drawings\"\u003ELayers, blocks and linked drawings\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayer tools by picking.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E and more, in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E menu of the Layers panel. Rename, merge and delete layers. Every layer change can be undone. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\"\u003EOrganize a drawing with layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECount blocks.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOUNT\u003C/code\u003E counts block references and exports the counts or an attribute schedule as CSV. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/blocks\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/blocks\"\u003EMake and insert blocks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDefine attributes and edit blocks.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E and the block editor (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E). See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELinked drawings.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXATTACH\u003C/code\u003E links another drawing, shown read-only with its own layers. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E reloads, binds or detaches it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"the-3d-tab\"\u003EThe 3D tab\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnion, subtract and intersect\u003C/strong\u003E solids (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNION\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESUBTRACT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINTERSECT\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERevolve and sweep\u003C/strong\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREVOLVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESWEEP\u003C/code\u003E), and extrude with a taper or along a path.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPoints snap to the 2D drawing on the ground, a drag selects 2D objects, and the 3D view is saved with the drawing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\"\u003EModel in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen in ModelCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in ModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E opens the drawing in the 3D modeling app in a new tab, where that app can take it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"lisp-and-your-own-aliases\"\u003ELISP and your own aliases\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERun LISP routines.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad application…\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E) loads \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E routines and their \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E dialogs, or drop them on the drawing. Their commands work like CadCraft&#039;s own. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/load-applications\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/load-applications\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/run-lisp\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/run-lisp\"\u003ERun LISP routines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECommand aliases.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIASEDIT\u003C/code\u003E keeps your own aliases in the familiar \u003Ccode\u003ELL, *LINE\u003C/code\u003E format, and imports a \u003Ccode\u003E.pgp\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-aliases\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-aliases\"\u003EEdit command aliases\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDelete blocks.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDELBLOCKS\u003C/code\u003E deletes a block&#039;s references everywhere, then its definition, in one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"add-ons-and-electrical\"\u003EAdd-ons and Electrical\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EElectrical comes with CadCraft.\u003C/strong\u003E A free add-on for electrical schematics and installation plans: IEC and ANSI symbols, wires with numbers, device tags, cross-references, terminal, cable and parts lists, circuits and a panel schedule. It is switched on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EElectrical\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel and in commands that start with \u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E, such as \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EELSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E. Its own help topics start with &quot;Electrical&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdd-ons.\u003C/strong\u003E Add-ons can add commands, toolbar buttons, panel and Properties sections, and file types (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E). Data they attach to objects is kept in DWG and DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"in-every-app\"\u003EIn every app\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpanish.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanguage\u003C/span\u003E offers &quot;Español (México)&quot;. It is a machine-made draft that has not been reviewed yet. Help topics are still in English. Command options show in your language, and typing the English word still works.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERecover your work.\u003C/strong\u003E If the tab or browser closes before you save, CadCraft offers your drawings back the next time it starts. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover unsaved work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFind a tool.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E finds a tool, a setting or a help topic. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/find-tools\" data-topic=\"basics/find-tools\"\u003EFind a tool or setting\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore panel windows.\u003C/strong\u003E Give each panel a window of its own, one per monitor. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels and use a second monitor\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"improved\"\u003EImproved\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening an Archicad project says what it is and how to get a DWG or IFC out of it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHatches with a standard pattern in opened drawings now show their pattern.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3D faces inside blocks come into the 3D tab again, once for every reference. In blocks and on sheets they are drawn flat.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe view cube&#039;s words come from the language file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fixed\"\u003EFixed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMirroring a block reference put it turned half a turn. It now lands where it belongs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-in-this-version-yet\"\u003ENot in this version yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EYou cannot enter a viewport on a sheet to work in the model through it. Edit on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab instead. Layers frozen in one viewport only are not shown that way yet. Both are planned for 0.5.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAssociative hatches, gradients, text styles with fonts, and spline objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"This is what changed in CadCraft 0.4, compared with 0.3, the version Try ran before. It is the biggest update so far, so the list is grouped by area, with the most important first. New Saving DWG and DXF Choose the DWG version. Save as offers DWG in the AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018 format. A DWG you open saves in its own version. From 2004 on, true colors are kept. See Open a DWG, edit it and save a copy. Sheets are kept. A saved DWG now keeps each sheet with its paper, what is drawn on it and its viewports. More survives a save. Dimensions save as real dimensions that other programs re-measure. Patterned hatches keep their pattern, scale and angle. Named views, the length format, multileaders, linetypes, lineweights and everything the Properties panel sets are kept too. DXF 2000. A third choice in Save as: a text DXF that keeps sheets, real dimensions, hatch patterns, true colors, lineweights and linetypes. See Files: what opens, what saves. More comes in. Multileaders in opened files are editable. Pictures show as a frame with the file name, and wipeouts as their frame, instead of being left out. Big DWGs open in the background, so the page keeps responding. Drawing and editing Trim by dragging. In TRIM, drag through the pieces you want gone: every piece the path crosses goes at once, and one Undo puts them back. Rest the pointer on a piece to see it greyed out first. Hold Shift to extend instead. See Trim and extend. Lasso selection. Press and drag on an empty spot to draw round objects: move right first for a window, left first for a crossing. Press Space while dragging for a fence. See Lasso selection. Chamfer, and a fuller Fillet. CHAMFER bevels a corner. FILLET now works on arcs, circles and polylines too, and joins parallel lines with a half circle. See Offset, trim, extend, fillet and chamfer walls. Hatch. Hatch (HATCH, H) fills an area with a solid fill or a standard pattern. Click inside the area, and closed shapes inside it stay empty. See Hatch and fill areas. Stretch, array, join, break and lengthen. STRETCH moves what lies inside a crossing window. ARRAY copies in rows and columns or round a center. JOIN, BREAK and LENGTHEN join, cut and resize lines, arcs and polylines. MATCHPROP copies one object's properties onto others. See Move, copy, rotate, scale, mirror, stretch and array. Feet and inches. Choose Length format in Current settings, or type UNITS: Architectural, Engineering, Fractional or Scientific. Type lengths as 12'6\" or 12'6-1/2\". See Units, paper sizes and scales. Linetypes and lineweights. Dashed, hidden, center and the other standard linetypes, and lineweights, for layers and objects. They show in plots. Press LWT in the status bar to see lineweights on screen too. Area and measuring. AREA measures an outline, a circle, a polyline or a hatch, with a running total. MEASUREGEOM measures a distance, radius, angle or area. Polar tracking, object snap tracking and dynamic input. Polar (F10), Track (F11) and Dyn (F12) in the status bar. See Snap to points on objects and Coordinate and distance entry. Zoom previous and named views. Z then P goes back to the view before. NAMEDVIEW (NV) saves the view under a name. Constraints survive mirror and trim. MIRROR and TRIM keep the constraints on what is left. See Constrain a drawing. Selecting Quick select and select similar. Edit › More › Quick select (QSELECT) selects every object that matches a rule, with a live count. SELECTSIMILAR selects everything like what you picked. More ways at Select objects. F for a fence, P for the previous selection, R to take objects out. Tab steps through objects on top of each other. See Select objects. Text and notes Paragraph text. Paragraph (MTEXT, T) draws a box for the width, then you type straight onto the drawing. Double-click any text to edit it in place. Single-line text is now DT. Multileaders. Leader (MLEADER) draws an arrow, a leader and a note as one object. Revision clouds. REVCLOUD draws a cloud round a rectangle, a polygon, a path you trace, or an object. Find and replace. Edit › More › Find and replace finds words in text, attribute values and dimension text. See Add text and labels. The Properties panel Rebuilt. Sections you can fold, a divider you can drag, auto-hide, and Ctrl+1 to open it. See The Properties panel. Many objects at once. A list at the top picks one kind of object in a mixed selection. Values that differ read Varies, and a new value goes to every object in one undo step. Every point with X, Y and Z, with a pick button beside each one. Every number field takes expressions and units, such as =2*45, 3'6\" or 900mm. Everything for dimensions, and transparency, thickness, hyperlinks, text styles, polyline widths and hatch backgrounds. Transparency, widths and backgrounds show on the drawing and in plots. Quick Properties. QP in the status bar shows the main properties by the cursor. Sheets, sheet sets and plotting Sheet sets. Gather sheets from many drawings in one numbered list. Fill title blocks from the set, publish the whole set as one PDF, keep a sheet list on the cover sheet, and archive everything as a zip. Type SHEETSET. See Sheet sets. Every sheet in one PDF. PUBLISH, or All sheets in one PDF in the Plot dialog, makes one PDF with a page and a bookmark per sheet. See Plot to PDF. Flat views of solids. VIEWBASE places front, top, side or isometric views of the 3D solids on a sheet, with hidden lines dashed. Several drawings and split screen Several drawings at once. Opening a drawing, or New, adds a tab over the drawing area instead of closing what is open. Copy and paste between drawings, tile them side by side, and link their views to compare two revisions. Split the screen. View › More › Split side by side (VPORTS) shows the drawing in two, three or four panes, each with its own zoom. Keep a sheet in one pane beside the model in another. The split is saved with the drawing. See Split the screen. Touch screens. Pinch to zoom, two fingers to pan, and hold a finger still for Enter. A key row under the command line gives Esc, Undo, Enter and the characters a tablet keyboard hides. On the 3D tab, three fingers orbit. See Mouse, trackpad and touch. Layers, blocks and linked drawings Layer tools by picking. LAYISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ, LAYMCUR and more, in the Tools menu of the Layers panel. Rename, merge and delete layers. Every layer change can be undone. See Organize a drawing with layers. Count blocks. COUNT counts block references and exports the counts or an attribute schedule as CSV. See Make and insert blocks. Define attributes and edit blocks. ATTDEF and the block editor (BEDIT). See Block attributes. Linked drawings. XATTACH links another drawing, shown read-only with its own layers. XREF reloads, binds or detaches it. The 3D tab Union, subtract and intersect solids (UNION, SUBTRACT, INTERSECT). Revolve and sweep (REVOLVE, SWEEP), and extrude with a taper or along a path. Points snap to the 2D drawing on the ground, a drag selects 2D objects, and the 3D view is saved with the drawing. See Model in 3D. Open in ModelCraft. View › More › Open in ModelCraft opens the drawing in the 3D modeling app in a new tab, where that app can take it. LISP and your own aliases Run LISP routines. View › More › Load application… (APPLOAD) loads .lsp routines and their .dcl dialogs, or drop them on the drawing. Their commands work like CadCraft's own. See Load applications and Run LISP routines. Command aliases. ALIASEDIT keeps your own aliases in the familiar LL, *LINE format, and imports a .pgp. See Edit command aliases. Delete blocks. DELBLOCKS deletes a block's references everywhere, then its definition, in one undo step. Add-ons and Electrical Electrical comes with CadCraft. A free add-on for electrical schematics and installation plans: IEC and ANSI symbols, wires with numbers, device tags, cross-references, terminal, cable and parts lists, circuits and a panel schedule. It is switched on in Settings › General › Add-ons. Its tools are in the Electrical section of the right panel and in commands that start with EL, such as ELSYMBOL. Its own help topics start with \"Electrical\". Add-ons. Add-ons can add commands, toolbar buttons, panel and Properties sections, and file types (IMPORT, EXPORT). Data they attach to objects is kept in DWG and DXF. In every app Spanish. Settings › General › Language offers \"Español (México)\". It is a machine-made draft that has not been reviewed yet. Help topics are still in English. Command options show in your language, and typing the English word still works. Recover your work. If the tab or browser closes before you save, CadCraft offers your drawings back the next time it starts. See Recover unsaved work. Find a tool. Ctrl+K finds a tool, a setting or a help topic. See Find a tool or setting. More panel windows. Give each panel a window of its own, one per monitor. See Arrange the panels and use a second monitor. Improved Opening an Archicad project says what it is and how to get a DWG or IFC out of it. Hatches with a standard pattern in opened drawings now show their pattern. 3D faces inside blocks come into the 3D tab again, once for every reference. In blocks and on sheets they are drawn flat. The view cube's words come from the language file. Fixed Mirroring a block reference put it turned half a turn. It now lands where it belongs. Not in this version yet You cannot enter a viewport on a sheet to work in the model through it. Edit on the Model tab instead. Layers frozen in one viewport only are not shown that way yet. Both are planned for 0.5. Associative hatches, gradients, text styles with fonts, and spline objects. Not built yet"},{"slug":"getting-started","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Getting started with CadCraft","summary":"What the CadCraft drafting app does, where it runs, and your first drawing from a blank screen to a saved file.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["start","first drawing","new","open","save","save as","begin","tutorial","introduction","quick start"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-need","text":"What you need","level":2},{"id":"the-idea-in-one-paragraph","text":"The idea in one paragraph","level":2},{"id":"your-first-drawing","text":"Your first drawing","level":2},{"id":"open-an-existing-drawing","text":"Open an existing drawing","level":2},{"id":"saving-what-to-expect","text":"Saving: what to expect","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to go next","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft is a 2D drafting app. You draw and edit lines, arcs, polylines, text, dimensions and blocks, set up sheets with title blocks and viewports, and plot them to PDF. It opens DWG and DXF drawings and saves them as DWG or DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt runs in your web browser. There is nothing to install: the site gives you the address, and the whole app loads into the page. Your drawings open from your own computer and are saved back to it. \u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you have drafted before, read \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coming-from\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coming-from\"\u003EComing from other drafting software\u003C/a\u003E after this page. Most habits carry over, and the few that do not are listed there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-need\"\u003EWhat you need\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari on a Mac, Windows or Linux computer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and platforms\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA mouse with a wheel is the most comfortable. A trackpad works; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA keyboard. CadCraft is driven by typed commands, like most drafting programs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-idea-in-one-paragraph\"\u003EThe idea in one paragraph\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou type a command (or click its button), and the command line at the bottom asks you questions one at a time: &quot;Specify first point&quot;, &quot;Specify next point&quot;. You answer each question by clicking in the drawing or by typing a value, then pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels. That is the whole interaction model; everything else is detail.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-drawing\"\u003EYour first drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou will draw a 4000 by 3000 rectangle out of lines, put a circle in the middle, and save the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen CadCraft. The command line at the bottom shows \u003Ccode\u003ECommand:\u003C/code\u003E and the drawing area shows a short &quot;Start drawing&quot; card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You do not need to click the command line first: typing anywhere goes there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe prompt changes to \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point:\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@4000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A line appears, running right. The \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E means &quot;from the last point&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,3000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-4000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E is the Close option: the last line goes back to the first point and the command ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E in the View group of the toolbar. The rectangle fills the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should now see four lines forming a rectangle, and the history above the command line lists what you typed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start the circle command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E2000,1500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA circle appears in the middle of the rectangle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E in the File group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1.dwg\u003C/code\u003E to your usual downloads folder, and the history says it saved a copy as DWG. That file opens in CadCraft again, and in most other CAD programs. (If you chose DXF in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E before, in this browser, a new drawing saves as \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1.dxf\u003C/code\u003E instead.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty command line to repeat the last command. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E instead of \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E if you prefer: outside of text, it does the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-an-existing-drawing\"\u003EOpen an existing drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E file in the file picker.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drag a DWG or DXF file from \u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EFinder\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows\" title=\"Windows\"\u003EFile Explorer\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:linux\" title=\"Linux\"\u003Eyour file manager\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E and drop it on the drawing area. The file is read in the page; it never leaves your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\"\u003EFiles: what opens, what saves\u003C/a\u003E for what is kept and what is converted.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving-what-to-expect\"\u003ESaving: what to expect\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving in a browser works differently from a desktop program:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E always \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloads a new copy\u003C/strong\u003E. It does not overwrite the file you opened. If a file with the same name is already in your downloads folder, the browser usually adds a number, such as \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1 (1).dwg\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E uses the drawing&#039;s format: DWG if you opened a DWG, DXF if you opened a DXF. To choose, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E, then pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E (and its version), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#saving\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"saving\"\u003ESaving\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe file you opened is never changed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening another file, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, puts it in its own tab over the drawing, so nothing you have open is closed. If you close a drawing&#039;s tab or leave the page with unsaved changes, CadCraft asks first: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy first\u003C/span\u003E, discard or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E. Closing the browser tab shows the browser&#039;s own &quot;Leave site?&quot; question instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving is not available yet. Where your files go and why they stay private is explained in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EYour files and privacy\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-to-go-next\"\u003EWhere to go next\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top to open this help beside the drawing at any time. It works with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/workspace\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/workspace\"\u003EThe CadCraft screen\u003C/a\u003E names every bar and panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/draw-with-coordinates\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/draw-with-coordinates\"\u003EDraw with coordinates\u003C/a\u003E is the best first walkthrough.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E lists every key.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E at the command line for a list of every command, or read the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\"\u003ECommand reference\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"CadCraft is a 2D drafting app. You draw and edit lines, arcs, polylines, text, dimensions and blocks, set up sheets with title blocks and viewports, and plot them to PDF. It opens DWG and DXF drawings and saves them as DWG or DXF. It runs in your web browser. There is nothing to install: the site gives you the address, and the whole app loads into the page. Your drawings open from your own computer and are saved back to it. Nothing is uploaded. If you have drafted before, read Coming from other drafting software after this page. Most habits carry over, and the few that do not are listed there. What you need A recent Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari on a Mac, Windows or Linux computer. See Browsers and platforms. A mouse with a wheel is the most comfortable. A trackpad works; see Mouse, trackpad and touch. A keyboard. CadCraft is driven by typed commands, like most drafting programs. The idea in one paragraph You type a command (or click its button), and the command line at the bottom asks you questions one at a time: \"Specify first point\", \"Specify next point\". You answer each question by clicking in the drawing or by typing a value, then pressing Enter. Pressing Esc cancels. That is the whole interaction model; everything else is detail. Your first drawing You will draw a 4000 by 3000 rectangle out of lines, put a circle in the middle, and save the file. Open CadCraft. The command line at the bottom shows Command: and the drawing area shows a short \"Start drawing\" card. Type L and press Enter. You do not need to click the command line first: typing anywhere goes there. The prompt changes to Specify first point:. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @4000,0 and press Enter. A line appears, running right. The @ means \"from the last point\". Type @0,3000 and press Enter. Type @-4000,0 and press Enter. Type C and press Enter. C is the Close option: the last line goes back to the first point and the command ends. Click Extents in the View group of the toolbar. The rectangle fills the screen. You should now see four lines forming a rectangle, and the history above the command line lists what you typed. Type C and press Enter to start the circle command. Type 2000,1500 and press Enter for the center. Type 500 and press Enter for the radius. A circle appears in the middle of the rectangle. Press Ctrl+S, or click Save in the File group. The browser downloads Drawing1.dwg to your usual downloads folder, and the history says it saved a copy as DWG. That file opens in CadCraft again, and in most other CAD programs. (If you chose DXF in Save as before, in this browser, a new drawing saves as Drawing1.dxf instead.) Press Enter on an empty command line to repeat the last command. Press Space instead of Enter if you prefer: outside of text, it does the same. Open an existing drawing Press Ctrl+O, or click Open. Choose a .dwg or .dxf file in the file picker. You can also drag a DWG or DXF file from Finder / File Explorer / your file manager and drop it on the drawing area. The file is read in the page; it never leaves your computer. See Files: what opens, what saves for what is kept and what is converted. Saving: what to expect Saving in a browser works differently from a desktop program: Save always downloads a new copy. It does not overwrite the file you opened. If a file with the same name is already in your downloads folder, the browser usually adds a number, such as Drawing1 (1).dwg. Save uses the drawing's format: DWG if you opened a DWG, DXF if you opened a DXF. To choose, press Ctrl+Shift+S or click Save as, then pick DWG (and its version), DXF R12 or DXF 2000. See Saving. The file you opened is never changed. Opening another file, or New, puts it in its own tab over the drawing, so nothing you have open is closed. If you close a drawing's tab or leave the page with unsaved changes, CadCraft asks first: Save a copy first, discard or Cancel. Closing the browser tab shows the browser's own \"Leave site?\" question instead. Cloud saving is not available yet. Where your files go and why they stay private is explained in Your files and privacy. Where to go next Press F1 or click Help in the bar at the top to open this help beside the drawing at any time. It works with no connection. The CadCraft screen names every bar and panel. Draw with coordinates is the best first walkthrough. Keyboard and mouse lists every key. Type HELP at the command line for a list of every command, or read the Command reference."},{"slug":"workspace","scope":"cadcraft","title":"The CadCraft screen","summary":"A tour of every bar, panel and tab in the drafting app, by the name it has on screen.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["screen","interface","toolbar","ribbon","panels","command line","status bar","tabs","layout tabs","properties","layers panel","tour"],"headings":[{"id":"the-app-bar","text":"The app bar","level":2},{"id":"the-toolbar","text":"The toolbar","level":2},{"id":"the-left-panel","text":"The left panel","level":2},{"id":"the-drawing-area","text":"The drawing area","level":2},{"id":"the-tabs-under-the-drawing","text":"The tabs under the drawing","level":2},{"id":"the-right-panel-properties","text":"The right panel: Properties","level":2},{"id":"the-command-line","text":"The command line","level":2},{"id":"the-status-bar","text":"The status bar","level":2},{"id":"the-plot-to-pdf-dialog","text":"The Plot to PDF dialog","level":2},{"id":"the-help","text":"The help","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe screen has seven parts. From top to bottom: the app bar, the toolbar, the drawing area with a panel on each side, the tabs under the drawing, the command line and the status bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-app-bar\"\u003EThe app bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe thin bar at the very top belongs to the site, not to the drawing. It shows the CadCraft mark, the app name, which version you are running, a link to the app&#039;s page, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E button, a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E button and two theme buttons, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E button (gear) holds CadCraft&#039;s own settings and the ones every app shares: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy of CadCraft, including its DWG reader, in this browser so it opens with no connection. Its label shows the state: &quot;Saving N of M&quot;, &quot;Offline ready&quot;, &quot;Could not save&quot; or &quot;Offline not available&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E buttons in the app bar only change the colors of the screen: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E is a light theme, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E a dark one. They have nothing to do with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab under the drawing, which switches between the drawing and its sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFull screen\u003C/span\u003E button, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (the Control key on every computer, Mac included), cycles through three screen modes: normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge of the screen to bring them back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-toolbar\"\u003EThe toolbar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the app bar is the toolbar. Every button runs a command, exactly as if you had typed it, so the command line asks the same questions either way. Hover a button to see its command alias. The groups are:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGroup\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButtons\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFile\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolyline\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECircle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArc\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolygon\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEllipse\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParagraph\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPoint\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EModify\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotate\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMirror\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOffset\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrim\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtend\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFillet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChamfer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExplode\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EErase\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArray\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch properties\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreak\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELengthen\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnnotate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinear\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAligned\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiameter\u003C/span\u003E (dimensions), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E (multileader), and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContinue dimension\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBaseline dimension\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevision cloud\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParametric\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAuto\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstrain\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E with each constraint (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003EConstrain a drawing\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlocks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit block\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefine attribute\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViewport\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E (to the clipboard), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E: a menu with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate and move\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect similar\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E, each with its key\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EView\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure distance\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure (radius, angle …)\u003C/span\u003E, and the split screen: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit side by side\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit stacked\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFour panes\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne pane\u003C/span\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E group is the \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E command (base point, then where each copy goes); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group puts objects on the clipboard for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E. The button of the command that is running stays highlighted until the command ends. Tooltips name the Windows shortcuts (&quot;Ctrl+O&quot;); on a Mac, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-left-panel\"\u003EThe left panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel on the left has three sections.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Every layer in the drawing, with a filled dot on the current one. Click a name to make that layer current. Each row has a color swatch (click it to choose a color) and four buttons: on/off (eye), freeze (snowflake), lock (padlock) and plot (printer). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E opens a box to add one. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\"\u003EOrganize a drawing with layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E The blocks defined in the drawing, how many times each is used, and an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E button for each. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/blocks\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/blocks\"\u003EMake and insert blocks\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Facts about the open file: name (with &quot;(changed)&quot; when there are unsaved edits), format, size, units, number of objects, layers, blocks and sheets. After you open a file, an &quot;About this file&quot; box lists anything CadCraft could not show or had to convert.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-drawing-area\"\u003EThe drawing area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe middle of the screen is the drawing. The pointer is a crosshair. When CadCraft wants a point, a small marker and a label (&quot;Endpoint&quot;, &quot;Midpoint&quot;) show where an object snap will land. When it wants objects, the crosshair has a pick box and objects under it light up.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the drawing is empty, a &quot;Start drawing&quot; card shows a few first commands. It goes away as soon as you run a command or open a file. You can drop a DWG or DXF file anywhere on this area to open it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-tabs-under-the-drawing\"\u003EThe tabs under the drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow the drawing are the tabs: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E first, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E, then one tab per sheet (layout), then a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E is the drawing itself, at full size, in drawing units.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E shows the drawing lying on the ground and the solids made from it. While it shows, the toolbar has a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E group in place of the drawing groups. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\"\u003EModel in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach sheet tab is a sheet of paper with a title block and viewports onto the model. What you draw there is in paper units (millimeters or inches).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E makes a new sheet with a border, a title block and one viewport. It is the same as the \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click a sheet tab to rename it. With a tab focused, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E move between tabs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach tab keeps its own zoom and pan. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\"\u003ESet up a sheet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-right-panel-properties\"\u003EThe right panel: Properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel on the right changes with what is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing selected, on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab:\u003C/strong\u003E the heading reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E. Here you set the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E for new objects, the drawing \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E, and the sizes for new dimensions (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow size\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing selected, on a sheet:\u003C/strong\u003E the heading reads &quot;Sheet:&quot; and the sheet name. You can rename the sheet, change its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrientation\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot this sheet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this sheet\u003C/span\u003E. The settings for new objects follow below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOne object selected:\u003C/strong\u003E its type as the heading (Line, Circle, Text, Dimension, Viewport …), then its layer, color and geometry. Type a new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to change it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeveral objects selected:\u003C/strong\u003E how many, and their common \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour\u003C/span\u003E. Changing either changes them all. &quot;Varies&quot; means they differ.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/grips-and-properties\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/grips-and-properties\"\u003EEdit with grips and Properties\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelow it, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E panel opens when you choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E: every parameter and dimensional constraint in one table. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/parameters\"\u003EUse parameters and equations\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-command-line\"\u003EThe command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the bottom is the command line: the history of what you did, and under it the prompt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe prompt label reads \u003Ccode\u003ECommand:\u003C/code\u003E when nothing is running. While a command runs, it shows the question, its options in square brackets and the default in angle brackets, for example \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify radius of circle [Diameter] &lt;500&gt;:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAs you type a command name, a hint to the right suggests matching commands. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to accept the first one. &quot;No such command&quot; means nothing matches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E bring back lines you typed before.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe history keeps the last 400 lines. Messages from commands, measurements from \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E, and save and plot reports appear here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-status-bar\"\u003EThe status bar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe very bottom row is the status bar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECoordinates\u003C/strong\u003E on the left: the X and Y of the crosshair. While a command has a base point, it also shows the distance and angle from it, for example \u003Ccode\u003EΔ 1200 &lt; 90°\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMessages\u003C/strong\u003E in the middle, such as &quot;Ortho on&quot; or &quot;Current layer: Walls&quot;. They fade after a few seconds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E on the right. Each is a toggle; a pressed button is on. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E shows lineweights on screen (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/a\u003E). The small arrow next to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObject snaps\u003C/span\u003E, where you tick which snaps are active. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/object-snaps\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/object-snaps\"\u003ESnap to points on objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-plot-to-pdf-dialog\"\u003EThe Plot to PDF dialog\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog, with the settings on the left and a live preview on the right. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-help\"\u003EThe help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button opens this help as a side panel; the drawing stays usable beside it. It works with no connection. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E inside the panel, closes it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/help\" data-topic=\"basics/help\"\u003EUsing this help\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The screen has seven parts. From top to bottom: the app bar, the toolbar, the drawing area with a panel on each side, the tabs under the drawing, the command line and the status bar. The app bar The thin bar at the very top belongs to the site, not to the drawing. It shows the CadCraft mark, the app name, which version you are running, a link to the app's page, a Help button (or F1), a Save for offline button, a Full screen button and two theme buttons, Paper and Model. The Settings button (gear) holds CadCraft's own settings and the ones every app shares: see CadCraft settings. Save for offline keeps a copy of CadCraft, including its DWG reader, in this browser so it opens with no connection. Its label shows the state: \"Saving N of M\", \"Offline ready\", \"Could not save\" or \"Offline not available\". See Working offline. The Paper and Model buttons in the app bar only change the colors of the screen: Paper is a light theme, Model a dark one. They have nothing to do with the Model tab under the drawing, which switches between the drawing and its sheets. The Full screen button, or Ctrl+Shift+F (the Control key on every computer, Mac included), cycles through three screen modes: normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. With the bars hidden, move the pointer to the top edge of the screen to bring them back. The toolbar Under the app bar is the toolbar. Every button runs a command, exactly as if you had typed it, so the command line asks the same questions either way. Hover a button to see its command alias. The groups are: Group Buttons File New, Open, Save, Save as, Plot Draw Line, Polyline, Circle, Arc, Rectangle, Polygon, Ellipse, Text, Paragraph, Hatch, Point Modify Move, Copy, Rotate, Scale, Mirror, Stretch, Offset, Trim, Extend, Fillet, Chamfer, Explode, Erase, and More: Array, Join, Match properties, Break and Lengthen Annotate Linear, Aligned, Angle, Radius, Diameter (dimensions), Leader (multileader), and More: Continue dimension, Baseline dimension and Revision cloud Parametric Auto, Constrain, Dimension, Parameters, and More with each constraint (see Constrain a drawing) Blocks Make, Insert, Library, and More: Edit block and Define attribute Sheets New sheet, Viewport Edit Undo, Redo, Copy (to the clipboard), Paste, and More: a menu with Cut, Copy, Paste, Paste in place, Duplicate and move, Delete, Select all, Select none, Quick select, Select similar and Find and replace, each with its key View Extents, Window, Previous, and More: Measure distance, Area, Measure (radius, angle …), and the split screen: Split side by side, Split stacked, Four panes, One pane (see Split the screen) Copy in the Modify group is the COPY command (base point, then where each copy goes); Copy in the Edit group puts objects on the clipboard for Paste. See Copy, paste and duplicate. The button of the command that is running stays highlighted until the command ends. Tooltips name the Windows shortcuts (\"Ctrl+O\"); on a Mac, Ctrl+O works too. The left panel The panel on the left has three sections. Layers. Every layer in the drawing, with a filled dot on the current one. Click a name to make that layer current. Each row has a color swatch (click it to choose a color) and four buttons: on/off (eye), freeze (snowflake), lock (padlock) and plot (printer). New layer opens a box to add one. See Organize a drawing with layers. Blocks. The blocks defined in the drawing, how many times each is used, and an Insert button for each. See Make and insert blocks. Drawing. Facts about the open file: name (with \"(changed)\" when there are unsaved edits), format, size, units, number of objects, layers, blocks and sheets. After you open a file, an \"About this file\" box lists anything CadCraft could not show or had to convert. The drawing area The middle of the screen is the drawing. The pointer is a crosshair. When CadCraft wants a point, a small marker and a label (\"Endpoint\", \"Midpoint\") show where an object snap will land. When it wants objects, the crosshair has a pick box and objects under it light up. When the drawing is empty, a \"Start drawing\" card shows a few first commands. It goes away as soon as you run a command or open a file. You can drop a DWG or DXF file anywhere on this area to open it. The tabs under the drawing Below the drawing are the tabs: Model first, then 3D, then one tab per sheet (layout), then a + button. Model is the drawing itself, at full size, in drawing units. 3D shows the drawing lying on the ground and the solids made from it. While it shows, the toolbar has a 3D group in place of the drawing groups. See Model in 3D. Each sheet tab is a sheet of paper with a title block and viewports onto the model. What you draw there is in paper units (millimeters or inches). + makes a new sheet with a border, a title block and one viewport. It is the same as the LAYOUT command. Double-click a sheet tab to rename it. With a tab focused, ← and → move between tabs. Each tab keeps its own zoom and pan. See Set up a sheet. The right panel: Properties The panel on the right changes with what is selected. Nothing selected, on the Model tab: the heading reads Current settings. Here you set the Layer, Colour and Text height for new objects, the drawing Units, and the sizes for new dimensions (Text height, Arrow size, Decimals, Overall scale). Nothing selected, on a sheet: the heading reads \"Sheet:\" and the sheet name. You can rename the sheet, change its Paper and Orientation, Plot this sheet or Delete this sheet. The settings for new objects follow below. One object selected: its type as the heading (Line, Circle, Text, Dimension, Viewport …), then its layer, color and geometry. Type a new value and press Enter to change it. Several objects selected: how many, and their common Layer and Colour. Changing either changes them all. \"Varies\" means they differ. See Edit with grips and Properties. Below it, the Parameters panel opens when you choose Parameters or type PARAMETERS: every parameter and dimensional constraint in one table. See Use parameters and equations. The command line At the bottom is the command line: the history of what you did, and under it the prompt. The prompt label reads Command: when nothing is running. While a command runs, it shows the question, its options in square brackets and the default in angle brackets, for example Specify radius of circle [Diameter] \u003C500\u003E:. As you type a command name, a hint to the right suggests matching commands. Press Tab to accept the first one. \"No such command\" means nothing matches. ↑ and ↓ bring back lines you typed before. The history keeps the last 400 lines. Messages from commands, measurements from DIST and LIST, and save and plot reports appear here. The status bar The very bottom row is the status bar. Coordinates on the left: the X and Y of the crosshair. While a command has a base point, it also shows the distance and angle from it, for example Δ 1200 \u003C 90°. Messages in the middle, such as \"Ortho on\" or \"Current layer: Walls\". They fade after a few seconds. Grid, Ortho, Polar, Snap, Track, Dyn and LWT on the right. Each is a toggle; a pressed button is on. LWT shows lineweights on screen (see Line weights and linetypes). The small arrow next to Snap opens Object snaps, where you tick which snaps are active. See Snap to points on objects. The Plot to PDF dialog Plot (or Ctrl+P) opens the Plot to PDF dialog, with the settings on the left and a live preview on the right. See Plot to PDF. The help F1 or the Help button opens this help as a side panel; the drawing stays usable beside it. It works with no connection. F1 again, or Esc inside the panel, closes it. See Using this help."},{"slug":"files","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Files: what opens, what saves","summary":"The formats CadCraft opens, saves and exports, what is kept or converted, and where the files go.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","pdf","r12","open","save","export","import","formats","conversion","download","saveas","dxfout","save as","dwg save","format","qsave","xref","xattach","linked drawing","external reference","bind","reload","detach"],"headings":[{"id":"at-a-glance","text":"At a glance","level":2},{"id":"opening-a-drawing","text":"Opening a drawing","level":2},{"id":"what-comes-in","text":"What comes in","level":3},{"id":"what-does-not-come-in-yet","text":"What does not come in yet","level":3},{"id":"saving","text":"Saving","level":2},{"id":"what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes","text":"What a DWG keeps and changes","level":2},{"id":"what-a-dwg-keeps","text":"What a DWG keeps","level":3},{"id":"what-a-dwg-changes","text":"What a DWG changes","level":3},{"id":"why-r12-and-what-converts","text":"Why R12, and what converts","level":3},{"id":"linked-drawings-external-references","text":"Linked drawings (external references)","level":2},{"id":"open-in-3d","text":"Open in 3D","level":2},{"id":"plotting-a-pdf","text":"Plotting a PDF","level":2},{"id":"where-the-files-go","text":"Where the files go","level":2},{"id":"archicad-projects","text":"Archicad projects","level":2},{"id":"if-the-page-closes-before-you-save","text":"If the page closes before you save","level":2},{"id":"online-and-offline","text":"Online and offline","level":2},{"id":"constraints-in-files","text":"Constraints in files","level":2},{"id":"not-available-yet","text":"Not available yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"at-a-glance\"\u003EAt a glance\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFormat\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOpen\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESave\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDXF (text, any version)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, as DXF R12 or DXF 2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBinary DXF is refused: save it as text DXF first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, as DWG (AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead and written by CadCraft&#039;s own DWG library (AutoCAD R13 to 2018 files). Your DWG is never changed. Damaged files, or versions the reader does not handle, are refused with a message.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYes, by plotting\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EVector PDF of a sheet, or of the model at a scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach drawing you open (or start with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E) gets its own tab over the drawing area; a new drawing nobody has touched yet is used instead of adding a tab. Click a tab to switch, its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E×\u003C/span\u003E to close it (CadCraft asks first if there are unsaved changes), or tile the open drawings side by side: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-a-drawing\"\u003EOpening a drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EOPEN\u003C/code\u003E, then choose the file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOr drag a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E file onto the drawing area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file is read inside your browser. It is not uploaded anywhere. The history reports how many objects and layers came in and how long it took, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E section of the left panel shows the format, for example &quot;DWG 2018 (AC1032)&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe DWG reader is part of CadCraft and is fetched with the first DWG you open after the page loads (a few hundred KB), so there is no separate download to wait for. It reads the file in the background, so the page keeps responding while a big DWG opens.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-comes-in\"\u003EWhat comes in\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, arcs, circles, polylines (with arc segments), ellipses, points, text and multi-line text, solids, hatches (the standard patterns drawn as their lines, others as a flat tone), block references with their attributes (values you can edit; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E), dimensions, and layouts with their viewports.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESplines come in as polylines that follow the curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMultileaders with one leader line and text come in as CadCraft multileaders you can edit (see \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMLEADER\u003C/code\u003E). Others (with a block, several leader lines or curved lines) are drawn as the other program drew them and move as one; the history says how many.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPictures (images) show as a frame with the picture&#039;s file name: CadCraft does not show the picture itself yet. Wipeouts show as their frame, and what is under them stays visible.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETables in a DWG come in as the other program drew them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E3D faces in model space come in on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab, not as 2D objects; so do those inside blocks, once per block reference in the model. In a block and on a sheet they are also drawn flat, as outlines. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\"\u003EModel in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers keep their color, on, frozen, locked and plot settings. DEFPOINTS never plots.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDimensions become real, editable CadCraft dimensions where their points can be read. They are first shown exactly as the other program drew them; as soon as you change one, CadCraft redraws it in its own style.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach layout becomes a sheet tab. In the other program every layout has a hidden first viewport that is the sheet itself; CadCraft leaves that one out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-does-not-come-in-yet\"\u003EWhat does not come in yet\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnything CadCraft cannot draw is counted, not guessed at. The &quot;About this file&quot; box in the left panel lists it as &quot;Not shown yet:&quot; followed by each kind of object and how many. Typical cases are tables in a DXF, and 3D solids, regions and meshes. Viewports that are twisted in the file are shown square to the sheet, and the box says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText is drawn in one Helvetica-style font. A label that fits on one line in the original program&#039;s narrower font can wrap onto two lines in CadCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving\"\u003ESaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are three ways to save. Each one downloads a copy.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave\u003C/strong\u003E: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E (also \u003Ccode\u003EQSAVE\u003C/code\u003E). It saves in the drawing&#039;s format: DWG if you opened a DWG, DXF if you opened a DXF. A DWG keeps the version it came in (a 2018 file saves as 2018). A new drawing uses the format and DWG version you last chose in Save as in this browser; if you never chose one, DWG in the AutoCAD 2000 format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave as\u003C/strong\u003E: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy as\u003C/span\u003E dialog offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E, with a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E list (AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E, each with the file name it will give, and the drawing&#039;s current format and version already picked. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E to download, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) to save nothing; the history then says &quot;Not saved.&quot; What you choose sticks: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E uses it for this drawing from then on, and new drawings in this browser start with it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/strong\u003E: type \u003Ccode\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/code\u003E. It always downloads a DXF R12 copy, and the next \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E still saves in the drawing&#039;s own format.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;drawing name&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;drawing name&gt;.dxf\u003C/code\u003E. Every save is a \u003Cstrong\u003Enew download\u003C/strong\u003E; the file you opened is never overwritten or changed. The history confirms it, for example &quot;Saved a copy as Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)). Your original Plan.dxf is unchanged.&quot; For a DXF it says &quot;(DXF R12, opens in any CAD program)&quot;. The lines under it list anything that changed on the way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen CadCraft runs in the desktop app, saving works the way it does in other desktop programs: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E writes the drawing back to the file you opened, in its own format and version, and the history says where it was saved. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E and the exports show the system&#039;s save dialog, and a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.ccsheets\u003C/code\u003E file you double-click opens in CadCraft.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a PDF, plot instead (see below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\"\u003EWhat a DWG keeps and changes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft writes the DWG itself, inside your browser, with the same DWG library it reads with. Pick the version in Save as: the AutoCAD 2000 format (file version AC1015) opens in the most programs, old ones included; the newer formats (2004, AC1018, up to 2018, AC1032) also keep true colors. Nothing is uploaded, and saving a DWG works offline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach version is checked by reading the saved files back with three other programs&#039; DWG readers and with CadCraft itself. A CadCraft DWG has not been opened in AutoCAD itself yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-a-dwg-keeps\"\u003EWhat a DWG keeps\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, arcs, circles, ellipses, points, and polylines with their arc segments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESingle-line text with its alignment and width factor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMulti-line and wrapped text, as multi-line text. The other program wraps it again from the same width.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObject colors: by layer, by block, the 255 standard colors, and true colors in the 2004 format and later.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers with their color, linetype, lineweight and their on, frozen, locked and plot settings; objects&#039; own linetypes, lineweights and linetype scales, the linetype patterns and the drawing&#039;s linetype scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe drawing units and the length format (decimal, feet and inches, fractions…).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks with their base point and attribute definitions, including invisible, constant and preset ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlock references with their scale, rotation and attribute values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESolid fills, and patterned hatches with their pattern, scale and angle (the pattern&#039;s own lines, so other programs draw the same lines CadCraft does).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDimensions, as real dimensions: other programs re-measure them when they are stretched. Each keeps its own settings (text height, arrow size, decimals, length format) and its text, and carries the drawn copy every DWG dimension has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESheets (layouts): each sheet with its paper size and units, what is drawn on it, and its viewports with their view and scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENamed views.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMultileaders, as multileaders.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayer and block names as you typed them, in upper and lower case. (DXF R12 writes them in capitals.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"what-a-dwg-changes\"\u003EWhat a DWG changes\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach of these is listed in the history after you save, when it applies to your drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn CadCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the saved DWG\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrue colors (picked with the color picker), in the 2000 format\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe nearest standard color: the 2000 format has only the 255. Save as 2004 or later to keep them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENamed views of a sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaper-space views: a DWG does not say which sheet, so when CadCraft opens the file again they belong to the first sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMulti-line text with a width factor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENormal letter width: in a DWG the width factor applies only to single-line text\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer or block names with characters DWG does not allow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChanged: a name from a linked drawing such as \u003Ccode\u003EBase|Walls\u003C/code\u003E becomes \u003Ccode\u003EBase$0$Walls\u003C/code\u003E (the way binding a linked drawing names it), and other characters become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"why-r12-and-what-converts\"\u003EWhy R12, and what converts\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you save as DXF, CadCraft writes DXF R12, the version every CAD program can open. It is older than some features, so a few things are written in a simpler form:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn CadCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn the saved R12 file\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMulti-line text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne text object per line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipses\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolylines that follow the ellipse\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatches (from opened files)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir outlines only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrue colors (picked with the color picker)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe nearest standard color\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELineweights, and objects&#039; own linetype scales\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft out: R12 has neither (linetypes are kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer and block names\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWritten in capitals; spaces and other characters become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, and names are cut to 31 characters (&quot;Title block&quot; becomes \u003Ccode\u003ETITLE_BLOCK\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayers set not to plot\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther programs will plot them (CadCraft remembers the setting when the drawing has a sheet)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReal dimensions with their own drawn block, so other programs show them as you see them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultileaders\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe lines, arrowhead and text they draw\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENamed views\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKept, with names in capitals; a sheet&#039;s views come back on the first sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESeveral sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first sheet is the paper space every program shows; the others are kept as blocks named \u003Ccode\u003ECCLAYOUT_1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECCLAYOUT_2\u003C/code\u003E … which CadCraft turns back into sheets when it opens the file\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history lists each of these that applied to your drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo keep working on a drawing over several days, save as DXF and open that DXF next time. Everything CadCraft can draw survives the round trip, including dimensions, blocks and all sheets.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"linked-drawings-external-references\"\u003ELinked drawings (external references)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXA\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink a drawing (xref)\u003C/span\u003E) links another DWG or DXF: pick the file, then click where it goes (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it at 0,0). It comes in as one block named after the file, at the right scale when the two drawings have different units. Its layers and blocks keep their names with the link&#039;s name in front — \u003Ccode\u003EPlan|Walls\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPlan|Door\u003C/code\u003E — so they never mix with yours: turn the linked layers on and off, freeze or lock them like your own. A link is read-only: change the other drawing and reload it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXR\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists the links (loaded or not, which file, how many references); \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E attaches; \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E reloads one from its file (pick the file again: the browser cannot reach your folders on its own); \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E binds one (it becomes ordinary blocks and layers named \u003Ccode\u003EPlan$0$Walls\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E detaches one (its references and layers go). Each is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA drawing you open may link files CadCraft cannot see: it says how many are not loaded. Use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E and pick each file to see them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you save, a loaded link is \u003Cstrong\u003Ebound\u003C/strong\u003E into the file (other programs see ordinary blocks and layers named \u003Ccode\u003EPlan$0$…\u003C/code\u003E), and the history says so. A link that is not loaded stays a link to its file, in DWG and DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-in-3d\"\u003EOpen in 3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in ModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOPENIN3D\u003C/code\u003E) opens the drawing in the 3D modeling app in a new tab, as a DWG, without uploading it. Changes made there stay there; save them there. The menu item shows only where that app can take a handed-over drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plotting-a-pdf\"\u003EPlotting a PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E. The PDF is made inside the browser and downloaded, or opened in a new tab for printing. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-files-go\"\u003EWhere the files go\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery save and every plot is a download, so files land wherever your browser puts downloads: usually the Downloads folder. To choose a folder each time, change the download setting in your browser; \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E shows where.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENothing you open or save is sent to us. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"archicad-projects\"\u003EArchicad projects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings, not Archicad&#039;s own files (\u003Ccode\u003E.pln\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.pla\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.tpl\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.bpn\u003C/code\u003E). Opening one tells you what it is. In Archicad, save the views or layouts you need as DWG and open those here, or export the model as IFC and open it in BimCraft. Where BimCraft can take the file over, CadCraft offers \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in BimCraft\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-the-page-closes-before-you-save\"\u003EIf the page closes before you save\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft keeps a copy of every drawing you change, in this browser only, a moment after you stop editing. If the tab closes, the browser stops or the computer restarts before you save, CadCraft asks \u003Cstrong\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/strong\u003E the next time it starts: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E opens the drawing again, unsaved, in its own tab. Saving the drawing removes its copy. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/recover\" data-topic=\"basics/recover\"\u003ERecover your work\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"online-and-offline\"\u003EOnline and offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening, drawing, saving (DWG and DXF) and plotting all happen on your computer, so they work with no connection once CadCraft has loaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo use CadCraft with no connection at all, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top while you are online. The label counts up (&quot;Saving 40 of 212&quot;) and ends at &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The saved copy includes the DWG reader, about 5 MB in all, so \u003Cstrong\u003EDWG files open offline too\u003C/strong\u003E once it is saved. Click the button again for \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave again (get the newest version)\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the offline copy\u003C/span\u003E. &quot;Could not save&quot; means some files did not download: check the connection and click to try again. &quot;Offline not available&quot; means this browser window cannot keep a copy (private windows and some managed computers).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWithout a saved copy, the DWG reader is fetched the first time you open a DWG after the page loads, so opening a DWG offline can fail; DXF files never need it. Don&#039;t reload the page while offline unless CadCraft is saved.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E for the details.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. Drawings save as DWG or DXF and plot normally, and DXF files open. DWG files open if CadCraft shows &quot;Offline ready&quot;, or if the DWG reader already loaded in this tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"constraints-in-files\"\u003EConstraints in files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConstraints and parameters (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003EConstrain a drawing\u003C/a\u003E) are saved in both DWG and DXF. Each constrained object carries a small CadCraft note (extended data), and an unused block named \u003Ccode\u003ECADCRAFT_PARAMETRIC\u003C/code\u003E holds the constraints and the parameter table. Other programs open the drawing normally and ignore both; they may delete the unused block when they purge. The notes refer to objects by their own key, not by the file&#039;s handles, so a program that renumbers handles does not break them. When another program has deleted or changed a constrained object, CadCraft drops the constraints on it when it opens the file and says how many. A file without them opens without constraints.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sketch exported from a part modeler (\u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E) opens too: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\"\u003EMove sketches to and from other part modelers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-available-yet\"\u003ENot available yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMultileaders as multileader objects in a saved DXF (R12 has none: they are saved as what they draw).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPictures (images) shown in the drawing, and wipeouts that hide what is under them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving a newer DXF version than R12.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA desktop version that works outside the browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E for the full list.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"At a glance Format Open Save Notes DXF (text, any version) Yes Yes, as DXF R12 or DXF 2000 Binary DXF is refused: save it as text DXF first. DWG Yes Yes, as DWG (AutoCAD 2000, 2004, 2007, 2010, 2013 or 2018) Read and written by CadCraft's own DWG library (AutoCAD R13 to 2018 files). Your DWG is never changed. Damaged files, or versions the reader does not handle, are refused with a message. PDF No Yes, by plotting Vector PDF of a sheet, or of the model at a scale. Each drawing you open (or start with New) gets its own tab over the drawing area; a new drawing nobody has touched yet is used instead of adding a tab. Click a tab to switch, its × to close it (CadCraft asks first if there are unsaved changes), or tile the open drawings side by side: see Split the screen. Opening a drawing Press Ctrl+O, click Open, or type OPEN, then choose the file. Or drag a .dwg or .dxf file onto the drawing area. The file is read inside your browser. It is not uploaded anywhere. The history reports how many objects and layers came in and how long it took, and the Drawing section of the left panel shows the format, for example \"DWG 2018 (AC1032)\". The DWG reader is part of CadCraft and is fetched with the first DWG you open after the page loads (a few hundred KB), so there is no separate download to wait for. It reads the file in the background, so the page keeps responding while a big DWG opens. What comes in Lines, arcs, circles, polylines (with arc segments), ellipses, points, text and multi-line text, solids, hatches (the standard patterns drawn as their lines, others as a flat tone), block references with their attributes (values you can edit; see Block attributes), dimensions, and layouts with their viewports. Splines come in as polylines that follow the curve. Multileaders with one leader line and text come in as CadCraft multileaders you can edit (see MLEADER). Others (with a block, several leader lines or curved lines) are drawn as the other program drew them and move as one; the history says how many. Pictures (images) show as a frame with the picture's file name: CadCraft does not show the picture itself yet. Wipeouts show as their frame, and what is under them stays visible. Tables in a DWG come in as the other program drew them. 3D faces in model space come in on the 3D tab, not as 2D objects; so do those inside blocks, once per block reference in the model. In a block and on a sheet they are also drawn flat, as outlines. See Model in 3D. Layers keep their color, on, frozen, locked and plot settings. DEFPOINTS never plots. Dimensions become real, editable CadCraft dimensions where their points can be read. They are first shown exactly as the other program drew them; as soon as you change one, CadCraft redraws it in its own style. Each layout becomes a sheet tab. In the other program every layout has a hidden first viewport that is the sheet itself; CadCraft leaves that one out. What does not come in yet Anything CadCraft cannot draw is counted, not guessed at. The \"About this file\" box in the left panel lists it as \"Not shown yet:\" followed by each kind of object and how many. Typical cases are tables in a DXF, and 3D solids, regions and meshes. Viewports that are twisted in the file are shown square to the sheet, and the box says so. Text is drawn in one Helvetica-style font. A label that fits on one line in the original program's narrower font can wrap onto two lines in CadCraft. Saving There are three ways to save. Each one downloads a copy. Save: press Ctrl+S, click Save, or type SAVE (also QSAVE). It saves in the drawing's format: DWG if you opened a DWG, DXF if you opened a DXF. A DWG keeps the version it came in (a 2018 file saves as 2018). A new drawing uses the format and DWG version you last chose in Save as in this browser; if you never chose one, DWG in the AutoCAD 2000 format. Save as: press Ctrl+Shift+S, click Save as, or type SAVEAS. The Save a copy as dialog offers DWG, with a Version list (AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000), DXF R12 and DXF 2000, each with the file name it will give, and the drawing's current format and version already picked. Click Save to download, or Cancel (or Esc) to save nothing; the history then says \"Not saved.\" What you choose sticks: Ctrl+S uses it for this drawing from then on, and new drawings in this browser start with it. DXFOUT: type DXFOUT. It always downloads a DXF R12 copy, and the next Ctrl+S still saves in the drawing's own format. The browser downloads \u003Cdrawing name\u003E.dwg or \u003Cdrawing name\u003E.dxf. Every save is a new download; the file you opened is never overwritten or changed. The history confirms it, for example \"Saved a copy as Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)). Your original Plan.dxf is unchanged.\" For a DXF it says \"(DXF R12, opens in any CAD program)\". The lines under it list anything that changed on the way. When CadCraft runs in the desktop app, saving works the way it does in other desktop programs: Ctrl+S writes the drawing back to the file you opened, in its own format and version, and the history says where it was saved. Save as and the exports show the system's save dialog, and a .dwg, .dxf or .ccsheets file you double-click opens in CadCraft. For a PDF, plot instead (see below). What a DWG keeps and changes CadCraft writes the DWG itself, inside your browser, with the same DWG library it reads with. Pick the version in Save as: the AutoCAD 2000 format (file version AC1015) opens in the most programs, old ones included; the newer formats (2004, AC1018, up to 2018, AC1032) also keep true colors. Nothing is uploaded, and saving a DWG works offline. Each version is checked by reading the saved files back with three other programs' DWG readers and with CadCraft itself. A CadCraft DWG has not been opened in AutoCAD itself yet. What a DWG keeps Lines, arcs, circles, ellipses, points, and polylines with their arc segments. Single-line text with its alignment and width factor. Multi-line and wrapped text, as multi-line text. The other program wraps it again from the same width. Object colors: by layer, by block, the 255 standard colors, and true colors in the 2004 format and later. Layers with their color, linetype, lineweight and their on, frozen, locked and plot settings; objects' own linetypes, lineweights and linetype scales, the linetype patterns and the drawing's linetype scale. The drawing units and the length format (decimal, feet and inches, fractions…). Blocks with their base point and attribute definitions, including invisible, constant and preset ones. Block references with their scale, rotation and attribute values. Solid fills, and patterned hatches with their pattern, scale and angle (the pattern's own lines, so other programs draw the same lines CadCraft does). Dimensions, as real dimensions: other programs re-measure them when they are stretched. Each keeps its own settings (text height, arrow size, decimals, length format) and its text, and carries the drawn copy every DWG dimension has. Sheets (layouts): each sheet with its paper size and units, what is drawn on it, and its viewports with their view and scale. Named views. Multileaders, as multileaders. Layer and block names as you typed them, in upper and lower case. (DXF R12 writes them in capitals.) What a DWG changes Each of these is listed in the history after you save, when it applies to your drawing. In CadCraft In the saved DWG True colors (picked with the color picker), in the 2000 format The nearest standard color: the 2000 format has only the 255. Save as 2004 or later to keep them Named views of a sheet Paper-space views: a DWG does not say which sheet, so when CadCraft opens the file again they belong to the first sheet Multi-line text with a width factor Normal letter width: in a DWG the width factor applies only to single-line text Layer or block names with characters DWG does not allow Changed: a name from a linked drawing such as Base|Walls becomes Base$0$Walls (the way binding a linked drawing names it), and other characters become _ Why R12, and what converts When you save as DXF, CadCraft writes DXF R12, the version every CAD program can open. It is older than some features, so a few things are written in a simpler form: In CadCraft In the saved R12 file Multi-line text One text object per line Ellipses Polylines that follow the ellipse Hatches (from opened files) Their outlines only True colors (picked with the color picker) The nearest standard color Lineweights, and objects' own linetype scales Left out: R12 has neither (linetypes are kept) Layer and block names Written in capitals; spaces and other characters become _, and names are cut to 31 characters (\"Title block\" becomes TITLE_BLOCK) Layers set not to plot Other programs will plot them (CadCraft remembers the setting when the drawing has a sheet) Dimensions Real dimensions with their own drawn block, so other programs show them as you see them Multileaders The lines, arrowhead and text they draw Named views Kept, with names in capitals; a sheet's views come back on the first sheet Several sheets The first sheet is the paper space every program shows; the others are kept as blocks named CCLAYOUT_1, CCLAYOUT_2 … which CadCraft turns back into sheets when it opens the file The history lists each of these that applied to your drawing. To keep working on a drawing over several days, save as DXF and open that DXF next time. Everything CadCraft can draw survives the round trip, including dimensions, blocks and all sheets. Linked drawings (external references) XATTACH (XA, or Blocks › More › Link a drawing (xref)) links another DWG or DXF: pick the file, then click where it goes (Enter puts it at 0,0). It comes in as one block named after the file, at the right scale when the two drawings have different units. Its layers and blocks keep their names with the link's name in front — Plan|Walls, Plan|Door — so they never mix with yours: turn the linked layers on and off, freeze or lock them like your own. A link is read-only: change the other drawing and reload it. XREF (XR): ? lists the links (loaded or not, which file, how many references); A attaches; R reloads one from its file (pick the file again: the browser cannot reach your folders on its own); B binds one (it becomes ordinary blocks and layers named Plan$0$Walls); D detaches one (its references and layers go). Each is one undo step. A drawing you open may link files CadCraft cannot see: it says how many are not loaded. Use XREF, R and pick each file to see them. When you save, a loaded link is bound into the file (other programs see ordinary blocks and layers named Plan$0$…), and the history says so. A link that is not loaded stays a link to its file, in DWG and DXF. Open in 3D View › More › Open in ModelCraft (OPENIN3D) opens the drawing in the 3D modeling app in a new tab, as a DWG, without uploading it. Changes made there stay there; save them there. The menu item shows only where that app can take a handed-over drawing. Plotting a PDF Press Ctrl+P, click Plot, or type PLOT. The PDF is made inside the browser and downloaded, or opened in a new tab for printing. See Plot to PDF. Where the files go Every save and every plot is a download, so files land wherever your browser puts downloads: usually the Downloads folder. To choose a folder each time, change the download setting in your browser; Browsers and computers shows where. Nothing you open or save is sent to us. See Where your files go. Archicad projects CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings, not Archicad's own files (.pln, .pla, .tpl, .bpn). Opening one tells you what it is. In Archicad, save the views or layouts you need as DWG and open those here, or export the model as IFC and open it in BimCraft. Where BimCraft can take the file over, CadCraft offers Open in BimCraft. If the page closes before you save CadCraft keeps a copy of every drawing you change, in this browser only, a moment after you stop editing. If the tab closes, the browser stops or the computer restarts before you save, CadCraft asks Recover your work? the next time it starts: Recover opens the drawing again, unsaved, in its own tab. Saving the drawing removes its copy. See Recover your work. Online and offline Opening, drawing, saving (DWG and DXF) and plotting all happen on your computer, so they work with no connection once CadCraft has loaded. To use CadCraft with no connection at all, click Save for offline in the bar at the top while you are online. The label counts up (\"Saving 40 of 212\") and ends at \"Offline ready\". The saved copy includes the DWG reader, about 5 MB in all, so DWG files open offline too once it is saved. Click the button again for Save again (get the newest version) or Remove the offline copy. \"Could not save\" means some files did not download: check the connection and click to try again. \"Offline not available\" means this browser window cannot keep a copy (private windows and some managed computers). Without a saved copy, the DWG reader is fetched the first time you open a DWG after the page loads, so opening a DWG offline can fail; DXF files never need it. Don't reload the page while offline unless CadCraft is saved. See Working offline for the details. You are offline now. Drawings save as DWG or DXF and plot normally, and DXF files open. DWG files open if CadCraft shows \"Offline ready\", or if the DWG reader already loaded in this tab. Constraints in files Constraints and parameters (see Constrain a drawing) are saved in both DWG and DXF. Each constrained object carries a small CadCraft note (extended data), and an unused block named CADCRAFT_PARAMETRIC holds the constraints and the parameter table. Other programs open the drawing normally and ignore both; they may delete the unused block when they purge. The notes refer to objects by their own key, not by the file's handles, so a program that renumbers handles does not break them. When another program has deleted or changed a constrained object, CadCraft drops the constraints on it when it opens the file and says how many. A file without them opens without constraints. A sketch exported from a part modeler (.json) opens too: see Move sketches to and from other part modelers. Not available yet Multileaders as multileader objects in a saved DXF (R12 has none: they are saved as what they draw). Pictures (images) shown in the drawing, and wipeouts that hide what is under them. Saving a newer DXF version than R12. Cloud saving and sync between computers. A desktop version that works outside the browser. See Not built yet for the full list."},{"slug":"draw-with-coordinates","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Draw with coordinates","summary":"Draw an exact floor outline by typing absolute, relative and polar coordinates, and by direct distance entry.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["line","pline","polyline","coordinates","relative","polar","absolute","direct distance","@","typing points","exact","ortho"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"before-you-start","text":"Before you start","level":2},{"id":"absolute-coordinates-the-first-point","text":"Absolute coordinates: the first point","level":2},{"id":"relative-coordinates-dx-dy","text":"Relative coordinates: @dx,dy","level":2},{"id":"direct-distance-entry-a-bare-number","text":"Direct distance entry: a bare number","level":2},{"id":"polar-coordinates-distance-angle","text":"Polar coordinates: @distance\u003Cangle","level":2},{"id":"the-same-outline-as-one-polyline","text":"The same outline as one polyline","level":2},{"id":"options-while-drawing","text":"Options while drawing","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe outline of an L-shaped room, 6000 by 4500 with a 2000 by 1500 notch, drawn exactly, plus a wall at 30 degrees. You will use all four ways of typing a point. Work in millimeters: one drawing unit stands for one millimeter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe quick reference for every format is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-start\"\u003EBefore you start\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen CadCraft. If a drawing is open, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so that nothing is selected. The right panel reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimetres\u003C/span\u003E. Nothing is resized; this only tells sheets and plots what a unit means.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"absolute-coordinates-the-first-point\"\u003EAbsolute coordinates: the first point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn absolute coordinate is a position measured from the origin, typed as \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line is now rubber-banding from the origin to the crosshair. The coordinates in the status bar show the distance and angle from the start point as you move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"relative-coordinates-dx-dy\"\u003ERelative coordinates: @dx,dy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA relative coordinate is typed as \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E: so far across, so far up, from the last point. Negative numbers go left or down.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A 6000 line runs to the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,3000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-2000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,1500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see four connected lines climbing up the right side of the room.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"direct-distance-entry-a-bare-number\"\u003EDirect distance entry: a bare number\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you type a single number, CadCraft goes that distance in the direction of the crosshair. Turn on Ortho so the direction snaps to horizontal or vertical.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar. The status bar says &quot;Ortho on&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to the left of the last point. The rubber band stays horizontal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The line goes 4000 to the left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair below the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. You are back at the start point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the empty prompt to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EF8 may control media playback on a Mac keyboard. Hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E and press F8, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou now have a closed L-shaped outline made of six separate lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E to see all of it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"polar-coordinates-distance-angle\"\u003EPolar coordinates: @distance&lt;angle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA polar coordinate is typed as \u003Ccode\u003E@distance&lt;angle\u003C/code\u003E. Angles are in degrees, measured counterclockwise from the positive X direction: 0 is right, 90 is up, 180 is left, 270 (or -90) is down.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E6000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start at the lower-right corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@2500&lt;-30\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A 2500 line leaves the corner, heading down and to the right at 30 degrees below horizontal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA polar coordinate without \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E, such as \u003Ccode\u003E2500&lt;30\u003C/code\u003E, is measured from the origin instead of from the last point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-outline-as-one-polyline\"\u003EThe same outline as one polyline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESix separate lines are fine for many jobs, but a polyline is one object: it offsets, measures and selects as a whole. Use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E with the same input.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,-6000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start below the first outline.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,3000\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-2000,0\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,1500\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-4000,0\u003C/code\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The polyline closes and the command ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new outline. The right panel shows &quot;Polyline&quot;, its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVertices\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E and, because it is closed, its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe area should read 24000000 (square millimeters, which is 24 square meters).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"options-while-drawing\"\u003EOptions while drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the last segment without leaving the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E closes back to the first point once there are at least two segments.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E switches to arc segments (each arc continues smoothly from the segment before), and \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E switches back to lines. In arc mode, the close option is \u003Ccode\u003ECL\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point:\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E starts from the last point you gave, so you can carry on where you stopped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping \u003Ccode\u003E6000,0\u003C/code\u003E when you meant \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E Without \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E, the point is measured from the origin, not from the last point, so the line shoots off somewhere unexpected. In CadCraft, \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E is always absolute. Press \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA comma typed as a decimal point.\u003C/strong\u003E Decimals use a period: \u003Ccode\u003E1250.5\u003C/code\u003E. A comma always separates X from Y.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe direct distance went the wrong way.\u003C/strong\u003E A bare number follows the crosshair. Move the crosshair to the side you want before you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and turn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E for straight lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Point or option keyword required.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft did not understand what you typed. Check for spaces inside the number or a missing comma, and type it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFeet and inches.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft reads plain numbers only. Type \u003Ccode\u003E150\u003C/code\u003E for 12&#039;-6&quot; in an inch drawing, or \u003Ccode\u003E12.5\u003C/code\u003E in a foot drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make The outline of an L-shaped room, 6000 by 4500 with a 2000 by 1500 notch, drawn exactly, plus a wall at 30 degrees. You will use all four ways of typing a point. Work in millimeters: one drawing unit stands for one millimeter. The quick reference for every format is in Coordinate and distance entry. Before you start Open CadCraft. If a drawing is open, click New first. Press Esc so that nothing is selected. The right panel reads Current settings. Set Units to Millimetres. Nothing is resized; this only tells sheets and plots what a unit means. Absolute coordinates: the first point An absolute coordinate is a position measured from the origin, typed as x,y. Type L and press Enter. The prompt reads Specify first point:. Type 0,0 and press Enter. The line is now rubber-banding from the origin to the crosshair. The coordinates in the status bar show the distance and angle from the start point as you move. Relative coordinates: @dx,dy A relative coordinate is typed as @dx,dy: so far across, so far up, from the last point. Negative numbers go left or down. Type @6000,0 and press Enter. A 6000 line runs to the right. Type @0,3000 and press Enter. Type @-2000,0 and press Enter. Type @0,1500 and press Enter. You should see four connected lines climbing up the right side of the room. Direct distance entry: a bare number When you type a single number, CadCraft goes that distance in the direction of the crosshair. Turn on Ortho so the direction snaps to horizontal or vertical. Press F8, or click Ortho in the status bar. The status bar says \"Ortho on\". Move the crosshair to the left of the last point. The rubber band stays horizontal. Type 4000 and press Enter. The line goes 4000 to the left. Move the crosshair below the last point. Type 4500 and press Enter. You are back at the start point. Press Enter on the empty prompt to end the command. F8 may control media playback on a Mac keyboard. Hold fn and press F8, or click Ortho. See Keys, mice and trackpads. You now have a closed L-shaped outline made of six separate lines. Click Extents to see all of it. Polar coordinates: @distance\u003Cangle A polar coordinate is typed as @distance\u003Cangle. Angles are in degrees, measured counterclockwise from the positive X direction: 0 is right, 90 is up, 180 is left, 270 (or -90) is down. Type L and press Enter. Type 6000,0 and press Enter to start at the lower-right corner. Type @2500\u003C-30 and press Enter. A 2500 line leaves the corner, heading down and to the right at 30 degrees below horizontal. Press Enter to end the command. A polar coordinate without @, such as 2500\u003C30, is measured from the origin instead of from the last point. The same outline as one polyline Six separate lines are fine for many jobs, but a polyline is one object: it offsets, measures and selects as a whole. Use PLINE with the same input. Type PL and press Enter. Type 0,-6000 and press Enter to start below the first outline. Type @6000,0, then press Enter. Type @0,3000, then press Enter. Type @-2000,0, then press Enter. Type @0,1500, then press Enter. Type @-4000,0, then press Enter. Type C and press Enter. The polyline closes and the command ends. Click the new outline. The right panel shows \"Polyline\", its Vertices, Length and, because it is closed, its Area. The area should read 24000000 (square millimeters, which is 24 square meters). Options while drawing In LINE and PLINE, type U and press Enter to take back the last segment without leaving the command. In LINE, C closes back to the first point once there are at least two segments. In PLINE, A switches to arc segments (each arc continues smoothly from the segment before), and L switches back to lines. In arc mode, the close option is CL. Pressing Enter at Specify first point: in LINE starts from the last point you gave, so you can carry on where you stopped. Common mistakes Typing 6000,0 when you meant @6000,0. Without @, the point is measured from the origin, not from the last point, so the line shoots off somewhere unexpected. In CadCraft, x,y is always absolute. Press U and Enter to take it back. A comma typed as a decimal point. Decimals use a period: 1250.5. A comma always separates X from Y. The direct distance went the wrong way. A bare number follows the crosshair. Move the crosshair to the side you want before you press Enter, and turn on Ortho for straight lines. \"Point or option keyword required.\" CadCraft did not understand what you typed. Check for spaces inside the number or a missing comma, and type it again. Feet and inches. CadCraft reads plain numbers only. Type 150 for 12'-6\" in an inch drawing, or 12.5 in a foot drawing."},{"slug":"object-snaps","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Snap to points on objects","summary":"Use object snaps, Ortho and the grid to pick exact endpoints, midpoints, centers and intersections with the mouse.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["osnap","object snap","endpoint","midpoint","center","intersection","perpendicular","quadrant","nearest","node","insertion","ortho","grid","f3","f8","f7","polar tracking","polar","polarang","object snap tracking","otrack","tracking","f10","f11"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"how-snapping-works","text":"How snapping works","level":2},{"id":"choose-your-snaps","text":"Choose your snaps","level":2},{"id":"steps","text":"Steps","level":2},{"id":"skip-a-snap-for-one-click","text":"Skip a snap for one click","level":2},{"id":"ortho","text":"Ortho","level":2},{"id":"polar-tracking","text":"Polar tracking","level":2},{"id":"object-snap-tracking","text":"Object snap tracking","level":2},{"id":"grid","text":"Grid","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-your-computer","text":"Function keys on your computer","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA rectangle with its two diagonals, a circle centered where they cross, and a line from the middle of one side square onto the circle. Every point is picked with the mouse, and every point is exact.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-snapping-works\"\u003EHow snapping works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a command asks for a point, CadCraft looks for snap points within about 10 pixels of the crosshair. When it finds one, a marker and a label appear (&quot;Endpoint&quot;, &quot;Intersection&quot;), and a click lands \u003Cstrong\u003Eexactly\u003C/strong\u003E on that point, not where the crosshair is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESnaps are on when \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar is pressed. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns them on and off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe snaps you can use are:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESnap\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELands on\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEndpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe ends of lines, arcs and polyline segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMidpoint\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of lines, arcs and polyline segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center of circles and arcs, and of polyline arc segments\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQuadrant\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe 0, 90, 180 and 270 degree points of circles and arcs\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIntersection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere two objects cross\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPerpendicular\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe point on an object square to the last point (needs a base point)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENearest\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe nearest point on any object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENode\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPoint objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsertion\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe insertion point of text and block references\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll of them are on by default except Nearest, which tends to win when you want something else.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-your-snaps\"\u003EChoose your snaps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the small arrow next to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObject snaps\u003C/span\u003E list opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick or untick snaps. Changes apply at once.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the arrow again to close the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft remembers your choice, and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E settings, in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps\"\u003ESteps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@4000,3000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A rectangle appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to the lower-left corner. The marker shows &quot;Endpoint&quot;. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the upper-right corner until &quot;Endpoint&quot; shows. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to repeat \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, and draw the other diagonal from the lower-right corner to the upper-left corner the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start a circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to where the diagonals cross. The marker shows &quot;Intersection&quot;. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E800\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see a circle centered exactly on the crossing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove to the middle of the left side of the rectangle until &quot;Midpoint&quot; shows. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair onto the circle, on its left side. The marker shows &quot;Perpendicular&quot; or &quot;Quadrant&quot;; both are the same point here. Click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new line runs from the middle of the left side and stops exactly on the circle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"skip-a-snap-for-one-click\"\u003ESkip a snap for one click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click. Object snaps are ignored for that click, and the point goes exactly where the crosshair is (or along the Ortho direction, if Ortho is on).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"ortho\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) keeps the next point straight across or straight up and down from the base point. It applies to clicks and to direct distance entry (a bare number typed at the prompt). Object snaps win over Ortho: if a snap marker shows, the click goes to the snap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"polar-tracking\"\u003EPolar tracking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) is Ortho at more angles. While a command has a base point (the last point of a line, a move&#039;s base point), the pointer lines up whenever it comes near a ray from it at a multiple of the polar step: a dotted line shows, and a label by the crosshair reads \u003Ccode\u003EPolar: 1200 &lt; 30°\u003C/code\u003E. Click to use that point, or type a distance: \u003Ccode\u003E1200\u003C/code\u003E goes exactly 1200 along the ray.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe step is 90° until you change it: type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and type \u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E (or 45, 22.5, 15 …). Polar and Ortho exclude each other: turning one on turns the other off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"object-snap-tracking\"\u003EObject snap tracking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) lines the pointer up with snap points you have rested on, so you can pick a point level with one corner and above another without drawing helper lines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETurn on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E, and start a command that asks for a point (\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERest the pointer on a snap point, such as a corner, for half a second, without clicking. A small cross marks it: it is acquired. Rest on it again to drop it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the pointer straight away from it, horizontally or vertically. A dotted line follows the pointer and the label reads \u003Ccode\u003ETrack: 600 &lt; 90°\u003C/code\u003E. Click, or type a distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAcquire a second point, and move to where the vertical through one meets the horizontal through the other: the pointer snaps to that crossing (\u003Ccode\u003ETrack: where two lines meet\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E on as well, acquired points track at every polar angle. Up to seven points are kept, until the command ends. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E turns tracking off for one click, like snaps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grid\"\u003EGrid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F7\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows a grid of dots behind the drawing, spaced in powers of ten that change as you zoom. It is a visual guide only: points do not snap to the grid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-your-computer\"\u003EFunction keys on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Polar and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Track.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 usually control Mission Control and media. Hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E with the key, or turn on standard function keys, as described in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E. The status bar buttons always work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, F7 is also the key for caret browsing. If Firefox asks whether to turn caret browsing on, choose \u003Cstrong\u003ENo\u003C/strong\u003E. Use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E button if the question keeps coming back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a touch screen there is no hover, so the snap marker only appears where your finger touches. Zoom in close for small targets, or type coordinates instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe click went to the wrong point.\u003C/strong\u003E Look at the marker label before you click: Endpoint wins over Midpoint when both are near. Zoom in, or untick snaps you do not need.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo marker appears.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E is off, or the snap you want is unticked, or the object is on a layer that is off or frozen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESnaps grab the wrong thing in a busy drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E Zoom in. The snap distance is measured in screen pixels, so zooming in makes it smaller on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting Shift to give a straight line.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft, Shift turns snaps off for one click. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E for straight lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpecting points to land on grid dots.\u003C/strong\u003E The grid does not snap. Type coordinates for round numbers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A rectangle with its two diagonals, a circle centered where they cross, and a line from the middle of one side square onto the circle. Every point is picked with the mouse, and every point is exact. How snapping works While a command asks for a point, CadCraft looks for snap points within about 10 pixels of the crosshair. When it finds one, a marker and a label appear (\"Endpoint\", \"Intersection\"), and a click lands exactly on that point, not where the crosshair is. Snaps are on when Snap in the status bar is pressed. F3 turns them on and off. The snaps you can use are: Snap Lands on Endpoint The ends of lines, arcs and polyline segments Midpoint The middle of lines, arcs and polyline segments Center The center of circles and arcs, and of polyline arc segments Quadrant The 0, 90, 180 and 270 degree points of circles and arcs Intersection Where two objects cross Perpendicular The point on an object square to the last point (needs a base point) Nearest The nearest point on any object Node Point objects Insertion The insertion point of text and block references All of them are on by default except Nearest, which tends to win when you want something else. Choose your snaps Click the small arrow next to Snap in the status bar. The Object snaps list opens. Tick or untick snaps. Changes apply at once. Click the arrow again to close the list. CadCraft remembers your choice, and the Grid, Ortho and Snap settings, in this browser. Steps Type REC and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @4000,3000 and press Enter. A rectangle appears. Click Extents. Type L and press Enter. Move the crosshair to the lower-left corner. The marker shows \"Endpoint\". Click. Move to the upper-right corner until \"Endpoint\" shows. Click. Press Enter to end the line. Press Enter again to repeat LINE, and draw the other diagonal from the lower-right corner to the upper-left corner the same way. Type C and press Enter to start a circle. Move the crosshair to where the diagonals cross. The marker shows \"Intersection\". Click. Type 800 and press Enter. You should see a circle centered exactly on the crossing. Type L and press Enter. Move to the middle of the left side of the rectangle until \"Midpoint\" shows. Click. Move the crosshair onto the circle, on its left side. The marker shows \"Perpendicular\" or \"Quadrant\"; both are the same point here. Click. Press Enter. The new line runs from the middle of the left side and stops exactly on the circle. Skip a snap for one click Hold Shift while you click. Object snaps are ignored for that click, and the point goes exactly where the crosshair is (or along the Ortho direction, if Ortho is on). Ortho Ortho (F8) keeps the next point straight across or straight up and down from the base point. It applies to clicks and to direct distance entry (a bare number typed at the prompt). Object snaps win over Ortho: if a snap marker shows, the click goes to the snap. Polar tracking Polar (F10) is Ortho at more angles. While a command has a base point (the last point of a line, a move's base point), the pointer lines up whenever it comes near a ray from it at a multiple of the polar step: a dotted line shows, and a label by the crosshair reads Polar: 1200 \u003C 30°. Click to use that point, or type a distance: 1200 goes exactly 1200 along the ray. The step is 90° until you change it: type POLARANG, press Enter, and type 30 (or 45, 22.5, 15 …). Polar and Ortho exclude each other: turning one on turns the other off. Object snap tracking Track (F11) lines the pointer up with snap points you have rested on, so you can pick a point level with one corner and above another without drawing helper lines. Turn on Snap and Track, and start a command that asks for a point (L). Rest the pointer on a snap point, such as a corner, for half a second, without clicking. A small cross marks it: it is acquired. Rest on it again to drop it. Move the pointer straight away from it, horizontally or vertically. A dotted line follows the pointer and the label reads Track: 600 \u003C 90°. Click, or type a distance. Acquire a second point, and move to where the vertical through one meets the horizontal through the other: the pointer snaps to that crossing (Track: where two lines meet). With Polar on as well, acquired points track at every polar angle. Up to seven points are kept, until the command ends. Shift turns tracking off for one click, like snaps. Grid Grid (F7) shows a grid of dots behind the drawing, spaced in powers of ten that change as you zoom. It is a visual guide only: points do not snap to the grid. Function keys on your computer F10 is Polar and F11 is Track. On a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 usually control Mission Control and media. Hold fn with the key, or turn on standard function keys, as described in Keys, mice and trackpads. The status bar buttons always work. In Firefox, F7 is also the key for caret browsing. If Firefox asks whether to turn caret browsing on, choose No. Use the Grid button if the question keeps coming back. On a touch screen there is no hover, so the snap marker only appears where your finger touches. Zoom in close for small targets, or type coordinates instead. Common mistakes The click went to the wrong point. Look at the marker label before you click: Endpoint wins over Midpoint when both are near. Zoom in, or untick snaps you do not need. No marker appears. Snap is off, or the snap you want is unticked, or the object is on a layer that is off or frozen. Snaps grab the wrong thing in a busy drawing. Zoom in. The snap distance is measured in screen pixels, so zooming in makes it smaller on the drawing. Expecting Shift to give a straight line. In CadCraft, Shift turns snaps off for one click. Use Ortho for straight lines. Expecting points to land on grid dots. The grid does not snap. Type coordinates for round numbers."},{"slug":"select-objects","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Select objects","summary":"Pick objects one by one, with a window or a crossing, before or during a command, and take objects back out of a selection.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["select","selection","window","crossing","noun-verb","pick","all","last","previous","fence","remove","add","tab","cycle","overlapping","qselect","quick select","filter","select similar","selectsimilar","shift","deselect","ctrl+a","pickfirst"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"pick-one-object-at-a-time","text":"Pick one object at a time","level":2},{"id":"window-and-crossing","text":"Window and crossing","level":2},{"id":"select-then-run-a-command","text":"Select, then run a command","level":2},{"id":"run-a-command-then-select","text":"Run a command, then select","level":2},{"id":"pick-one-of-several-objects-on-top-of-each-other","text":"Pick one of several objects on top of each other","level":2},{"id":"select-by-a-rule-quick-select","text":"Select by a rule: Quick select","level":2},{"id":"select-similar","text":"Select similar","level":2},{"id":"select-now-use-later","text":"Select now, use later","level":2},{"id":"select-everything","text":"Select everything","level":2},{"id":"what-cannot-be-selected","text":"What cannot be selected","level":2},{"id":"erase-quickly","text":"Erase quickly","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to choose the objects a command works on. CadCraft has two ways to work, and both are fine:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESelect first, then the command\u003C/strong\u003E (noun-verb). Select objects, then type \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E for Move. The command takes your selection and goes straight on to its next question.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECommand first, then select\u003C/strong\u003E (verb-noun). Type \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E; the command asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E; you select, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-one-object-at-a-time\"\u003EPick one object at a time\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith no command running, click a line. It is highlighted and its grips (small squares) appear. The right panel shows its properties.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick another object. It is added to the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click a selected object. It is taken out of the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Nothing is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"window-and-crossing\"\u003EWindow and crossing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick once on empty space, move, and click again: a rectangle. Pressing and dragging draws a freehand lasso instead, with the same two kinds (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E; Settings can make a drag draw the rectangle). Which objects you get depends on the direction:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou move\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBox\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelects\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft to right\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly objects \u003Cstrong\u003Efully inside\u003C/strong\u003E the box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight to left\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrossing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects inside the box \u003Cstrong\u003Eand\u003C/strong\u003E objects it touches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a few overlapping lines and circles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick empty space to the left of some objects, move right, and click again. Only the objects completely inside are selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick empty space to the right, move left across some objects, and click again. Everything the box touches is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and draw a box or a lasso over some selected objects. They come out of the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-then-run-a-command\"\u003ESelect, then run a command\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect two circles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says \u003Ccode\u003E2 found\u003C/code\u003E, and the prompt goes straight to \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E. Finish the move (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/modify-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/modify-objects\"\u003EMove, copy, rotate, scale and mirror\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a command ends, the selection is cleared, so the next command starts fresh.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECommands that take a selection this way: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E always ask you to pick objects one at a time instead; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E take a pick or a path you drag through the pieces (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-a-command-then-select\"\u003ERun a command, then select\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake sure nothing is selected (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick objects, or drag windows and crossings. The history counts each pick (\u003Ccode\u003E1 found\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3 found\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to finish selecting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe objects are erased and the history says how many.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt you can also type:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: every object you can edit in the open tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ELAST\u003C/code\u003E), then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the most recently drawn object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Previous): the objects the last command worked on, if they are still there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (Fence): click or type points along a line; every object the line crosses is added. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the fence; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back its last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E (Window) or \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (Crossing): two corners, whichever way you drag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (Remove): from now on, clicks, windows and fences take objects \u003Cstrong\u003Eout\u003C/strong\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ERemove objects:\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (Add) switches back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E: the list of these in the history.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese options are not shown in the prompt, as in AutoCAD.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pick-one-of-several-objects-on-top-of-each-other\"\u003EPick one of several objects on top of each other\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere objects overlap or touch, a click takes the nearest one. To get another:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold the pointer still over the spot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The next object there is highlighted, and the status bar says which one it is (&quot;2 of 3: Line on layer Walls&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again until the one you want is highlighted, then click without moving.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMoving the pointer starts over. This works with no command running, at \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E and wherever a command asks you to pick one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-by-a-rule-quick-select\"\u003ESelect by a rule: Quick select\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EQS\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E) selects every object that matches a rule, such as every circle with a radius under 50, or every block reference called DOOR-900.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EObject type\u003C/span\u003E (the list counts each type), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperty\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, and for some types \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERadius\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText contents\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlock name\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch pattern\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOperator\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EValue\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E matches with wildcards (\u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-*\u003C/code\u003E); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E takes every object of the type. The count at the bottom shows how many match as you choose.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHow to apply\u003C/span\u003E: include the matches, or everything else. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply to\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent selection\u003C/span\u003E narrows what you had selected; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAppend to current selection\u003C/span\u003E adds to it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect\u003C/span\u003E. Then run the command you want: it uses the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-similar\"\u003ESelect similar\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one or more objects and type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESELECTSIMILAR\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESIM\u003C/code\u003E), or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect similar\u003C/span\u003E. Every object of the same type on the same layer and with the same color is selected; block references also need the same block, hatches the same pattern. Type \u003Ccode\u003ESE\u003C/code\u003E at its \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E prompt to choose what must match.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-now-use-later\"\u003ESelect now, use later\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ESELECT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, choose objects with any of the ways above, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. They stay selected for the next command, and \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E picks them again at a later \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-everything\"\u003ESelect everything\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith no command running, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect all\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu, to select every object you can edit on the open tab. If you have typed something at the command line, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selects that text instead: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to clear it first. At a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt, type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect none\u003C/span\u003E in the same menu, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, clears the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you would rather have each click replace the selection (and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E add to it), set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClicking objects\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplaces the selection\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-cannot-be-selected\"\u003EWhat cannot be selected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EObjects on a layer that is \u003Cstrong\u003Eoff\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003Efrozen\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003Elocked\u003C/strong\u003E. Locked objects stay visible, and you can still snap to them, but you cannot select or change them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn a sheet tab, the model shown inside a viewport. You select the sheet&#039;s own objects (the title block, notes, the viewport frames). To edit the model, go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-quickly\"\u003EErase quickly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect objects, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when the command line does not have the cursor). On a Mac keyboard, the key labeled delete works once you have clicked in the drawing; while the command line has the cursor, press fn and delete together. This runs \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E on the selection. If nothing happens, you have typed something at the command line: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe window took too little.\u003C/strong\u003E You dragged left to right, which only takes what is fully inside. Drag right to left for a crossing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA click on empty space started a box.\u003C/strong\u003E One click on empty space starts a window; the second click finishes it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to drop it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command used objects you did not mean.\u003C/strong\u003E Something was still selected when you started it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before a command to start with nothing selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing is selected after a command.\u003C/strong\u003E That is on purpose: a finished command clears the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to choose the objects a command works on. CadCraft has two ways to work, and both are fine: Select first, then the command (noun-verb). Select objects, then type M for Move. The command takes your selection and goes straight on to its next question. Command first, then select (verb-noun). Type M; the command asks Select objects:; you select, then press Enter. Pick one object at a time With no command running, click a line. It is highlighted and its grips (small squares) appear. The right panel shows its properties. Click another object. It is added to the selection. Hold Shift and click a selected object. It is taken out of the selection. Press Esc. Nothing is selected. Window and crossing Click once on empty space, move, and click again: a rectangle. Pressing and dragging draws a freehand lasso instead, with the same two kinds (see Lasso selection; Settings can make a drag draw the rectangle). Which objects you get depends on the direction: You move Box Selects Left to right Window Only objects fully inside the box Right to left Crossing Objects inside the box and objects it touches Draw a few overlapping lines and circles. Click empty space to the left of some objects, move right, and click again. Only the objects completely inside are selected. Press Esc. Click empty space to the right, move left across some objects, and click again. Everything the box touches is selected. Hold Shift and draw a box or a lasso over some selected objects. They come out of the selection. Select, then run a command Select two circles. Type M and press Enter. The history says 2 found, and the prompt goes straight to Specify base point. Finish the move (see Move, copy, rotate, scale and mirror). When a command ends, the selection is cleared, so the next command starts fresh. Commands that take a selection this way: ERASE, MOVE, COPY, ROTATE, SCALE, MIRROR, EXPLODE, BLOCK and LIST. OFFSET, FILLET and CHAMFER always ask you to pick objects one at a time instead; TRIM and EXTEND take a pick or a path you drag through the pieces (see Trim and extend). Run a command, then select Make sure nothing is selected (Esc). Type E and press Enter. The prompt reads Select objects:. Click objects, or drag windows and crossings. The history counts each pick (1 found, 3 found). Press Enter, press Space or right-click to finish selecting. The objects are erased and the history says how many. At a Select objects: prompt you can also type: ALL, then Enter: every object you can edit in the open tab. L (or LAST), then Enter: the most recently drawn object. P (Previous): the objects the last command worked on, if they are still there. F (Fence): click or type points along a line; every object the line crosses is added. Press Enter to end the fence; U takes back its last point. W (Window) or C (Crossing): two corners, whichever way you drag. R (Remove): from now on, clicks, windows and fences take objects out. The prompt reads Remove objects:. A (Add) switches back. ?: the list of these in the history. These options are not shown in the prompt, as in AutoCAD. Pick one of several objects on top of each other Where objects overlap or touch, a click takes the nearest one. To get another: Hold the pointer still over the spot. Press Tab. The next object there is highlighted, and the status bar says which one it is (\"2 of 3: Line on layer Walls\"). Press Tab again until the one you want is highlighted, then click without moving. Moving the pointer starts over. This works with no command running, at Select objects: and wherever a command asks you to pick one object. Select by a rule: Quick select QSELECT (QS, or Edit › More › Quick select) selects every object that matches a rule, such as every circle with a radius under 50, or every block reference called DOOR-900. Open Quick select. Choose Object type (the list counts each type), then Property: Layer, Color, and for some types Length, Radius, Area, Text height, Text contents, Block name or Hatch pattern. Choose Operator and Value. * matches with wildcards (DOOR-*); Select all takes every object of the type. The count at the bottom shows how many match as you choose. How to apply: include the matches, or everything else. Apply to Current selection narrows what you had selected; Append to current selection adds to it. Click Select. Then run the command you want: it uses the selection. Select similar Select one or more objects and type SELECTSIMILAR (SIM), or choose Edit › More › Select similar. Every object of the same type on the same layer and with the same color is selected; block references also need the same block, hatches the same pattern. Type SE at its Select objects prompt to choose what must match. Select now, use later Type SELECT and press Enter, choose objects with any of the ways above, and press Enter. They stay selected for the next command, and P picks them again at a later Select objects: prompt. Select everything With no command running, press Ctrl+A, or choose Select all in the toolbar's Edit › More menu, to select every object you can edit on the open tab. If you have typed something at the command line, Ctrl+A selects that text instead: press Esc to clear it first. At a Select objects: prompt, type ALL. Select none in the same menu, or Esc, clears the selection. If you would rather have each click replace the selection (and Shift add to it), set Clicking objects to Replaces the selection in CadCraft settings. What cannot be selected Objects on a layer that is off, frozen or locked. Locked objects stay visible, and you can still snap to them, but you cannot select or change them. On a sheet tab, the model shown inside a viewport. You select the sheet's own objects (the title block, notes, the viewport frames). To edit the model, go to the Model tab. Erase quickly Select objects, then press Delete (or Backspace when the command line does not have the cursor). On a Mac keyboard, the key labeled delete works once you have clicked in the drawing; while the command line has the cursor, press fn and delete together. This runs ERASE on the selection. If nothing happens, you have typed something at the command line: press Esc, or type E and press Enter instead. Common mistakes The window took too little. You dragged left to right, which only takes what is fully inside. Drag right to left for a crossing. A click on empty space started a box. One click on empty space starts a window; the second click finishes it. Press Esc or right-click to drop it. The command used objects you did not mean. Something was still selected when you started it. Press Esc before a command to start with nothing selected. Nothing is selected after a command. That is on purpose: a finished command clears the selection."},{"slug":"lasso-selection","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Lasso selection","summary":"Press and drag a freehand outline round objects to select them, as a window, a crossing or a fence; switch with Space while you drag.","section":"tasks","order":31,"keywords":["lasso","lassomode","drag","freehand","select","selection","window","crossing","fence","space","polygon","touch","pen","rectangle"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"draw-a-lasso","text":"Draw a lasso","level":2},{"id":"window-crossing-and-fence","text":"Window, crossing and fence","level":2},{"id":"inside-a-command","text":"Inside a command","level":2},{"id":"the-rectangle","text":"The rectangle","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to select objects by drawing round them, and how to get the rectangle back when you want it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-a-lasso\"\u003EDraw a lasso\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a few lines and circles close together.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith no command running, press the mouse button on an empty spot, and drag round some of them, moving \u003Cstrong\u003Eright\u003C/strong\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELet go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the objects wholly inside the outline are selected. The outline is drawn solid while you drag: this is a window lasso.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"window-crossing-and-fence\"\u003EWindow, crossing and fence\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhich objects you get depends on the first move of your drag, and on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou drag\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELasso\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelects\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight first\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow (solid outline)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOnly objects \u003Cstrong\u003Ewholly inside\u003C/strong\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft first\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECrossing (dashed, green)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects inside \u003Cstrong\u003Eand\u003C/strong\u003E objects the outline touches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow, then crossing, then fence\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFence (a dashed path, not closed): only objects the path crosses\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress on an empty spot and drag round some objects, moving \u003Cstrong\u003Eleft\u003C/strong\u003E first. Everything the outline touches is selected too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress and drag again; while still holding the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E once. The outline turns into a crossing; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again for a fence.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you let go takes the objects out of the selection instead (with &quot;Clicking objects: Adds&quot; in Settings).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"inside-a-command\"\u003EInside a command\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA lasso works wherever CadCraft asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E: draw it, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E takes a crossing lasso as its window, so you can stretch an L-shaped part of a drawing in one go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E use a drag differently: there it trims (or extends) every piece the path crosses. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-rectangle\"\u003EThe rectangle\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA click on empty space, a move, and a second click always draws the rectangle window (left to right) or crossing (right to left).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo make a drag draw the rectangle again, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDragging to select\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERectangle\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E for the lasso. The choice is kept in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a finger or a pen the same way. A tap still picks one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing was selected.\u003C/strong\u003E The lasso was a window and nothing lay wholly inside. Start the drag to the left, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drag moved a grip.\u003C/strong\u003E Pressing on a selected object&#039;s grip moves the grip. Start the lasso on an empty spot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpace ended the command.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switches the lasso only while the button is held down; otherwise it is Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to select objects by drawing round them, and how to get the rectangle back when you want it. Draw a lasso Draw a few lines and circles close together. With no command running, press the mouse button on an empty spot, and drag round some of them, moving right first. Let go. Only the objects wholly inside the outline are selected. The outline is drawn solid while you drag: this is a window lasso. Window, crossing and fence Which objects you get depends on the first move of your drag, and on Space: You drag Lasso Selects Right first Window (solid outline) Only objects wholly inside Left first Crossing (dashed, green) Objects inside and objects the outline touches Press Space while dragging Window, then crossing, then fence Fence (a dashed path, not closed): only objects the path crosses Press on an empty spot and drag round some objects, moving left first. Everything the outline touches is selected too. Press Esc. Press and drag again; while still holding the button, press Space once. The outline turns into a crossing; press Space again for a fence. Shift while you let go takes the objects out of the selection instead (with \"Clicking objects: Adds\" in Settings). Inside a command A lasso works wherever CadCraft asks Select objects: draw it, then press Enter to finish. STRETCH takes a crossing lasso as its window, so you can stretch an L-shaped part of a drawing in one go. TRIM and EXTEND use a drag differently: there it trims (or extends) every piece the path crosses. See Trim and extend. The rectangle A click on empty space, a move, and a second click always draws the rectangle window (left to right) or crossing (right to left). To make a drag draw the rectangle again, open Settings › CadCraft › Dragging to select and choose Rectangle, or type LASSOMODE, then 0. Type LASSOMODE, then 1 for the lasso. The choice is kept in this browser. Drag a finger or a pen the same way. A tap still picks one object. Common mistakes Nothing was selected. The lasso was a window and nothing lay wholly inside. Start the drag to the left, or press Space while dragging. The drag moved a grip. Pressing on a selected object's grip moves the grip. Start the lasso on an empty spot. Space ended the command. Space switches the lasso only while the button is held down; otherwise it is Enter."},{"slug":"modify-objects","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Move, copy, rotate, scale, mirror, stretch and array","summary":"Lay out furniture in a room with the basic modify commands, using base points, typed distances and undo.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["move","copy","rotate","scale","mirror","stretch","array","join","matchprop","match properties","painter","arrayrect","arraypolar","polar array","erase","explode","undo","redo","base point","displacement","modify"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-pieces","text":"Draw the pieces","level":2},{"id":"move-base-point-then-second-point","text":"Move: base point, then second point","level":2},{"id":"copy-as-many-times-as-you-like","text":"Copy, as many times as you like","level":2},{"id":"mirror-the-row-to-the-other-side","text":"Mirror the row to the other side","level":2},{"id":"rotate-a-chair","text":"Rotate a chair","level":2},{"id":"scale-the-table","text":"Scale the table","level":2},{"id":"undo-and-redo","text":"Undo and redo","level":2},{"id":"stretch-a-room-wider","text":"Stretch a room wider","level":2},{"id":"array-rows-and-columns-or-round-a-center","text":"Array: rows and columns, or round a center","level":2},{"id":"join-pieces-into-one-polyline","text":"Join pieces into one polyline","level":2},{"id":"match-properties","text":"Match properties","level":2},{"id":"erase-and-explode","text":"Erase and explode","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA table with one chair, copied round the table, turned, mirrored and resized. Along the way you use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-pieces\"\u003EDraw the pieces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type \u003Ccode\u003E@1800,900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E300,-700\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type \u003Ccode\u003E@450,450\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is a chair, below the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-base-point-then-second-point\"\u003EMove: base point, then second point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery move, copy, rotate and scale starts from a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebase point\u003C/strong\u003E: the point that is carried along, or turned about.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the chair to select it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says \u003Ccode\u003E1 found\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the chair&#039;s upper-left corner (Endpoint) and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E@0,200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chair moves up 200, closer to the table.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E at \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E to give a displacement instead: CadCraft then asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify displacement\u003C/code\u003E, and you type how far across and up, such as \u003Ccode\u003E0,200\u003C/code\u003E (no \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E needed).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-as-many-times-as-you-like\"\u003ECopy, as many times as you like\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the chair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the chair&#039;s lower-left corner and click as the base point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A copy appears 600 to the right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E again and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A second copy appears 1200 to the right of the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E keeps going until you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Each copy is placed by the distance from the base point to the point you give. An \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E point counts from the last point you entered, which after the first copy is that copy&#039;s point, so \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E twice gives copies at 600 and 1200. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the last copy without leaving the command.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see three chairs in a row below the table.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E is for copies inside one drawing, placed by a base point. To carry objects to another drawing or tab, or to paste them later, use the clipboard: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E makes one copy that moves with the pointer. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror-the-row-to-the-other-side\"\u003EMirror the row to the other side\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a crossing (right to left) over the three chairs, without touching the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the Midpoint of the table&#039;s left side and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the Midpoint of the table&#039;s right side and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EErase source objects? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see three chairs below the table and three mirrored chairs above it. Text in a mirrored selection stays readable: it is placed mirrored but not written backwards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rotate-a-chair\"\u003ERotate a chair\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the rightmost chair below the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the chair&#039;s lower-right corner and click as the base point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe chair turns 90 degrees counterclockwise about the base point. A negative angle turns clockwise. Instead of typing, you can click a second point: the angle is measured from the base point to that point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scale-the-table\"\u003EScale the table\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the table&#039;s lower-left corner and click as the base point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1.2\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe table is 20% bigger, growing away from the base point. A factor below 1 makes it smaller.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you click a point instead of typing the scale factor, the factor is the distance from the base point to that point. Clicking one drawing unit away gives a factor of 1. Typing the number is usually what you want.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"undo-and-redo\"\u003EUndo and redo\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E button, or \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the last command. A whole command is one step: undoing a \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E that made five copies removes all five.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E button, or \u003Ccode\u003EREDO\u003C/code\u003E brings it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe history says what was undone (&quot;Undid COPY&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft keeps the last 500 steps. Layer settings (on, freeze, lock, color) are settings, not edits, and are not undone.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stretch-a-room-wider\"\u003EStretch a room wider\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E) moves only what lies inside a crossing window: corners and line ends inside it move, everything else stays put, so the room gets wider instead of moving.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a window from right to left over the right-hand wall, so it covers the wall&#039;s corners and the ends of anything meeting it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a base point, then type \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rectangle is 600 wider; lines that ended on the wall still end on it. An arc with one end inside keeps its height, circles and text move when their center or insertion point is inside, and a dimension measures its new length. Objects picked one by one, without a window, simply move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"array-rows-and-columns-or-round-a-center\"\u003EArray: rows and columns, or round a center\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAY\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArray\u003C/span\u003E) copies the selection in a grid or in a circle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a chair and type \u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for Rectangular.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA preview of 4 columns and 3 rows appears. Type \u003Ccode\u003ECOU\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E5\u003C/code\u003E for columns and \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E for rows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E between columns and \u003Ccode\u003E-900\u003C/code\u003E between rows (negative goes down).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to make the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor a ring of chairs round a table, choose \u003Ccode\u003EPO\u003C/code\u003E (Polar) instead, click the table&#039;s center, and set \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E (items), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (fill angle: 360 for all the way round, 180 for half) and \u003Ccode\u003EROT\u003C/code\u003E (whether the copies turn to face the center). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAYRECT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAYPOLAR\u003C/code\u003E skip the question.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-pieces-into-one-polyline\"\u003EJoin pieces into one polyline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin\u003C/span\u003E): select lines, arcs and open polylines whose ends meet, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. They become one polyline, closed when they go all the way round, which you can then offset in one go. Lines that lie on one line become one line even with gaps between them; arcs of one circle become one arc, or the circle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"match-properties\"\u003EMatch properties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMATCHPROP\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EMA\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch properties\u003C/span\u003E): click the object whose look you want, then select the objects to change and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. They take its layer and color; a text takes its height, a dimension its sizes, a hatch its pattern. Type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Settings) at the second question to turn any of these off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-and-explode\"\u003EErase and explode\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) removes the selected objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E) breaks a polyline into lines and arcs, a block reference into its objects, and a dimension into lines, arrowheads and text. Exploded dimensions no longer measure themselves.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping a distance without \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E at the second point.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E600,0\u003C/code\u003E is the absolute point 600,0, not &quot;600 to the right&quot;. Use \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E, or a bare \u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E with the crosshair pointing the right way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe rotation went the wrong way.\u003C/strong\u003E Positive angles turn counterclockwise.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo removed more than expected.\u003C/strong\u003E One undo takes back the whole last command, including every copy it made.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command did not ask to select objects.\u003C/strong\u003E Objects were already selected, so it used them. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first to start with nothing selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A table with one chair, copied round the table, turned, mirrored and resized. Along the way you use MOVE, COPY, ROTATE, MIRROR, SCALE and UNDO. Draw the pieces Type REC and press Enter, type 0,0 and press Enter, then type @1800,900 and press Enter. This is the table. Type REC and press Enter, type 300,-700 and press Enter, then type @450,450 and press Enter. This is a chair, below the table. Click Extents. Move: base point, then second point Every move, copy, rotate and scale starts from a base point: the point that is carried along, or turned about. Click the chair to select it. Type M and press Enter. The history says 1 found. At Specify base point, snap to the chair's upper-left corner (Endpoint) and click. At Specify second point, type @0,200 and press Enter. The chair moves up 200, closer to the table. Type D at Specify base point to give a displacement instead: CadCraft then asks Specify displacement, and you type how far across and up, such as 0,200 (no @ needed). Copy, as many times as you like Click the chair. Type CO and press Enter. Snap to the chair's lower-left corner and click as the base point. Type @600,0 and press Enter. A copy appears 600 to the right. Type @600,0 again and press Enter. A second copy appears 1200 to the right of the original. Press Enter to end the command. COPY keeps going until you press Enter. Each copy is placed by the distance from the base point to the point you give. An @ point counts from the last point you entered, which after the first copy is that copy's point, so @600,0 twice gives copies at 600 and 1200. Type U and press Enter to take back the last copy without leaving the command. You should see three chairs in a row below the table. COPY is for copies inside one drawing, placed by a base point. To carry objects to another drawing or tab, or to paste them later, use the clipboard: Ctrl+C, then Ctrl+V. Ctrl+D makes one copy that moves with the pointer. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Mirror the row to the other side Drag a crossing (right to left) over the three chairs, without touching the table. Type MI and press Enter. At Specify first point of mirror line, snap to the Midpoint of the table's left side and click. At Specify second point of mirror line, snap to the Midpoint of the table's right side and click. At Erase source objects? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E, press Enter to keep them. You should see three chairs below the table and three mirrored chairs above it. Text in a mirrored selection stays readable: it is placed mirrored but not written backwards. Rotate a chair Click the rightmost chair below the table. Type RO and press Enter. Snap to the chair's lower-right corner and click as the base point. Type 90 and press Enter. The chair turns 90 degrees counterclockwise about the base point. A negative angle turns clockwise. Instead of typing, you can click a second point: the angle is measured from the base point to that point. Scale the table Select the table. Type SC and press Enter. Snap to the table's lower-left corner and click as the base point. Type 1.2 and press Enter. The table is 20% bigger, growing away from the base point. A factor below 1 makes it smaller. If you click a point instead of typing the scale factor, the factor is the distance from the base point to that point. Clicking one drawing unit away gives a factor of 1. Typing the number is usually what you want. Undo and redo Ctrl+Z, the Undo button, or U then Enter undoes the last command. A whole command is one step: undoing a COPY that made five copies removes all five. Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+Shift+Z, the Redo button, or REDO brings it back. The history says what was undone (\"Undid COPY\"). CadCraft keeps the last 500 steps. Layer settings (on, freeze, lock, color) are settings, not edits, and are not undone. Stretch a room wider STRETCH (S, or Stretch) moves only what lies inside a crossing window: corners and line ends inside it move, everything else stays put, so the room gets wider instead of moving. Type S and press Enter. Drag a window from right to left over the right-hand wall, so it covers the wall's corners and the ends of anything meeting it. Press Enter. Click a base point, then type @600,0 and press Enter. The rectangle is 600 wider; lines that ended on the wall still end on it. An arc with one end inside keeps its height, circles and text move when their center or insertion point is inside, and a dimension measures its new length. Objects picked one by one, without a window, simply move. Array: rows and columns, or round a center ARRAY (AR, or Array) copies the selection in a grid or in a circle. Select a chair and type AR, press Enter. Press Enter for Rectangular. A preview of 4 columns and 3 rows appears. Type COU, press Enter, then 5 for columns and 2 for rows. Type S, press Enter, then 600 between columns and -900 between rows (negative goes down). Press Enter to make the copies. For a ring of chairs round a table, choose PO (Polar) instead, click the table's center, and set I (items), F (fill angle: 360 for all the way round, 180 for half) and ROT (whether the copies turn to face the center). ARRAYRECT and ARRAYPOLAR skip the question. Join pieces into one polyline JOIN (J, or Modify › More › Join): select lines, arcs and open polylines whose ends meet, and press Enter. They become one polyline, closed when they go all the way round, which you can then offset in one go. Lines that lie on one line become one line even with gaps between them; arcs of one circle become one arc, or the circle. Match properties MATCHPROP (MA, or Modify › More › Match properties): click the object whose look you want, then select the objects to change and press Enter. They take its layer and color; a text takes its height, a dimension its sizes, a hatch its pattern. Type S (Settings) at the second question to turn any of these off. Erase and explode ERASE (E, or Delete) removes the selected objects. EXPLODE (X) breaks a polyline into lines and arcs, a block reference into its objects, and a dimension into lines, arrowheads and text. Exploded dimensions no longer measure themselves. Common mistakes Typing a distance without @ at the second point. 600,0 is the absolute point 600,0, not \"600 to the right\". Use @600,0, or a bare 600 with the crosshair pointing the right way. The rotation went the wrong way. Positive angles turn counterclockwise. Undo removed more than expected. One undo takes back the whole last command, including every copy it made. The command did not ask to select objects. Objects were already selected, so it used them. Press Esc first to start with nothing selected."},{"slug":"copy-paste","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Copy, paste and duplicate","summary":"Copy objects to CadCraft's clipboard, paste them where you click or in place, duplicate with the pointer, and bring work across from another drawing or tab.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["copy","cut","paste","paste in place","duplicate","clipboard","copyclip","cutclip","pasteclip","pasteorig","copybase","ctrl c","ctrl v","ctrl x","ctrl d","between drawings","another tab","base point"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"copy-and-paste-where-you-click","text":"Copy and paste where you click","level":2},{"id":"paste-in-place","text":"Paste in place","level":2},{"id":"duplicate-and-move","text":"Duplicate and move","level":2},{"id":"cut","text":"Cut","level":2},{"id":"between-drawings-and-tabs","text":"Between drawings and tabs","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to copy objects to the clipboard and paste them where you click, paste them at their own coordinates, duplicate them with the pointer, cut them, and carry them into another drawing. Everything here is also in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-and-paste-where-you-click\"\u003ECopy and paste where you click\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the objects: click them, or drag a window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group. The command line says how many objects were copied. They stay selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E. The copy appears on the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of the copied objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where that corner goes, or type a point such as \u003Ccode\u003E2000,500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Object snaps and Ortho work as usual.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pasted objects are selected, so you can move or change them straight away. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the whole paste away.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWant the copy to hang from a point you choose, such as the corner of a wall? Type \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E, click the base point, select the objects, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pasting then places that point where you click. To hang every copy from its center instead of its lower-left corner, change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste point\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-in-place\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. The copy goes in at exactly the coordinates it was copied from, with no click. Use it to carry objects into another drawing at the same place, or from a sheet into another sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"duplicate-and-move\"\u003EDuplicate and move\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate and move\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA copy sits on top of the original and moves with the pointer. Click where it goes. You can also type a displacement such as \u003Ccode\u003E@0,-600\u003C/code\u003E, or a distance toward the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the click leaves nothing behind. The duplicate is one undo step and does not touch the clipboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut\"\u003ECut\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. The objects go on the clipboard and are erased from the drawing, in one undo step. Paste them back with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"between-drawings-and-tabs\"\u003EBetween drawings and tabs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe clipboard keeps its copy when you open another drawing, and it is shared by every CadCraft tab in this browser. Copy in one tab, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in another.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks the objects use come with them. If the drawing you paste into already has a block of the same name, its own definition is used, as AutoCAD does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayers the objects use are added, with the color and settings they had.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe copy is not rescaled when the two drawings use different units: CadCraft says so, and you can use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E afterwards.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EViewports stay on their sheet; they are left out of a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe clipboard is CadCraft&#039;s own. You cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or paste from another program into CadCraft: save as DWG or DXF instead. A very large copy may be too big to share between tabs; the command line then says only this tab can paste it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E pasted text into the command line.\u003C/strong\u003E You had typed something there, so the keys worked on the text. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to clear it, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENothing happened, and the status bar says to finish the current command.\u003C/strong\u003E Another command is running. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe paste landed far away.\u003C/strong\u003E It hangs from the lower-left corner of what you copied, which may be a distant object you selected by accident. Use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E, or copy with \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Nothing pasted&quot; with a reason.\u003C/strong\u003E What another tab left on the clipboard could not be read (it was damaged, or changed by hand). Copy the objects again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee the full list of keys in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/keyboard\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/keyboard\"\u003EKeyboard and mouse\u003C/a\u003E, and every prompt in the \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands#clipboard\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\" data-hash=\"clipboard\"\u003ECommand reference\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to copy objects to the clipboard and paste them where you click, paste them at their own coordinates, duplicate them with the pointer, cut them, and carry them into another drawing. Everything here is also in the toolbar's Edit group: Copy, Paste and the More menu. Copy and paste where you click Select the objects: click them, or drag a window. Press Ctrl+C, or click Copy in the Edit group. The command line says how many objects were copied. They stay selected. Press Ctrl+V, or click Paste. The copy appears on the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of the copied objects. Click where that corner goes, or type a point such as 2000,500 and press Enter. Object snaps and Ortho work as usual. The pasted objects are selected, so you can move or change them straight away. One Ctrl+Z takes the whole paste away. Want the copy to hang from a point you choose, such as the corner of a wall? Type COPYBASE, click the base point, select the objects, and press Enter. Pasting then places that point where you click. To hang every copy from its center instead of its lower-left corner, change Paste point in CadCraft settings. Paste in place Press Ctrl+Shift+V, or choose Paste in place in the More menu. The copy goes in at exactly the coordinates it was copied from, with no click. Use it to carry objects into another drawing at the same place, or from a sheet into another sheet. Duplicate and move Select the objects. Press Ctrl+D, or choose Duplicate and move in the More menu. A copy sits on top of the original and moves with the pointer. Click where it goes. You can also type a displacement such as @0,-600, or a distance toward the cursor. Esc before the click leaves nothing behind. The duplicate is one undo step and does not touch the clipboard. Cut Press Ctrl+X, or choose Cut in the More menu. The objects go on the clipboard and are erased from the drawing, in one undo step. Paste them back with Ctrl+V or Ctrl+Shift+V. Between drawings and tabs The clipboard keeps its copy when you open another drawing, and it is shared by every CadCraft tab in this browser. Copy in one tab, then press Ctrl+V in another. Blocks the objects use come with them. If the drawing you paste into already has a block of the same name, its own definition is used, as AutoCAD does. Layers the objects use are added, with the color and settings they had. The copy is not rescaled when the two drawings use different units: CadCraft says so, and you can use SCALE afterwards. Viewports stay on their sheet; they are left out of a copy. The clipboard is CadCraft's own. You cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or paste from another program into CadCraft: save as DWG or DXF instead. A very large copy may be too big to share between tabs; the command line then says only this tab can paste it. Common mistakes Ctrl+V pasted text into the command line. You had typed something there, so the keys worked on the text. Press Esc to clear it, then press Ctrl+V again. Nothing happened, and the status bar says to finish the current command. Another command is running. Press Esc first. The paste landed far away. It hangs from the lower-left corner of what you copied, which may be a distant object you selected by accident. Use Paste in place, or copy with COPYBASE. \"Nothing pasted\" with a reason. What another tab left on the clipboard could not be read (it was damaged, or changed by hand). Copy the objects again. See the full list of keys in Keyboard and mouse, and every prompt in the Command reference."},{"slug":"split-screen","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Split the screen","summary":"Show the drawing in two, three or four panes with their own zoom, put a sheet beside the model, and open several drawings side by side.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["split","split screen","panes","vports","viewports","tiled","tile","side by side","stacked","two drawings","tabs","document tabs","compare","divider","model and sheet","several drawings","open drawings"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"split-one-drawing-into-panes","text":"Split one drawing into panes","level":2},{"id":"draw-from-one-pane-into-another","text":"Draw from one pane into another","level":2},{"id":"the-model-beside-a-sheet","text":"The model beside a sheet","level":2},{"id":"several-drawings-in-one-window","text":"Several drawings in one window","level":2},{"id":"pan-and-zoom-together","text":"Pan and zoom together","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to look at one drawing in several panes at once, each with its own zoom, and how to open several drawings and see them side by side.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"split-one-drawing-into-panes\"\u003ESplit one drawing into panes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a drawing, or draw a few lines and circles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit side by side\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing area splits into two panes, both showing the same view. The active pane has a blue frame and gets the crosshair.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick in the other pane. It becomes active. (That first click only switches panes; click again to pick.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EZoom with the wheel. Only that pane zooms.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the line between the panes to make one wider. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn ←\"\u003Efn ←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EHome\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a focused divider puts it back in the middle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E also takes \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E (two panes on one side and a big one on the other: \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode\u003E4\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Ccode\u003ESI\u003C/code\u003E for one pane again. The active pane&#039;s view then fills the area. On a sheet, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E adds a viewport to the sheet instead (as AutoCAD); type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLIT\u003C/code\u003E there, or use the View menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-from-one-pane-into-another\"\u003EDraw from one pane into another\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESplit side by side, and zoom each pane onto a different corner of the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and click the first point in the left pane.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick once in the right pane to make it active, then click the second point there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line runs between the two places. A command goes on as you change panes, and the rubber band shows in every pane.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-model-beside-a-sheet\"\u003EThe model beside a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESplit side by side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the right pane, then click a sheet&#039;s tab under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe right pane shows the sheet; the left one still shows the model. Each pane keeps its own space, so you can draw on the model and watch the sheet&#039;s viewport follow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"several-drawings-in-one-window\"\u003ESeveral drawings in one window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a drawing. Then open another (or press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach drawing opens in its own tab over the drawing area. Click a tab to switch; the command line, the panels and undo follow the drawing you switch to. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in one drawing and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E in another copies objects between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETile the open drawings side by side\u003C/span\u003E (the split icon at the end of the tabs), or the stacked icon beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach pane now shows one of the open drawings, up to four. Click a pane to work in that drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E×\u003C/span\u003E on a tab to close that drawing. If it has unsaved changes, CadCraft asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening a drawing while the window holds only a new, untouched drawing uses that tab instead of adding one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pan-and-zoom-together\"\u003EPan and zoom together\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo compare two revisions, tile them side by side, then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink the panes&#039; views\u003C/span\u003E (the link icon at the end of the tabs), or choose it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINKVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EON\u003C/code\u003E. Now a pan or zoom in one pane moves every pane showing the model (or every pane showing a sheet) to the same place, so the two drawings stay lined up. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINKVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EOFF\u003C/code\u003E lets each pane move on its own again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMy click did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E It made that pane active. Click again to pick or draw.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe wheel zoomed the wrong pane.\u003C/strong\u003E The wheel zooms the pane under the pointer and makes it active.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe panes went back to one.\u003C/strong\u003E Opening a drawing shows it as it was saved; a drawing saved without panes opens in one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELeaving the page warns about unsaved changes I can&#039;t see.\u003C/strong\u003E Another tab holds a drawing with changes. Switch to it and save, or leave without saving.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to look at one drawing in several panes at once, each with its own zoom, and how to open several drawings and see them side by side. Split one drawing into panes Open a drawing, or draw a few lines and circles. Open View › More and choose Split side by side (or type VPORTS, then 2, then Enter). The drawing area splits into two panes, both showing the same view. The active pane has a blue frame and gets the crosshair. Click in the other pane. It becomes active. (That first click only switches panes; click again to pick.) Zoom with the wheel. Only that pane zooms. Drag the line between the panes to make one wider. Home on a focused divider puts it back in the middle. VPORTS also takes 3 (two panes on one side and a big one on the other: L or R), 4, and SI for one pane again. The active pane's view then fills the area. On a sheet, VPORTS adds a viewport to the sheet instead (as AutoCAD); type SPLIT there, or use the View menu. Draw from one pane into another Split side by side, and zoom each pane onto a different corner of the drawing. Type L and press Enter, and click the first point in the left pane. Click once in the right pane to make it active, then click the second point there. The line runs between the two places. A command goes on as you change panes, and the rubber band shows in every pane. The model beside a sheet Split side by side. Click the right pane, then click a sheet's tab under the drawing. The right pane shows the sheet; the left one still shows the model. Each pane keeps its own space, so you can draw on the model and watch the sheet's viewport follow. Several drawings in one window Open a drawing. Then open another (or press New). Each drawing opens in its own tab over the drawing area. Click a tab to switch; the command line, the panels and undo follow the drawing you switch to. Ctrl+C in one drawing and Ctrl+V in another copies objects between them. Click Tile the open drawings side by side (the split icon at the end of the tabs), or the stacked icon beside it. Each pane now shows one of the open drawings, up to four. Click a pane to work in that drawing. Click the × on a tab to close that drawing. If it has unsaved changes, CadCraft asks first. Opening a drawing while the window holds only a new, untouched drawing uses that tab instead of adding one. Pan and zoom together To compare two revisions, tile them side by side, then click Link the panes' views (the link icon at the end of the tabs), or choose it in View › More, or type LINKVIEWS and ON. Now a pan or zoom in one pane moves every pane showing the model (or every pane showing a sheet) to the same place, so the two drawings stay lined up. LINKVIEWS OFF lets each pane move on its own again. Common mistakes My click did nothing. It made that pane active. Click again to pick or draw. The wheel zoomed the wrong pane. The wheel zooms the pane under the pointer and makes it active. The panes went back to one. Opening a drawing shows it as it was saved; a drawing saved without panes opens in one. Leaving the page warns about unsaved changes I can't see. Another tab holds a drawing with changes. Switch to it and save, or leave without saving."},{"slug":"load-applications","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Load applications (APPLOAD)","summary":"Load LISP routines and their dialog files, unload them, load them again from the history, and keep the ones you use in the Startup Suite.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["appload","ap","load application","lisp","lsp","dcl","routine","startup suite","unload","history","drop","load","customize","manage"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"load-a-routine","text":"Load a routine","level":2},{"id":"unload-a-routine","text":"Unload a routine","level":2},{"id":"load-a-routine-every-time-cadcraft-starts","text":"Load a routine every time CadCraft starts","level":2},{"id":"load-a-file-again-from-the-history","text":"Load a file again from the history","level":2},{"id":"files-cadcraft-can-t-load","text":"Files CadCraft can't load","level":2},{"id":"stop-a-routine","text":"Stop a routine","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to load a LISP routine (a \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E file) so its commands work in CadCraft, how to unload it, and how to have it load every time CadCraft starts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-a-routine\"\u003ELoad a routine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad application…\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EAP\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad a file…\u003C/span\u003E and choose one or more \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E files. A routine that opens a dialog usually comes with a \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E file: choose it too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe dialog says what each file made, for example &quot;Loaded delblocks.lsp: 1 command, DELBLOCKS.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E, type the routine&#039;s command and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also drop \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E files on the drawing, or on the Load applications dialog: they load at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe file is read here, in your browser. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at an empty command line to run the routine&#039;s command again, the same as any other command. Your command aliases can reach it too: see &quot;Edit command aliases&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"unload-a-routine\"\u003EUnload a routine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/span\u003E, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoaded applications\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnload\u003C/span\u003E beside the file. The commands it made are gone until you load it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-a-routine-every-time-cadcraft-starts\"\u003ELoad a routine every time CadCraft starts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELoad the routine.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoaded applications\u003C/span\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd to Startup Suite\u003C/span\u003E beside it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom now on it loads by itself whenever CadCraft starts in this browser. To stop that, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove\u003C/span\u003E beside it under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStartup Suite\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Startup Suite keeps a copy of each file in this browser&#039;s storage. If you change the routine, load the new file and add it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"load-a-file-again-from-the-history\"\u003ELoad a file again from the history\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHistory\u003C/span\u003E lists the files you loaded in this browser, newest first. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad\u003C/span\u003E beside one to load it again, without looking for it on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-cadcraft-can-t-load\"\u003EFiles CadCraft can&#039;t load\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.fas\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.vlx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecompiled or protected LISP\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eload the \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E source instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.arx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.crx\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.dbx\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enative Windows programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethere is no way to run these in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.dvb\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea VBA project\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethere is no way to run these in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E.craft\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.lua\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.py\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECraftScript scripts\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eopen them in PartCraft&#039;s Script workspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe Load applications dialog says which of these a file is when you choose it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"stop-a-routine\"\u003EStop a routine\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. At one of the routine&#039;s prompts, Esc cancels it the way the routine expects (its own clean-up runs). If it is busy and not asking anything, Esc stops it outright and says so; your loaded routines stay loaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEverything a routine changes in the drawing is one step: one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes it all back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to load a LISP routine (a .lsp file) so its commands work in CadCraft, how to unload it, and how to have it load every time CadCraft starts. Load a routine Open View › More › Load application…, or type APPLOAD (or AP) and press Enter. Click Load a file… and choose one or more .lsp files. A routine that opens a dialog usually comes with a .dcl file: choose it too. The dialog says what each file made, for example \"Loaded delblocks.lsp: 1 command, DELBLOCKS.\" Click Close, type the routine's command and press Enter. You can also drop .lsp and .dcl files on the drawing, or on the Load applications dialog: they load at once. The file is read here, in your browser. Nothing is uploaded. Press Enter at an empty command line to run the routine's command again, the same as any other command. Your command aliases can reach it too: see \"Edit command aliases\". Unload a routine In Load applications, under Loaded applications, click Unload beside the file. The commands it made are gone until you load it again. Load a routine every time CadCraft starts Load the routine. Under Loaded applications, click Add to Startup Suite beside it. From now on it loads by itself whenever CadCraft starts in this browser. To stop that, click Remove beside it under Startup Suite. The Startup Suite keeps a copy of each file in this browser's storage. If you change the routine, load the new file and add it again. Load a file again from the history History lists the files you loaded in this browser, newest first. Click Load beside one to load it again, without looking for it on your computer. Files CadCraft can't load File What it is What to do .fas, .vlx compiled or protected LISP load the .lsp source instead .arx, .crx, .dbx native Windows programs there is no way to run these in CadCraft .dvb a VBA project there is no way to run these in CadCraft .craft, .lua, .py CraftScript scripts open them in PartCraft's Script workspace The Load applications dialog says which of these a file is when you choose it. Stop a routine Press Esc. At one of the routine's prompts, Esc cancels it the way the routine expects (its own clean-up runs). If it is busy and not asking anything, Esc stops it outright and says so; your loaded routines stay loaded. Everything a routine changes in the drawing is one step: one Ctrl+Z takes it all back."},{"slug":"sheet-sets","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Sheet sets","summary":"Bring sheets from many drawings together in one numbered list, fill their title blocks from the set, publish them as one PDF and archive the set as a zip.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["sheet set","sheet set manager","ssm","sheetset","ccsheets","dst","sheet list","sheet index","title block","fields","publish","renumber","archive","etransmit","transmittal","subsets","cover sheet","drawing list"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"start-a-set","text":"Start a set","level":2},{"id":"add-sheets","text":"Add sheets","level":2},{"id":"arrange-the-set","text":"Arrange the set","level":2},{"id":"fill-title-blocks-from-the-set","text":"Fill title blocks from the set","level":2},{"id":"a-sheet-list-on-the-cover-sheet","text":"A sheet list on the cover sheet","level":2},{"id":"publish-the-set","text":"Publish the set","level":2},{"id":"archive-the-set","text":"Archive the set","level":2},{"id":"the-set-file","text":"The set file","level":2},{"id":"troubleshooting","text":"Troubleshooting","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to collect sheets (layouts) from several drawing files into a \u003Cstrong\u003Esheet set\u003C/strong\u003E, number and title them, have their title blocks filled in for you, publish the whole set as one PDF, and pack everything into one zip to send.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet set lives in a small file of its own, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.ccsheets\u003C/code\u003E, in the folder that holds the drawings. The set file lists the sheets with paths from that folder, so you can move or copy the whole folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpening a folder to read and write needs Chrome or Edge (they can let a page work in a folder you choose), or the desktop app. Other browsers open a set read only: you can look at it and publish it, and the set file downloads instead of saving.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"start-a-set\"\u003EStart a set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFind the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet set\u003C/span\u003E section in the left panel, and open its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet\u003C/span\u003E menu. Typing \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESHEETSET\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ESSM\u003C/code\u003E) also brings you there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew set…\u003C/span\u003E. Give the set a name, and if you like the project name and number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick the folder that holds the drawings. The set file is saved there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo open a set later, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen set…\u003C/span\u003E and pick the same folder. If the folder holds more than one set, CadCraft asks which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-sheets\"\u003EAdd sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd sheets from a drawing…\u003C/span\u003E and pick one of the drawings in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft reads that drawing in the background (it does not open on screen) and lists its sheets. Tick the ones you want and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect a sheet and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F2\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF2\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename and renumber…\u003C/span\u003E) to give it a number and a title, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA-101\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EFloor plan\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet from template…\u003C/span\u003E makes a new drawing from another drawing&#039;s sheet (its title block and viewports), named after the new sheet, and adds it. The set remembers the drawing you used as its template.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"arrange-the-set\"\u003EArrange the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESubsets\u003C/strong\u003E group sheets, such as Architectural › Plans: select where it goes and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew subset…\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOrder\u003C/strong\u003E: drag a sheet to another place in the list, or onto a subset to move it there. With the keyboard, select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↑\"\u003E⌥↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ↓\"\u003E⌥↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpen a sheet\u003C/strong\u003E: double-click it (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E). Its drawing opens in its own tab, on that sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove\u003C/strong\u003E: select it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The drawing itself is not changed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo set change\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo set change\u003C/span\u003E step through what you changed in the set.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenumber\u003C/strong\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERenumber sheets…\u003C/span\u003E numbers every sheet, or one subset&#039;s, in the order of the list: a prefix, a start and a step (\u003Ccode\u003EA-\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E101\u003C/code\u003E give A-101, A-102 …).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change is saved in the set file at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-title-blocks-from-the-set\"\u003EFill title blocks from the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA title block gets its text from the set when its attributes are tagged with a field&#039;s name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETag\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it shows\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETNUMBER\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ESHEET_NO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDWG_NO\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETTITLE\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ETITLE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHEET_TITLE\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s title\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETDESC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s description\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPROJECTNAME\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPROJECT\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPROJECTNUMBER\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPROJECT_NO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EJOB_NO\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe project number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETSETNAME\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe set&#039;s name\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESHEETINDEX\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHEETCOUNT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;3 of 12&quot;, and 12\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYour own, such as \u003Ccode\u003ECLIENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you wrote for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet fields…\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet fields…\u003C/span\u003E holds the project name and number and your own fields, one per line as \u003Ccode\u003ENAME = value\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate drawings\u003C/span\u003E writes the numbers, titles and fields into the title blocks of every drawing in the set, and renames each sheet&#039;s layout tab as the set says (by default number and title, such as \u003Ccode\u003EA-101 Floor plan\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESet fields…\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayout tab names\u003C/span\u003E changes that). Each drawing is saved in place, in its own format and version. After you renumber or rename sheets, run it again and the tabs and title blocks follow.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-sheet-list-on-the-cover-sheet\"\u003EA sheet list on the cover sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect the cover sheet in the list and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the sheet list on this sheet\u003C/span\u003E. A table of every sheet (number and title, under its subset) goes in the sheet&#039;s upper left corner. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate drawings\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPublish to PDF\u003C/span\u003E keep it up to date. It is one block, \u003Ccode\u003ECC_SHEETLIST\u003C/code\u003E: move it like any block reference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"publish-the-set\"\u003EPublish the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPublish to PDF\u003C/span\u003E makes one PDF with a page per sheet, in the set&#039;s order, at each sheet&#039;s own paper size, with a bookmark per sheet (&quot;A-101 — Floor plan&quot;). With a subset selected, CadCraft asks whether to publish just that subset. The title blocks in the PDF are filled from the set even before you update the drawings; the drawings themselves are not changed by publishing. The PDF downloads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"archive-the-set\"\u003EArchive the set\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchive as a zip\u003C/span\u003E packs the set file, every drawing it uses and what those drawings link to (linked drawings, and theirs in turn, and pictures), keeping their folders, plus a \u003Ccode\u003ETransmittal.txt\u003C/code\u003E that lists every file. A link that is not in the set&#039;s folder is listed there as missing. Links are found by their path from the drawing, then by their file name anywhere in the folder.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-set-file\"\u003EThe set file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ccode\u003E.ccsheets\u003C/code\u003E file is JSON you can read in any text editor:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E{\n  &quot;format&quot;: &quot;cadcraft-sheetset&quot;, &quot;version&quot;: 1,\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Riverside Clinic&quot;,\n  &quot;properties&quot;: { &quot;PROJECTNAME&quot;: &quot;Riverside Clinic&quot;, &quot;PROJECTNUMBER&quot;: &quot;2026-014&quot; },\n  &quot;layoutNames&quot;: &quot;number-title&quot;,\n  &quot;sheets&quot;: [],\n  &quot;subsets&quot;: [ { &quot;id&quot;: &quot;plans&quot;, &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Plans&quot;, &quot;sheets&quot;: [\n    { &quot;id&quot;: &quot;p1&quot;, &quot;file&quot;: &quot;plans/Level 1.dwg&quot;, &quot;layout&quot;: &quot;A-101 Level 1 floor plan&quot;, &quot;number&quot;: &quot;A-101&quot;, &quot;title&quot;: &quot;Level 1 floor plan&quot; }\n  ] } ]\n}\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPaths are relative to the set&#039;s folder, with \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E between folders. CadCraft checks the file when it opens it: an absolute path, a sheet without a file or layout, or text that is not plain is left out, and the history says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"troubleshooting\"\u003ETroubleshooting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat you see\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhy\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This folder is read only here&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser cannot write in a folder\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse Chrome or Edge, or the desktop app; meanwhile \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload the set file\u003C/span\u003E keeps your changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA sheet is left out of the PDF: &quot;has no layout named …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe sheet&#039;s layout was renamed or removed in its drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the drawing and check its tabs; remove the sheet and add it again\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA title block stays empty\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts attributes have other tags\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERename the attribute tags to the field names above (BEDIT)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;already has a layout named … so that tab was not renamed&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo sheets of one drawing would get the same tab name\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive one of them another number or title\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to collect sheets (layouts) from several drawing files into a sheet set, number and title them, have their title blocks filled in for you, publish the whole set as one PDF, and pack everything into one zip to send. A sheet set lives in a small file of its own, \u003Cname\u003E.ccsheets, in the folder that holds the drawings. The set file lists the sheets with paths from that folder, so you can move or copy the whole folder. Opening a folder to read and write needs Chrome or Edge (they can let a page work in a folder you choose), or the desktop app. Other browsers open a set read only: you can look at it and publish it, and the set file downloads instead of saving. Start a set Find the Sheet set section in the left panel, and open its Set menu. Typing SHEETSET (or SSM) also brings you there. Choose New set…. Give the set a name, and if you like the project name and number. Pick the folder that holds the drawings. The set file is saved there. To open a set later, choose Open set… and pick the same folder. If the folder holds more than one set, CadCraft asks which. Add sheets Choose Add sheets from a drawing… and pick one of the drawings in the folder. CadCraft reads that drawing in the background (it does not open on screen) and lists its sheets. Tick the ones you want and click OK. Select a sheet and press F2 (or Rename and renumber…) to give it a number and a title, such as A-101 and Floor plan. New sheet from template… makes a new drawing from another drawing's sheet (its title block and viewports), named after the new sheet, and adds it. The set remembers the drawing you used as its template. Arrange the set Subsets group sheets, such as Architectural › Plans: select where it goes and choose New subset…. Order: drag a sheet to another place in the list, or onto a subset to move it there. With the keyboard, select it and press Alt+↑ or Alt+↓. Open a sheet: double-click it (or press Enter). Its drawing opens in its own tab, on that sheet. Remove: select it and press Delete. The drawing itself is not changed. Undo: Undo set change and Redo set change step through what you changed in the set. Renumber: Renumber sheets… numbers every sheet, or one subset's, in the order of the list: a prefix, a start and a step (A- and 101 give A-101, A-102 …). Every change is saved in the set file at once. Fill title blocks from the set A title block gets its text from the set when its attributes are tagged with a field's name: Tag What it shows SHEETNUMBER (or SHEET_NO, DWG_NO) The sheet's number SHEETTITLE (or TITLE, SHEET_TITLE) The sheet's title SHEETDESC The sheet's description PROJECTNAME (or PROJECT) The project name PROJECTNUMBER (or PROJECT_NO, JOB_NO) The project number SHEETSETNAME The set's name SHEETINDEX, SHEETCOUNT \"3 of 12\", and 12 Your own, such as CLIENT What you wrote for it in Set fields… Set fields… holds the project name and number and your own fields, one per line as NAME = value. Update drawings writes the numbers, titles and fields into the title blocks of every drawing in the set, and renames each sheet's layout tab as the set says (by default number and title, such as A-101 Floor plan; Set fields… › Layout tab names changes that). Each drawing is saved in place, in its own format and version. After you renumber or rename sheets, run it again and the tabs and title blocks follow. A sheet list on the cover sheet Select the cover sheet in the list and choose Put the sheet list on this sheet. A table of every sheet (number and title, under its subset) goes in the sheet's upper left corner. Update drawings and Publish to PDF keep it up to date. It is one block, CC_SHEETLIST: move it like any block reference. Publish the set Publish to PDF makes one PDF with a page per sheet, in the set's order, at each sheet's own paper size, with a bookmark per sheet (\"A-101 — Floor plan\"). With a subset selected, CadCraft asks whether to publish just that subset. The title blocks in the PDF are filled from the set even before you update the drawings; the drawings themselves are not changed by publishing. The PDF downloads. Archive the set Archive as a zip packs the set file, every drawing it uses and what those drawings link to (linked drawings, and theirs in turn, and pictures), keeping their folders, plus a Transmittal.txt that lists every file. A link that is not in the set's folder is listed there as missing. Links are found by their path from the drawing, then by their file name anywhere in the folder. The set file The .ccsheets file is JSON you can read in any text editor: { \"format\": \"cadcraft-sheetset\", \"version\": 1, \"name\": \"Riverside Clinic\", \"properties\": { \"PROJECTNAME\": \"Riverside Clinic\", \"PROJECTNUMBER\": \"2026-014\" }, \"layoutNames\": \"number-title\", \"sheets\": [], \"subsets\": [ { \"id\": \"plans\", \"name\": \"Plans\", \"sheets\": [ { \"id\": \"p1\", \"file\": \"plans/Level 1.dwg\", \"layout\": \"A-101 Level 1 floor plan\", \"number\": \"A-101\", \"title\": \"Level 1 floor plan\" } ] } ] } Paths are relative to the set's folder, with / between folders. CadCraft checks the file when it opens it: an absolute path, a sheet without a file or layout, or text that is not plain is left out, and the history says so. Troubleshooting What you see Why What to do \"This folder is read only here\" The browser cannot write in a folder Use Chrome or Edge, or the desktop app; meanwhile Download the set file keeps your changes A sheet is left out of the PDF: \"has no layout named …\" The sheet's layout was renamed or removed in its drawing Open the drawing and check its tabs; remove the sheet and add it again A title block stays empty Its attributes have other tags Rename the attribute tags to the field names above (BEDIT) \"already has a layout named … so that tab was not renamed\" Two sheets of one drawing would get the same tab name Give one of them another number or title"},{"slug":"command-aliases","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Edit command aliases","summary":"Keep your own command aliases in one text file in the familiar format, import and export a .pgp file, and reload it with REINIT.","section":"tasks","order":49,"keywords":["alias","aliases","aliasedit","pgp","shortcut","short name","reinit","keyboard","customize","edit aliases"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"add-an-alias","text":"Add an alias","level":2},{"id":"lines-cadcraft-refuses","text":"Lines CadCraft refuses","level":2},{"id":"bring-your-aliases-from-another-program","text":"Bring your aliases from another program","level":2},{"id":"reload-the-file","text":"Reload the file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to add your own short names for commands, for example \u003Ccode\u003ELL\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, all in one text file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-an-alias\"\u003EAdd an alias\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit aliases…\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIASEDIT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd a line with the alias, a comma and the command after a star:\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cpre class=\"hcode\"\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELL,     *LINE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/pre\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The command line says how many of your aliases are in use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ELL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: LINE starts.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour aliases sit on top of CadCraft&#039;s own. A line of yours wins over a built-in alias with the same name; remove your line and the built-in one is back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lines-cadcraft-refuses\"\u003ELines CadCraft refuses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe editor lists each problem with its line number while you type:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe command doesn&#039;t exist;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe alias is itself a command&#039;s name (\u003Ccode\u003ELINE, *CIRCLE\u003C/code\u003E would hide LINE);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe line isn&#039;t \u003Ccode\u003EALIAS, *COMMAND\u003C/code\u003E;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe same alias is on two lines (the later one wins).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines that start a program on your computer, like \u003Ccode\u003ENOTEPAD, START, 1, *File to edit: ,\u003C/code\u003E, are skipped with a note: an app in the browser can&#039;t start programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bring-your-aliases-from-another-program\"\u003EBring your aliases from another program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the alias editor, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EImport .pgp…\u003C/span\u003E and choose the file. You can also drop a \u003Ccode\u003E.pgp\u003C/code\u003E file on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck the problems listed (commands CadCraft doesn&#039;t have are refused, the rest work).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport .pgp\u003C/span\u003E saves CadCraft&#039;s aliases and yours as one file in the same format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reload-the-file\"\u003EReload the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREINIT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to apply your aliases file again. It is kept in this browser; the desktop app will keep it in its own folder, and the cloud service will sync it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to add your own short names for commands, for example LL for LINE, all in one text file. Add an alias Open View › More › Edit aliases…, or type ALIASEDIT and press Enter. Add a line with the alias, a comma and the command after a star: LL, *LINE Click Save. The command line says how many of your aliases are in use. Type LL and press Enter: LINE starts. Your aliases sit on top of CadCraft's own. A line of yours wins over a built-in alias with the same name; remove your line and the built-in one is back. Lines CadCraft refuses The editor lists each problem with its line number while you type: the command doesn't exist; the alias is itself a command's name (LINE, *CIRCLE would hide LINE); the line isn't ALIAS, *COMMAND; the same alias is on two lines (the later one wins). Lines that start a program on your computer, like NOTEPAD, START, 1, *File to edit: ,, are skipped with a note: an app in the browser can't start programs. Bring your aliases from another program In the alias editor, click Import .pgp… and choose the file. You can also drop a .pgp file on the drawing. Check the problems listed (commands CadCraft doesn't have are refused, the rest work). Click Save. Export .pgp saves CadCraft's aliases and yours as one file in the same format. Reload the file Type REINIT and press Enter to apply your aliases file again. It is kept in this browser; the desktop app will keep it in its own folder, and the cloud service will sync it."},{"slug":"offset-trim-extend-fillet","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Offset, trim, extend, fillet and chamfer walls","summary":"Turn single lines into double walls with Offset, clean the corners with Fillet, and cut and lengthen lines with Trim and Extend.","section":"tasks","order":50,"keywords":["offset","trim","extend","fillet","chamfer","bevel","break","breakatpoint","lengthen","walls","corner","cut","lengthen","parallel","radius","cleanup"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-inside-face","text":"Draw the inside face","level":2},{"id":"offset-the-outside-face","text":"Offset the outside face","level":2},{"id":"clean-up-the-outer-corners-with-fillet","text":"Clean up the outer corners with Fillet","level":2},{"id":"bevel-a-corner-with-chamfer","text":"Bevel a corner with Chamfer","level":2},{"id":"cut-a-doorway-with-trim","text":"Cut a doorway with Trim","level":2},{"id":"extend-a-line-to-meet-another","text":"Extend a line to meet another","level":2},{"id":"break-and-lengthen","text":"Break and lengthen","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA small room with 200-thick walls: the inside face is drawn, the outside face is offset, the corners are cleaned up, and a doorway is cut through one wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-inside-face\"\u003EDraw the inside face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@5000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,4000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-5000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou have four separate lines. (A closed polyline would offset in one go; separate lines show how Fillet cleans corners.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"offset-the-outside-face\"\u003EOffset the outside face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify offset distance [Through] &lt;Through&gt;:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to offset\u003C/code\u003E, click the bottom line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point on side to offset\u003C/code\u003E, click anywhere below it. A parallel line appears 200 below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the right line, then click to its right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the top line, then click above it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the left line, then click to its left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see four outer lines, each the same length as its inner line, so the outer corners do not meet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the distance prompt to use the last distance again. Type \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E for Through: then each copy goes through the point you click, at whatever distance that is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/code\u003E works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Offsetting a closed polyline offsets the whole outline, corners included.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clean-up-the-outer-corners-with-fillet\"\u003EClean up the outer corners with Fillet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E joins two lines at a corner: it trims or extends both so they meet. With a radius of 0 the corner is square.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first line [Radius]:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the last fillet you did used a radius, type \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the outer bottom line near its right end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line\u003C/code\u003E, click the outer right line near its bottom end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe two lines now meet in a sharp corner, and the command ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to repeat \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E, and do the next corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERepeat until all four outer corners meet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick each line on the part you want to \u003Cstrong\u003Ekeep\u003C/strong\u003E. With a radius above 0, Fillet adds an arc between the lines instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E at the first prompt to do all four corners in one go, or draw the outside face as one closed polyline and type \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E: every corner is done at once. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E as you click the second line for a sharp corner without changing the radius.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"bevel-a-corner-with-chamfer\"\u003EBevel a corner with Chamfer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E cuts a corner off with a straight line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ECHA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E300\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the first distance, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to use 300 for the second too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the inner top line near its left end, then the inner left line near its top end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe corner is cut back 300 along each line and a bevel line joins them. Type \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E instead of \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E to give a length on the first line and an angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cut-a-doorway-with-trim\"\u003ECut a doorway with Trim\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirst draw the two door jambs across the bottom wall, then trim the wall lines between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1000,200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,-600\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to repeat \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1900,200\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,-600\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou now have two short lines crossing the bottom wall 900 apart.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the inner bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears. (Rest the pointer on it first: the piece that will go turns grey and dashed.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the outer bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the part of each jamb that sticks out above the wall, and the part below it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou should see a 900 opening in the bottom wall, with the jambs closing the wall ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Quick mode (the default) Trim does not ask you to choose cutting edges first: every visible object is a cutting edge. Pick the piece you want gone, or drag a path through several pieces to trim them all at once. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put back the last pick or drag without leaving the command. Fence, crossing, chosen edges and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to extend are in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extend-a-line-to-meet-another\"\u003EExtend a line to meet another\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E lengthens a line, arc or polyline end until it meets the next object in that direction.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E1000,2000\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@2000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. The line stops 2000 short of the right wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new line near its right end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line now runs to the inner face of the right wall. Every visible object is a boundary; pick near the end that should grow. Inside \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click does the same.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"break-and-lengthen\"\u003EBreak and lengthen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModify\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBreak\u003C/span\u003E) cuts a gap without a cutting edge: click the object where the gap starts (that click is the first point), then click where it ends. On a circle the part counter-clockwise from the first point to the second goes. \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E at the second question lets you give the first point again, and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAKATPOINT\u003C/code\u003E splits an object in two at one point with no gap.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELEN\u003C/code\u003E) changes a length at one end:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ELEN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Click a line to see its current length; the prompt asks again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose how: \u003Ccode\u003EDE\u003C/code\u003E (Delta: an amount to add, negative to take off; \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E for an arc&#039;s angle), \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Percent: 50 halves it), \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (Total: the length it should be) or \u003Ccode\u003EDY\u003C/code\u003E (Dynamic: drag the end).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick each object near the end that should move. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last one; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELines, arcs and open polylines (their end segment) can be lengthened.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA whole line disappeared.\u003C/strong\u003E Nothing crossed it, so Quick mode erased it. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E to put it back; check that the cutting line really crosses it (zoom in) and is not on a layer that is off.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No boundary in that direction.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Extend found nothing to run into. Pick nearer the end that should grow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Radius is too large.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The arc would run past the end of one of the lines. Use a smaller radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Pick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or type \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E to round every corner of it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe offset went to the wrong side.\u003C/strong\u003E The side is where you click after picking the object. Undo with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click on the other side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Cannot offset that far.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The offset would turn the arc, circle or polyline inside out. Use a smaller distance.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A small room with 200-thick walls: the inside face is drawn, the outside face is offset, the corners are cleaned up, and a doorway is cut through one wall. Draw the inside face Type L and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @5000,0 and press Enter. Type @0,4000 and press Enter. Type @-5000,0 and press Enter. Type C and press Enter. Click Extents. You have four separate lines. (A closed polyline would offset in one go; separate lines show how Fillet cleans corners.) Offset the outside face Type O and press Enter. The prompt reads Specify offset distance [Through] \u003CThrough\u003E:. Type 200 and press Enter. At Select object to offset, click the bottom line. At Specify point on side to offset, click anywhere below it. A parallel line appears 200 below. Click the right line, then click to its right. Click the top line, then click above it. Click the left line, then click to its left. Press Enter to end the command. You should see four outer lines, each the same length as its inner line, so the outer corners do not meet. Press Enter at the distance prompt to use the last distance again. Type T for Through: then each copy goes through the point you click, at whatever distance that is. OFFSET works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Offsetting a closed polyline offsets the whole outline, corners included. Clean up the outer corners with Fillet FILLET joins two lines at a corner: it trims or extends both so they meet. With a radius of 0 the corner is square. Type F and press Enter. The prompt reads Select first line [Radius]:. If the last fillet you did used a radius, type R, press Enter, type 0 and press Enter. Click the outer bottom line near its right end. At Select second line, click the outer right line near its bottom end. The two lines now meet in a sharp corner, and the command ends. Press Enter to repeat FILLET, and do the next corner. Repeat until all four outer corners meet. Click each line on the part you want to keep. With a radius above 0, Fillet adds an arc between the lines instead. Type M at the first prompt to do all four corners in one go, or draw the outside face as one closed polyline and type P: every corner is done at once. Hold Shift as you click the second line for a sharp corner without changing the radius. Bevel a corner with Chamfer CHAMFER cuts a corner off with a straight line. Type CHA and press Enter. Type D and press Enter. Type 300 and press Enter for the first distance, then press Enter to use 300 for the second too. Click the inner top line near its left end, then the inner left line near its top end. The corner is cut back 300 along each line and a bevel line joins them. Type A instead of D to give a length on the first line and an angle. Cut a doorway with Trim First draw the two door jambs across the bottom wall, then trim the wall lines between them. Type L and press Enter. Type 1000,200 and press Enter. Type @0,-600 and press Enter, then press Enter again. Press Enter to repeat LINE. Type 1900,200 and press Enter. Type @0,-600 and press Enter, then press Enter again. You now have two short lines crossing the bottom wall 900 apart. Type TR and press Enter. The history says Current settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend. Click the inner bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears. (Rest the pointer on it first: the piece that will go turns grey and dashed.) Click the outer bottom line between the two jambs. That piece disappears too. Click the part of each jamb that sticks out above the wall, and the part below it. Press Enter to end the command. You should see a 900 opening in the bottom wall, with the jambs closing the wall ends. In Quick mode (the default) Trim does not ask you to choose cutting edges first: every visible object is a cutting edge. Pick the piece you want gone, or drag a path through several pieces to trim them all at once. Type U and press Enter to put back the last pick or drag without leaving the command. Fence, crossing, chosen edges and Shift to extend are in Trim and extend. Extend a line to meet another EXTEND lengthens a line, arc or polyline end until it meets the next object in that direction. Type L and press Enter. Type 1000,2000 and press Enter. Type @2000,0 and press Enter, then press Enter again. The line stops 2000 short of the right wall. Type EX and press Enter. Click the new line near its right end. Press Enter to end the command. The line now runs to the inner face of the right wall. Every visible object is a boundary; pick near the end that should grow. Inside TRIM, Shift+click does the same. Break and lengthen BREAK (BR, or Modify › More › Break) cuts a gap without a cutting edge: click the object where the gap starts (that click is the first point), then click where it ends. On a circle the part counter-clockwise from the first point to the second goes. F at the second question lets you give the first point again, and BREAKATPOINT splits an object in two at one point with no gap. LENGTHEN (LEN) changes a length at one end: Type LEN and press Enter. Click a line to see its current length; the prompt asks again. Choose how: DE (Delta: an amount to add, negative to take off; A for an arc's angle), P (Percent: 50 halves it), T (Total: the length it should be) or DY (Dynamic: drag the end). Click each object near the end that should move. U takes back the last one; Enter ends. Lines, arcs and open polylines (their end segment) can be lengthened. Common mistakes A whole line disappeared. Nothing crossed it, so Quick mode erased it. Type U to put it back; check that the cutting line really crosses it (zoom in) and is not on a layer that is off. \"No boundary in that direction.\" Extend found nothing to run into. Pick nearer the end that should grow. \"Radius is too large.\" The arc would run past the end of one of the lines. Use a smaller radius. \"Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.\" Pick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or type P to round every corner of it. The offset went to the wrong side. The side is where you click after picking the object. Undo with Ctrl+Z and click on the other side. \"Cannot offset that far.\" The offset would turn the arc, circle or polyline inside out. Use a smaller distance."},{"slug":"trim-and-extend","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Trim and extend","summary":"Cut lines back where others cross them and lengthen them to meet others, one piece at a time or many at once by dragging a path through them.","section":"tasks","order":52,"keywords":["trim","extend","tr","ex","quick trim","drag","freehand","path","fence","crossing","shift","cutting edges","boundary edges","edgemode","mode","standard","quick","erase","preview","walls","cleanup"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"trim-one-piece","text":"Trim one piece","level":2},{"id":"trim-many-pieces-in-one-drag","text":"Trim many pieces in one drag","level":2},{"id":"extend-instead-with-shift","text":"Extend instead, with Shift","level":2},{"id":"trim-inside-a-window","text":"Trim inside a window","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-cutting-edges-standard-mode","text":"Choose the cutting edges (Standard mode)","level":2},{"id":"edges-that-would-reach","text":"Edges that would reach","level":2},{"id":"erase-without-leaving","text":"Erase without leaving","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E work, and the fast ways to use them: drag through many pieces at once, hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to switch between trimming and extending, and see what will go before you click.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-one-piece\"\u003ETrim one piece\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a long horizontal line and two short lines across it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERest the pointer on the long line between the two short ones. That piece turns grey and dashed: it is what will go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick. The piece disappears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Quick mode (the default) every visible object is a cutting edge, so you never choose edges first. Click the piece you want gone. An object that nothing crosses is erased whole, and the history says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-many-pieces-in-one-drag\"\u003ETrim many pieces in one drag\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a long horizontal line and ten short vertical lines across it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress the mouse button above the long line, to the left of the first vertical line, and drag across all ten above the long line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you drag, the path shows as a dashed line, and every piece it has crossed turns grey. Let go: all ten top pieces are trimmed in one step. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to put them all back at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also click two empty spots: the line between them works like a dragged path. Type \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (Fence) to click a run of points instead, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag a finger the same way. A tap on a piece trims it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extend-instead-with-shift\"\u003EExtend instead, with Shift\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a vertical line, and five short horizontal lines to its left that stop before it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EHold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Rest the pointer on a short line: the part it would grow by shows grey and dashed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EStill holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, drag down through the five short lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll five now run to the vertical line. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works the other way round in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E): a click extends, a \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click trims. Extend lengthens the end nearest where you click or where the path crosses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trim-inside-a-window\"\u003ETrim inside a window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick two corners round the pieces to remove.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery piece that lies at least partly inside the window is trimmed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-cutting-edges-standard-mode\"\u003EChoose the cutting edges (Standard mode)\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESometimes only one line should cut, such as a wall face with many other lines crossing it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E (mOde) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Standard) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect cutting edges\u003C/code\u003E, click the edge lines (or drag a window), then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with nothing selected makes every object an edge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick or drag through the pieces to remove.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOnly the chosen edges cut, and an object no edge crosses is left alone. The mode is kept until you close the page; type \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EQ\u003C/code\u003E to go back to Quick. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (cuTting edges) chooses new edges at any time; in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E it is \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E (Boundary edges).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edges-that-would-reach\"\u003EEdges that would reach\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (Edge) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (Extend) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENow a cutting line or arc that stops short counts as if it went on: a short line cuts where it would cross if it were longer. Type \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E (No extend) to switch back. This is the same as EDGEMODE 1 and 0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"erase-without-leaving\"\u003EErase without leaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (eRase) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, pick the objects to erase, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. TRIM carries on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA whole line disappeared.\u003C/strong\u003E In Quick mode an object nothing crosses is erased. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E to put it back, or switch to Standard mode (\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Nothing to trim there: no chosen edge crosses that object.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Standard mode only cuts at the edges you chose. Type \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E to choose them again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No boundary in that direction.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Extend found nothing ahead. Click nearer the end that should grow, or turn on \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (Edge, Extend) for boundaries that stop short.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drag drew a lasso instead.\u003C/strong\u003E A drag only trims while TRIM or EXTEND is asking for an object. With no command running, a drag selects (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly lines, arcs, circles and polylines can be trimmed.\u003C/strong\u003E Explode a block first to trim its lines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How TRIM and EXTEND work, and the fast ways to use them: drag through many pieces at once, hold Shift to switch between trimming and extending, and see what will go before you click. Trim one piece Draw a long horizontal line and two short lines across it. Type TR and press Enter. The history says Current settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend. Rest the pointer on the long line between the two short ones. That piece turns grey and dashed: it is what will go. Click. The piece disappears. Press Enter to end the command. In Quick mode (the default) every visible object is a cutting edge, so you never choose edges first. Click the piece you want gone. An object that nothing crosses is erased whole, and the history says so. Trim many pieces in one drag Draw a long horizontal line and ten short vertical lines across it. Type TR and press Enter. Press the mouse button above the long line, to the left of the first vertical line, and drag across all ten above the long line. While you drag, the path shows as a dashed line, and every piece it has crossed turns grey. Let go: all ten top pieces are trimmed in one step. Type U and press Enter to put them all back at once. You can also click two empty spots: the line between them works like a dragged path. Type F (Fence) to click a run of points instead, then press Enter. Drag a finger the same way. A tap on a piece trims it. Extend instead, with Shift Draw a vertical line, and five short horizontal lines to its left that stop before it. Type TR and press Enter. Hold Shift. Rest the pointer on a short line: the part it would grow by shows grey and dashed. Still holding Shift, drag down through the five short lines. All five now run to the vertical line. Shift works the other way round in EXTEND (EX): a click extends, a Shift+click trims. Extend lengthens the end nearest where you click or where the path crosses. Trim inside a window Type TR and press Enter. Type C and press Enter. Click two corners round the pieces to remove. Every piece that lies at least partly inside the window is trimmed. Choose the cutting edges (Standard mode) Sometimes only one line should cut, such as a wall face with many other lines crossing it. Type TR and press Enter. Type O (mOde) and press Enter, then type S (Standard) and press Enter. At Select cutting edges, click the edge lines (or drag a window), then press Enter. Pressing Enter with nothing selected makes every object an edge. Click or drag through the pieces to remove. Only the chosen edges cut, and an object no edge crosses is left alone. The mode is kept until you close the page; type O, then Q to go back to Quick. T (cuTting edges) chooses new edges at any time; in EXTEND it is B (Boundary edges). Edges that would reach Type TR and press Enter. Type E (Edge) and press Enter, then E (Extend) and press Enter. Now a cutting line or arc that stops short counts as if it went on: a short line cuts where it would cross if it were longer. Type E, then N (No extend) to switch back. This is the same as EDGEMODE 1 and 0. Erase without leaving Type R (eRase) and press Enter, pick the objects to erase, and press Enter. TRIM carries on. Common mistakes A whole line disappeared. In Quick mode an object nothing crosses is erased. Type U to put it back, or switch to Standard mode (O, S). \"Nothing to trim there: no chosen edge crosses that object.\" Standard mode only cuts at the edges you chose. Type T to choose them again. \"No boundary in that direction.\" Extend found nothing ahead. Click nearer the end that should grow, or turn on E (Edge, Extend) for boundaries that stop short. The drag drew a lasso instead. A drag only trims while TRIM or EXTEND is asking for an object. With no command running, a drag selects (see Lasso selection). Only lines, arcs, circles and polylines can be trimmed. Explode a block first to trim its lines."},{"slug":"grips-and-properties","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Edit with grips and Properties","summary":"Stretch, move and resize objects by their grips, and type exact lengths, radii, angles and coordinates in the Properties panel.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["grips","properties","stretch","length","radius","coordinates","edit","quick properties","properties palette","change","layer","colour","color"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"grips","text":"Grips","level":2},{"id":"move-a-grip","text":"Move a grip","level":3},{"id":"properties","text":"Properties","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo quick ways to change an object without a command: drag its \u003Cstrong\u003Egrips\u003C/strong\u003E, or type new values in the \u003Cstrong\u003EProperties\u003C/strong\u003E panel on the right.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grips\"\u003EGrips\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you select objects (up to 200 at a time) with no command running, small squares appear on them. These are grips:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EObject\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EGrips\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBoth ends, and the middle (moves the whole line)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center (moves it) and four points on the circle (change the radius)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center, and both ends (change where it starts and stops)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery vertex\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText, point, block reference\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe insertion point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts defining points and the dimension line position\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe center (moves it) and the four corners (resize it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"move-a-grip\"\u003EMove a grip\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a line and click it. Three grips appear.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the grip at one end. The status bar says &quot;Click where this grip goes (Esc to cancel). Snaps and Ortho apply.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair. A preview follows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new position, or snap to a point on another object and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe end moves and the rest of the line stays put. You can also press on a grip and drag it, then let go where it should go.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot type a coordinate for a grip. For an exact position, snap to a point, or type the value in Properties.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to cancel a grip move.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"properties\"\u003EProperties\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe right panel shows the properties of what is selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a line. The panel heading reads &quot;Line&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E2500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe line is now exactly 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or clicking elsewhere applies a value; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step, also when many objects change at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFields take expressions (\u003Ccode\u003E=2*45\u003C/code\u003E) and lengths with units (\u003Ccode\u003E3&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E900mm\u003C/code\u003E), every point has X, Y and Z, and several objects can be changed together. All of it is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/properties-panel\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo grips appear.\u003C/strong\u003E A command is running (press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), or more than 200 objects are selected, or the object is on a locked layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe grip went to the wrong place.\u003C/strong\u003E A snap caught it. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click to ignore snaps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping in Properties did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click elsewhere to apply. A refused entry (zero or negative lengths and radii, text that is not a value) turns the field red and says why.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyped letters went into a Properties field.\u003C/strong\u003E While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn Two quick ways to change an object without a command: drag its grips, or type new values in the Properties panel on the right. Grips When you select objects (up to 200 at a time) with no command running, small squares appear on them. These are grips: Object Grips Line Both ends, and the middle (moves the whole line) Circle The center (moves it) and four points on the circle (change the radius) Arc The center, and both ends (change where it starts and stops) Polyline Every vertex Text, point, block reference The insertion point Ellipse The center Dimension Its defining points and the dimension line position Viewport The center (moves it) and the four corners (resize it) Move a grip Draw a line and click it. Three grips appear. Click the grip at one end. The status bar says \"Click where this grip goes (Esc to cancel). Snaps and Ortho apply.\" Move the crosshair. A preview follows. Click the new position, or snap to a point on another object and click. The end moves and the rest of the line stays put. You can also press on a grip and drag it, then let go where it should go. You cannot type a coordinate for a grip. For an exact position, snap to a point, or type the value in Properties. Press Esc or right-click to cancel a grip move. Properties The right panel shows the properties of what is selected. Click a line. The panel heading reads \"Line\". Click in Length, type 2500 and press Enter. The line is now exactly 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle. Enter or clicking elsewhere applies a value; Esc puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step, also when many objects change at once. Fields take expressions (=2*45) and lengths with units (3'6\", 900mm), every point has X, Y and Z, and several objects can be changed together. All of it is in The Properties panel. Common mistakes No grips appear. A command is running (press Esc), or more than 200 objects are selected, or the object is on a locked layer. The grip went to the wrong place. A snap caught it. Hold Shift while you click to ignore snaps. Typing in Properties did nothing. Press Enter or click elsewhere to apply. A refused entry (zero or negative lengths and radii, text that is not a value) turns the field red and says why. Typed letters went into a Properties field. While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press Enter or Esc, then type the command."},{"slug":"use-with-ai-assistants","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Use CadCraft with an AI assistant","summary":"Let your own AI assistant or script work with CadCraft: what it may do, how to connect Claude Desktop, Claude Code and other MCP clients, and how you stay in charge (permissions, undo, the activity log, Disconnect).","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["ai","assistant","agent","mcp","model context protocol","claude","claude code","claude desktop","script","automation","command line","cli","json","token","permissions","disconnect","activity"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"turn-it-on","text":"Turn it on","level":2},{"id":"connect-your-assistant","text":"Connect your assistant","level":2},{"id":"you-stay-in-charge","text":"You stay in charge","level":2},{"id":"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for","text":"What an assistant can ask for","level":2},{"id":"work-on-files-without-opening-cadcraft","text":"Work on files without opening CadCraft","level":2},{"id":"if-something-doesn-t-work","text":"If something doesn't work","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) look at a drawing, run CadCraft&#039;s commands, plot and save, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft has \u003Cstrong\u003Eno AI of its own\u003C/strong\u003E and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: Claude Desktop, Claude Code or any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). CadCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are two ways in:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe drawing you have open.\u003C/strong\u003E The assistant works in this window, next to you, through CadCraft Studio on your computer. This is what \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E is for.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrawings on your computer, without opening the app.\u003C/strong\u003E The CadCraft MCP server and the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E command line read and write DWG and DXF files in folders you name. See &quot;Work on files without opening CadCraft&quot; below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"turn-it-on\"\u003ETurn it on\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E (the gear in the app bar, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, tick \u003Cstrong\u003ELet AI assistants and scripts use CadCraft\u003C/strong\u003E. It is off until you do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose what it may do. \u003Cstrong\u003ERead\u003C/strong\u003E is ticked to start with; tick the others only when you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat the assistant can do\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELook at the drawing: describe it, list and find objects, see the selection, describe the view and take a picture of it, check it against layer and drafting rules\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun commands on the drawing and change the selection. Each action is one step you can undo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave the drawing (on the desktop, to its file; in a browser, as a download)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot to PDF and make other files\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant at all, and if it asks for one anyway, CadCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset CadCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E turns it off too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"connect-your-assistant\"\u003EConnect your assistant\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAssistants connect through \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft Studio\u003C/strong\u003E on your computer: it runs a small server that only this computer can reach (address \u003Ccode\u003E127.0.0.1\u003C/code\u003E), protected by a token that Studio makes each time it starts. Open Studio, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConnect an assistant\u003C/span\u003E, and copy the address and the token.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClaude Code:\u003C/strong\u003E run the command Studio shows, which looks like \u003Ccode\u003Eclaude mcp add --transport http cadcraft http://127.0.0.1:47615/mcp --header &quot;Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;&quot;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClaude Desktop and other MCP clients:\u003C/strong\u003E add a server of type HTTP with the address, and the header \u003Ccode\u003EAuthorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Studio shows the settings to paste.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E in Studio makes every connected assistant reconnect with the new token. Never share the token: anyone with it can use the apps you switched on, for as long as Studio is running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser tab can&#039;t open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"you-stay-in-charge\"\u003EYou stay in charge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEvery action is one undo step\u003C/strong\u003E, labelled with the assistant&#039;s name for it, for example &quot;Assistant: LINE&quot;. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the assistant&#039;s last action as a whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou see what it does.\u003C/strong\u003E Its commands appear in the command history with \u003Cstrong\u003E[Assistant]\u003C/strong\u003E in front, and the drawing redraws as it works.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIt waits for you.\u003C/strong\u003E While you are in the middle of a command, the assistant can read but not change the drawing; it is told to try again when you are done.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EYou know when one is connected.\u003C/strong\u003E A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected. Click it to see what it may do, or to \u003Cstrong\u003EDisconnect\u003C/strong\u003E it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EActivity.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EActivity\u003C/span\u003E lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused, and the undo step it made. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave the log\u003C/span\u003E keeps a copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-an-assistant-can-ask-for\"\u003EWhat an assistant can ask for\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETool\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPermission\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eapp_info\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_describe\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eview_describe\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing&#039;s name, units, layers, blocks, sheets; what is on screen\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eobjects_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eobject_get\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eselection_get\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects with their geometry, length and area; the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_check\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERules such as allowed layers, nothing on layer 0, colours ByLayer, text heights\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eview_screenshot\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture of the drawing as shown\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ecommand_run\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETypes at the command line, one prompt answer at a time, for example \u003Ccode\u003ELINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@100,0\u003C/code\u003E, Enter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eselection_set\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eundo\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelects objects for the next command; undoes its own steps\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_save\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft&#039;s own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Edrawing_plot\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA PDF of a sheet or of the model, at a scale or fitted\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECommands that need a dialog of their own (picking a file, the library, Find) are not run for an assistant: it is told to ask you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-on-files-without-opening-cadcraft\"\u003EWork on files without opening CadCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe CadCraft MCP server (\u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft-mcp\u003C/code\u003E) and command line (\u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft\u003C/code\u003E) run CadCraft&#039;s own drawing code in Node.js, with no window. They need Node.js 20 or newer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe MCP server\u003C/strong\u003E works only inside the folders you name, and only with the permissions you give it: \u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp --root ~/Drawings --allow read,edit\u003C/code\u003E. Without \u003Ccode\u003E--root\u003C/code\u003E it can&#039;t open any file; without \u003Ccode\u003E--allow\u003C/code\u003E it can only read. It can also read IFC models, takeoffs and the markups in PDF sets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command line\u003C/strong\u003E is for your own scripts: \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft info plan.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft run cleanup.scr plan.dwg -o plan-clean.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft convert plan.dwg plan.dxf\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft plot plan.dwg plan.pdf --all\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft check plan.dwg --rules rules.json\u003C/code\u003E. Add \u003Ccode\u003E--json\u003C/code\u003E for output a program can read.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf \u003Ccode\u003Enpx @getcadcraft/mcp\u003C/code\u003E says the package is not found, it has not been published yet. News on getcadcraft.com will say when it is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"if-something-doesn-t-work\"\u003EIf something doesn&#039;t work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe assistant sees no CadCraft tools:\u003C/strong\u003E check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;needs the edit permission&quot;:\u003C/strong\u003E tick \u003Cstrong\u003EEdit\u003C/strong\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAssistants\u003C/span\u003E, then ask again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;401&quot; or &quot;token&quot; errors:\u003C/strong\u003E the token changed (Studio restarted, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew token\u003C/span\u003E). Copy the new one into the assistant&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIts change was wrong:\u003C/strong\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. One press takes back one whole action of the assistant.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to let an AI assistant (or a script you wrote) look at a drawing, run CadCraft's commands, plot and save, and how to decide what it is allowed to touch. CadCraft has no AI of its own and sends nothing anywhere. You bring the assistant: Claude Desktop, Claude Code or any program that speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP). CadCraft only gives it tools, and you choose which. There are two ways in: The drawing you have open. The assistant works in this window, next to you, through CadCraft Studio on your computer. This is what Settings › CadCraft › Assistants is for. Drawings on your computer, without opening the app. The CadCraft MCP server and the cadcraft command line read and write DWG and DXF files in folders you name. See \"Work on files without opening CadCraft\" below. Turn it on Open Settings (the gear in the app bar, or Ctrl+,) and go to the CadCraft tab. Under Assistants, tick Let AI assistants and scripts use CadCraft. It is off until you do. Choose what it may do. Read is ticked to start with; tick the others only when you need them. Permission What the assistant can do Read Look at the drawing: describe it, list and find objects, see the selection, describe the view and take a picture of it, check it against layer and drafting rules Edit Run commands on the drawing and change the selection. Each action is one step you can undo Save Save the drawing (on the desktop, to its file; in a browser, as a download) Export Plot to PDF and make other files Tools outside the permissions you ticked are not offered to the assistant at all, and if it asks for one anyway, CadCraft refuses and writes it in the activity log. Turning the switch off ends every connection at once. Reset CadCraft settings turns it off too. Connect your assistant Assistants connect through CadCraft Studio on your computer: it runs a small server that only this computer can reach (address 127.0.0.1), protected by a token that Studio makes each time it starts. Open Studio, choose Connect an assistant, and copy the address and the token. Claude Code: run the command Studio shows, which looks like claude mcp add --transport http cadcraft http://127.0.0.1:47615/mcp --header \"Authorization: Bearer \u003Ctoken\u003E\". Claude Desktop and other MCP clients: add a server of type HTTP with the address, and the header Authorization: Bearer \u003Ctoken\u003E. Studio shows the settings to paste. New token in Studio makes every connected assistant reconnect with the new token. Never share the token: anyone with it can use the apps you switched on, for as long as Studio is running. A browser tab can't open a connection for an assistant: in the browser apps the switch has no effect until you use CadCraft Studio on your computer. You stay in charge Every action is one undo step, labelled with the assistant's name for it, for example \"Assistant: LINE\". Press Ctrl+Z to take back the assistant's last action as a whole. You see what it does. Its commands appear in the command history with [Assistant] in front, and the drawing redraws as it works. It waits for you. While you are in the middle of a command, the assistant can read but not change the drawing; it is told to try again when you are done. You know when one is connected. A button in the app bar names the assistant while it is connected. Click it to see what it may do, or to Disconnect it. Activity. Settings › CadCraft › Assistants › Activity lists every call: when, which tool, done or refused, and the undo step it made. Save the log keeps a copy. What an assistant can ask for Tool Permission What it does app_info, drawing_describe, view_describe Read The drawing's name, units, layers, blocks, sheets; what is on screen objects_list, object_get, selection_get Read Objects with their geometry, length and area; the selection drawing_check Read Rules such as allowed layers, nothing on layer 0, colours ByLayer, text heights view_screenshot Read A picture of the drawing as shown command_run Edit Types at the command line, one prompt answer at a time, for example LINE, 0,0, @100,0, Enter selection_set, undo Edit Selects objects for the next command; undoes its own steps drawing_save Save CadCraft's own Save drawing_plot Export A PDF of a sheet or of the model, at a scale or fitted Commands that need a dialog of their own (picking a file, the library, Find) are not run for an assistant: it is told to ask you. Work on files without opening CadCraft The CadCraft MCP server (cadcraft-mcp) and command line (cadcraft) run CadCraft's own drawing code in Node.js, with no window. They need Node.js 20 or newer. The MCP server works only inside the folders you name, and only with the permissions you give it: npx @getcadcraft/mcp --root ~/Drawings --allow read,edit. Without --root it can't open any file; without --allow it can only read. It can also read IFC models, takeoffs and the markups in PDF sets. The command line is for your own scripts: cadcraft info plan.dwg, cadcraft run cleanup.scr plan.dwg -o plan-clean.dwg, cadcraft convert plan.dwg plan.dxf, cadcraft plot plan.dwg plan.pdf --all, cadcraft check plan.dwg --rules rules.json. Add --json for output a program can read. If npx @getcadcraft/mcp says the package is not found, it has not been published yet. News on getcadcraft.com will say when it is. If something doesn't work The assistant sees no CadCraft tools: check that the switch is on in this window, and that the window is open in Studio. \"needs the edit permission\": tick Edit under Assistants, then ask again. \"401\" or \"token\" errors: the token changed (Studio restarted, or New token). Copy the new one into the assistant's settings. Its change was wrong: press Ctrl+Z. One press takes back one whole action of the assistant."},{"slug":"properties-panel","scope":"cadcraft","title":"The Properties panel","summary":"See and change everything about the selected objects, many at once: coordinates with Z, sizes, text, dimension settings, transparency and more. Fields take expressions and lengths with units.","section":"tasks","order":61,"keywords":["properties","properties palette","ctrl+1","pr","ch","mo","ddmodify","varies","quick select","pickadd","coordinates","z","elevation","thickness","transparency","material","hyperlink","expression","quickcalc","vertex","dimension style","tolerance","decimals"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"open-it","text":"Open it","level":2},{"id":"fold-it-away","text":"Fold it away","level":3},{"id":"sections","text":"Sections","level":2},{"id":"change-a-value","text":"Change a value","level":2},{"id":"expressions-and-units","text":"Expressions and units","level":3},{"id":"change-many-objects-at-once","text":"Change many objects at once","level":2},{"id":"coordinates","text":"Coordinates","level":2},{"id":"general","text":"General","level":2},{"id":"what-each-type-shows","text":"What each type shows","level":2},{"id":"polyline-vertices","text":"Polyline vertices","level":3},{"id":"dimensions","text":"Dimensions","level":2},{"id":"nothing-selected","text":"Nothing selected","level":2},{"id":"the-buttons-at-the-top","text":"The buttons at the top","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to open the Properties panel, change one object or a hundred in one step, type exact coordinates (with Z), use expressions and units in any number field, and what each kind of object shows.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-it\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel sits on the right. To bring it to the front:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 1\"\u003E⌘1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPROPERTIES\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EPR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMO\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click an object (text and multileaders open their text editor instead).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe panel is a movable panel like the others: drag its grip to dock it left, right or at the bottom, or drop it over the drawing to float it (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/arrange-panels\" data-topic=\"basics/arrange-panels\"\u003EArrange the panels and use a second monitor\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fold-it-away\"\u003EFold it away\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pin button at the top right of the panel turns on \u003Cstrong\u003Eauto-hide\u003C/strong\u003E: the panel folds to a strip at the right edge and the drawing gets the room. Point at the strip (or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command 1\"\u003E⌘1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and it opens over the drawing; move away and it folds again. Click the pin again to keep it open. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPROPERTIESCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E folds it too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sections\"\u003ESections\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EProperties are grouped in sections you can fold: \u003Cstrong\u003EGeneral\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EGeometry\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EText\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELines and arrows\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EFit\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPrimary units\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EAlternate units\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ETolerances\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EPattern\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EMisc\u003C/strong\u003E, and for blocks \u003Cstrong\u003EAttributes\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ELibrary item\u003C/strong\u003E. Click a section&#039;s name to fold or open it; CadCraft remembers which ones you folded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the thin line between the names and the values to make the names wider or narrower. Double-click it to put it back. With the keyboard, focus it and use the arrow keys.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-value\"\u003EChange a value\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a line. The heading reads &quot;Line&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E. While you edit, the field shows the full value, not the rounded one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E2500\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The line is now 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or clicking elsewhere applies the value; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"expressions-and-units\"\u003EExpressions and units\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery number field takes more than a plain number:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou get\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E=2*45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E90\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E=sqrt(2)*100\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E141.4214\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/4\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.25, 6.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E150, 150.5 (inches, in a drawing in feet and inches)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E900mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E3.5m\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2ft\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConverted into the drawing&#039;s units\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E45d30&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E45°30&#039;15&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E45.5°, 45.5042° (angle fields)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E0.5r\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100g\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERadians and grads, as degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA length with a unit needs the drawing to have units: set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew objects\u003C/span\u003E (nothing selected) first. A bad entry is refused with a note under the panel and nothing changes; it is never saved as 0. Functions: sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, sqrt, pow, exp, ln, log, abs, round, ceil, floor, min, max, if; angles in degrees.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-many-objects-at-once\"\u003EChange many objects at once\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect several objects, for example 8 lines and 4 dimensions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe heading says &quot;12 objects&quot;, and a list at the top offers \u003Cstrong\u003EAll (12)\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003ELine (8)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension (4)\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAll\u003C/strong\u003E shows what every object has (layer, color, linetype…). Pick \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension (4)\u003C/strong\u003E to see dimension settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange a value, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll four dimensions change, in one undo step. A field that reads \u003Cstrong\u003EVaries\u003C/strong\u003E means the objects differ; typing a value sets it on all of them. Objects on locked layers are left as they are, and the note says how many.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"coordinates\"\u003ECoordinates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery point shows \u003Cstrong\u003EX\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EY\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EZ\u003C/strong\u003E: start and end of a line, centers, insertion points, text positions, the current polyline vertex. Lines also show \u003Cstrong\u003EDelta X\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EDelta Y\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EDelta Z\u003C/strong\u003E (read-only). Polylines and hatches have an \u003Cstrong\u003EElevation\u003C/strong\u003E (one Z for the whole object).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe crosshair button beside a point&#039;s name lets you pick it in the drawing instead: click it, then click the new position (snaps work). Values are world coordinates.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"general\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EField\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColor\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype scale\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAs in the layers panel; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E follows the layer\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETransparency\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy layer, by block, or 0 to 90%\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThickness\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHow far lines, circles, arcs, polylines, points and text extend in Z\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBy layer, by block, Global or a name a drawing brought\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot style\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead-only: drawings plot by color (a color-dependent table)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHyperlink\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA web address kept with the object\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-each-type-shows\"\u003EWhat each type shows\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESelected\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou can change\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart and end X, Y, Z; length; angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECircle\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter; radius; diameter; circumference; area (each sets the radius)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter; radius; start and end angle; total angle; chord (shows arc length and area)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEllipse\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter; major and minor radius; radius ratio; major axis angle; start and end angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe vertex browser (below); global width; elevation; closed; area when closed (scales it about its center)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EContents; style; justify; height; rotation; width factor; obliquing angle; defined width; line spacing; annotative; position\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBlock reference\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInsertion point; rotation; scale X, Y, Z and uniform scale; attribute values\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPattern; scale; angle; origin; associative; island detection; background color; elevation\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee below\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultileader\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText; height; width; text side; arrowhead size; landing distance; arrowhead point\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EViewport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EScale; center and size on the sheet; view center in the model; on; display locked\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"polyline-vertices\"\u003EPolyline vertices\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent vertex\u003C/span\u003E shows which vertex the fields below it belong to. Use the arrow buttons, or type a number, to move to another vertex; there is no limit on how many. Each vertex has X, Y, start and end segment width, and the bulge of the segment that starts there (0 = straight).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension shows its \u003Cstrong\u003EDimension style\u003C/strong\u003E, what it measures, its points, and every setting:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELines and arrows:\u003C/strong\u003E arrowhead for each end, arrow size, tick size, colors and lineweights of the dimension and extension lines, hiding either half or either extension line, extension line offset and extension.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText:\u003C/strong\u003E override (leave empty for the measured value; \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E stands for it inside your own text), style, color, height, offset, vertical and horizontal position, always horizontal, rotation, background fill.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFit:\u003C/strong\u003E overall scale, baseline spacing, what moves when space is short, text movement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrimary units:\u003C/strong\u003E format, decimals, round off, prefix, suffix, measurement scale, decimal separator, suppress leading and trailing zeros; for angles the angle format and decimals.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAlternate units:\u003C/strong\u003E on or off, format, decimals, multiplier (25.4 turns inches into millimeters), round off, prefix and suffix.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETolerances:\u003C/strong\u003E none, symmetrical, deviation, limits or basic, with upper and lower values, decimals, height and position.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging any of them on an opened dimension drops the drawing the other program made of it, and CadCraft draws it again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe horizontal text position (beside the first or second extension line) and text below the line are drawn on linear and aligned dimensions; on other dimensions they are kept and saved but not drawn yet. The Fit choices are kept and saved but not drawn yet: CadCraft places text by its own rule (above the line, outside when it does not fit).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"nothing-selected\"\u003ENothing selected\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith nothing selected the panel shows:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew objects:\u003C/strong\u003E the layer, color, linetype, lineweight and text height new objects get; the drawing&#039;s units and length format; the current layer&#039;s linetype and lineweight; sizes for new dimensions. On a sheet, the sheet&#039;s name, paper and orientation come first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EView:\u003C/strong\u003E the center, height and width of what is on screen. Type a value to move the view there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMisc:\u003C/strong\u003E the UCS icon on or off, the UCS (always World: CadCraft has no user coordinate systems yet) and the annotation scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-buttons-at-the-top\"\u003EThe buttons at the top\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDoes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFunnel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E: select by type and property\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPointer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESELECT\u003C/code\u003E: select objects at the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlus or 1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhether a click adds to the selection (plus) or replaces it (1); the same setting as Settings, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAuto-hide, above\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyping in a field did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click elsewhere to apply it. A red field and a note mean the entry was refused.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA length with a unit is refused.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing has no units. Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E with nothing selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOnly Layer and Color show.\u003C/strong\u003E Several kinds of objects are selected and \u003Cstrong\u003EAll\u003C/strong\u003E is chosen. Pick one kind in the list at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESome objects did not change.\u003C/strong\u003E They are on a locked layer. Unlock the layer in the layers panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETyped letters went into a field.\u003C/strong\u003E While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then type the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How to open the Properties panel, change one object or a hundred in one step, type exact coordinates (with Z), use expressions and units in any number field, and what each kind of object shows. Open it The panel sits on the right. To bring it to the front: Press Ctrl+1, or type PROPERTIES (or PR, CH, MO). Double-click an object (text and multileaders open their text editor instead). The panel is a movable panel like the others: drag its grip to dock it left, right or at the bottom, or drop it over the drawing to float it (see Arrange the panels and use a second monitor). Fold it away The pin button at the top right of the panel turns on auto-hide: the panel folds to a strip at the right edge and the drawing gets the room. Point at the strip (or press Ctrl+1) and it opens over the drawing; move away and it folds again. Click the pin again to keep it open. PROPERTIESCLOSE folds it too. Sections Properties are grouped in sections you can fold: General, Geometry, Text, Lines and arrows, Fit, Primary units, Alternate units, Tolerances, Pattern, Misc, and for blocks Attributes and Library item. Click a section's name to fold or open it; CadCraft remembers which ones you folded. Drag the thin line between the names and the values to make the names wider or narrower. Double-click it to put it back. With the keyboard, focus it and use the arrow keys. Change a value Click a line. The heading reads \"Line\". Click in Length. While you edit, the field shows the full value, not the rounded one. Type 2500 and press Enter. The line is now 2500 long, keeping its start point and angle. Enter or clicking elsewhere applies the value; Esc puts the old value back. Every change is one undo step. Expressions and units Every number field takes more than a plain number: You type You get =2*45 90 =sqrt(2)*100 141.4214 1/4, 6-1/2 0.25, 6.5 12'6\", 12'-6 1/2\" 150, 150.5 (inches, in a drawing in feet and inches) 900mm, 3.5m, 2ft Converted into the drawing's units 45d30', 45°30'15\" 45.5°, 45.5042° (angle fields) 0.5r, 100g Radians and grads, as degrees A length with a unit needs the drawing to have units: set Units under New objects (nothing selected) first. A bad entry is refused with a note under the panel and nothing changes; it is never saved as 0. Functions: sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, sqrt, pow, exp, ln, log, abs, round, ceil, floor, min, max, if; angles in degrees. Change many objects at once Select several objects, for example 8 lines and 4 dimensions. The heading says \"12 objects\", and a list at the top offers All (12), Line (8) and Dimension (4). All shows what every object has (layer, color, linetype…). Pick Dimension (4) to see dimension settings. Change a value, for example Decimals to 1. All four dimensions change, in one undo step. A field that reads Varies means the objects differ; typing a value sets it on all of them. Objects on locked layers are left as they are, and the note says how many. Coordinates Every point shows X, Y and Z: start and end of a line, centers, insertion points, text positions, the current polyline vertex. Lines also show Delta X, Delta Y and Delta Z (read-only). Polylines and hatches have an Elevation (one Z for the whole object). The crosshair button beside a point's name lets you pick it in the drawing instead: click it, then click the new position (snaps work). Values are world coordinates. General Field What it is Layer, Color, Linetype, Linetype scale, Lineweight As in the layers panel; By layer follows the layer Transparency By layer, by block, or 0 to 90% Thickness How far lines, circles, arcs, polylines, points and text extend in Z Material By layer, by block, Global or a name a drawing brought Plot style Read-only: drawings plot by color (a color-dependent table) Hyperlink A web address kept with the object What each type shows Selected You can change Line Start and end X, Y, Z; length; angle Circle Center; radius; diameter; circumference; area (each sets the radius) Arc Center; radius; start and end angle; total angle; chord (shows arc length and area) Ellipse Center; major and minor radius; radius ratio; major axis angle; start and end angle Polyline The vertex browser (below); global width; elevation; closed; area when closed (scales it about its center) Text Contents; style; justify; height; rotation; width factor; obliquing angle; defined width; line spacing; annotative; position Block reference Insertion point; rotation; scale X, Y, Z and uniform scale; attribute values Hatch Pattern; scale; angle; origin; associative; island detection; background color; elevation Dimension See below Multileader Text; height; width; text side; arrowhead size; landing distance; arrowhead point Viewport Scale; center and size on the sheet; view center in the model; on; display locked Polyline vertices Current vertex shows which vertex the fields below it belong to. Use the arrow buttons, or type a number, to move to another vertex; there is no limit on how many. Each vertex has X, Y, start and end segment width, and the bulge of the segment that starts there (0 = straight). Dimensions A dimension shows its Dimension style, what it measures, its points, and every setting: Lines and arrows: arrowhead for each end, arrow size, tick size, colors and lineweights of the dimension and extension lines, hiding either half or either extension line, extension line offset and extension. Text: override (leave empty for the measured value; \u003C\u003E stands for it inside your own text), style, color, height, offset, vertical and horizontal position, always horizontal, rotation, background fill. Fit: overall scale, baseline spacing, what moves when space is short, text movement. Primary units: format, decimals, round off, prefix, suffix, measurement scale, decimal separator, suppress leading and trailing zeros; for angles the angle format and decimals. Alternate units: on or off, format, decimals, multiplier (25.4 turns inches into millimeters), round off, prefix and suffix. Tolerances: none, symmetrical, deviation, limits or basic, with upper and lower values, decimals, height and position. Changing any of them on an opened dimension drops the drawing the other program made of it, and CadCraft draws it again. The horizontal text position (beside the first or second extension line) and text below the line are drawn on linear and aligned dimensions; on other dimensions they are kept and saved but not drawn yet. The Fit choices are kept and saved but not drawn yet: CadCraft places text by its own rule (above the line, outside when it does not fit). Nothing selected With nothing selected the panel shows: New objects: the layer, color, linetype, lineweight and text height new objects get; the drawing's units and length format; the current layer's linetype and lineweight; sizes for new dimensions. On a sheet, the sheet's name, paper and orientation come first. View: the center, height and width of what is on screen. Type a value to move the view there. Misc: the UCS icon on or off, the UCS (always World: CadCraft has no user coordinate systems yet) and the annotation scale. The buttons at the top Button Does Funnel QSELECT: select by type and property Pointer SELECT: select objects at the command line Plus or 1 Whether a click adds to the selection (plus) or replaces it (1); the same setting as Settings, CadCraft Pin Auto-hide, above Common mistakes Typing in a field did nothing. Press Enter or click elsewhere to apply it. A red field and a note mean the entry was refused. A length with a unit is refused. The drawing has no units. Set Units with nothing selected. Only Layer and Color show. Several kinds of objects are selected and All is chosen. Pick one kind in the list at the top. Some objects did not change. They are on a locked layer. Unlock the layer in the layers panel. Typed letters went into a field. While a field has the cursor, keys type there. Press Enter or Esc, then type the command."},{"slug":"layers","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Organize a drawing with layers","summary":"Create layers for walls, doors and notes, set colors, make a layer current, and turn layers off, freeze, lock or keep them from plotting.","section":"tasks","order":70,"keywords":["layer","layers","la","current layer","freeze","thaw","lock","off","on","plot","no plot","colour","color","layer properties","layiso","layuniso","isolate","layoff","layfrz","laymcur","layon","laythw","rename","laymrg","merge","laydel","delete layer"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"the-layers-panel","text":"The Layers panel","level":2},{"id":"create-layers","text":"Create layers","level":2},{"id":"give-each-layer-a-color","text":"Give each layer a color","level":2},{"id":"draw-on-a-layer","text":"Draw on a layer","level":2},{"id":"move-objects-to-another-layer","text":"Move objects to another layer","level":2},{"id":"off-freeze-lock-and-plot","text":"Off, freeze, lock and plot","level":2},{"id":"work-on-layers-by-picking-objects","text":"Work on layers by picking objects","level":2},{"id":"rename-merge-and-delete-layers","text":"Rename, merge and delete layers","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThree layers, Walls, Doors and Notes, each with its own color, with objects drawn on each and moved between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-layers-panel\"\u003EThe Layers panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayers live in the panel on the left, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E. Each row shows, from left to right:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea dot: filled for the \u003Cstrong\u003Ecurrent\u003C/strong\u003E layer, where new objects go;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ea color swatch;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe layer name;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efour buttons: on/off (eye), freeze (snowflake), lock (padlock) and plot (printer).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery drawing has a layer called \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E. Typing \u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E and pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E jumps to the panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"create-layers\"\u003ECreate layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E above the list. A name box opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EWalls\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar says &quot;Added layer Walls and made it current.&quot; The new layer gets color 7 (black on the light theme, white on the dark one).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E again, type \u003Ccode\u003EDoors\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E again, type \u003Ccode\u003ENotes\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENotes is now current.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"give-each-layer-a-color\"\u003EGive each layer a color\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the color swatch on the Walls row. Your system&#039;s color picker opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose a color and confirm.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe swatch and everything on Walls that is colored \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA picked color that matches one of the 255 standard CAD colors is stored as that number. Any other color is kept exactly in CadCraft, but is saved as the nearest standard color in a DWG or DXF file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-on-a-layer\"\u003EDraw on a layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the name \u003Cstrong\u003EWalls\u003C/strong\u003E in the list. Its dot fills and the status bar says &quot;Current layer: Walls&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a rectangle (\u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E). It goes on Walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cstrong\u003EDoors\u003C/strong\u003E, and draw an arc (\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E). It goes on Doors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can also choose the current layer in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E on the right (nothing selected), under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-objects-to-another-layer\"\u003EMove objects to another layer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect the objects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, choose the new layer in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThey move to that layer and take its color if their color is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"off-freeze-lock-and-plot\"\u003EOff, freeze, lock and plot\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EButton\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn/off (eye)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHides the layer&#039;s objects. They cannot be seen, selected or snapped to.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFreeze (snowflake)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame effect as off in CadCraft. Kept separate because other programs treat them differently, and the setting is saved.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELock (padlock)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects stay visible and you can snap to them, but they cannot be selected or changed. Good for a background you trace over.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot (printer)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhen pressed, the layer is shown on screen but \u003Cstrong\u003Eleft out of plots\u003C/strong\u003E and PDFs. Use it for construction lines and notes to yourself.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHover a button to see what it will do (&quot;Turn off&quot;, &quot;Freeze&quot;, &quot;Lock&quot;, &quot;Do not plot this layer&quot;).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot turn off the current layer: the status bar says &quot;That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.&quot; Clicking a layer&#039;s name to make it current also turns it on and thaws it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery layer change, in the panel or by a command, is one undo step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E puts it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"work-on-layers-by-picking-objects\"\u003EWork on layers by picking objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou do not need to know a layer&#039;s name: pick an object on it. Type the command, or open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect objects, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: only their layers stay on, every other layer is turned off. If the current layer is turned off, one of the isolated layers becomes current.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns back on the layers the last \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E turned off, and gives back the current layer it changed.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick objects one after another: each one&#039;s layer is turned off. Type \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E to take back the last one, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe same, freezing each layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick an object: its layer becomes current (and is turned on and thawed). With one object already selected, that one is used.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYON\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETurns every layer on.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYTHW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThaws every layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe current layer is never turned off or frozen this way: the history says so, and you can make another layer current with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E first. Picking a block reference uses the reference&#039;s own layer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"rename-merge-and-delete-layers\"\u003ERename, merge and delete layers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E: type \u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E, the old name and the new one. Every object on the layer follows. (\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E renames a block the same way, with every reference to it.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E: click an object on each layer to merge (or type \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E and a name), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then pick the target layer and answer \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E. The objects move to the target — in the model, on sheets and inside blocks — and the merged layers go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E: pick an object on the layer (or \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E and its name) and answer \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E. The layer goes \u003Cstrong\u003Ewith every object on it\u003C/strong\u003E, everywhere. Layer 0, DEFPOINTS and the current layer cannot be deleted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach is one undo step. All three are in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E at the top of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one wall line, then type \u003Ccode\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Only Walls stays on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw or change what you need on Walls.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Doors and Notes come back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENew lines went on the wrong layer.\u003C/strong\u003E Check which dot is filled before you draw. A new layer becomes current as soon as you add it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn object will not select.\u003C/strong\u003E Its layer is locked, off or frozen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe layer still prints in another program.\u003C/strong\u003E DXF R12 cannot store &quot;do not plot&quot;. CadCraft keeps the setting for itself when the drawing has a sheet, but other programs will plot that layer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayer names in capitals after saving.\u003C/strong\u003E DXF R12 stores names in capitals, so \u003Ccode\u003EWalls\u003C/code\u003E comes back as \u003Ccode\u003EWALLS\u003C/code\u003E, and spaces become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERenaming, merging or deleting a layer.\u003C/strong\u003E Use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E (also in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E): see below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make Three layers, Walls, Doors and Notes, each with its own color, with objects drawn on each and moved between them. The Layers panel Layers live in the panel on the left, under Layers. Each row shows, from left to right: a dot: filled for the current layer, where new objects go; a color swatch; the layer name; four buttons: on/off (eye), freeze (snowflake), lock (padlock) and plot (printer). Every drawing has a layer called 0. Typing LA and pressing Enter jumps to the panel. Create layers Click New layer above the list. A name box opens. Type Walls and press Enter (or click Add). The status bar says \"Added layer Walls and made it current.\" The new layer gets color 7 (black on the light theme, white on the dark one). Click New layer again, type Doors and press Enter. Click New layer again, type Notes and press Enter. Notes is now current. Give each layer a color Click the color swatch on the Walls row. Your system's color picker opens. Choose a color and confirm. The swatch and everything on Walls that is colored By layer change. A picked color that matches one of the 255 standard CAD colors is stored as that number. Any other color is kept exactly in CadCraft, but is saved as the nearest standard color in a DWG or DXF file. Draw on a layer Click the name Walls in the list. Its dot fills and the status bar says \"Current layer: Walls\". Draw a rectangle (REC). It goes on Walls. Click Doors, and draw an arc (A). It goes on Doors. You can also choose the current layer in Current settings on the right (nothing selected), under Layer. Move objects to another layer Select the objects. In the right panel, choose the new layer in Layer. They move to that layer and take its color if their color is By layer. Off, freeze, lock and plot Button What it does On/off (eye) Hides the layer's objects. They cannot be seen, selected or snapped to. Freeze (snowflake) Same effect as off in CadCraft. Kept separate because other programs treat them differently, and the setting is saved. Lock (padlock) Objects stay visible and you can snap to them, but they cannot be selected or changed. Good for a background you trace over. Plot (printer) When pressed, the layer is shown on screen but left out of plots and PDFs. Use it for construction lines and notes to yourself. Hover a button to see what it will do (\"Turn off\", \"Freeze\", \"Lock\", \"Do not plot this layer\"). You cannot turn off the current layer: the status bar says \"That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.\" Clicking a layer's name to make it current also turns it on and thaws it. Every layer change, in the panel or by a command, is one undo step: Ctrl+Z puts it back. Work on layers by picking objects You do not need to know a layer's name: pick an object on it. Type the command, or open Tools at the top of the Layers panel. Command What it does LAYISO Select objects, press Enter: only their layers stay on, every other layer is turned off. If the current layer is turned off, one of the isolated layers becomes current. LAYUNISO Turns back on the layers the last LAYISO turned off, and gives back the current layer it changed. LAYOFF Click objects one after another: each one's layer is turned off. Type U to take back the last one, Enter to finish. LAYFRZ The same, freezing each layer. LAYMCUR Click an object: its layer becomes current (and is turned on and thawed). With one object already selected, that one is used. LAYON Turns every layer on. LAYTHW Thaws every layer. The current layer is never turned off or frozen this way: the history says so, and you can make another layer current with LAYMCUR first. Picking a block reference uses the reference's own layer. Rename, merge and delete layers RENAME: type LA, the old name and the new one. Every object on the layer follows. (B renames a block the same way, with every reference to it.) LAYMRG: click an object on each layer to merge (or type N and a name), press Enter, then pick the target layer and answer Y. The objects move to the target — in the model, on sheets and inside blocks — and the merged layers go. LAYDEL: pick an object on the layer (or N and its name) and answer Y. The layer goes with every object on it, everywhere. Layer 0, DEFPOINTS and the current layer cannot be deleted. Each is one undo step. All three are in Tools at the top of the Layers panel. Click one wall line, then type LAYISO and press Enter. Only Walls stays on. Draw or change what you need on Walls. Type LAYUNISO and press Enter. Doors and Notes come back. Common mistakes New lines went on the wrong layer. Check which dot is filled before you draw. A new layer becomes current as soon as you add it. An object will not select. Its layer is locked, off or frozen. The layer still prints in another program. DXF R12 cannot store \"do not plot\". CadCraft keeps the setting for itself when the drawing has a sheet, but other programs will plot that layer. Layer names in capitals after saving. DXF R12 stores names in capitals, so Walls comes back as WALLS, and spaces become _. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them. Renaming, merging or deleting a layer. Use RENAME, LAYMRG or LAYDEL (also in Tools): see below."},{"slug":"text","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Add text and labels","summary":"Place room labels with single-line text, write wrapped notes with paragraph text typed in place, and edit text afterwards.","section":"tasks","order":80,"keywords":["text","dtext","mtext","mleader","multileader","leader","revcloud","revision cloud","cloud","find","replace","find and replace","paragraph","wrap","line spacing","label","note","annotation","height","justify","rotation","edit text","ddedit","textedit","font"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"text-height-and-drawing-scale","text":"Text height and drawing scale","level":2},{"id":"place-a-centered-label","text":"Place a centered label","level":2},{"id":"write-a-note-that-wraps","text":"Write a note that wraps","level":2},{"id":"several-single-lines-instead","text":"Several single lines instead","level":3},{"id":"justification-options","text":"Justification options","level":2},{"id":"edit-text","text":"Edit text","level":2},{"id":"point-at-something-multileaders","text":"Point at something: multileaders","level":2},{"id":"mark-changes-with-a-revision-cloud","text":"Mark changes with a revision cloud","level":2},{"id":"find-and-replace-words","text":"Find and replace words","level":2},{"id":"font","text":"Font","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETwo room labels, centered in their rooms, a note that wraps to its width, then a correction to one of the labels.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"text-height-and-drawing-scale\"\u003EText height and drawing scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText height is in drawing units. In a drawing in millimeters that will be plotted at 1:50, text 2.5 mm tall on paper must be 2.5 × 50 = 125 units tall in the model. On a sheet tab you draw in paper units, so 2.5 there is 2.5 mm on paper.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-a-centered-label\"\u003EPlace a centered label\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar). The prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of text [Justify]:\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] &lt;L&gt;:\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EMC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point of text\u003C/code\u003E, click the middle of a room.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify height\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E250\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle of text &lt;0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EKITCHEN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe label appears, centered on the point you clicked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on the empty \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text\u003C/code\u003E prompt to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"write-a-note-that-wraps\"\u003EWrite a note that wraps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EParagraph text (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E) wraps to a width and is typed straight onto the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParagraph\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner\u003C/code\u003E, click where the note&#039;s top-left corner goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo change the height first, type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E250\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width]\u003C/code\u003E, click to the right of the first corner. The distance across is the width the text wraps at; how far down you click does not limit it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA box opens over the drawing with the cursor in it. Type \u003Ccode\u003EALL DIMENSIONS ARE IN MILLIMETERS UNLESS NOTED\u003C/code\u003E. The words wrap at the width as you type.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start a new paragraph and type \u003Ccode\u003ECHECK ON SITE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOK\u003C/span\u003E, or click anywhere outside the box. The note is one object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe bar above the box changes \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine spacing\u003C/span\u003E (1 is normal, 1.5 is half as much again), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWidth\u003C/span\u003E (0 = no wrapping) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJustify\u003C/span\u003E while you type. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E closes it without adding anything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe options at the second corner: \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E height, \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E justification (\u003Ccode\u003ETL\u003C/code\u003E top left … \u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E bottom right: the corner or middle of the box that stays put), \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E line spacing, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E rotation, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E a typed width instead of the second corner.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile the box is open, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E types a space and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts a new paragraph. A saved DWG keeps paragraph text as MTEXT; a DXF R12 has no paragraph text, so each line is saved as its own text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"several-single-lines-instead\"\u003ESeveral single lines instead\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E) asks for one line at a time: each \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts the next line, 1.667 times the text height lower, and each line is its own object. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty line to finish.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"justification-options\"\u003EJustification options\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOption\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EThe point you click is\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeft (default)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe left end of the baseline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECenter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of the baseline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe right end of the baseline\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMiddle, MC\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe middle of the text, both across and up and down\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETL\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe top-left corner\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"edit-text\"\u003EEdit text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click a text object. It opens in the same box as new paragraph text, with the cursor at the end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChange the words.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click outside the box. The text on the drawing changes; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves it as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXTEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EED\u003C/code\u003E) does the same: pick one text after another, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when done.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a text to change it in the right panel instead: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWrap width\u003C/span\u003E (0 = no wrapping), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine spacing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation\u003C/span\u003E, the X and Y position and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJustify\u003C/span\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJustify\u003C/span\u003E list also offers Bottom left, which text from other programs sometimes uses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"point-at-something-multileaders\"\u003EPoint at something: multileaders\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E group (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMLEADER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMLD\u003C/code\u003E) draws an arrow, a leader line, a short landing and a note, as one object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E. At \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader arrowhead location\u003C/code\u003E, click what the note is about.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader landing location\u003C/code\u003E, click where the note goes. The landing turns toward the side you clicked.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the note in the box that opens, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E at the first question set the arrowhead size and the landing length; the text height comes from the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E for new dimensions, times \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E. Double-click a multileader to change its words. Grips move the arrowhead and the landing; the note follows the landing. In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWrap width\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrowhead size\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELanding distance\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMultileaders save as real multileaders in DWG. DXF has no multileader in R12 or 2000, so there they are saved as the lines, arrowhead and text they draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mark-changes-with-a-revision-cloud\"\u003EMark changes with a revision cloud\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevision cloud\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREVCLOUD\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERC\u003C/code\u003E) draws a cloud of arcs round what changed. The history shows the arc length and the type it will use.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangular\u003C/strong\u003E (the first time): click two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EPolygonal\u003C/strong\u003E: click the corners, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the cloud.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EFreehand\u003C/strong\u003E: click where it starts and move the pointer round the area without clicking; an arc is added every arc length. Click or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to close it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EObject\u003C/strong\u003E: pick a closed polyline, circle or ellipse (or a line, arc or open polyline); it becomes the cloud. \u003Ccode\u003EReverse direction?\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E turns the arcs inward.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EArc length\u003C/strong\u003E: the length of each arc. Choose one that suits the plot scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe cloud is a closed polyline of arcs, so it saves in every format.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-replace-words\"\u003EFind and replace words\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFIND\u003C/code\u003E) looks through text, the values of block attributes and dimension text on the open tab (or only in the selected objects).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the words in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E. The list shows each place they are, and the count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMatch case\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhole words only\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUse wildcards (* and ?)\u003C/span\u003E if you need them, and untick what not to look in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESelect found\u003C/span\u003E to select those objects, or type the new words in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace with\u003C/span\u003E (the list shows what each becomes) and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplace all\u003C/span\u003E. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes every replacement.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension&#039;s measured value is not text, so it is never replaced: in \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt; TYP.\u003C/code\u003E, only \u003Ccode\u003ETYP.\u003C/code\u003E can be.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"font\"\u003EFont\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll text is drawn in a Helvetica-style font, on screen and in PDFs. Text from opened drawings keeps its words, height and position, but not its original font.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn plotted PDFs, characters outside the Western European set (for example Greek, Cyrillic or Chinese) print as &quot;?&quot;, and the history says how many there were.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe text is too small to see.\u003C/strong\u003E The height is in drawing units. In a large drawing, use a large height (see above), or zoom in.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe command ended after one line.\u003C/strong\u003E You pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty line in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E. Start it again, or use paragraph text (\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E), where \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E starts a new paragraph.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E opened a box instead of asking for a line.\u003C/strong\u003E As in AutoCAD, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E is paragraph text (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E); single-line text is \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe text wraps in a different place in AutoCAD.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft wraps with Helvetica widths; another font can fit a word more or less on a line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText in a mirrored copy.\u003C/strong\u003E It is placed mirrored but stays readable left to right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make Two room labels, centered in their rooms, a note that wraps to its width, then a correction to one of the labels. Text height and drawing scale Text height is in drawing units. In a drawing in millimeters that will be plotted at 1:50, text 2.5 mm tall on paper must be 2.5 × 50 = 125 units tall in the model. On a sheet tab you draw in paper units, so 2.5 there is 2.5 mm on paper. Place a centered label Type DT and press Enter (or click Text in the toolbar). The prompt reads Specify start point of text [Justify]:. Type J and press Enter. At Enter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] \u003CL\u003E:, type MC and press Enter. At Specify insertion point of text, click the middle of a room. At Specify height, type 250 and press Enter. At Specify rotation angle of text \u003C0\u003E, press Enter. At Enter text, type KITCHEN and press Enter. The label appears, centered on the point you clicked. Press Enter on the empty Enter text prompt to end the command. Write a note that wraps Paragraph text (MTEXT) wraps to a width and is typed straight onto the drawing. Type T and press Enter, or click Paragraph in the toolbar. At Specify first corner, click where the note's top-left corner goes. To change the height first, type H, press Enter, type 250 and press Enter. At Specify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width], click to the right of the first corner. The distance across is the width the text wraps at; how far down you click does not limit it. A box opens over the drawing with the cursor in it. Type ALL DIMENSIONS ARE IN MILLIMETERS UNLESS NOTED. The words wrap at the width as you type. Press Enter to start a new paragraph and type CHECK ON SITE. Press Ctrl+Enter, click OK, or click anywhere outside the box. The note is one object. The bar above the box changes Height, Line spacing (1 is normal, 1.5 is half as much again), Width (0 = no wrapping) and Justify while you type. Esc or Cancel closes it without adding anything. The options at the second corner: H height, J justification (TL top left … BR bottom right: the corner or middle of the box that stays put), L line spacing, R rotation, W a typed width instead of the second corner. While the box is open, Space types a space and Enter starts a new paragraph. A saved DWG keeps paragraph text as MTEXT; a DXF R12 has no paragraph text, so each line is saved as its own text. Several single lines instead TEXT (DT) asks for one line at a time: each Enter starts the next line, 1.667 times the text height lower, and each line is its own object. Press Enter on an empty line to finish. Justification options Option The point you click is Left (default) The left end of the baseline Center The middle of the baseline Right The right end of the baseline Middle, MC The middle of the text, both across and up and down TL The top-left corner Edit text Double-click a text object. It opens in the same box as new paragraph text, with the cursor at the end. Change the words. Press Ctrl+Enter or click outside the box. The text on the drawing changes; Esc leaves it as it was. TEXTEDIT (ED) does the same: pick one text after another, and press Enter when done. Select a text to change it in the right panel instead: Text, Height, Wrap width (0 = no wrapping), Line spacing, Rotation, the X and Y position and Justify. The Justify list also offers Bottom left, which text from other programs sometimes uses. Point at something: multileaders Leader in the Annotate group (MLEADER, MLD) draws an arrow, a leader line, a short landing and a note, as one object. Click Leader. At Specify leader arrowhead location, click what the note is about. At Specify leader landing location, click where the note goes. The landing turns toward the side you clicked. Type the note in the box that opens, and press Ctrl+Enter. A and L at the first question set the arrowhead size and the landing length; the text height comes from the Text height for new dimensions, times Overall scale. Double-click a multileader to change its words. Grips move the arrowhead and the landing; the note follows the landing. In Properties: Text, Height, Wrap width, Arrowhead size and Landing distance. Multileaders save as real multileaders in DWG. DXF has no multileader in R12 or 2000, so there they are saved as the lines, arrowhead and text they draw. Mark changes with a revision cloud Annotate › More › Revision cloud (REVCLOUD, RC) draws a cloud of arcs round what changed. The history shows the arc length and the type it will use. Rectangular (the first time): click two corners. P Polygonal: click the corners, Enter closes the cloud. F Freehand: click where it starts and move the pointer round the area without clicking; an arc is added every arc length. Click or press Enter to close it. O Object: pick a closed polyline, circle or ellipse (or a line, arc or open polyline); it becomes the cloud. Reverse direction? Y turns the arcs inward. A Arc length: the length of each arc. Choose one that suits the plot scale. The cloud is a closed polyline of arcs, so it saves in every format. Find and replace words Edit › More › Find and replace (FIND) looks through text, the values of block attributes and dimension text on the open tab (or only in the selected objects). Type the words in Find. The list shows each place they are, and the count. Choose Match case, Whole words only or Use wildcards (* and ?) if you need them, and untick what not to look in. Click Select found to select those objects, or type the new words in Replace with (the list shows what each becomes) and click Replace all. One Ctrl+Z undoes every replacement. A dimension's measured value is not text, so it is never replaced: in \u003C\u003E TYP., only TYP. can be. Font All text is drawn in a Helvetica-style font, on screen and in PDFs. Text from opened drawings keeps its words, height and position, but not its original font. In plotted PDFs, characters outside the Western European set (for example Greek, Cyrillic or Chinese) print as \"?\", and the history says how many there were. Common mistakes The text is too small to see. The height is in drawing units. In a large drawing, use a large height (see above), or zoom in. The command ended after one line. You pressed Enter on an empty line in TEXT. Start it again, or use paragraph text (T), where Enter starts a new paragraph. T opened a box instead of asking for a line. As in AutoCAD, T is paragraph text (MTEXT); single-line text is DT. The text wraps in a different place in AutoCAD. CadCraft wraps with Helvetica widths; another font can fit a word more or less on a line. Text in a mirrored copy. It is placed mirrored but stays readable left to right."},{"slug":"hatch","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Hatch and fill areas","summary":"Fill an area with a solid color or a standard pattern by clicking inside it or by picking closed shapes, set the pattern's scale and angle, and change a hatch afterwards.","section":"tasks","order":85,"keywords":["hatch","bhatch","fill","solid fill","pattern","ansi31","ansi37","brick","earth","net","section lines","poché","island","boundary","scale","angle"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"hatch-by-clicking-inside-an-area","text":"Hatch by clicking inside an area","level":2},{"id":"a-solid-fill","text":"A solid fill","level":2},{"id":"hatch-closed-shapes-you-pick","text":"Hatch closed shapes you pick","level":2},{"id":"patterns","text":"Patterns","level":2},{"id":"change-a-hatch","text":"Change a hatch","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA wall section filled with the ANSI31 pattern, a column in it left empty, and a solid fill in the column.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hatch-by-clicking-inside-an-area\"\u003EHatch by clicking inside an area\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw a rectangle (\u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@2000,1200\u003C/code\u003E) and a circle inside it (\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1000,600\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E300\u003C/code\u003E). Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw\u003C/span\u003E group, or type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says which pattern is current, for example \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent hatch: ANSI31, scale 1, angle 0°\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EANSI31\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E20\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Pattern sizes are on paper, so a drawing plotted at 1:20 wants scale 20.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EPick internal point\u003C/code\u003E, click inside the rectangle, outside the circle. The hatch shows as a preview.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The rectangle is hatched; the circle inside stays empty, because it is an island.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick inside several areas before pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to make them one hatch. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last area.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-solid-fill\"\u003EA solid fill\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003ESOLID\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick inside the circle, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hatch-closed-shapes-you-pick\"\u003EHatch closed shapes you pick\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EInstead of clicking inside, type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Select objects) at \u003Ccode\u003EPick internal point\u003C/code\u003E and pick closed polylines, circles or closed ellipses, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Shapes inside others are left empty in turn (the outer one hatched, the next one in empty, and so on).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"patterns\"\u003EPatterns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E at the pattern question for the list. The standard ones are ANSI31 to ANSI38, LINE, NET, NET3, DASH, SQUARE, BRICK, DOTS and EARTH, and SOLID. In a drawing in inches (or with feet and inches as its length format) the inch sizes of the patterns are used, as in AutoCAD; otherwise the millimeter ones.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAngle\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E) turns the whole pattern: ANSI31 at 90° slopes the other way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-hatch\"\u003EChange a hatch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect it (click one of its lines). In \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPattern\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPattern scale\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPattern angle\u003C/span\u003E. Hatches from opened drawings show their pattern when it is one of the standard ones; others show as a light tone of their color.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No closed boundary round that point.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E There is a gap in the outline, or part of it is off screen: CadCraft looks only at what is on screen. Close the gap (zoom in on the corners), or zoom out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The pattern is too dense to draw at this scale.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The lines would be closer than the drawing can show. Use a larger scale: try the plot scale (20 for 1:20).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe pattern looks huge or tiny.\u003C/strong\u003E The scale is in drawing units per pattern unit; see step 4 above.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMoving the outline does not move the hatch.\u003C/strong\u003E A hatch is not tied to its boundary. Move both, or hatch again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A wall section filled with the ANSI31 pattern, a column in it left empty, and a solid fill in the column. Hatch by clicking inside an area Draw a rectangle (REC, 0,0, @2000,1200) and a circle inside it (C, 1000,600, 300). Click Extents. Click Hatch in the Draw group, or type H and press Enter. The history says which pattern is current, for example Current hatch: ANSI31, scale 1, angle 0°. Type P and press Enter, type ANSI31 and press Enter. Type SC and press Enter, type 20 and press Enter. Pattern sizes are on paper, so a drawing plotted at 1:20 wants scale 20. At Pick internal point, click inside the rectangle, outside the circle. The hatch shows as a preview. Press Enter. The rectangle is hatched; the circle inside stays empty, because it is an island. Click inside several areas before pressing Enter to make them one hatch. U takes back the last area. A solid fill Type H and press Enter. Type P, press Enter, type SOLID and press Enter. Click inside the circle, then press Enter. Hatch closed shapes you pick Instead of clicking inside, type S (Select objects) at Pick internal point and pick closed polylines, circles or closed ellipses, then press Enter. Shapes inside others are left empty in turn (the outer one hatched, the next one in empty, and so on). Patterns Type ? at the pattern question for the list. The standard ones are ANSI31 to ANSI38, LINE, NET, NET3, DASH, SQUARE, BRICK, DOTS and EARTH, and SOLID. In a drawing in inches (or with feet and inches as its length format) the inch sizes of the patterns are used, as in AutoCAD; otherwise the millimeter ones. Angle (A) turns the whole pattern: ANSI31 at 90° slopes the other way. Change a hatch Select it (click one of its lines). In Properties: Pattern, Pattern scale and Pattern angle. Hatches from opened drawings show their pattern when it is one of the standard ones; others show as a light tone of their color. Common mistakes \"No closed boundary round that point.\" There is a gap in the outline, or part of it is off screen: CadCraft looks only at what is on screen. Close the gap (zoom in on the corners), or zoom out. \"The pattern is too dense to draw at this scale.\" The lines would be closer than the drawing can show. Use a larger scale: try the plot scale (20 for 1:20). The pattern looks huge or tiny. The scale is in drawing units per pattern unit; see step 4 above. Moving the outline does not move the hatch. A hatch is not tied to its boundary. Move both, or hatch again."},{"slug":"dimensions","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Dimension a plan","summary":"Add linear, aligned, angular, radius and diameter dimensions, chain them with Continue and Baseline, and override the text.","section":"tasks","order":90,"keywords":["dimension","dim","dimlinear","dli","dimaligned","dimangular","dimradius","dimdiameter","dimcontinue","dimbaseline","chain","string","annotate"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-something-to-dimension","text":"Draw something to dimension","level":2},{"id":"linear-horizontal-or-vertical","text":"Linear: horizontal or vertical","level":2},{"id":"continue-a-string-of-dimensions","text":"Continue: a string of dimensions","level":2},{"id":"baseline-dimensions-stacked-from-one-line","text":"Baseline: dimensions stacked from one line","level":2},{"id":"aligned-true-length-of-a-sloping-line","text":"Aligned: true length of a sloping line","level":2},{"id":"radius-and-diameter","text":"Radius and diameter","level":2},{"id":"angular","text":"Angular","level":2},{"id":"change-the-text","text":"Change the text","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-follow-the-geometry","text":"Dimensions follow the geometry","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimensioned room: overall width and height, a string of dimensions along one wall, an angled wall, a curved corner and an angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBefore you start, read \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E if your drawing is large: dimension text is 2.5 drawing units tall by default, which is tiny in a drawing measured in millimeters. For the steps below, type \u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-something-to-dimension\"\u003EDraw something to dimension\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@6000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@1500&lt;60\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,2700\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@-6750,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The outline closes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to start a circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick inside the room, away from the walls, for the center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E600\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is a column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"linear-horizontal-or-vertical\"\u003ELinear: horizontal or vertical\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first extension line origin\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-left corner (Endpoint) and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second extension line origin\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-right corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair below the room. The dimension follows, horizontal.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place the dimension line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says \u003Ccode\u003EDimension text = 6000\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere you place the line decides the direction: below or above gives a horizontal dimension, left or right gives a vertical one. To force it, type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E (horizontal), \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E (vertical) or \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (rotated, then an angle) before you place it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the first prompt instead of picking a point, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment. CadCraft takes both ends for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"continue-a-string-of-dimensions\"\u003EContinue: a string of dimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EContinue dimension\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E) carries on from the last linear or aligned dimension you made, each new one starting where the last one ended.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the first origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E2000,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the second origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick below the room to place it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E4500,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A second dimension follows on from the first, on the same line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the lower-right corner and click. A third one completes the string.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHere the points are typed as absolute coordinates on purpose. An \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E point counts from the last point you gave, and the last point is where you placed the previous dimension line, not the end of the dimension. Absolute points, snaps or a bare distance with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E on are safer in a dimension string.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to take back the last dimension in the string without leaving the command.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"baseline-dimensions-stacked-from-one-line\"\u003EBaseline: dimensions stacked from one line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMBASELINE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAnnotate\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBaseline dimension\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E) measures every new dimension from the first origin of the last one, and stacks each one a step further out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the lower-left corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the upper-left corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair to the left of the room and click. A vertical dimension appears.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESnap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall (Endpoint) and click. A second vertical dimension appears, one step further out than the first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"aligned-true-length-of-a-sloping-line\"\u003EAligned: true length of a sloping line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to select an object.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the 60-degree wall.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove outward and click to place.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text reads 1500: the true length, measured along the wall.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"radius-and-diameter\"\u003ERadius and diameter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the column circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair. The leader points from the center toward the crosshair.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place the text.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text reads \u003Ccode\u003ER600\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E) does the same with a ⌀ sign and the diameter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"angular\"\u003EAngular\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe walls here are one polyline, so measure the corner by its vertex. (Between two separate lines, you can click the lines instead.)\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle vertex\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-right corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the lower-left corner and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E, snap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair inside the room, near the corner, and click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe text reads \u003Ccode\u003E120°\u003C/code\u003E, the inside angle of the corner. Placed on the other side of the corner, the dimension measures the angle on that side instead. Click an arc at the first prompt to dimension the angle it spans, or click two lines to dimension the angle between them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-the-text\"\u003EChange the text\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo write your own text, type \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before you place the dimension, then type the text. In your text, \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E stands for the measured value: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt; TYP.\u003C/code\u003E gives \u003Ccode\u003E6000 TYP.\u003C/code\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on an empty line to keep the measured value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change an existing dimension:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, type in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E. Leave it empty for the measured value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-follow-the-geometry\"\u003EDimensions follow the geometry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension remembers its points. Drag one of its grips, or move, rotate or scale it with the objects it measures, and it measures again. If you stretch the geometry without moving the dimension, the dimension does not follow: it keeps its own points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe text and arrows are microscopic or huge.\u003C/strong\u003E Set the overall scale: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The point you clicked for an angular dimension sits on one of the legs. Move it into the angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EContinue started from the wrong dimension.\u003C/strong\u003E It always continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made in this session. Make (or remake) the one to continue from first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe dimension came out vertical.\u003C/strong\u003E You placed it to the side. Type \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E before placing it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFeet and inches.\u003C/strong\u003E Dimensions show decimal numbers in drawing units. Architectural (feet-inch) display is not built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A dimensioned room: overall width and height, a string of dimensions along one wall, an angled wall, a curved corner and an angle. Before you start, read Set dimension sizes if your drawing is large: dimension text is 2.5 drawing units tall by default, which is tiny in a drawing measured in millimeters. For the steps below, type DIMSCALE, press Enter, type 50 and press Enter first. Draw something to dimension Type PL and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @6000,0 and press Enter. Type @1500\u003C60 and press Enter. Type @0,2700 and press Enter. Type @-6750,0 and press Enter. Type C and press Enter. The outline closes. Type C and press Enter to start a circle. Click inside the room, away from the walls, for the center. Type 600 and press Enter. This is a column. Click Extents. Linear: horizontal or vertical Type DLI and press Enter. At Specify first extension line origin, snap to the lower-left corner (Endpoint) and click. At Specify second extension line origin, snap to the lower-right corner and click. Move the crosshair below the room. The dimension follows, horizontal. Click to place the dimension line. The history says Dimension text = 6000. Where you place the line decides the direction: below or above gives a horizontal dimension, left or right gives a vertical one. To force it, type H (horizontal), V (vertical) or R (rotated, then an angle) before you place it. Press Enter at the first prompt instead of picking a point, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment. CadCraft takes both ends for you. Continue: a string of dimensions DIMCONTINUE (Annotate › More › Continue dimension, or DCO) carries on from the last linear or aligned dimension you made, each new one starting where the last one ended. Type DLI and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter for the first origin. Type 2000,0 and press Enter for the second origin. Click below the room to place it. Type DCO and press Enter. Type 4500,0 and press Enter. A second dimension follows on from the first, on the same line. Snap to the lower-right corner and click. A third one completes the string. Press Enter to end the command. Here the points are typed as absolute coordinates on purpose. An @ point counts from the last point you gave, and the last point is where you placed the previous dimension line, not the end of the dimension. Absolute points, snaps or a bare distance with Ortho on are safer in a dimension string. Type U and press Enter to take back the last dimension in the string without leaving the command. Baseline: dimensions stacked from one line DIMBASELINE (Annotate › More › Baseline dimension, or DBA) measures every new dimension from the first origin of the last one, and stacks each one a step further out. Type DLI and press Enter. Snap to the lower-left corner and click. Snap to the upper-left corner and click. Move the crosshair to the left of the room and click. A vertical dimension appears. Type DBA and press Enter. Snap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall (Endpoint) and click. A second vertical dimension appears, one step further out than the first. Press Enter to end. Aligned: true length of a sloping line Type DAL and press Enter. Press Enter to select an object. Click the 60-degree wall. Move outward and click to place. The text reads 1500: the true length, measured along the wall. Radius and diameter Type DRA and press Enter. Click the column circle. Move the crosshair. The leader points from the center toward the crosshair. Click to place the text. The text reads R600. DIMDIAMETER (DDI) does the same with a ⌀ sign and the diameter. Angular The walls here are one polyline, so measure the corner by its vertex. (Between two separate lines, you can click the lines instead.) Type DAN and press Enter. At Select arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex, press Enter. At Specify angle vertex, snap to the lower-right corner and click. At Specify first angle endpoint, snap to the lower-left corner and click. At Specify second angle endpoint, snap to the upper end of the 60-degree wall and click. Move the crosshair inside the room, near the corner, and click. The text reads 120°, the inside angle of the corner. Placed on the other side of the corner, the dimension measures the angle on that side instead. Click an arc at the first prompt to dimension the angle it spans, or click two lines to dimension the angle between them. Change the text To write your own text, type T and press Enter before you place the dimension, then type the text. In your text, \u003C\u003E stands for the measured value: \u003C\u003E TYP. gives 6000 TYP.. Press Enter on an empty line to keep the measured value. To change an existing dimension: Click it. In the right panel, type in Text. Leave it empty for the measured value. Press Enter. Dimensions follow the geometry A dimension remembers its points. Drag one of its grips, or move, rotate or scale it with the objects it measures, and it measures again. If you stretch the geometry without moving the dimension, the dimension does not follow: it keeps its own points. Common mistakes The text and arrows are microscopic or huge. Set the overall scale: see Set dimension sizes. \"Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.\" The point you clicked for an angular dimension sits on one of the legs. Move it into the angle. Continue started from the wrong dimension. It always continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made in this session. Make (or remake) the one to continue from first. The dimension came out vertical. You placed it to the side. Type H before placing it. Feet and inches. Dimensions show decimal numbers in drawing units. Architectural (feet-inch) display is not built yet."},{"slug":"dimension-settings","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Set dimension sizes, decimals and scale","summary":"Choose text height, arrow size, decimals and overall scale for new dimensions, change existing ones, and understand how dimension styles work in CadCraft.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["dimension style","dimstyle","dimscale","dimtxt","dimasz","dimdec","dimgap","dimexe","dimexo","overall scale","decimals","arrow size","annotation scale"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"how-dimension-sizes-work","text":"How dimension sizes work","level":2},{"id":"set-the-sizes-for-new-dimensions","text":"Set the sizes for new dimensions","level":2},{"id":"in-the-current-settings-panel","text":"In the Current settings panel","level":3},{"id":"at-the-command-line","text":"At the command line","level":3},{"id":"feet-and-inches-in-dimensions","text":"Feet and inches in dimensions","level":3},{"id":"change-dimensions-you-already-made","text":"Change dimensions you already made","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-from-opened-drawings","text":"Dimensions from opened drawings","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow big dimension text and arrows are, how to set them for a drawing plotted at 1:50 or 1:100, and how to change dimensions you already made.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-dimension-sizes-work\"\u003EHow dimension sizes work\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery dimension carries its own set of sizes: text height, arrow size, decimals, the gap round the text, how far extension lines run past the dimension line and how far they start from the object. The defaults are close to the common ISO style:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESetting\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDefault\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText height\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArrow size\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMASZ\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDecimals\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2 (trailing zeros dropped)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGap round the text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.625\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMGAP\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtension past the dimension line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1.25\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset from the object\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.625\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXO\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOverall scale\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery size is multiplied by the \u003Cstrong\u003Eoverall scale\u003C/strong\u003E. Set the sizes as you want them on paper (2.5 mm text), and set the overall scale to the plot scale (50 for 1:50). The text is then 2.5 × 50 = 125 drawing units tall, which plots at 2.5 mm.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are no named dimension styles to create or switch between yet. Instead, CadCraft has one set of settings for new dimensions, and each dimension keeps the settings it was made with. Dimensions in opened drawings keep the sizes from their style in the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-sizes-for-new-dimensions\"\u003ESet the sizes for new dimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEither way below changes only dimensions you make afterwards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"in-the-current-settings-panel\"\u003EIn the Current settings panel\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected. On the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab the right panel reads \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew dimensions\u003C/span\u003E, click in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow size\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E the same way if you want other values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar confirms each one, for example &quot;Overall scale for new dimensions: 50&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"at-the-command-line\"\u003EAt the command line\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter new value for DIMSCALE &lt;1&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history confirms &quot;DIMSCALE = 50. New dimensions use it; select existing ones to change theirs.&quot; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMASZ\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMGAP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMEXO\u003C/code\u003E work the same way. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the prompt to keep the current value.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"feet-and-inches-in-dimensions\"\u003EFeet and inches in dimensions\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew dimensions use the drawing&#039;s length format: set it with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E), and the next dimension reads \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMLUNIT\u003C/code\u003E sets only the dimensions&#039; format (2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional). For a dimension you already made, select it and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFormat\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E; with a fractional format, its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFraction\u003C/span\u003E box takes the power of two (4 = 1/16).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-dimensions-you-already-made\"\u003EChange dimensions you already made\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one dimension.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe right panel shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKind\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasured\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText height\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArrow size\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a new value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dimension redraws at once. For angular dimensions, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimals (°)\u003C/span\u003E sets the decimals of the angle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith several dimensions selected, the panel shows only \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EColour\u003C/span\u003E. Change the sizes one dimension at a time, or erase and redraw them with the new settings.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-from-opened-drawings\"\u003EDimensions from opened drawings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA dimension from an opened DWG or DXF is shown exactly as the other program drew it. Its panel says &quot;Shown as the program that made it drew it. Change it and CadCraft draws it again in its own style.&quot; As soon as you change any value, move a grip, or move the dimension, CadCraft redraws it with its own arrowheads and font, using the sizes it read from the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome dimensions can only be read as drawn, not as editable dimensions; the panel then shows &quot;Drawn from&quot; and a block name, and the &quot;About this file&quot; box counts them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChanging DIMSCALE did not change anything on screen.\u003C/strong\u003E The setting is for new dimensions. Select existing ones and change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOverall scale\u003C/span\u003E in the panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDecimals show fewer digits than set.\u003C/strong\u003E Trailing zeros are dropped: 2 decimals show \u003Ccode\u003E1250\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E1250.5\u003C/code\u003E, not \u003Ccode\u003E1250.00\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText is the right height on paper in a viewport but wrong in a model plot.\u003C/strong\u003E The overall scale must match the scale you plot at. Plotting at Fit to paper gives an odd scale; choose a standard scale in the plot dialog.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EUnits such as mm or ft in the text.\u003C/strong\u003E Not added automatically. Type them in an override: \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt; mm\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn How big dimension text and arrows are, how to set them for a drawing plotted at 1:50 or 1:100, and how to change dimensions you already made. How dimension sizes work Every dimension carries its own set of sizes: text height, arrow size, decimals, the gap round the text, how far extension lines run past the dimension line and how far they start from the object. The defaults are close to the common ISO style: Setting Default Command Text height 2.5 DIMTXT Arrow size 2.5 DIMASZ Decimals 2 (trailing zeros dropped) DIMDEC Gap round the text 0.625 DIMGAP Extension past the dimension line 1.25 DIMEXE Offset from the object 0.625 DIMEXO Overall scale 1 DIMSCALE Every size is multiplied by the overall scale. Set the sizes as you want them on paper (2.5 mm text), and set the overall scale to the plot scale (50 for 1:50). The text is then 2.5 × 50 = 125 drawing units tall, which plots at 2.5 mm. There are no named dimension styles to create or switch between yet. Instead, CadCraft has one set of settings for new dimensions, and each dimension keeps the settings it was made with. Dimensions in opened drawings keep the sizes from their style in the file. Set the sizes for new dimensions Either way below changes only dimensions you make afterwards. In the Current settings panel Press Esc so nothing is selected. On the Model tab the right panel reads Current settings. Under New dimensions, click in Overall scale, type 50 and press Enter. Set Text height, Arrow size and Decimals the same way if you want other values. The status bar confirms each one, for example \"Overall scale for new dimensions: 50\". At the command line Type DIMSCALE and press Enter. At Enter new value for DIMSCALE \u003C1\u003E, type 50 and press Enter. The history confirms \"DIMSCALE = 50. New dimensions use it; select existing ones to change theirs.\" DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE and DIMEXO work the same way. Press Enter at the prompt to keep the current value. Feet and inches in dimensions New dimensions use the drawing's length format: set it with Length format in Current settings or UNITS (see Feet and inches), and the next dimension reads 12'-6 1/2\". DIMLUNIT sets only the dimensions' format (2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional). For a dimension you already made, select it and choose Format in Properties; with a fractional format, its Fraction box takes the power of two (4 = 1/16). Change dimensions you already made Click one dimension. The right panel shows Kind, Measured, Text, Text height, Arrow size, Decimals and Overall scale. Type a new value and press Enter. The dimension redraws at once. For angular dimensions, Decimals (°) sets the decimals of the angle. With several dimensions selected, the panel shows only Layer and Colour. Change the sizes one dimension at a time, or erase and redraw them with the new settings. Dimensions from opened drawings A dimension from an opened DWG or DXF is shown exactly as the other program drew it. Its panel says \"Shown as the program that made it drew it. Change it and CadCraft draws it again in its own style.\" As soon as you change any value, move a grip, or move the dimension, CadCraft redraws it with its own arrowheads and font, using the sizes it read from the file. Some dimensions can only be read as drawn, not as editable dimensions; the panel then shows \"Drawn from\" and a block name, and the \"About this file\" box counts them. Common mistakes Changing DIMSCALE did not change anything on screen. The setting is for new dimensions. Select existing ones and change Overall scale in the panel. Decimals show fewer digits than set. Trailing zeros are dropped: 2 decimals show 1250 and 1250.5, not 1250.00. Text is the right height on paper in a viewport but wrong in a model plot. The overall scale must match the scale you plot at. Plotting at Fit to paper gives an odd scale; choose a standard scale in the plot dialog. Units such as mm or ft in the text. Not added automatically. Type them in an override: \u003C\u003E mm."},{"slug":"blocks","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Make and insert blocks","summary":"Turn a drawn door into a block, place it again at other positions, scales and angles, and tidy up unused blocks.","section":"tasks","order":110,"keywords":["block","bmake","insert","ddinsert","symbol","purge","explode","redefine","base point","reference","count","bcount","schedule","csv","attributes","data extraction"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-the-door","text":"Draw the door","level":2},{"id":"make-the-block","text":"Make the block","level":2},{"id":"insert-the-block","text":"Insert the block","level":2},{"id":"insert-turned-or-scaled","text":"Insert turned or scaled","level":2},{"id":"change-a-placed-block","text":"Change a placed block","level":2},{"id":"redefine-a-block","text":"Redefine a block","level":2},{"id":"count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule","text":"Count blocks and export a schedule","level":2},{"id":"explode-and-purge","text":"Explode and purge","level":2},{"id":"block-names","text":"Block names","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA door symbol made into a block called \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E, then placed three times: as drawn, turned 90 degrees, and mirrored.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA block is a named group of objects. You draw it once; every place you use it is a \u003Cstrong\u003Eblock reference\u003C/strong\u003E that points back to the one definition. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E section in the left panel lists the blocks in the drawing and how often each is used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-the-door\"\u003EDraw the door\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E@0,900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. This is the door leaf.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to give the center first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the center.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E900,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the start point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E0,900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the end point. This is the swing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EArcs go counterclockwise from the start point to the end point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-block\"\u003EMake the block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMake\u003C/span\u003E in the Blocks group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion base point\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. This is the hinge: the point that lands where you place the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, drag a window round the leaf and the arc.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says \u003Ccode\u003EBlock &quot;DOOR-900&quot; made from 2 objects, and they were replaced by one reference to it.\u003C/code\u003E The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E panel lists DOOR-900 with \u003Ccode\u003E1×\u003C/code\u003E beside it: one reference. Click the door: it now selects as one object, a &quot;Block reference&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou can select the objects first and then type \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E. CadCraft still asks for the name and base point, then uses your selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-the-block\"\u003EInsert the block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E next to DOOR-900 in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name &lt;DOOR-900&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. (The panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E button fills in the name for you.)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe door follows the crosshair. Click where the hinge should go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA second door appears.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-turned-or-scaled\"\u003EInsert turned or scaled\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to accept the name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point [Scale/Rotate]\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The preview turns.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to place it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E instead to set a scale factor; it applies to both directions. The scale and rotation are remembered for the next insert. To mirror a door, insert it and then use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E on the reference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-placed-block\"\u003EChange a placed block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a block reference. In the right panel you can change its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale X\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale Y\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERotation\u003C/span\u003E. A negative \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale X\u003C/span\u003E flips it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"redefine-a-block\"\u003ERedefine a block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo change every door at once, make a block with the same name:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the new version of the door.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and type \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EBlock &quot;DOOR-900&quot; already exists. Redefine it?\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGive the base point and select the new objects, as before.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery reference to DOOR-900 now shows the new version.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr edit the block itself: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit block\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E) opens it on its own, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave block\u003C/span\u003E puts it back. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes#change-a-block-the-block-editor\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\" data-hash=\"change-a-block-the-block-editor\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule\"\u003ECount blocks and export a schedule\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOUNT\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount blocks\u003C/span\u003E), then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eclick one door: every door in this space is selected, and the history says how many there are here and in the whole drawing;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eor press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the history lists every block with its count, most first;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eor type \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E for a CSV of the counts (in the model, on sheets, in total) or \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E for one row per reference with its layer, position, rotation and every attribute value — a schedule you can open in a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnnamed blocks (dimensions, tables) and references inside other blocks are not counted. Cells that start like a formula (\u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E) get a leading \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E so a spreadsheet shows them as text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"explode-and-purge\"\u003EExplode and purge\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E) turns a selected block reference back into separate objects. The definition stays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPURGE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPU\u003C/code\u003E) removes block definitions that nothing uses any more. The history says how many it removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"block-names\"\u003EBlock names\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA name can be up to 255 characters and can contain spaces, but it cannot start with an asterisk, and cannot contain \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&gt;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E\\\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E:\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E*\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E|\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E or a backtick. When you save DXF R12, names are written in capitals, other characters than letters, digits, \u003Ccode\u003E$\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, and names are cut to 31 characters; the history says how many changed. Short names in capitals, digits and hyphens, like \u003Ccode\u003EDOOR-900\u003C/code\u003E, survive unchanged. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them, upper and lower case; only characters DWG does not allow change (a \u003Ccode\u003E|\u003C/code\u003E becomes \u003Ccode\u003E$0$\u003C/code\u003E, others become \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe block landed far from the crosshair.\u003C/strong\u003E The base point was not on the objects. Make the block again with the base point on a corner or a hinge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;No objects selected; nothing made.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before selecting anything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library (or make one with BLOCK).&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Type \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E to bring a block in from another drawing, or \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E for the library. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\"\u003EInsert from a file or the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;A viewport cannot go in a block.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Leave viewport frames out of the selection.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A door symbol made into a block called DOOR-900, then placed three times: as drawn, turned 90 degrees, and mirrored. A block is a named group of objects. You draw it once; every place you use it is a block reference that points back to the one definition. The Blocks section in the left panel lists the blocks in the drawing and how often each is used. Draw the door Type L and press Enter. Type 0,0 and press Enter. Type @0,900 and press Enter, then press Enter again. This is the door leaf. Type A and press Enter. Type C and press Enter to give the center first. Type 0,0 and press Enter for the center. Type 900,0 and press Enter for the start point. Type 0,900 and press Enter for the end point. This is the swing. Click Extents. Arcs go counterclockwise from the start point to the end point. Make the block Type B and press Enter, or click Make in the Blocks group. At Enter block name, type DOOR-900 and press Enter. At Specify insertion base point, type 0,0 and press Enter. This is the hinge: the point that lands where you place the block. At Select objects, drag a window round the leaf and the arc. Press Enter. The history says Block \"DOOR-900\" made from 2 objects, and they were replaced by one reference to it. The Blocks panel lists DOOR-900 with 1× beside it: one reference. Click the door: it now selects as one object, a \"Block reference\". You can select the objects first and then type B. CadCraft still asks for the name and base point, then uses your selection. Insert the block Type I and press Enter, or click Insert next to DOOR-900 in the Blocks panel. At Enter block name \u003CDOOR-900\u003E, press Enter. (The panel's Insert button fills in the name for you.) The door follows the crosshair. Click where the hinge should go. A second door appears. Insert turned or scaled Type I and press Enter, then press Enter to accept the name. At Specify insertion point [Scale/Rotate], type R and press Enter. Type 90 and press Enter. The preview turns. Click to place it. Type S instead to set a scale factor; it applies to both directions. The scale and rotation are remembered for the next insert. To mirror a door, insert it and then use MIRROR on the reference. Change a placed block Select a block reference. In the right panel you can change its X, Y, Scale X, Scale Y and Rotation. A negative Scale X flips it. Redefine a block To change every door at once, make a block with the same name: Draw the new version of the door. Type B and press Enter, and type DOOR-900. At Block \"DOOR-900\" already exists. Redefine it?, type Y and press Enter. Give the base point and select the new objects, as before. Every reference to DOOR-900 now shows the new version. Or edit the block itself: Blocks › More › Edit block (BEDIT) opens it on its own, and Save block puts it back. See Block attributes. Count blocks and export a schedule Type COUNT (or Blocks › More › Count blocks), then: click one door: every door in this space is selected, and the history says how many there are here and in the whole drawing; or press Enter: the history lists every block with its count, most first; or type E, then C for a CSV of the counts (in the model, on sheets, in total) or A for one row per reference with its layer, position, rotation and every attribute value — a schedule you can open in a spreadsheet. Unnamed blocks (dimensions, tables) and references inside other blocks are not counted. Cells that start like a formula (=, +, -, @) get a leading ' so a spreadsheet shows them as text. Explode and purge EXPLODE (X) turns a selected block reference back into separate objects. The definition stays. PURGE (PU) removes block definitions that nothing uses any more. The history says how many it removed. Block names A name can be up to 255 characters and can contain spaces, but it cannot start with an asterisk, and cannot contain \u003C \u003E / \\ \" : ; ? * | = or a backtick. When you save DXF R12, names are written in capitals, other characters than letters, digits, $, _ and - become _, and names are cut to 31 characters; the history says how many changed. Short names in capitals, digits and hyphens, like DOOR-900, survive unchanged. A saved DWG keeps names as you typed them, upper and lower case; only characters DWG does not allow change (a | becomes $0$, others become _). Common mistakes The block landed far from the crosshair. The base point was not on the objects. Make the block again with the base point on a corner or a hinge. \"No objects selected; nothing made.\" You pressed Enter before selecting anything. \"This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library (or make one with BLOCK).\" Type F to bring a block in from another drawing, or L for the library. See Insert from a file or the library. \"A viewport cannot go in a block.\" Leave viewport frames out of the selection."},{"slug":"attributes","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Block attributes","summary":"See and change the values a block reference carries, such as an item number or a maker, and keep them when you save a copy.","section":"tasks","order":115,"keywords":["attdef","define attribute","bedit","block editor","bclose","attribute","attributes","attdef","attrib","tag","item number","value","invisible","constant","block","properties","list","equipment tag"],"headings":[{"id":"what-attributes-are","text":"What attributes are","level":2},{"id":"where-attributes-come-from","text":"Where attributes come from","level":2},{"id":"define-attributes-for-your-own-blocks","text":"Define attributes for your own blocks","level":2},{"id":"change-a-block-the-block-editor","text":"Change a block: the block editor","level":2},{"id":"fill-them-in-when-you-place-a-block","text":"Fill them in when you place a block","level":2},{"id":"see-and-change-the-values","text":"See and change the values","level":2},{"id":"list-them","text":"List them","level":2},{"id":"moving-turning-and-exploding","text":"Moving, turning and exploding","level":2},{"id":"saving","text":"Saving","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-attributes-are\"\u003EWhat attributes are\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn attribute is a named value that travels with a block reference: an equipment tag&#039;s item number, a room tag&#039;s name, a door&#039;s fire rating. The block&#039;s definition says which attributes it has (each has a \u003Cstrong\u003Etag\u003C/strong\u003E, a \u003Cstrong\u003Eprompt\u003C/strong\u003E, a \u003Cstrong\u003Edefault\u003C/strong\u003E and a place, size and angle for its text). Every reference to the block carries its own values.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome attributes are \u003Cstrong\u003Einvisible\u003C/strong\u003E: they hold data, such as a maker or a model number, but never draw. Some are \u003Cstrong\u003Econstant\u003C/strong\u003E: the same for every reference, fixed by the block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-attributes-come-from\"\u003EWhere attributes come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks in DWG and DXF files you open keep their attributes, with each reference&#039;s values.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks you bring in with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFile\u003C/span\u003E option keep theirs. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\"\u003EInsert from a file or the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELibrary items come in as blocks with attributes: a visible item number and the item&#039;s data, invisible.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBlocks you make yourself: define attributes with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E first (below), then select them with the other objects when you make the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"define-attributes-for-your-own-blocks\"\u003EDefine attributes for your own blocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw the block&#039;s lines, for example a room tag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefine attribute\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EATT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe history shows the modes (\u003Ccode\u003EInvisible=No Constant=No Preset=No\u003C/code\u003E). Type \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E to switch one, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E when they are right. Invisible values are kept but not drawn; a constant one has the same value in every reference; a preset one is not asked for when you insert the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the tag (for example \u003Ccode\u003EROOM_NO\u003C/code\u003E: upper case, no spaces), the prompt (\u003Ccode\u003ERoom number\u003C/code\u003E) and a default value (\u003Ccode\u003E101\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the value goes, and give the height and the rotation. The tag shows on the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake the block with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E, selecting the lines and the attribute definitions together. The reference that replaces them gets the default values; each new \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E asks for them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA selected attribute definition shows \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETag\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrompt\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E and the modes in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"change-a-block-the-block-editor\"\u003EChange a block: the block editor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit block\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBEDIT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBE\u003C/code\u003E) opens a block on its own: type its name (or select a reference first). A bar at the top says which block you are editing. Draw, erase and change its objects with the usual commands, and add or change attribute definitions. Then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave block\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E): every reference shows the change, and one \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it all. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EBCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E) leaves the block as it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESave or discard in the block editor before you save the drawing: a file saved while the editor is open has the block as it was before.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-them-in-when-you-place-a-block\"\u003EFill them in when you place a block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type the block name and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft asks for each value, for example \u003Ccode\u003EEnter Item number (ITEM) &lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Type the value and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the default shown in angle brackets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EValues the block marks as preset, and constant ones, are not asked for. They take their default.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-and-change-the-values\"\u003ESee and change the values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a block reference.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the right panel, under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAttributes\u003C/span\u003E, each tag has its value. Invisible ones say \u003Ccode\u003Ehidden\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a value, type the new one and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing shows the new value at once. Each change is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe values of a library item&#039;s own fields (its id, version, license and attribution) are shown but not editable, so that checking for updates keeps working. Its item number and quantity are yours to change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"list-them\"\u003EList them\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect references and type \u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E (for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E). The history shows each attribute as \u003Ccode\u003ETAG = value\u003C/code\u003E, with \u003Ccode\u003E(invisible)\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E(constant)\u003C/code\u003E where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-turning-and-exploding\"\u003EMoving, turning and exploding\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E keep the values; the text moves with the block and stays readable.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E turns the visible attributes into plain text with their values. The invisible ones are dropped.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"saving\"\u003ESaving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA saved DWG and a saved DXF both keep every attribute: each block&#039;s definitions (invisible, constant and preset ones too) and each reference&#039;s values. Other CAD programs read them as ordinary block attributes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a DXF R12, a value longer than 255 characters is cut to 255, and the history says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA value does not show on the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E It is invisible, or it is empty. Invisible values still show in the right panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe value I typed went to the command line.\u003C/strong\u003E Click into the value box in the right panel first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What attributes are An attribute is a named value that travels with a block reference: an equipment tag's item number, a room tag's name, a door's fire rating. The block's definition says which attributes it has (each has a tag, a prompt, a default and a place, size and angle for its text). Every reference to the block carries its own values. Some attributes are invisible: they hold data, such as a maker or a model number, but never draw. Some are constant: the same for every reference, fixed by the block. Where attributes come from Blocks in DWG and DXF files you open keep their attributes, with each reference's values. Blocks you bring in with INSERT and the File option keep theirs. See Insert from a file or the library. Library items come in as blocks with attributes: a visible item number and the item's data, invisible. Blocks you make yourself: define attributes with ATTDEF first (below), then select them with the other objects when you make the block. Define attributes for your own blocks Draw the block's lines, for example a room tag. Choose Blocks › More › Define attribute, or type ATT and press Enter. The history shows the modes (Invisible=No Constant=No Preset=No). Type I, C or P to switch one, and press Enter when they are right. Invisible values are kept but not drawn; a constant one has the same value in every reference; a preset one is not asked for when you insert the block. Type the tag (for example ROOM_NO: upper case, no spaces), the prompt (Room number) and a default value (101). Click where the value goes, and give the height and the rotation. The tag shows on the drawing. Make the block with BLOCK, selecting the lines and the attribute definitions together. The reference that replaces them gets the default values; each new INSERT asks for them. A selected attribute definition shows Tag, Prompt, Default and the modes in Properties. Change a block: the block editor Blocks › More › Edit block (BEDIT, BE) opens a block on its own: type its name (or select a reference first). A bar at the top says which block you are editing. Draw, erase and change its objects with the usual commands, and add or change attribute definitions. Then click Save block (or type BCLOSE and S): every reference shows the change, and one Ctrl+Z undoes it all. Discard changes (BCLOSE, D) leaves the block as it was. Save or discard in the block editor before you save the drawing: a file saved while the editor is open has the block as it was before. Fill them in when you place a block Type I and press Enter, type the block name and press Enter. Click where it goes. CadCraft asks for each value, for example Enter Item number (ITEM) \u003C\u003E. Type the value and press Enter, or press Enter to keep the default shown in angle brackets. Values the block marks as preset, and constant ones, are not asked for. They take their default. See and change the values Click a block reference. In the right panel, under Attributes, each tag has its value. Invisible ones say hidden. Click a value, type the new one and press Enter. The drawing shows the new value at once. Each change is one step: Ctrl+Z undoes it. The values of a library item's own fields (its id, version, license and attribution) are shown but not editable, so that checking for updates keeps working. Its item number and quantity are yours to change. List them Select references and type LI (for LIST). The history shows each attribute as TAG = value, with (invisible) or (constant) where that applies. Moving, turning and exploding MOVE, ROTATE, SCALE and MIRROR keep the values; the text moves with the block and stays readable. EXPLODE turns the visible attributes into plain text with their values. The invisible ones are dropped. Saving A saved DWG and a saved DXF both keep every attribute: each block's definitions (invisible, constant and preset ones too) and each reference's values. Other CAD programs read them as ordinary block attributes. In a DXF R12, a value longer than 255 characters is cut to 255, and the history says so. Common mistakes A value does not show on the drawing. It is invisible, or it is empty. Invisible values still show in the right panel. The value I typed went to the command line. Click into the value box in the right panel first."},{"slug":"insert-from-file-and-library","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Insert from a file or the library","summary":"Bring a block, or a whole drawing, in from another DWG or DXF, and place items from LibraryCraft, a file or another library, with their credits and updates.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["insert","file","library","librarycraft","item","equipment","block from another drawing","wblock","design center","credits","attribution","license","update","feed","item.json"],"headings":[{"id":"insert-a-block-from-another-drawing","text":"Insert a block from another drawing","level":2},{"id":"when-a-name-is-already-used","text":"When a name is already used","level":3},{"id":"open-the-library","text":"Open the library","level":2},{"id":"place-an-item","text":"Place an item","level":2},{"id":"check-for-updates","text":"Check for updates","level":2},{"id":"credit-what-you-used","text":"Credit what you used","level":2},{"id":"sign-in-to-the-library","text":"Sign in to the library","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"insert-a-block-from-another-drawing\"\u003EInsert a block from another drawing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E in the Blocks group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name or [File/Library]\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the DWG or DXF on this computer. It is read here; it is not opened, and nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert from\u003C/span\u003E dialog, pick a block from the list (type in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFilter blocks\u003C/span\u003E to find it), or keep \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EThe whole drawing\u003C/span\u003E. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInsert\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where it goes, and fill in any attributes it asks for.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole drawing comes in as one block named after the file. Blocks inside the block you picked come too. The layers they use are added to this drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the other drawing is in different units (inches and millimeters, say), CadCraft scales the reference to match and says so in the history.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"when-a-name-is-already-used\"\u003EWhen a name is already used\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf this drawing already has a block with the same name, CadCraft asks:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep existing\u003C/span\u003E uses this drawing&#039;s block of that name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename\u003C/span\u003E brings the new one in as \u003Ccode\u003EName (2)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe whole insert is one step: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the reference and the copied definitions away again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-library\"\u003EOpen the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary…\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E panel, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary\u003C/span\u003E in the Blocks group, or type \u003Ccode\u003ELIB\u003C/code\u003E. At the \u003Ccode\u003EINSERT\u003C/code\u003E prompt, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E opens it too.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe library has three sources, as tabs:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibraryCraft\u003C/span\u003E: the CadCraft library. Search by name, maker or model, and filter by category.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFiles…\u003C/span\u003E: an item file (\u003Ccode\u003Eitem.json\u003C/code\u003E) from any library, or a drawing, spreadsheet or 3D model to make items from: a DWG or DXF (each block becomes an item), a CSV with name and size columns, or a glTF or OBJ model. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOther feeds\u003C/span\u003E: other libraries that answer the same way LibraryCraft does. Add their address once; this browser remembers it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach card shows a preview, the name, the maker and model, the license and the version. A withdrawn item is marked \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWithdrawn\u003C/span\u003E with the reason; CadCraft asks before you place one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-an-item\"\u003EPlace an item\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E on a card.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the item goes. Type \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E first to turn it, or \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E to scale it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAt \u003Ccode\u003EEnter Item number (CC-ITEM)\u003C/code\u003E, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the next free number, or type your own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe item becomes a block named \u003Ccode\u003ELIB_&lt;name&gt;_v&lt;version&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, drawn in this drawing&#039;s units. If the drawing has no units, the item goes in at millimeters and the history says so; set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIts data rides along as invisible attributes: the item&#039;s id and version, license and attribution, maker, model, category and sizes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-for-updates\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the library.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck for updates\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach library item in the drawing is listed with its item numbers and whether it is up to date, has a newer version (and what changed), or was withdrawn. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate\u003C/span\u003E on a row, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUpdate all\u003C/span\u003E. Every reference moves to the new version&#039;s block and keeps its item number, quantity, place, angle and layer. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes an update.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"credit-what-you-used\"\u003ECredit what you used\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELibrary credits\u003C/span\u003E. Each item is listed with its attribution line and license.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy attribution\u003C/span\u003E copies one item&#039;s line; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy all\u003C/span\u003E copies every line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload CSV\u003C/span\u003E saves the list with the item numbers, makers, models, licenses and attributions. It opens in any spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELicenses such as CC BY ask you to credit the author where you use the work. Put the lines on the drawing, in the specification or in the project notes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sign-in-to-the-library\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESign in to the library\u003C/span\u003E. A small window opens on the site; once you are signed in there, CadCraft can show your team&#039;s items and register what you place, so you hear when an item you used changes. The sign-in lasts for this browser tab. Revoke it any time from your account&#039;s security page.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Making items from IFC types is in BimCraft.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E IFC and family files are read in the BimCraft app. Make the item there or on LibraryCraft, then use its \u003Ccode\u003Eitem.json\u003C/code\u003E here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;The library could not be reached.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E You are offline, or the library is down. Items already in the drawing are not affected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAn item is far too big or too small.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing&#039;s units are wrong or not set. Undo, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E, and place it again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Insert a block from another drawing Type I and press Enter, or click Insert in the Blocks group. At Enter block name or [File/Library], type F and press Enter. Choose the DWG or DXF on this computer. It is read here; it is not opened, and nothing is uploaded. In the Insert from dialog, pick a block from the list (type in Filter blocks to find it), or keep The whole drawing. Click Insert. Click where it goes, and fill in any attributes it asks for. The whole drawing comes in as one block named after the file. Blocks inside the block you picked come too. The layers they use are added to this drawing. If the other drawing is in different units (inches and millimeters, say), CadCraft scales the reference to match and says so in the history. When a name is already used If this drawing already has a block with the same name, CadCraft asks: Keep existing uses this drawing's block of that name. Rename brings the new one in as Name (2). The whole insert is one step: Ctrl+Z takes the reference and the copied definitions away again. Open the library Click Library… in the Blocks panel, or Library in the Blocks group, or type LIB. At the INSERT prompt, L opens it too. The library has three sources, as tabs: LibraryCraft: the CadCraft library. Search by name, maker or model, and filter by category. Files…: an item file (item.json) from any library, or a drawing, spreadsheet or 3D model to make items from: a DWG or DXF (each block becomes an item), a CSV with name and size columns, or a glTF or OBJ model. Nothing is uploaded. Other feeds: other libraries that answer the same way LibraryCraft does. Add their address once; this browser remembers it. Each card shows a preview, the name, the maker and model, the license and the version. A withdrawn item is marked Withdrawn with the reason; CadCraft asks before you place one. Place an item Click Place on a card. Click where the item goes. Type R first to turn it, or S to scale it. At Enter Item number (CC-ITEM), press Enter for the next free number, or type your own. The item becomes a block named LIB_\u003Cname\u003E_v\u003Cversion\u003E, drawn in this drawing's units. If the drawing has no units, the item goes in at millimeters and the history says so; set Units in the right panel first. Its data rides along as invisible attributes: the item's id and version, license and attribution, maker, model, category and sizes. See Block attributes. Check for updates Open the library. Click Check for updates. Each library item in the drawing is listed with its item numbers and whether it is up to date, has a newer version (and what changed), or was withdrawn. Click Update on a row, or Update all. Every reference moves to the new version's block and keeps its item number, quantity, place, angle and layer. Ctrl+Z undoes an update. Credit what you used Click Library credits. Each item is listed with its attribution line and license. Copy attribution copies one item's line; Copy all copies every line. Download CSV saves the list with the item numbers, makers, models, licenses and attributions. It opens in any spreadsheet. Licenses such as CC BY ask you to credit the author where you use the work. Put the lines on the drawing, in the specification or in the project notes. Sign in to the library Click Sign in to the library. A small window opens on the site; once you are signed in there, CadCraft can show your team's items and register what you place, so you hear when an item you used changes. The sign-in lasts for this browser tab. Revoke it any time from your account's security page. Common mistakes \"Making items from IFC types is in BimCraft.\" IFC and family files are read in the BimCraft app. Make the item there or on LibraryCraft, then use its item.json here. \"The library could not be reached.\" You are offline, or the library is down. Items already in the drawing are not affected. An item is far too big or too small. The drawing's units are wrong or not set. Undo, set Units, and place it again."},{"slug":"sheets-and-viewports","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Set up a sheet with a title block and viewports","summary":"Make a sheet (layout) with a border and title block, set the viewport scale, add a second viewport for a detail, and fill in the title block.","section":"tasks","order":120,"keywords":["layout","sheet","paper space","viewport","mview","vpview","title block","scale","paper size","border","vports","plot scale"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"model-and-sheets","text":"Model and sheets","level":2},{"id":"before-you-start","text":"Before you start","level":2},{"id":"make-the-sheet","text":"Make the sheet","level":2},{"id":"choose-the-paper","text":"Choose the paper","level":2},{"id":"set-the-viewport-scale","text":"Set the viewport scale","level":2},{"id":"show-part-of-the-model-in-a-viewport","text":"Show part of the model in a viewport","level":2},{"id":"add-a-second-viewport-for-a-detail","text":"Add a second viewport for a detail","level":2},{"id":"fill-in-the-title-block","text":"Fill in the title block","level":2},{"id":"draw-on-the-sheet","text":"Draw on the sheet","level":2},{"id":"more-sheets","text":"More sheets","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn A3 sheet with a title block, the whole plan in one viewport at 1:100, and a second viewport showing one corner at 1:20.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"model-and-sheets\"\u003EModel and sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou draw the building on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, at full size: a 6 m wall is 6000 units long in millimeters. A \u003Cstrong\u003Esheet\u003C/strong\u003E (a layout) is a piece of paper, measured in millimeters or inches. A \u003Cstrong\u003Eviewport\u003C/strong\u003E is a window cut in the sheet that shows the model at a scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"before-you-start\"\u003EBefore you start\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen or draw a plan on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to the unit you drew in, for example \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimetres\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnits matter: a viewport scale such as 1:100 means one millimeter on the paper shows 100 real millimeters, so CadCraft needs to know what one drawing unit is. A drawing left as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E is taken to be in the paper&#039;s unit (millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-the-sheet\"\u003EMake the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E button to the right of the tabs under the drawing, or type \u003Ccode\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew sheet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA new tab, \u003Ccode\u003ELayout1\u003C/code\u003E, opens. You should see a sheet on a darker desk: a border, a title block in the lower-right corner and one viewport above it showing the whole model at the largest standard scale that fits. The history names the paper: ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, Tabloid (ANSI B) for drawings in inches or feet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E to fit the sheet on screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"choose-the-paper\"\u003EChoose the paper\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected. The right panel heading reads &quot;Sheet: Layout1&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EName\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EA-101 Plan\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The tab is renamed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E, choose a size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrientation\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandscape\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPortrait\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging the paper does not move or resize what is already on the sheet. After changing it, check that the border, the title block and the viewport still fit, and move or resize them with grips. Switching between millimeter and inch paper keeps the numbers, so everything changes size: the status bar warns you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"set-the-viewport-scale\"\u003ESet the viewport scale\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the viewport&#039;s frame (its edge). The panel heading reads &quot;Viewport&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe model inside the viewport redraws at 1:100, centered where it was. The list offers metric scales from 10:1 to 1:10000; drawings in inches or feet also get architectural scales (1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; and the like) and engineering scales (1&quot; = 20&#039; and the like). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit the whole model\u003C/span\u003E sets an exact, non-standard scale that fits everything.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf the plan is no longer centered, change \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView centre X\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView centre Y\u003C/span\u003E: they are the model point shown in the middle of the viewport.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"show-part-of-the-model-in-a-viewport\"\u003EShow part of the model in a viewport\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith the viewport selected, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a window on the model…\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel (or type \u003Ccode\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECadCraft switches to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab. Click one corner of the area to show.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the opposite corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are back on the sheet, and the viewport shows that area at the largest standard scale that fits; the history says which. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the whole model\u003C/span\u003E goes back to the whole plan at a standard scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-a-second-viewport-for-a-detail\"\u003EAdd a second viewport for a detail\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake room on the sheet: select the first viewport and drag a corner grip to make it smaller.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EMV\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViewport\u003C/span\u003E in the Sheets group.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick one corner of the new viewport on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the opposite corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe new viewport shows the whole model. The history says its scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the new viewport&#039;s frame.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a window on the model…\u003C/span\u003E and pick a small area round one corner of the plan.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Ccode\u003E1:20\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EViewport frames are on the layer \u003Ccode\u003EViewports\u003C/code\u003E, which is set not to plot, so the frames show on screen but not in the PDF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"fill-in-the-title-block\"\u003EFill in the title block\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe title block is made of ordinary lines and text on the layer \u003Ccode\u003ETitle block\u003C/code\u003E. It starts with the drawing name, the scale of the first viewport, today&#039;s date and the sheet number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDouble-click the large title text. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E box in the right panel gets the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the title of the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo the same for the other fields. Use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/code\u003E to write in the empty &quot;DRAWN BY&quot; cell. The SCALE text does not update itself when you change a viewport&#039;s scale: edit it by hand.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-on-the-sheet\"\u003EDraw on the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAnything you draw on a sheet tab is on the paper, in paper units: text 3.5 high is 3.5 mm tall on an ISO sheet. Notes, north arrows and sheet borders belong here.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou cannot edit the model through a viewport yet. To change the plan, go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab; every viewport shows the change.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-sheets\"\u003EMore sheets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E adds another sheet (\u003Ccode\u003ELayout2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELayout3\u003C/code\u003E …) with the next sheet number. To delete the open sheet, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete this sheet\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel. It goes without asking; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E brings it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlot a sheet with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot this sheet\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe viewport shows nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E The model is empty, or the view is aimed somewhere else. Select the frame and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the whole model\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe scale list shows only ratios and no feet-and-inch scales.\u003C/strong\u003E Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet\u003C/span\u003E on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E1:100 looks ten times too big or small.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing units are wrong. Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to what one unit really is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EClicking the plan inside the viewport selects nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E On a sheet you select the sheet&#039;s own objects. Edit the plan on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;Viewports go on a layout.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E only works on a sheet tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA viewport twisted in another program shows square.\u003C/strong\u003E Twisted viewports are not built yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make An A3 sheet with a title block, the whole plan in one viewport at 1:100, and a second viewport showing one corner at 1:20. Model and sheets You draw the building on the Model tab, at full size: a 6 m wall is 6000 units long in millimeters. A sheet (a layout) is a piece of paper, measured in millimeters or inches. A viewport is a window cut in the sheet that shows the model at a scale. Before you start Open or draw a plan on the Model tab. Press Esc so nothing is selected. In Current settings, set Units to the unit you drew in, for example Millimetres. Units matter: a viewport scale such as 1:100 means one millimeter on the paper shows 100 real millimeters, so CadCraft needs to know what one drawing unit is. A drawing left as Unitless is taken to be in the paper's unit (millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper). Make the sheet Click the + button to the right of the tabs under the drawing, or type LAYOUT and press Enter, or click New sheet. A new tab, Layout1, opens. You should see a sheet on a darker desk: a border, a title block in the lower-right corner and one viewport above it showing the whole model at the largest standard scale that fits. The history names the paper: ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, Tabloid (ANSI B) for drawings in inches or feet. Click Extents to fit the sheet on screen. Choose the paper Press Esc so nothing is selected. The right panel heading reads \"Sheet: Layout1\". In Name, type A-101 Plan and press Enter. The tab is renamed. In Paper, choose a size. In Orientation, choose Landscape or Portrait. Changing the paper does not move or resize what is already on the sheet. After changing it, check that the border, the title block and the viewport still fit, and move or resize them with grips. Switching between millimeter and inch paper keeps the numbers, so everything changes size: the status bar warns you. Set the viewport scale Click the viewport's frame (its edge). The panel heading reads \"Viewport\". In Scale, choose 1:100. The model inside the viewport redraws at 1:100, centered where it was. The list offers metric scales from 10:1 to 1:10000; drawings in inches or feet also get architectural scales (1/4\" = 1'-0\" and the like) and engineering scales (1\" = 20' and the like). Fit the whole model sets an exact, non-standard scale that fits everything. If the plan is no longer centered, change View centre X and View centre Y: they are the model point shown in the middle of the viewport. Show part of the model in a viewport With the viewport selected, click Pick a window on the model… in the right panel (or type VPVIEW and press Enter). CadCraft switches to the Model tab. Click one corner of the area to show. Click the opposite corner. You are back on the sheet, and the viewport shows that area at the largest standard scale that fits; the history says which. Show the whole model goes back to the whole plan at a standard scale. Add a second viewport for a detail Make room on the sheet: select the first viewport and drag a corner grip to make it smaller. Type MV and press Enter, or click Viewport in the Sheets group. Click one corner of the new viewport on the sheet. Click the opposite corner. The new viewport shows the whole model. The history says its scale. Click the new viewport's frame. Click Pick a window on the model… and pick a small area round one corner of the plan. In Scale, choose 1:20. Viewport frames are on the layer Viewports, which is set not to plot, so the frames show on screen but not in the PDF. Fill in the title block The title block is made of ordinary lines and text on the layer Title block. It starts with the drawing name, the scale of the first viewport, today's date and the sheet number. Double-click the large title text. The Text box in the right panel gets the cursor. Type the title of the sheet. Press Tab. Do the same for the other fields. Use TEXT to write in the empty \"DRAWN BY\" cell. The SCALE text does not update itself when you change a viewport's scale: edit it by hand. Draw on the sheet Anything you draw on a sheet tab is on the paper, in paper units: text 3.5 high is 3.5 mm tall on an ISO sheet. Notes, north arrows and sheet borders belong here. You cannot edit the model through a viewport yet. To change the plan, go to the Model tab; every viewport shows the change. More sheets Each + adds another sheet (Layout2, Layout3 …) with the next sheet number. To delete the open sheet, press Esc and click Delete this sheet in the right panel. It goes without asking; Ctrl+Z brings it back. Plot a sheet with Plot this sheet or Ctrl+P; see Plot to PDF. Common mistakes The viewport shows nothing. The model is empty, or the view is aimed somewhere else. Select the frame and click Show the whole model. The scale list shows only ratios and no feet-and-inch scales. Set Units to Inches or Feet on the Model tab. 1:100 looks ten times too big or small. The drawing units are wrong. Set Units to what one unit really is. Clicking the plan inside the viewport selects nothing. On a sheet you select the sheet's own objects. Edit the plan on the Model tab. \"Viewports go on a layout.\" MVIEW only works on a sheet tab. A viewport twisted in another program shows square. Twisted viewports are not built yet."},{"slug":"constraints","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Constrain a drawing","summary":"Keep lines horizontal, ends together and sizes fixed with geometric and dimensional constraints, and let the drawing follow when you change a size.","section":"tasks","order":125,"keywords":["constraint","constraints","parametric","geomconstraint","gcon","dimconstraint","dcon","gchorizontal","gcvertical","gcparallel","gcperpendicular","gccoincident","gctangent","gcequal","gcfix","gcsymmetric","gccollinear","gcconcentric","dclinear","dchorizontal","dcvertical","dcaligned","dcradius","dcdiameter","dcangular","dcconvert","autoconstrain","delconstraint","constraintbar","cbar","dcdisplay","degrees of freedom","fully constrained"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"draw-and-constrain-the-outline","text":"Draw and constrain the outline","level":2},{"id":"add-the-sizes","text":"Add the sizes","level":2},{"id":"moving-constrained-objects","text":"Moving constrained objects","level":2},{"id":"mirror-and-trim-constrained-objects","text":"Mirror and trim constrained objects","level":2},{"id":"the-constraints","text":"The constraints","level":2},{"id":"see-select-and-remove","text":"See, select and remove","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plate whose width you change in one place: its sides stay square, one corner stays put, its height stays half its width.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConstraints work on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab. Each one you add is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw-and-constrain-the-outline\"\u003EDraw and constrain the outline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E100,50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says &quot;4 constraints applied&quot;. Small boxes with \u003Cstrong\u003EH\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EV\u003C/strong\u003E appear beside the sides: these are the constraint bars.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EGCFIX\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Click near the lower-left corner. A box with ⊕ appears: that corner stays where it is.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN\u003C/code\u003E adds coincident constraints where ends nearly meet and horizontal or vertical ones where lines are nearly level or plumb. Type \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E at its prompt to change how near (0.05 drawing units and 1° to start with).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-the-sizes\"\u003EAdd the sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick near the lower-left corner, then near the lower-right corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick below the rectangle for the dimension line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe prompt shows \u003Ccode\u003EDimension text &lt;d1=100&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth=120\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe rectangle is now 120 wide, and the label reads \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth=120\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click the right side.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick to the right of it, type \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth/2\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is 60 high. The label reads \u003Ccode\u003Efx: d1=width/2\u003C/code\u003E: an expression drives it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history now says the outline is fully constrained. To change its size, change \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth\u003C/code\u003E (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/parameters\"\u003EUse parameters and equations\u003C/a\u003E), or double-click the \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth=120\u003C/code\u003E label, type a new value and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"moving-constrained-objects\"\u003EMoving constrained objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove, rotate, scale, grips and the Properties panel all keep the constraints: what you moved is held, and the rest follows. When the constraints cannot follow, the edit is not made and the history says why, for example &quot;Not done: the constraints hold that object where it is&quot;. Erasing an object removes its constraints with it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mirror-and-trim-constrained-objects\"\u003EMirror and trim constrained objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E copies the constraints among the objects you mirror onto the copies. Sides (which way a tangent touches, which side a distance is measured to) are worked out again for the mirrored copy, and copied dimensions get names of their own, as a paste does. With \u003Ccode\u003EErase source objects\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E, the constraints stay on the mirrored objects. The history says how many came along.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E keeps a line&#039;s or an arc&#039;s constraints on what is left: a constraint on an end goes to the piece that still has that end, a rule about direction (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHorizontal\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVertical\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParallel\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPerpendicular\u003C/span\u003E) holds on every piece, and a dimension between the two outer ends keeps measuring them. Constraints that held the part that was cut away, or a length the cut changed (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEqual\u003C/span\u003E, an arc&#039;s sweep), go. A trimmed circle keeps its center and radius constraints. Trimmed polylines lose theirs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-constraints\"\u003EThe constraints\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECommand\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKeeps\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea line or segment level or plumb (\u003Ccode\u003E2P\u003C/code\u003E: two points level or plumb)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPARALLEL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPERPENDICULAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOLLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo lines parallel, square or on one line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOINCIDENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo points together; \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E: a point on a line, circle or arc\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCTANGENT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea line and a circle or arc, or two of them, touching\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCONCENTRIC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCEQUAL\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo circles or arcs on one centre; two lengths or two radii equal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCSYMMETRIC\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo points, lines or circles mirrored across a line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCFIX\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea point (click near it) or a whole object (\u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E) where it is\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCALIGNED\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea distance\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCRADIUS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea radius, a diameter, an angle between two lines or an arc&#039;s angle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGEOMCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E ask which one; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EConstrain\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDimension\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E group run them, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E there lists each. Where you click picks the part: a click near an end, the middle or a center takes that point; a click on a polyline takes the segment under it. The first object stays and the second moves to meet it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA constraint that conflicts with others, or adds nothing because the others already imply it, is refused with a message and nothing moves.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"see-select-and-remove\"\u003ESee, select and remove\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick a box in a constraint bar (or a coincident square, or a dimension label) to select that constraint; the history names it. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to remove it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDELCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E removes every constraint from the objects you select.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECONSTRAINTBAR\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E shows every bar, \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E hides them; select objects first to show or hide theirs. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow constraint bars\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHide constraint bars\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E shows or hides the dimensional constraints. They never plot.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCCONVERT\u003C/code\u003E turns dimensions drawn on lines, circles and arcs into dimensional constraints.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you have used AutoCAD&#039;s parametric drawing, the command names and prompts are the same. Constraints and parameters are saved in DWG and DXF files in a form other programs ignore; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#constraints-in-files\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"constraints-in-files\"\u003EFiles\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will make A plate whose width you change in one place: its sides stay square, one corner stays put, its height stays half its width. Constraints work on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points on the Model tab. Each one you add is one undo step. Draw and constrain the outline Type REC and press Enter. Type 0,0, Enter, 100,50, Enter. Type AUTOCONSTRAIN and press Enter. Type ALL, Enter, then Enter again. The history says \"4 constraints applied\". Small boxes with H and V appear beside the sides: these are the constraint bars. Type GCFIX and press Enter. Click near the lower-left corner. A box with ⊕ appears: that corner stays where it is. AUTOCONSTRAIN adds coincident constraints where ends nearly meet and horizontal or vertical ones where lines are nearly level or plumb. Type S at its prompt to change how near (0.05 drawing units and 1° to start with). Add the sizes Type DCHORIZONTAL and press Enter. Click near the lower-left corner, then near the lower-right corner. Click below the rectangle for the dimension line. The prompt shows Dimension text \u003Cd1=100\u003E. Type width=120 and press Enter. The rectangle is now 120 wide, and the label reads width=120. Type DCVERTICAL and press Enter, type O and press Enter, then click the right side. Click to the right of it, type width/2 and press Enter. It is 60 high. The label reads fx: d1=width/2: an expression drives it. The history now says the outline is fully constrained. To change its size, change width (see Use parameters and equations), or double-click the width=120 label, type a new value and choose Apply. Moving constrained objects Move, rotate, scale, grips and the Properties panel all keep the constraints: what you moved is held, and the rest follows. When the constraints cannot follow, the edit is not made and the history says why, for example \"Not done: the constraints hold that object where it is\". Erasing an object removes its constraints with it. Mirror and trim constrained objects MIRROR copies the constraints among the objects you mirror onto the copies. Sides (which way a tangent touches, which side a distance is measured to) are worked out again for the mirrored copy, and copied dimensions get names of their own, as a paste does. With Erase source objects Y, the constraints stay on the mirrored objects. The history says how many came along. TRIM keeps a line's or an arc's constraints on what is left: a constraint on an end goes to the piece that still has that end, a rule about direction (Horizontal, Vertical, Parallel, Perpendicular) holds on every piece, and a dimension between the two outer ends keeps measuring them. Constraints that held the part that was cut away, or a length the cut changed (Equal, an arc's sweep), go. A trimmed circle keeps its center and radius constraints. Trimmed polylines lose theirs. The constraints Command Keeps GCHORIZONTAL, GCVERTICAL a line or segment level or plumb (2P: two points level or plumb) GCPARALLEL, GCPERPENDICULAR, GCCOLLINEAR two lines parallel, square or on one line GCCOINCIDENT two points together; O: a point on a line, circle or arc GCTANGENT a line and a circle or arc, or two of them, touching GCCONCENTRIC, GCEQUAL two circles or arcs on one centre; two lengths or two radii equal GCSYMMETRIC two points, lines or circles mirrored across a line GCFIX a point (click near it) or a whole object (O) where it is DCLINEAR, DCHORIZONTAL, DCVERTICAL, DCALIGNED a distance DCRADIUS, DCDIAMETER, DCANGULAR a radius, a diameter, an angle between two lines or an arc's angle GEOMCONSTRAINT and DIMCONSTRAINT ask which one; Constrain and Dimension in the Parametric group run them, and More there lists each. Where you click picks the part: a click near an end, the middle or a center takes that point; a click on a polyline takes the segment under it. The first object stays and the second moves to meet it. A constraint that conflicts with others, or adds nothing because the others already imply it, is refused with a message and nothing moves. See, select and remove Click a box in a constraint bar (or a coincident square, or a dimension label) to select that constraint; the history names it. Press Delete to remove it. DELCONSTRAINT removes every constraint from the objects you select. CONSTRAINTBAR: S shows every bar, H hides them; select objects first to show or hide theirs. More has Show constraint bars and Hide constraint bars. DCDISPLAY shows or hides the dimensional constraints. They never plot. DCCONVERT turns dimensions drawn on lines, circles and arcs into dimensional constraints. If you have used AutoCAD's parametric drawing, the command names and prompts are the same. Constraints and parameters are saved in DWG and DXF files in a form other programs ignore; see Files."},{"slug":"parameters","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Use parameters and equations","summary":"Name sizes, drive them with expressions like width / 2, and type a number on a driven size to change what drives it.","section":"tasks","order":126,"keywords":["parameters","par","parameters manager","equations","expressions","variables","two-way","design intent","user parameter","fx"],"headings":[{"id":"one-table-for-every-size","text":"One table for every size","level":2},{"id":"add-your-own","text":"Add your own","level":2},{"id":"expressions","text":"Expressions","level":2},{"id":"at-the-command-line","text":"At the command line","level":2},{"id":"what-is-refused","text":"What is refused","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"one-table-for-every-size\"\u003EOne table for every size\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E panel (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E button in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E group, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E) lists every dimensional constraint (\u003Ccode\u003Ed1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erad1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edia1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eang1\u003C/code\u003E, or the name you gave it) and your own parameters together. Any name can be used in any expression.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach row has:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EName\u003C/strong\u003E: type a new one to rename it; every expression that uses it follows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EExpression\u003C/strong\u003E: a number (\u003Ccode\u003E40\u003C/code\u003E) or an expression (\u003Ccode\u003Ewidth / 2 + gap\u003C/code\u003E). Type in it and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: the drawing follows.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EValue\u003C/strong\u003E: what it works out to. On a row an expression drives, you can type a number here too: CadCraft works out the parameter behind it. With \u003Ccode\u003Ed1 = width / 2\u003C/code\u003E, typing \u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E in d1&#039;s value sets \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth\u003C/code\u003E to 60.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen two parameters drive a value (\u003Ccode\u003Ewidth / 2 + gap\u003C/code\u003E), a dialog asks which one to change. Every change is one undo step (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-your-own\"\u003EAdd your own\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a name (\u003Ccode\u003Egap\u003C/code\u003E) and a value (\u003Ccode\u003E10\u003C/code\u003E), then choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUse it: in d1&#039;s expression, type \u003Ccode\u003Ewidth / 2 + gap\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENames start with a letter or \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E and hold letters, digits and \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E. Function names, \u003Ccode\u003Epi\u003C/code\u003E and unit names cannot be names.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"expressions\"\u003EExpressions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWrite\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E+ - * / ^ %\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eadd, subtract, multiply, divide, power, remainder\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E( )\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Egrouping\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esin cos tan asin acos atan atan2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etrigonometry in degrees\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Esqrt pow exp ln log abs sign\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eroots, powers, logarithms\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eceil floor int round min max\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Erounding and limits\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Eif(a &gt; b, x, y)\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea choice\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Epi\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3.14159…\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENumbers may carry a unit: \u003Ccode\u003E25mm\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E1in\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2&#039;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E90deg\u003C/code\u003E. Lengths with a unit are turned into millimetres, so use units only in drawings measured in millimetres; elsewhere type plain numbers.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"at-the-command-line\"\u003EAt the command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E-PARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E does the same without the panel: \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E new, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E edit (a dimensional constraint too), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E rename, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E delete, \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-refused\"\u003EWhat is refused\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAn expression that uses itself, directly or through others: &quot;width, d1 use each other.&quot;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA value a size cannot take: a negative length, an angle over 180°.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA value the drawing cannot take with its other constraints: nothing changes, and the message names the constraints in the way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDeleting a parameter something uses: the panel offers to write its value into them first. A dimensional constraint&#039;s name is deleted by deleting the constraint.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"One table for every size The Parameters panel (the Parameters button in the Parametric group, or type PARAMETERS) lists every dimensional constraint (d1, rad1, dia1, ang1, or the name you gave it) and your own parameters together. Any name can be used in any expression. Each row has: Name: type a new one to rename it; every expression that uses it follows. Expression: a number (40) or an expression (width / 2 + gap). Type in it and press Enter: the drawing follows. Value: what it works out to. On a row an expression drives, you can type a number here too: CadCraft works out the parameter behind it. With d1 = width / 2, typing 30 in d1's value sets width to 60. When two parameters drive a value (width / 2 + gap), a dialog asks which one to change. Every change is one undo step (Ctrl+Z). Add your own Open the Parameters panel. Type a name (gap) and a value (10), then choose Add. Use it: in d1's expression, type width / 2 + gap and press Enter. Names start with a letter or _ and hold letters, digits and _. Function names, pi and unit names cannot be names. Expressions Write Means + - * / ^ % add, subtract, multiply, divide, power, remainder ( ) grouping sin cos tan asin acos atan atan2 trigonometry in degrees sqrt pow exp ln log abs sign roots, powers, logarithms ceil floor int round min max rounding and limits if(a \u003E b, x, y) a choice pi 3.14159… Numbers may carry a unit: 25mm, 1in, 2', 90deg. Lengths with a unit are turned into millimetres, so use units only in drawings measured in millimetres; elsewhere type plain numbers. At the command line -PARAMETERS does the same without the panel: N new, E edit (a dimensional constraint too), R rename, D delete, ? list. What is refused An expression that uses itself, directly or through others: \"width, d1 use each other.\" A value a size cannot take: a negative length, an angle over 180°. A value the drawing cannot take with its other constraints: nothing changes, and the message names the constraints in the way. Deleting a parameter something uses: the panel offers to write its value into them first. A dimensional constraint's name is deleted by deleting the constraint."},{"slug":"solidworks-sketches","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Move sketches to and from other part modelers","summary":"Write a macro that rebuilds your drawing as one constrained sketch in a part modeler, and open a sketch exported from one.","section":"tasks","order":127,"keywords":["sketch","macro","swb","vba","export","part modeler","global variables","equations","relations","cadcraft-sw-export","swbout"],"headings":[{"id":"send-a-drawing-as-a-sketch","text":"Send a drawing as a sketch","level":2},{"id":"open-a-sketch-from-a-part","text":"Open a sketch from a part","level":2}],"html":"\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolidWorks is not available where CadCraft is built, so neither direction has been tested in SolidWorks yet. What CadCraft writes is checked for structure only. Check the result before you rely on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"send-a-drawing-as-a-sketch\"\u003ESend a drawing as a sketch\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConstrain and dimension the drawing (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003EConstrain a drawing\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParametric\u003C/span\u003E group, open \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E and choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMacro for SolidWorks (.swb)\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESWBOUT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA file named after the drawing, ending \u003Ccode\u003E.swb\u003C/code\u003E, downloads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn SolidWorks, choose \u003Cstrong\u003ETools &gt; Macro &gt; Run\u003C/strong\u003E and pick that file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe macro makes a new part from your default part template, opens a sketch on the Front Plane and draws the model&#039;s lines, circles, arcs, points and polylines in it, with:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eyour constraints as sketch relations,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eyour dimensional constraints as dimensions with the same names (\u003Ccode\u003Ed1@Sketch1\u003C/code\u003E),\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eyour own parameters as global variables,\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eevery expression as an equation.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe part uses the drawing&#039;s length unit. Text, blocks, hatches, dimensions and sheets are not part of the sketch; the history lists what was left out. Expressions with no equation form there (\u003Ccode\u003Eif\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emin\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emax\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eround\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E%\u003C/code\u003E and a few others) are written as their current value, and the history says which. Anything the macro cannot make is written to a log file next to the macro, and a message sums up at the end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-a-sketch-from-a-part\"\u003EOpen a sketch from a part\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn SolidWorks, run the CadCraft export macro (download it in the PartCraft app: File &gt; Export &gt; Export macro to bring parts back) on the part. It writes a \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file in the \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft-sw-export\u003C/code\u003E format next to the part.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn CadCraft, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E and pick that \u003Ccode\u003E.json\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen the part has several sketches, choose one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sketch becomes the drawing, in millimetres: its lines, arcs, circles and points, its relations as constraints, its dimensions as dimensional constraints named as they were (\u003Ccode\u003ED1\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ED2\u003C/code\u003E …), its global variables as parameters, and the equations on its dimensions as their expressions. The history lists what was not carried over, such as relations CadCraft does not have. Where the sketch sits in the part is not kept: it is drawn flat, as seen on its plane.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"SolidWorks is not available where CadCraft is built, so neither direction has been tested in SolidWorks yet. What CadCraft writes is checked for structure only. Check the result before you rely on it. Send a drawing as a sketch Constrain and dimension the drawing (see Constrain a drawing). In the Parametric group, open More and choose Macro for SolidWorks (.swb), or type SWBOUT. A file named after the drawing, ending .swb, downloads. In SolidWorks, choose Tools \u003E Macro \u003E Run and pick that file. The macro makes a new part from your default part template, opens a sketch on the Front Plane and draws the model's lines, circles, arcs, points and polylines in it, with: your constraints as sketch relations, your dimensional constraints as dimensions with the same names (d1@Sketch1), your own parameters as global variables, every expression as an equation. The part uses the drawing's length unit. Text, blocks, hatches, dimensions and sheets are not part of the sketch; the history lists what was left out. Expressions with no equation form there (if, min, max, round, % and a few others) are written as their current value, and the history says which. Anything the macro cannot make is written to a log file next to the macro, and a message sums up at the end. Open a sketch from a part In SolidWorks, run the CadCraft export macro (download it in the PartCraft app: File \u003E Export \u003E Export macro to bring parts back) on the part. It writes a .json file in the cadcraft-sw-export format next to the part. In CadCraft, choose Open and pick that .json file. When the part has several sketches, choose one. The sketch becomes the drawing, in millimetres: its lines, arcs, circles and points, its relations as constraints, its dimensions as dimensional constraints named as they were (D1, D2 …), its global variables as parameters, and the equations on its dimensions as their expressions. The history lists what was not carried over, such as relations CadCraft does not have. Where the sketch sits in the part is not kept: it is drawn flat, as seen on its plane."},{"slug":"3d-solids","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Model in 3D","summary":"See the drawing in 3D on the 3D tab and make simple solids from it: extrude (with a taper or along a path), revolve, push and pull faces, boxes, cylinders and wedges, join, subtract and intersect them, then move, copy, rotate and erase them, and place flat 2D views of them on a sheet.","section":"tasks","order":128,"keywords":["3d","three d","solid","solids","extrude","ext","revolve","rev","sweep","taper","path","presspull","press pull","push pull","box","cylinder","cyl","wedge","3dorbit","orbit","view","vscurrent","visual style","shaded","hidden line","wireframe","x-ray","xray","iso","perspective","parallel","glb","gltf","obj","stl","export3d","stlout","objout","glbout","3dface","view cube","union","uni","subtract","su","intersect","in","boolean","combine","join solids","viewbase","flatshot","flat view","2d view","drawing view","front view","top view","side view","hidden lines"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"extrude-a-shape","text":"Extrude a shape","level":2},{"id":"revolve-and-sweep","text":"Revolve and sweep","level":2},{"id":"push-and-pull-a-face","text":"Push and pull a face","level":2},{"id":"boxes-cylinders-and-wedges","text":"Boxes, cylinders and wedges","level":2},{"id":"move-copy-rotate-and-erase-solids","text":"Move, copy, rotate and erase solids","level":2},{"id":"join-subtract-and-intersect-solids","text":"Join, subtract and intersect solids","level":2},{"id":"flat-views-on-a-sheet","text":"Flat views on a sheet","level":2},{"id":"look-around","text":"Look around","level":2},{"id":"save-and-export","text":"Save and export","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA 1000 × 600 room footprint extruded into a block 400 high, a face pulled up, and a cylinder beside it — then the model saved with the drawing and exported for a 3D viewer or a printer.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab sits beside \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E under the drawing. It shows the model-space drawing lying on the ground in its layer colors (layers that are off or frozen stay hidden) and the solids you make. The first time it opens, the 3D view loads; after that it switches at once.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"extrude-a-shape\"\u003EExtrude a shape\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOn the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, type \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E0,0\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@1000,600\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab. The toolbar shows the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E group in place of the drawing groups.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtrude\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTRUDE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEXT\u003C/code\u003E). Click inside the rectangle, then press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E400\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The history says &quot;Extruded 1 solid, 400 high&quot; with its volume.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClosed polylines (arcs included), rectangles, polygons, circles and ellipses extrude; lines and open polylines do not, and the history says why. You can also select the shapes on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab first and type \u003Ccode\u003EEXTRUDE\u003C/code\u003E there. Instead of typing the height you can click it: the outline follows the cursor up and down. A negative height goes down. Type \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E at the height prompt to delete the 2D shapes after extruding (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E again keeps them).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt the height prompt, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (Taper angle) asks for an angle in degrees: the sides lean in by it, so the top is smaller (a negative angle makes it larger); an angle too steep for the shape is refused. \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Path) sweeps the shapes along a path instead (see below).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"revolve-and-sweep\"\u003ERevolve and sweep\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREVOLVE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EREV\u003C/code\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERevolve about an axis\u003C/span\u003E): select closed shapes, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click the two ends of the axis (or type \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E for the drawing&#039;s axis through 0,0), then type an angle or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for all the way round. The shape turns about the axis and stands up out of the ground: draw half a vase&#039;s outline beside a line, revolve it about the line. The shape may touch the axis but not cross it. A full turn has 48 steps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESWEEP\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESweep along a path\u003C/span\u003E, or \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E at EXTRUDE&#039;s height prompt): select closed shapes (the profile), press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then click the path: a line, polyline, arc, circle or ellipse. The profile stands up across the path, its middle on it, and runs along it; corners are mitered, and a closed path makes a ring. A small rectangle swept along a room&#039;s outline makes a skirting; a circle along a polyline makes a pipe.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"push-and-pull-a-face\"\u003EPush and pull a face\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPress/pull\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPRESSPULL\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the top face of the block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003E100\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or move the cursor and click. The block is 500 high.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"boxes-cylinders-and-wedges\"\u003EBoxes, cylinders and wedges\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBOX\u003C/code\u003E: click or type two corners, then a height.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECYLINDER\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ECYL\u003C/code\u003E): the center, the radius, then a height. Cylinders and extruded circles have 48 sides, shaded smooth.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EWEDGE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EWE\u003C/code\u003E): two corners and a height. It is full height at the first corner and slopes to nothing at the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the 3D tab a click lands on the face or edge under the cursor, else on the ground, so a box can stand on another solid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-copy-rotate-and-erase-solids\"\u003EMove, copy, rotate and erase solids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn the 3D tab, a click selects a solid; drag left to right for a window, right to left for a crossing. Then:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E: click a base point and where it goes, or type \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;100,0,0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E@100,0\u003C/code\u003E) for a distance along X, Y and Z.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E: the same, leaving the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/code\u003E: type an angle (it turns about the middle of the selection, around Z), or click the center, a start point and the angle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"join-subtract-and-intersect-solids\"\u003EJoin, subtract and intersect solids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E in the 3D toolbar, or type the command:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNION\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EUNI\u003C/code\u003E): select the solids, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: they become one solid.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESUBTRACT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ESU\u003C/code\u003E): select the solids to keep and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then the ones to take away and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A hole through a block: draw a cylinder through it, then subtract the cylinder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EINTERSECT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EIN\u003C/code\u003E): select the solids, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: only what they all have in common is left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe history says the volume of the result. Each is one undo step. The first one you run loads the 3D boolean engine (about 0.5 MB; included in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E). Only closed solids can be combined: a solid with open edges (from another program&#039;s loose 3D faces) is refused with that reason. The result is one solid; curved faces stay made of flat facets, as the cylinder was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDouble-click a solid to edit its faces and edges on their own; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes it again. Every 3D change is one undo step in the same history as the drawing: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the last thing you did on either tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"flat-views-on-a-sheet\"\u003EFlat views on a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a sheet tab (or stay on \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E), then type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003EFLATSHOT\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the view: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the front, or \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E (top), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (right), \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E (left), \u003Ccode\u003EBA\u003C/code\u003E (back), \u003Ccode\u003EBO\u003C/code\u003E (bottom), \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E (isometric).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E shows hidden lines, dashed; \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E leaves them out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the scale CadCraft suggests: on a sheet, the largest standard scale (such as 1:20) at which the view fits in under half the paper. Or type one: \u003Ccode\u003E1:50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2:1\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick, or type, where the view&#039;s center goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe view is one block reference, \u003Ccode\u003EVIEW-FRONT-1\u003C/code\u003E and so on: visible edges on layer VIEW-VISIBLE (0.5 mm), hidden ones on VIEW-HIDDEN (dashed, 0.25 mm). Move it, dimension it and plot it like any other drawing. It is a picture of the solids when you made it: after changing them, erase the view and run \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E again. For more views, run it once per view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"look-around\"\u003ELook around\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with the wheel to orbit, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E + wheel drag or the right button to pan, and turn the wheel to zoom at the cursor. On a trackpad, two fingers orbit and a pinch zooms. A right click without dragging is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, as on the other tabs. \u003Ccode\u003E3DORBIT\u003C/code\u003E lets the left button orbit until you press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EViews\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEW\u003C/code\u003E): Iso, Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right, Perspective or Parallel, and Zoom to everything.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStyle\u003C/span\u003E (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVSCURRENT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EVS\u003C/code\u003E): Shaded, Shaded with edges, Hidden line, Wireframe or X-ray.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints you click snap to the solids (corners, edges, faces) and to the 2D drawing on the ground: the ends and middles of its lines and arcs, and points along them. The 2D lines are never selected on the 3D tab. When a command asks for 2D objects (EXTRUDE, REVOLVE, SWEEP), a click picks one, a drag left to right takes what is wholly inside the window, and a drag right to left takes what it crosses, as on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA view cube sits at the top right of the 3D tab: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, and the house button goes back to the iso view. That is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutomatic\u003C/span\u003E, the start of \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E. Set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView cube\u003C/span\u003E, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab shows a cube too, looking from the top; clicking any other side opens the 3D tab looking from there. Set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAxis icon\u003C/span\u003E, both tabs show the axis marker instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-and-export\"\u003ESave and export\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESaving keeps the 3D model, in DWG and in DXF. Every face goes in as a 3D face on the layer \u003Ccode\u003ECC-3D\u003C/code\u003E, so other programs show the solids, and CadCraft keeps its own exact copy in the file, which it reads back when you open the drawing again, with the 3D view as it was (camera, projection and visual style). 3D faces made by other programs come in on the 3D tab as loose faces, kept together as one group. A 3D face inside a block comes in once for each reference to that block in the model, placed and turned as the reference is; in the block itself, and on a sheet, a 3D face is drawn flat, as its outline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E in the 3D group downloads the solids alone: GLB for viewers and the web (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGLBOUT\u003C/code\u003E), OBJ (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOBJOUT\u003C/code\u003E) or STL for 3D printing, in millimeters (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTLOUT\u003C/code\u003E). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT3D\u003C/code\u003E asks which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe 3D model is real size. A drawing without units counts as millimeters, so \u003Ccode\u003E1000\u003C/code\u003E is one meter. If you change the drawing&#039;s units later (Properties, with nothing selected), the solids are scaled with them so they stay on the drawing, and the history says so. One \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E undoes the change of units and the scaling together.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E","text":"What you will make A 1000 × 600 room footprint extruded into a block 400 high, a face pulled up, and a cylinder beside it — then the model saved with the drawing and exported for a 3D viewer or a printer. The 3D tab sits beside Model under the drawing. It shows the model-space drawing lying on the ground in its layer colors (layers that are off or frozen stay hidden) and the solids you make. The first time it opens, the 3D view loads; after that it switches at once. Extrude a shape On the Model tab, type REC and press Enter. Type 0,0, Enter, @1000,600, Enter. Click the 3D tab. The toolbar shows the 3D group in place of the drawing groups. Click Extrude (or type EXTRUDE, EXT). Click inside the rectangle, then press Enter. Type 400 and press Enter. The history says \"Extruded 1 solid, 400 high\" with its volume. Closed polylines (arcs included), rectangles, polygons, circles and ellipses extrude; lines and open polylines do not, and the history says why. You can also select the shapes on the Model tab first and type EXTRUDE there. Instead of typing the height you can click it: the outline follows the cursor up and down. A negative height goes down. Type D at the height prompt to delete the 2D shapes after extruding (D again keeps them). At the height prompt, T (Taper angle) asks for an angle in degrees: the sides lean in by it, so the top is smaller (a negative angle makes it larger); an angle too steep for the shape is refused. P (Path) sweeps the shapes along a path instead (see below). Revolve and sweep REVOLVE (REV, or More › Revolve about an axis): select closed shapes, press Enter, then click the two ends of the axis (or type X or Y for the drawing's axis through 0,0), then type an angle or press Enter for all the way round. The shape turns about the axis and stands up out of the ground: draw half a vase's outline beside a line, revolve it about the line. The shape may touch the axis but not cross it. A full turn has 48 steps. SWEEP (or More › Sweep along a path, or P at EXTRUDE's height prompt): select closed shapes (the profile), press Enter, then click the path: a line, polyline, arc, circle or ellipse. The profile stands up across the path, its middle on it, and runs along it; corners are mitered, and a closed path makes a ring. A small rectangle swept along a room's outline makes a skirting; a circle along a polyline makes a pipe. Push and pull a face Click Press/pull (or type PRESSPULL). Click the top face of the block. Type 100 and press Enter, or move the cursor and click. The block is 500 high. Boxes, cylinders and wedges BOX: click or type two corners, then a height. CYLINDER (CYL): the center, the radius, then a height. Cylinders and extruded circles have 48 sides, shaded smooth. WEDGE (WE): two corners and a height. It is full height at the first corner and slopes to nothing at the other. On the 3D tab a click lands on the face or edge under the cursor, else on the ground, so a box can stand on another solid. Move, copy, rotate and erase solids On the 3D tab, a click selects a solid; drag left to right for a window, right to left for a crossing. Then: MOVE: click a base point and where it goes, or type \u003C100,0,0\u003E (or @100,0) for a distance along X, Y and Z. COPY: the same, leaving the original. ROTATE: type an angle (it turns about the middle of the selection, around Z), or click the center, a start point and the angle. ERASE or Delete. Join, subtract and intersect solids Open More in the 3D toolbar, or type the command: UNION (UNI): select the solids, press Enter: they become one solid. SUBTRACT (SU): select the solids to keep and press Enter, then the ones to take away and press Enter. A hole through a block: draw a cylinder through it, then subtract the cylinder. INTERSECT (IN): select the solids, press Enter: only what they all have in common is left. The history says the volume of the result. Each is one undo step. The first one you run loads the 3D boolean engine (about 0.5 MB; included in Save for offline). Only closed solids can be combined: a solid with open edges (from another program's loose 3D faces) is refused with that reason. The result is one solid; curved faces stay made of flat facets, as the cylinder was. Double-click a solid to edit its faces and edges on their own; Esc closes it again. Every 3D change is one undo step in the same history as the drawing: Ctrl+Z undoes the last thing you did on either tab. Flat views on a sheet Open a sheet tab (or stay on Model), then type VIEWBASE (or FLATSHOT). Choose the view: Enter for the front, or T (top), R (right), L (left), BA (back), BO (bottom), I (isometric). Enter shows hidden lines, dashed; H leaves them out. Enter takes the scale CadCraft suggests: on a sheet, the largest standard scale (such as 1:20) at which the view fits in under half the paper. Or type one: 1:50, 2:1. Click, or type, where the view's center goes. The view is one block reference, VIEW-FRONT-1 and so on: visible edges on layer VIEW-VISIBLE (0.5 mm), hidden ones on VIEW-HIDDEN (dashed, 0.25 mm). Move it, dimension it and plot it like any other drawing. It is a picture of the solids when you made it: after changing them, erase the view and run VIEWBASE again. For more views, run it once per view. Look around Drag with the wheel to orbit, Shift + wheel drag or the right button to pan, and turn the wheel to zoom at the cursor. On a trackpad, two fingers orbit and a pinch zooms. A right click without dragging is Enter, as on the other tabs. 3DORBIT lets the left button orbit until you press Enter or Esc. Views (or VIEW): Iso, Top, Bottom, Front, Back, Left, Right, Perspective or Parallel, and Zoom to everything. Style (or VSCURRENT, VS): Shaded, Shaded with edges, Hidden line, Wireframe or X-ray. Points you click snap to the solids (corners, edges, faces) and to the 2D drawing on the ground: the ends and middles of its lines and arcs, and points along them. The 2D lines are never selected on the 3D tab. When a command asks for 2D objects (EXTRUDE, REVOLVE, SWEEP), a click picks one, a drag left to right takes what is wholly inside the window, and a drag right to left takes what it crosses, as on the Model tab. A view cube sits at the top right of the 3D tab: click a face, edge or corner to look from there, drag it to orbit, and the house button goes back to the iso view. That is Automatic, the start of Corner indicator in CadCraft settings. Set to View cube, the Model tab shows a cube too, looking from the top; clicking any other side opens the 3D tab looking from there. Set to Axis icon, both tabs show the axis marker instead. Save and export Saving keeps the 3D model, in DWG and in DXF. Every face goes in as a 3D face on the layer CC-3D, so other programs show the solids, and CadCraft keeps its own exact copy in the file, which it reads back when you open the drawing again, with the 3D view as it was (camera, projection and visual style). 3D faces made by other programs come in on the 3D tab as loose faces, kept together as one group. A 3D face inside a block comes in once for each reference to that block in the model, placed and turned as the reference is; in the block itself, and on a sheet, a 3D face is drawn flat, as its outline. Export in the 3D group downloads the solids alone: GLB for viewers and the web (GLBOUT), OBJ (OBJOUT) or STL for 3D printing, in millimeters (STLOUT). EXPORT3D asks which. The 3D model is real size. A drawing without units counts as millimeters, so 1000 is one meter. If you change the drawing's units later (Properties, with nothing selected), the solids are scaled with them so they stay on the drawing, and the history says so. One Ctrl+Z undoes the change of units and the scaling together."},{"slug":"plot-pdf","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Plot to PDF","summary":"Make a PDF of a sheet at full size, or of the model at a scale or fitted to the paper, and print it from the browser.","section":"tasks","order":130,"keywords":["plot","print","pdf","publish","exportpdf","paper","scale","monochrome","line weight","pen","page setup","plot window"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-make","text":"What you will make","level":2},{"id":"plot-a-sheet","text":"Plot a sheet","level":2},{"id":"every-sheet-in-one-pdf","text":"Every sheet in one PDF","level":2},{"id":"plot-the-model","text":"Plot the model","level":2},{"id":"print","text":"Print","level":2},{"id":"settings-that-are-remembered","text":"Settings that are remembered","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-make\"\u003EWhat you will make\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA PDF of a sheet at full size, then a quick PDF of the model at 1:100, and a printout.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe PDF is made inside your browser; nothing is uploaded. It is a vector PDF: lines stay sharp at any zoom, and text can be searched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-a-sheet\"\u003EPlot a sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the sheet&#039;s tab under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E, or type \u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog opens. It says which sheet it plots and on what paper, &quot;at full size … as you see it&quot;. The preview on the right shows the sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInk\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlack (monochrome)\u003C/span\u003E for black lines, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing colours\u003C/span\u003E to keep the colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine weight\u003C/span\u003E, choose a pen width from 0.13 mm to 0.70 mm. Lines with the default lineweight print at this width; lines with their own lineweight (layer or object) print at that.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe browser downloads a PDF named after the drawing and the sheet, for example \u003Ccode\u003EDrawing1-A-101 Plan.pdf\u003C/code\u003E. The history reports its size in bytes and its paper size in millimeters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA sheet always plots at 1:1 on its own paper. To change the paper, change it on the sheet itself (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheets-and-viewports\"\u003ESet up a sheet\u003C/a\u003E). Layers set not to plot are left out, and so are viewport frames.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"every-sheet-in-one-pdf\"\u003EEvery sheet in one PDF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith two or more sheets, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog has \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets in one PDF\u003C/span\u003E; or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes every sheet; \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Some) asks for their names, separated by commas. One PDF downloads with a page per sheet, in tab order, each at its own paper size, and a bookmark per sheet with its name. It uses the ink and pen width you last chose in the dialog.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-the-model\"\u003EPlot the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog says it plots the model onto a sheet of paper, and shows more settings:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E, choose what to plot:\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEverything (extents)\u003C/span\u003E: the whole drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWhat is on screen\u003C/span\u003E: the area you see in the drawing now.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA window…\u003C/span\u003E: then click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick\u003C/span\u003E, click two corners on the drawing, and the dialog comes back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaper\u003C/span\u003E, choose a size: ISO A4 to A0, Letter, Legal, Tabloid and ANSI C and D, or ARCH C, D and E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrientation\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELandscape\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPortrait\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScale\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E or a scale such as \u003Ccode\u003E1:100\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the settings, a line reports the scale and how big the area comes out on the paper. If it is bigger than the paper, it says so: the edges are cut off. Choose a smaller scale, a bigger paper, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA model plot leaves a 5 mm margin round the edge, because most printers cannot print to the edge. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E gives an odd scale (the history and the info line say &quot;(fitted)&quot;); choose a standard scale when the drawing must measure true.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EScales read in real units. For 1:100 to be right, set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E to what one drawing unit is. A unitless drawing is taken to be in millimeters.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"print\"\u003EPrint\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the dialog, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen to print\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe PDF opens in a new browser tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrint from that tab with the browser&#039;s print command (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E there).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn the printer settings, choose &quot;Actual size&quot; or 100% (not &quot;Fit&quot;) to keep the scale.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari may show the PDF and wait for you to use \u003Cstrong\u003EFile\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf your browser blocks the new tab, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead and says so in the history. Allow pop-ups for the site to open it in a tab next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"settings-that-are-remembered\"\u003ESettings that are remembered\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe dialog remembers your last paper, orientation, scale, ink and line weight in this browser. A picked window is kept until you reload the page; after that the dialog starts at \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEverything (extents)\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe PDF is blank or the buttons are grey.\u003C/strong\u003E There is nothing to plot (an empty model), or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA window…\u003C/span\u003E is chosen and no window is picked yet (&quot;Pick the window to plot.&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EText shows &quot;?&quot; in the PDF.\u003C/strong\u003E The PDF uses the built-in Helvetica font, which only has Western European characters. The history counts how many characters were replaced.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAll lines are the same thickness.\u003C/strong\u003E Their lineweight is the default: give layers or objects a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConstruction lines printed.\u003C/strong\u003E Set their layer not to plot: click its printer button in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe printout is the wrong size.\u003C/strong\u003E The printer scaled it. Print at actual size.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opened the browser&#039;s print dialog.\u003C/strong\u003E The cursor was in a box in the right panel, or another dialog was open. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave the box, then try again, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E did nothing.\u003C/strong\u003E A command is running. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will make A PDF of a sheet at full size, then a quick PDF of the model at 1:100, and a printout. The PDF is made inside your browser; nothing is uploaded. It is a vector PDF: lines stay sharp at any zoom, and text can be searched. Plot a sheet Click the sheet's tab under the drawing. Press Ctrl+P, click Plot, or type PLOT and press Enter. The Plot to PDF dialog opens. It says which sheet it plots and on what paper, \"at full size … as you see it\". The preview on the right shows the sheet. In Ink, choose Black (monochrome) for black lines, or Drawing colours to keep the colors. In Line weight, choose a pen width from 0.13 mm to 0.70 mm. Lines with the default lineweight print at this width; lines with their own lineweight (layer or object) print at that. Click Download PDF. The browser downloads a PDF named after the drawing and the sheet, for example Drawing1-A-101 Plan.pdf. The history reports its size in bytes and its paper size in millimeters. A sheet always plots at 1:1 on its own paper. To change the paper, change it on the sheet itself (see Set up a sheet). Layers set not to plot are left out, and so are viewport frames. Every sheet in one PDF With two or more sheets, the Plot to PDF dialog has All sheets in one PDF; or type PUBLISH. Enter takes every sheet; S (Some) asks for their names, separated by commas. One PDF downloads with a page per sheet, in tab order, each at its own paper size, and a bookmark per sheet with its name. It uses the ink and pen width you last chose in the dialog. Plot the model Click the Model tab. Press Ctrl+P. The dialog says it plots the model onto a sheet of paper, and shows more settings: In Area, choose what to plot: Everything (extents): the whole drawing. What is on screen: the area you see in the drawing now. A window…: then click Pick, click two corners on the drawing, and the dialog comes back. In Paper, choose a size: ISO A4 to A0, Letter, Legal, Tabloid and ANSI C and D, or ARCH C, D and E. In Orientation, choose Landscape or Portrait. In Scale, choose Fit to paper or a scale such as 1:100. Under the settings, a line reports the scale and how big the area comes out on the paper. If it is bigger than the paper, it says so: the edges are cut off. Choose a smaller scale, a bigger paper, or Fit to paper. Click Download PDF. A model plot leaves a 5 mm margin round the edge, because most printers cannot print to the edge. Fit to paper gives an odd scale (the history and the info line say \"(fitted)\"); choose a standard scale when the drawing must measure true. Scales read in real units. For 1:100 to be right, set Units in Current settings to what one drawing unit is. A unitless drawing is taken to be in millimeters. Print In the dialog, click Open to print. The PDF opens in a new browser tab. Print from that tab with the browser's print command (Ctrl+P there). In the printer settings, choose \"Actual size\" or 100% (not \"Fit\") to keep the scale. Safari may show the PDF and wait for you to use File, then Print. If your browser blocks the new tab, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead and says so in the history. Allow pop-ups for the site to open it in a tab next time. Settings that are remembered The dialog remembers your last paper, orientation, scale, ink and line weight in this browser. A picked window is kept until you reload the page; after that the dialog starts at Everything (extents). Common mistakes The PDF is blank or the buttons are grey. There is nothing to plot (an empty model), or A window… is chosen and no window is picked yet (\"Pick the window to plot.\"). Text shows \"?\" in the PDF. The PDF uses the built-in Helvetica font, which only has Western European characters. The history counts how many characters were replaced. All lines are the same thickness. Their lineweight is the default: give layers or objects a Lineweight in the right panel (see Line weights and linetypes). Construction lines printed. Set their layer not to plot: click its printer button in the Layers panel. The printout is the wrong size. The printer scaled it. Print at actual size. Ctrl+P opened the browser's print dialog. The cursor was in a box in the right panel, or another dialog was open. Press Enter or Esc to leave the box, then try again, or click Plot. Ctrl+P did nothing. A command is running. Press Esc first."},{"slug":"open-dwg-save-dxf","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Open a DWG, edit it and save a copy","summary":"Open a drawing someone sent you, check what came in, make a change, and send back a copy as DWG or DXF.","section":"tasks","order":140,"keywords":["dwg","dxf","open","import","export","save","r12","round trip","consultant","xref","check drawing","saveas","save as","dwg save","format"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-do","text":"What you will do","level":2},{"id":"open-the-file","text":"Open the file","level":2},{"id":"check-what-came-in","text":"Check what came in","level":2},{"id":"make-your-change","text":"Make your change","level":2},{"id":"plot-a-sheet-for-review","text":"Plot a sheet for review","level":2},{"id":"save-a-copy","text":"Save a copy","level":2},{"id":"save-as-dwg-or-dxf","text":"Save as DWG or DXF","level":2},{"id":"what-the-other-office-will-see","text":"What the other office will see","level":2},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-do\"\u003EWhat you will do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen a DWG from a consultant, check what CadCraft could and could not show, add a note, plot a sheet to PDF and save a copy to send back, as DWG or DXF.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-file\"\u003EOpen the file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status line shows &quot;Reading …&quot; while the drawing is read. When it is done, the drawing fills the screen, and the history says, for example, &quot;Opened plan.dwg: 18,240 objects on 64 layers in 2310 ms.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline. If the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E button reads &quot;Offline ready&quot;, DWG files open normally. If not, and no DWG has been opened in this tab yet, opening a DWG can fail because CadCraft&#039;s DWG reader is fetched with the first DWG: open a DXF instead, or save CadCraft for offline use next time you are online. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-what-came-in\"\u003ECheck what came in\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELook at the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E section of the left panel: format and version, units, number of objects and layers, and sheets.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the &quot;About this file&quot; box under it, if there is one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe box lists:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot shown yet:\u003C/strong\u003E objects CadCraft cannot draw yet, by kind and count. They are left out, not guessed at.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELayouts (sheets) came in:\u003C/strong\u003E open them with the tabs under the drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwisted viewports:\u003C/strong\u003E shown square to the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESplines converted to polylines\u003C/strong\u003E, and dimensions that could only be read as drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick through the tabs under the drawing to see each sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECheck \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E (nothing selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab). If it says \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E but you know the drawing is in millimeters, set it, so that sheet and plot scales read right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you expected a part of the drawing and cannot see it, check the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel: the layer may be off or frozen in the file. Click its name to make it current; that turns it on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"make-your-change\"\u003EMake your change\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMake a layer for your notes: click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew layer\u003C/span\u003E, type \u003Ccode\u003ENOTES\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick where the note goes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a text height that suits the drawing (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to keep the rotation at 0.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType your note and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to finish.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEdits to imported dimensions are fine: as soon as you change one, CadCraft redraws it in its own style.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"plot-a-sheet-for-review\"\u003EPlot a sheet for review\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the sheet&#039;s tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDownload PDF\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-a-copy\"\u003ESave a copy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou opened a DWG, so the browser downloads \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;name&gt;.dwg\u003C/code\u003E, in the version the DWG came in. The history says, for example, &quot;Saved a copy as Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)).&quot; and lists, on the lines under it, anything that changed on the way.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead those lines before you send the file. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\"\u003EWhat a DWG keeps and changes\u003C/a\u003E explains each one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYour original DWG is never changed. Every save is a new download.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-as-dwg-or-dxf\"\u003ESave as DWG or DXF\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETo choose the format yourself:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy as\u003C/span\u003E dialog opens with three choices, each with the file name it will give: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E, with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E list, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E. The drawing&#039;s current format and version are already picked.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick the format you want. For DWG, pick the version the other office uses, or an older one: AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy downloads. From now on, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E saves this drawing in that format, and new drawings start with it too, in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E saves nothing, and the history says &quot;Not saved.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhich one to pick:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDWG\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the most: sheets with their viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, layer and block names as you typed them, and multi-line text as multi-line text. The AutoCAD 2000 version opens in the most programs; 2004 and later also keep true colors.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/strong\u003E is a text file that keeps almost what a DWG keeps: every sheet as a layout with its viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, true colours, lineweights, linetypes, and names as you typed them. It opens in AutoCAD 2000 and later and most CAD programs. Multileaders become the lines, arrowheads and text they draw. A DXF opened in version 2000 or later saves as DXF 2000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDXF R12\u003C/strong\u003E opens in almost any CAD program. It keeps the first sheet with its viewports, and CadCraft&#039;s other sheets. It simplifies newer things, such as multi-line text and hatches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-other-office-will-see\"\u003EWhat the other office will see\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a DWG:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, arcs, circles, ellipses, polylines, text, multi-line text, blocks with their attributes, solid fills, hatch patterns and layers as you see them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EReal dimensions: the other program re-measures them when they are stretched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery sheet, with its paper and its viewports.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMultileaders as multileaders. In the AutoCAD 2000 version, true colors become the nearest standard color.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a DXF R12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELines, arcs, circles, polylines, text, block references and dimensions as you see them. Dimensions stay real dimensions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELayer names in capitals, with spaces turned into \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe first sheet as the drawing&#039;s paper space, with its viewports. Further sheets arrive as blocks named \u003Ccode\u003ECCLAYOUT_1\u003C/code\u003E and so on; if they need every sheet as a layout, plot PDFs of each sheet for them as well.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMulti-line text as separate lines, ellipses as polylines, hatches as outlines.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEither way, anything listed as &quot;Not shown yet&quot; when you opened the DWG is \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E in the copy. Tell them, and send a PDF as well.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\"\u003ECommon mistakes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The file has another extension. Check it is a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E The DWG is damaged or in a version the reader does not handle. Ask for a DXF, or for the DWG saved in an older version.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF.&quot;\u003C/strong\u003E Ask for, or save, a text (ASCII) DXF.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe saved file is called \u003Ccode\u003Ename (1).dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Ename (1).dxf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/strong\u003E The browser does that when a file with the same name is already in the folder.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThey have an older program that cannot open the DWG.\u003C/strong\u003E Save as DWG with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAutoCAD 2000\u003C/span\u003E, or as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E: almost any CAD program opens it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EParts of the drawing are missing.\u003C/strong\u003E Linked drawings (xrefs) are not loaded until you pick their files (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E), and pictures show as frames with their file names.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will do Open a DWG from a consultant, check what CadCraft could and could not show, add a note, plot a sheet to PDF and save a copy to send back, as DWG or DXF. Open the file Press Ctrl+O, or click Open. Choose the .dwg file. The status line shows \"Reading …\" while the drawing is read. When it is done, the drawing fills the screen, and the history says, for example, \"Opened plan.dwg: 18,240 objects on 64 layers in 2310 ms.\" You are offline. If the Save for offline button reads \"Offline ready\", DWG files open normally. If not, and no DWG has been opened in this tab yet, opening a DWG can fail because CadCraft's DWG reader is fetched with the first DWG: open a DXF instead, or save CadCraft for offline use next time you are online. See Working offline. Check what came in Look at the Drawing section of the left panel: format and version, units, number of objects and layers, and sheets. Read the \"About this file\" box under it, if there is one. The box lists: Not shown yet: objects CadCraft cannot draw yet, by kind and count. They are left out, not guessed at. Layouts (sheets) came in: open them with the tabs under the drawing. Twisted viewports: shown square to the sheet. Splines converted to polylines, and dimensions that could only be read as drawn. Click through the tabs under the drawing to see each sheet. Check Units in Current settings (nothing selected, Model tab). If it says Unitless but you know the drawing is in millimeters, set it, so that sheet and plot scales read right. If you expected a part of the drawing and cannot see it, check the Layers panel: the layer may be off or frozen in the file. Click its name to make it current; that turns it on. Make your change Make a layer for your notes: click New layer, type NOTES and press Enter. Type DT and press Enter. Click where the note goes. Type a text height that suits the drawing (see Add text and labels) and press Enter. Press Enter to keep the rotation at 0. Type your note and press Enter. Press Enter again to finish. Edits to imported dimensions are fine: as soon as you change one, CadCraft redraws it in its own style. Plot a sheet for review Click the sheet's tab. Press Ctrl+P. Click Download PDF. See Plot to PDF. Save a copy Press Ctrl+S, or click Save. You opened a DWG, so the browser downloads \u003Cname\u003E.dwg, in the version the DWG came in. The history says, for example, \"Saved a copy as Plan.dwg (DWG (AutoCAD 2018)).\" and lists, on the lines under it, anything that changed on the way. Read those lines before you send the file. What a DWG keeps and changes explains each one. Your original DWG is never changed. Every save is a new download. Save as DWG or DXF To choose the format yourself: Press Ctrl+Shift+S, or click Save as. The Save a copy as dialog opens with three choices, each with the file name it will give: DWG, with its Version list, DXF R12 and DXF 2000. The drawing's current format and version are already picked. Click the format you want. For DWG, pick the version the other office uses, or an older one: AutoCAD 2018, 2013, 2010, 2007, 2004 or 2000. Click Save. The copy downloads. From now on, Ctrl+S saves this drawing in that format, and new drawings start with it too, in this browser. Cancel or Esc saves nothing, and the history says \"Not saved.\" Which one to pick: DWG keeps the most: sheets with their viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, layer and block names as you typed them, and multi-line text as multi-line text. The AutoCAD 2000 version opens in the most programs; 2004 and later also keep true colors. DXF 2000 is a text file that keeps almost what a DWG keeps: every sheet as a layout with its viewports, real dimensions, hatch patterns, true colours, lineweights, linetypes, and names as you typed them. It opens in AutoCAD 2000 and later and most CAD programs. Multileaders become the lines, arrowheads and text they draw. A DXF opened in version 2000 or later saves as DXF 2000. DXF R12 opens in almost any CAD program. It keeps the first sheet with its viewports, and CadCraft's other sheets. It simplifies newer things, such as multi-line text and hatches. What the other office will see In a DWG: Lines, arcs, circles, ellipses, polylines, text, multi-line text, blocks with their attributes, solid fills, hatch patterns and layers as you see them. Real dimensions: the other program re-measures them when they are stretched. Every sheet, with its paper and its viewports. Multileaders as multileaders. In the AutoCAD 2000 version, true colors become the nearest standard color. In a DXF R12: Lines, arcs, circles, polylines, text, block references and dimensions as you see them. Dimensions stay real dimensions. Layer names in capitals, with spaces turned into _. The first sheet as the drawing's paper space, with its viewports. Further sheets arrive as blocks named CCLAYOUT_1 and so on; if they need every sheet as a layout, plot PDFs of each sheet for them as well. Multi-line text as separate lines, ellipses as polylines, hatches as outlines. Either way, anything listed as \"Not shown yet\" when you opened the DWG is not in the copy. Tell them, and send a PDF as well. Common mistakes \"CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings.\" The file has another extension. Check it is a .dwg or .dxf. \"This drawing could not be read.\" The DWG is damaged or in a version the reader does not handle. Ask for a DXF, or for the DWG saved in an older version. \"This is a binary DXF.\" Ask for, or save, a text (ASCII) DXF. The saved file is called name (1).dwg or name (1).dxf. The browser does that when a file with the same name is already in the folder. They have an older program that cannot open the DWG. Save as DWG with Version AutoCAD 2000, or as DXF R12: almost any CAD program opens it. Parts of the drawing are missing. Linked drawings (xrefs) are not loaded until you pick their files (XREF, R), and pictures show as frames with their file names."},{"slug":"coming-from","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Coming from other drafting software","summary":"How familiar commands, keys and habits map to CadCraft, and the differences that trip up experienced drafters.","section":"switching","order":10,"keywords":["switching","differences","migrate","habits","aliases","dynamic input","pickfirst","pickadd","qsave","saveas","save as","mtext","ctb","polar tracking","xref","keyboard shortcuts","familiar","copyclip","pasteclip","pasteorig","copybase","clipboard"],"headings":[{"id":"what-maps-directly","text":"What maps directly","level":2},{"id":"differences-that-trip-people-up","text":"Differences that trip people up","level":2},{"id":"typed-points-are-absolute","text":"Typed points are absolute","level":3},{"id":"shift-skips-snaps-it-does-not-give-ortho","text":"Shift skips snaps; it does not give Ortho","level":3},{"id":"right-click-is-always-enter","text":"Right-click is always Enter","level":3},{"id":"no-space-bar-panning","text":"No Space-bar panning","level":3},{"id":"trim-and-extend","text":"Trim and Extend","level":3},{"id":"fillet-and-chamfer","text":"Fillet and chamfer","level":3},{"id":"paragraph-text-and-single-line-text","text":"Paragraph text and single-line text","level":3},{"id":"save-means-download-a-copy","text":"Save means \"download a copy\"","level":3},{"id":"layers-simpler-and-not-undone","text":"Layers: simpler, and not undone","level":3},{"id":"dimension-styles-are-per-dimension","text":"Dimension styles are per dimension","level":3},{"id":"plotting-is-simpler","text":"Plotting is simpler","level":3},{"id":"viewports-are-not-entered","text":"Viewports are not entered","level":3},{"id":"tracking-and-dynamic-input-yes-grid-snap-not-yet","text":"Tracking and dynamic input yes, grid snap not yet","level":3},{"id":"feet-and-inches","text":"Feet and inches","level":3},{"id":"copy-and-paste-stays-inside-cadcraft","text":"Copy and paste stays inside CadCraft","level":3},{"id":"browser-keys-you-cannot-use","text":"Browser keys you cannot use","level":3},{"id":"commands-you-may-reach-for-that-are-not-here-yet","text":"Commands you may reach for that are not here yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EIf you have used AutoCAD, or a program built on the same model, most of what you know works here: the command line, aliases such as \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, Enter or Space to repeat, window and crossing selection, object snaps on F3, Ortho on F8, model space with layouts and viewports. This page lists what maps directly, and then the differences that catch people out. Read the second half even if you skim the first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-maps-directly\"\u003EWhat maps directly\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EHabit\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EIn CadCraft\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPOL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame commands, same aliases\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame dimension commands\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMTXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMASZ\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMDEC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMGAP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMEXE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMEXO\u003C/code\u003E typed at the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame, for new dimensions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E to make a block, \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E to insert, \u003Ccode\u003EPU\u003C/code\u003E to purge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame (purge removes unused blocks only)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPLOT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping \u003Ccode\u003E_LINE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E-LAYER\u003C/code\u003E from a script or a habit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe leading \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is ignored\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter or Space on an empty prompt repeats the last command\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEsc cancels; Esc again clears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@d&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E, typed angles counterclockwise from east\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow left to right, crossing right to left, click-click; press and drag for a lasso, Space cycles window / crossing / fence (LASSOMODE)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect first, then the command (noun-verb)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame; a finished command clears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E at Select objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift+click removes from the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame. Settings can make a click replace the selection instead, as PICKADD 0 does: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+Shift+V (COPYCLIP, CUTCLIP, PASTECLIP, PASTEORIG), COPYBASE\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame commands and keys; pasting places the copy by its lower-left corner, as PASTECLIP does\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF3 object snap, F7 grid, F8 Ortho\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame keys\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click the wheel to zoom extents\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMirrored text stays readable\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame (as with MIRRTEXT 0)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+A\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame; on a Mac, Command works too\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\"\u003ECommand reference\u003C/a\u003E lists every command and alias.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"differences-that-trip-people-up\"\u003EDifferences that trip people up\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"typed-points-are-absolute\"\u003ETyped points are absolute\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith dynamic input on, the other program treats a typed \u003Ccode\u003E100,0\u003C/code\u003E for a second point as relative. \u003Cstrong\u003ECadCraft&#039;s command line does not:\u003C/strong\u003E there, \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E is always measured from the origin. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) on, use its fields instead: distance, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, angle. Use \u003Ccode\u003E@100,0\u003C/code\u003E for &quot;100 to the right of the last point&quot;, or move the crosshair the right way and type a bare \u003Ccode\u003E100\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"shift-skips-snaps-it-does-not-give-ortho\"\u003EShift skips snaps; it does not give Ortho\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHolding Shift while you click ignores object snaps for that click. It does not temporarily switch Ortho. Use F8 or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"right-click-is-always-enter\"\u003ERight-click is always Enter\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no right-click menu. A right-click in the drawing is Enter: it ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command. During a grip move or a selection box, it cancels instead. Drag with the right button to pan.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"no-space-bar-panning\"\u003ENo Space-bar panning\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESpace is Enter, so holding Space and dragging does not pan. Pan with the middle button or the right button, or zoom out and back in at another spot with the wheel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and Extend\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETrim and Extend start in Quick mode: every visible object is a cutting edge or a boundary, the piece under the pointer shows what will go, a dragged path trims every piece it crosses, and an object nothing crosses is erased. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E switches between trim and extend. \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E switches to Standard mode (choose the edges first), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E are fence and crossing, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E is EDGEMODE, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E erases. There is no \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Project) option: CadCraft trims in the plane of the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fillet-and-chamfer\"\u003EFillet and chamfer\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/code\u003E take the options you know: Undo, Polyline, Radius (or Distance, Angle and mEthod), Trim and Multiple, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click for a sharp corner. A fillet between a polyline and a line adds the arc as its own object instead of joining them into the polyline, and polyline corners next to an arc segment are left as they are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"paragraph-text-and-single-line-text\"\u003EParagraph text and single-line text\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EMT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EMTEXT\u003C/code\u003E are paragraph text typed in place, with a width, line spacing and justification; \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EDTEXT\u003C/code\u003E are single-line text, as you know them. There are no text styles, columns or in-text formatting (bold, stacked fractions); everything draws in a Helvetica-style font.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"save-means-download-a-copy\"\u003ESave means &quot;download a copy&quot;\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EQSAVE\u003C/code\u003E download a copy in the drawing&#039;s format: a DWG you opened saves as DWG, a DXF as DXF. Nothing is overwritten in place, and opened files are never changed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) is a small dialog, not a file browser: pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E (with its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E, AutoCAD 2000 to 2018) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The browser decides where the download goes. \u003Ccode\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/code\u003E always downloads DXF R12.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA DWG you opened saves in the version it came in; a new drawing saves in the AutoCAD 2000 format unless you pick another. Sheets, dimensions and hatch patterns are kept. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#saving\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"saving\"\u003EFiles: what opens, what saves\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layers-simpler-and-not-undone\"\u003ELayers: simpler, and not undone\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEvery layer change (on, freeze, lock, color, plot, linetype, lineweight, current layer) is one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOff and freeze have the same effect in CadCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThere is no layer properties manager and no layer states; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E rename, merge and delete layers. Linetypes and lineweights are set in the right panel (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/a\u003E); \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E work by picking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving to DXF R12 writes layer names in capitals. A DWG keeps them as you typed them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimension-styles-are-per-dimension\"\u003EDimension styles are per dimension\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are no named dimension styles to manage. \u003Ccode\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E and the other settings change what new dimensions use; each dimension keeps its own sizes, which you change in the Properties panel. Dimensions follow the drawing&#039;s length format (decimal, or feet and inches with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E), or their own \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFormat\u003C/span\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plotting-is-simpler\"\u003EPlotting is simpler\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere are no plot style tables (CTB or STB) and no page setups. A plot is black or in drawing colors; lines print at their lineweight, the others at one pen width. Sheets plot at 1:1 on their own paper; the model plots at a chosen scale or fitted. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"viewports-are-not-entered\"\u003EViewports are not entered\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDouble-clicking inside a viewport does not take you into model space through it. To edit the model, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab. To change what a viewport shows, select its frame and use the Properties panel or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"tracking-and-dynamic-input-yes-grid-snap-not-yet\"\u003ETracking and dynamic input yes, grid snap not yet\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe grid is for looking at only; points do not snap to it (there is no F9 snap mode). Polar tracking (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, step set with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/code\u003E) and object snap tracking (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: rest on a snap point to acquire it) work as you know them; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/object-snaps#polar-tracking\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/object-snaps\" data-hash=\"polar-tracking\"\u003ESnap to objects\u003C/a\u003E. Dynamic input (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) shows distance and angle (or X and Y) by the crosshair; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry#dynamic-input\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-hash=\"dynamic-input\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"feet-and-inches\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/span\u003E (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E), and type lengths as you know them: \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E. Use a hyphen, not a space, before a fraction: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E too, so that sheets and plots offer architectural scales. The format is not yet kept in saved files. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copy-and-paste-stays-inside-cadcraft\"\u003ECopy and paste stays inside CadCraft\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copy, cut and paste objects within a drawing, between drawings, and between CadCraft tabs in the same browser. The clipboard is CadCraft&#039;s own: you cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or objects from another program into CadCraft. Pasting from a drawing in other units does not rescale: use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/code\u003E afterwards. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is a duplicate that moves with the pointer, a CadCraft addition. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"browser-keys-you-cannot-use\"\u003EBrowser keys you cannot use\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E from the browser: they open and close windows and tabs. Use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E button or type \u003Ccode\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"commands-you-may-reach-for-that-are-not-here-yet\"\u003ECommands you may reach for that are not here yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETABLE\u003C/code\u003E are not built. Typing one says &quot;Unknown command&quot;. \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/a\u003E lists what to do instead today.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"If you have used AutoCAD, or a program built on the same model, most of what you know works here: the command line, aliases such as L, C, TR and O, Enter or Space to repeat, window and crossing selection, object snaps on F3, Ortho on F8, model space with layouts and viewports. This page lists what maps directly, and then the differences that catch people out. Read the second half even if you skim the first. What maps directly Habit In CadCraft L, PL, C, A, REC, POL, EL, PO, T, DT Same commands, same aliases M, CO, RO, SC, MI, O, TR, EX, F, X, E Same DLI, DAL, DAN, DRA, DDI, DCO, DBA Same dimension commands DIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO typed at the command line Same, for new dimensions B to make a block, I to insert, PU to purge Same (purge removes unused blocks only) LAYOUT, MV, PLOT, DI, ID, LI, Z then E or W Same Typing _LINE or -LAYER from a script or a habit The leading _, -, . or ' is ignored Enter or Space on an empty prompt repeats the last command Same Esc cancels; Esc again clears the selection Same @dx,dy, @d\u003Ca, typed angles counterclockwise from east Same Window left to right, crossing right to left, click-click; press and drag for a lasso, Space cycles window / crossing / fence (LASSOMODE) Same Select first, then the command (noun-verb) Same; a finished command clears the selection ALL and L at Select objects Same Shift+click removes from the selection Same. Settings can make a click replace the selection instead, as PICKADD 0 does: see CadCraft settings Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X, Ctrl+V, Ctrl+Shift+V (COPYCLIP, CUTCLIP, PASTECLIP, PASTEORIG), COPYBASE Same commands and keys; pasting places the copy by its lower-left corner, as PASTECLIP does F3 object snap, F7 grid, F8 Ortho Same keys Double-click the wheel to zoom extents Same Mirrored text stays readable Same (as with MIRRTEXT 0) Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+S, Ctrl+O, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+A Same; on a Mac, Command works too The Command reference lists every command and alias. Differences that trip people up Typed points are absolute With dynamic input on, the other program treats a typed 100,0 for a second point as relative. CadCraft's command line does not: there, x,y is always measured from the origin. With Dyn (F12) on, use its fields instead: distance, Tab, angle. Use @100,0 for \"100 to the right of the last point\", or move the crosshair the right way and type a bare 100. Shift skips snaps; it does not give Ortho Holding Shift while you click ignores object snaps for that click. It does not temporarily switch Ortho. Use F8 or the Ortho button. Right-click is always Enter There is no right-click menu. A right-click in the drawing is Enter: it ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command. During a grip move or a selection box, it cancels instead. Drag with the right button to pan. No Space-bar panning Space is Enter, so holding Space and dragging does not pan. Pan with the middle button or the right button, or zoom out and back in at another spot with the wheel. Trim and Extend Trim and Extend start in Quick mode: every visible object is a cutting edge or a boundary, the piece under the pointer shows what will go, a dragged path trims every piece it crosses, and an object nothing crosses is erased. Shift switches between trim and extend. O switches to Standard mode (choose the edges first), F and C are fence and crossing, E is EDGEMODE, R erases. There is no P (Project) option: CadCraft trims in the plane of the drawing. Fillet and chamfer FILLET and CHAMFER take the options you know: Undo, Polyline, Radius (or Distance, Angle and mEthod), Trim and Multiple, and Shift+click for a sharp corner. A fillet between a polyline and a line adds the arc as its own object instead of joining them into the polyline, and polyline corners next to an arc segment are left as they are. Paragraph text and single-line text T, MT and MTEXT are paragraph text typed in place, with a width, line spacing and justification; DT and DTEXT are single-line text, as you know them. There are no text styles, columns or in-text formatting (bold, stacked fractions); everything draws in a Helvetica-style font. Save means \"download a copy\" Ctrl+S, SAVE and QSAVE download a copy in the drawing's format: a DWG you opened saves as DWG, a DXF as DXF. Nothing is overwritten in place, and opened files are never changed. SAVEAS (Ctrl+Shift+S) is a small dialog, not a file browser: pick DWG (with its Version, AutoCAD 2000 to 2018) or DXF R12 and click Save. The browser decides where the download goes. DXFOUT always downloads DXF R12. A DWG you opened saves in the version it came in; a new drawing saves in the AutoCAD 2000 format unless you pick another. Sheets, dimensions and hatch patterns are kept. See Files: what opens, what saves. Layers: simpler, and not undone Every layer change (on, freeze, lock, color, plot, linetype, lineweight, current layer) is one undo step. Off and freeze have the same effect in CadCraft. There is no layer properties manager and no layer states; RENAME, LAYMRG and LAYDEL rename, merge and delete layers. Linetypes and lineweights are set in the right panel (see Line weights and linetypes); LAYISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ and LAYMCUR work by picking. Saving to DXF R12 writes layer names in capitals. A DWG keeps them as you typed them. Dimension styles are per dimension There are no named dimension styles to manage. DIMSCALE and the other settings change what new dimensions use; each dimension keeps its own sizes, which you change in the Properties panel. Dimensions follow the drawing's length format (decimal, or feet and inches with UNITS), or their own Format. See Set dimension sizes. Plotting is simpler There are no plot style tables (CTB or STB) and no page setups. A plot is black or in drawing colors; lines print at their lineweight, the others at one pen width. Sheets plot at 1:1 on their own paper; the model plots at a chosen scale or fitted. See Plot to PDF. Viewports are not entered Double-clicking inside a viewport does not take you into model space through it. To edit the model, use the Model tab. To change what a viewport shows, select its frame and use the Properties panel or VPVIEW. Tracking and dynamic input yes, grid snap not yet The grid is for looking at only; points do not snap to it (there is no F9 snap mode). Polar tracking (F10, step set with POLARANG) and object snap tracking (F11: rest on a snap point to acquire it) work as you know them; see Snap to objects. Dynamic input (F12) shows distance and angle (or X and Y) by the crosshair; see Coordinate and distance entry. Feet and inches Choose Length format Architectural or Engineering (or type UNITS), and type lengths as you know them: 12'6\", 12'6-1/2\". Use a hyphen, not a space, before a fraction: Space is Enter. Set Units to Inches too, so that sheets and plots offer architectural scales. The format is not yet kept in saved files. See Feet and inches. Copy and paste stays inside CadCraft Ctrl+C, Ctrl+X and Ctrl+V copy, cut and paste objects within a drawing, between drawings, and between CadCraft tabs in the same browser. The clipboard is CadCraft's own: you cannot paste CadCraft objects into another program, or objects from another program into CadCraft. Pasting from a drawing in other units does not rescale: use SCALE afterwards. Ctrl+D is a duplicate that moves with the pointer, a CadCraft addition. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Browser keys you cannot use A web page cannot take Ctrl+N, Ctrl+W or Ctrl+T from the browser: they open and close windows and tabs. Use the New button or type NEW. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Commands you may reach for that are not here yet PEDIT, XREF and TABLE are not built. Typing one says \"Unknown command\". Not built yet lists what to do instead today."},{"slug":"settings","scope":"cadcraft","title":"CadCraft settings","summary":"The settings that belong to the drafting app: cursor size, where a pasted copy hangs from the cursor, what a click does to the selection, and the corner indicator.","section":"reference","order":5,"keywords":["settings","preferences","options","corner indicator","view cube","axis icon","ucs icon","cursor","crosshair","pick box","paste point","base point","clicking objects","selection","pickadd","shift","reset"],"headings":[{"id":"cursor","text":"Cursor","level":2},{"id":"paste-point","text":"Paste point","level":2},{"id":"clicking-objects","text":"Clicking objects","level":2},{"id":"dragging-to-select","text":"Dragging to select","level":2},{"id":"corner-indicator","text":"Corner indicator","level":2},{"id":"grid-ortho-and-snaps","text":"Grid, Ortho and snaps","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E with the gear in the app bar or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command ,\"\u003E⌘,\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+,\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. The first tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E, holds the settings that belong to this app; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E holds the ones every app in the family shares (theme, panel text size and widths, the toolbar&#039;s look and order). CadCraft&#039;s settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset CadCraft settings\u003C/span\u003E puts every one back to the start.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cursor\"\u003ECursor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESmall\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EMedium\u003C/strong\u003E (the start) or \u003Cstrong\u003ELarge\u003C/strong\u003E: the size of the crosshair and of the pick box in the drawing. A bigger pick box also picks objects from a little farther away, which helps on a touch screen or a very fine drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paste-point\"\u003EPaste point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere a pasted copy hangs from the cursor after \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/code\u003E), until you click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELower-left corner\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): the lower-left corner of the copied objects&#039; extents, as in AutoCAD.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECenter\u003C/strong\u003E: the middle of their extents. Handy for placing a symbol by eye.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA copy made with \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E always hangs from the base point you picked. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) ignores this setting: it keeps the original coordinates. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clicking-objects\"\u003EClicking objects\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat a click in the drawing does to the selection when no command is running:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAdds to the selection\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): each click or window adds objects; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click, or a \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+window, takes them out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EReplaces the selection\u003C/strong\u003E: a click or window selects only what it picks. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click adds an object, or takes out one that is already selected; a \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+window adds.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects:\u003C/code\u003E prompt inside a command, clicks always add (and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E removes), whatever this setting says. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/select-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/select-objects\"\u003ESelect objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dragging-to-select\"\u003EDragging to select\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELasso\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): a freehand outline. Moving right first selects what lies wholly inside it, left first what it touches; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging switches between window, crossing and fence.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERectangle\u003C/strong\u003E: the box, as a click and a second click draw it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E1\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E0\u003C/code\u003E sets the same. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"corner-indicator\"\u003ECorner indicator\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat sits in the corner of the drawing:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAutomatic\u003C/strong\u003E (the start): the axis icon on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab and the view cube on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAxis icon\u003C/strong\u003E: the X and Y marker at the bottom left of the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, and a small X, Y and Z marker on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EView cube\u003C/strong\u003E: a cube at the top right of both tabs. On the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab it shows the drawing from the top, and clicking another face, edge or corner opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E3D\u003C/span\u003E tab looking from there. On the 3D tab it turns with the view: click to look from a side, drag to orbit, and the house goes back to the iso view. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\"\u003EModel in 3D\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENone\u003C/strong\u003E: nothing in the corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets show neither.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"grid-ortho-and-snaps\"\u003EGrid, Ortho and snaps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are not in the dialog: the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E buttons in the status bar (or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F7\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) and the list of object snaps beside them are remembered as you leave them. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/object-snaps\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/object-snaps\"\u003ESnap to points on objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Open Settings with the gear in the app bar or Ctrl+,. The first tab, CadCraft, holds the settings that belong to this app; General holds the ones every app in the family shares (theme, panel text size and widths, the toolbar's look and order). CadCraft's settings exist only in the app: you change them here, and this browser remembers them. Reset CadCraft settings puts every one back to the start. Cursor Small, Medium (the start) or Large: the size of the crosshair and of the pick box in the drawing. A bigger pick box also picks objects from a little farther away, which helps on a touch screen or a very fine drawing. Paste point Where a pasted copy hangs from the cursor after Ctrl+V (PASTECLIP), until you click: Lower-left corner (the start): the lower-left corner of the copied objects' extents, as in AutoCAD. Center: the middle of their extents. Handy for placing a symbol by eye. A copy made with COPYBASE always hangs from the base point you picked. Paste in place (Ctrl+Shift+V) ignores this setting: it keeps the original coordinates. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Clicking objects What a click in the drawing does to the selection when no command is running: Adds to the selection (the start): each click or window adds objects; Shift+click, or a Shift+window, takes them out. Replaces the selection: a click or window selects only what it picks. Shift+click adds an object, or takes out one that is already selected; a Shift+window adds. At a Select objects: prompt inside a command, clicks always add (and Shift removes), whatever this setting says. See Select objects. Dragging to select What pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws: Lasso (the start): a freehand outline. Moving right first selects what lies wholly inside it, left first what it touches; Space while dragging switches between window, crossing and fence. Rectangle: the box, as a click and a second click draw it. LASSOMODE 1 or 0 sets the same. See Lasso selection. Corner indicator What sits in the corner of the drawing: Automatic (the start): the axis icon on the Model tab and the view cube on the 3D tab. Axis icon: the X and Y marker at the bottom left of the Model tab, and a small X, Y and Z marker on the 3D tab. View cube: a cube at the top right of both tabs. On the Model tab it shows the drawing from the top, and clicking another face, edge or corner opens the 3D tab looking from there. On the 3D tab it turns with the view: click to look from a side, drag to orbit, and the house goes back to the iso view. See Model in 3D. None: nothing in the corner. Sheets show neither. Grid, Ortho and snaps These are not in the dialog: the Grid, Ortho and Snap buttons in the status bar (or F7, F8, F3) and the list of object snaps beside them are remembered as you leave them. See Snap to points on objects."},{"slug":"keyboard","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Keyboard and mouse","summary":"Every key, mouse action and command-line key in the CadCraft drafting app, as the code handles them.","section":"reference","order":10,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcuts","keys","mouse","wheel","pan","zoom","right click","middle button","f3","f7","f8","ctrl","command key","hotkeys","copy","cut","paste","duplicate","clipboard","select all"],"headings":[{"id":"typing-commands","text":"Typing commands","level":2},{"id":"drawing-and-editing","text":"Drawing and editing","level":2},{"id":"copy-cut-and-paste","text":"Copy, cut and paste","level":2},{"id":"toggles","text":"Toggles","level":2},{"id":"help","text":"Help","level":2},{"id":"screen","text":"Screen","level":2},{"id":"dialogs","text":"Dialogs","level":2},{"id":"panel-fields","text":"Panel fields","level":2},{"id":"mouse","text":"Mouse","level":2},{"id":"keys-the-browser-keeps","text":"Keys the browser keeps","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts are shown for your computer. On a Mac, every \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command\"\u003E⌘\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E shortcut below uses the Command key, and the Control key works too. How keys are written, and how to use F-keys on a Mac, is in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-commands\"\u003ETyping commands\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAny letter, digit or symbol\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGoes to the command line, wherever the pointer is, unless a box in a panel has the cursor\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESends what you typed. On an empty prompt: repeats the last command, ends a command, finishes a selection, or accepts the default in \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;…&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESame as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, except while CadCraft asks for text (text, block names, dimension text), where it types a space\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECancels the command. With no command running: cancels a grip move or a selection box, then clears the selection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAccepts the suggested command shown to the right of the command line. With nothing typed and the pointer over overlapping objects: highlights the next one there (click to pick it)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↑\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↑\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"↓\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E↓\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESteps through the lines you typed before (the last 100)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-and-editing\"\u003EDrawing and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo the last command\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERedo\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect every object you can edit on the open tab (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESELECTALL\u003C/code\u003E). If you have typed something at the command line, it selects that text instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase the selected objects (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EERASE\u003C/code\u003E), unless you have typed something at the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EErase the selected objects when the command line does not have the cursor; at the command line it deletes what you typed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith no command running: clear the selection (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDESELECT\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave a copy in the drawing&#039;s format (DWG or DXF)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave as: choose DWG or DXF, then save a copy\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command O\"\u003E⌘O\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+O\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen a DWG or DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUndo, redo, select all and plot do nothing while a command is running: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"copy-cut-and-paste\"\u003ECopy, cut and paste\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy the selection to the clipboard (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPYCLIP\u003C/code\u003E). Nothing selected: CadCraft asks for objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECut: copy the selection, then erase it (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECUTCLIP\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste: the copy follows the cursor; click, or type a point, to put it down (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPaste in place: at the coordinates it was copied from (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPASTEORIG\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate: a copy of the selection moves with the pointer; click to put it down, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves nothing (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDUPLICATE\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe same actions are in the toolbar&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E) and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. While another command is running, finish it or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E first; pressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again while a copy is following the cursor starts over. When you have typed something at the command line, or a command is asking for text, these keys copy, cut and paste that text instead. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"toggles\"\u003EToggles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EToggle\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStatus bar button\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F3\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF3\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObject snaps on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F7\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF7\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrid on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F8\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF8\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrtho on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F10\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF10\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPolar tracking on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F11\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObject snap tracking on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETrack\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDynamic input on or off\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 are usually Mission Control and media keys. Hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E with them, or turn on standard function keys in System Settings, as described in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E. The status bar buttons always work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EF7 in Firefox can also ask to turn on caret browsing. Choose \u003Cstrong\u003ENo\u003C/strong\u003E, and use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E button if it keeps asking.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"help\"\u003EHelp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens or closes this help, beside the drawing. It works even while a command is running, and never reaches the command line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith the cursor in the help panel: clears the search, or closes the panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button in the bar at the top does the same as \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. While you type in the help&#039;s search box, nothing reaches the command line. Click on the drawing to give the keys back to CadCraft; the panel stays open beside it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a Mac keyboard, hold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E and press F1, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"screen\"\u003EScreen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECycle normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. This uses the Control key on every computer, Mac included.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"←\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"→\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003E→\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWith a tab under the drawing focused: the previous or next tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn full screen, the browser may use the first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to leave full screen. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to cancel a command.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dialogs\"\u003EDialogs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloses the dialog (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E, or a question such as &quot;Unsaved changes&quot;, where it means \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves between the dialog&#039;s settings and buttons\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a dialog is open, the drawing&#039;s keys are off.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"panel-fields\"\u003EPanel fields\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn a box in the right or left panel:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKey\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplies the value and leaves the box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPuts the old value back and leaves the box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EApplies and moves to the next box\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile a box has the cursor, letters type into it, not into the command line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse\"\u003EMouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EAction\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPicks a point when a command asks for one; picks an object when it asks for objects; otherwise selects an object, or a grip\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShift+click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERemoves a selected object from the selection (with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClicking objects\u003C/span\u003E set to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReplaces the selection\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ESettings\u003C/a\u003E: adds it). While picking a point: ignores object snaps for that click\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on empty space, then click again\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA selection box: left to right is a window, right to left a crossing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag on empty space\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA lasso: moving right first is a window, left first a crossing; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging cycles window, crossing and fence; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E drops it (LASSOMODE 0, or Settings, makes it the rectangle again)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag through pieces in TRIM or EXTEND\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrims (or extends) every piece the path crosses; hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for the other one\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag a grip\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMoves the grip (or click the grip, then click where it goes)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelects it and puts the cursor in its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E box in the right panel\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter. During a grip move or a selection box: cancels it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the right button\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPans\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZooms in and out about the crosshair\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with the wheel (middle button)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPans\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDouble-click the wheel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZooms to the whole drawing (extents)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrop a DWG or DXF on the drawing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens it\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a trackpad or a touch screen, see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/mouse-trackpad-touch\"\u003EMouse, trackpad and touch\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EKeys the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always belong to the browser. CadCraft does not use them: use \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E or type \u003Ccode\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E for a new drawing. CadCraft&#039;s clipboard is its own: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E copy objects between CadCraft drawings and tabs, not to or from other programs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opens your browser&#039;s history instead of redoing, use \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command Z\"\u003E⇧⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E","text":"Shortcuts are shown for your computer. On a Mac, every Ctrl shortcut below uses the Command key, and the Control key works too. How keys are written, and how to use F-keys on a Mac, is in Keys, mice and trackpads. Typing commands Key What it does Any letter, digit or symbol Goes to the command line, wherever the pointer is, unless a box in a panel has the cursor Enter Sends what you typed. On an empty prompt: repeats the last command, ends a command, finishes a selection, or accepts the default in \u003C…\u003E Space Same as Enter, except while CadCraft asks for text (text, block names, dimension text), where it types a space Esc Cancels the command. With no command running: cancels a grip move or a selection box, then clears the selection Tab Accepts the suggested command shown to the right of the command line. With nothing typed and the pointer over overlapping objects: highlights the next one there (click to pick it) ↑ ↓ Steps through the lines you typed before (the last 100) Drawing and editing Key What it does Ctrl+Z Undo the last command Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z Redo Ctrl+A Select every object you can edit on the open tab (SELECTALL). If you have typed something at the command line, it selects that text instead Delete Erase the selected objects (ERASE), unless you have typed something at the command line Backspace Erase the selected objects when the command line does not have the cursor; at the command line it deletes what you typed Esc With no command running: clear the selection (DESELECT) Ctrl+S Save a copy in the drawing's format (DWG or DXF) Ctrl+Shift+S Save as: choose DWG or DXF, then save a copy Ctrl+O Open a DWG or DXF Ctrl+P Plot to PDF Undo, redo, select all and plot do nothing while a command is running: press Esc first. Copy, cut and paste Key What it does Ctrl+C Copy the selection to the clipboard (COPYCLIP). Nothing selected: CadCraft asks for objects Ctrl+X Cut: copy the selection, then erase it (CUTCLIP) Ctrl+V Paste: the copy follows the cursor; click, or type a point, to put it down (PASTECLIP) Ctrl+Shift+V Paste in place: at the coordinates it was copied from (PASTEORIG) Ctrl+D Duplicate: a copy of the selection moves with the pointer; click to put it down, Esc leaves nothing (DUPLICATE) The same actions are in the toolbar's Edit group (Copy, Paste) and its More menu. While another command is running, finish it or press Esc first; pressing Ctrl+V or Ctrl+D again while a copy is following the cursor starts over. When you have typed something at the command line, or a command is asking for text, these keys copy, cut and paste that text instead. See Copy, paste and duplicate. Toggles Key Toggle Status bar button F3 Object snaps on or off Snap F7 Grid on or off Grid F8 Ortho on or off Ortho F10 Polar tracking on or off Polar F11 Object snap tracking on or off Track F12 Dynamic input on or off Dyn On a Mac, F3, F7 and F8 are usually Mission Control and media keys. Hold fn with them, or turn on standard function keys in System Settings, as described in Keys, mice and trackpads. The status bar buttons always work. F7 in Firefox can also ask to turn on caret browsing. Choose No, and use the Grid button if it keeps asking. Help Key What it does F1 Opens or closes this help, beside the drawing. It works even while a command is running, and never reaches the command line. Esc With the cursor in the help panel: clears the search, or closes the panel The Help button in the bar at the top does the same as F1. While you type in the help's search box, nothing reaches the command line. Click on the drawing to give the keys back to CadCraft; the panel stays open beside it. On a Mac keyboard, hold fn and press F1, or click Help. Screen Key What it does Ctrl+Shift+F Cycle normal, full screen, and full screen with the bars hidden. This uses the Control key on every computer, Mac included. ← → With a tab under the drawing focused: the previous or next tab In full screen, the browser may use the first Esc to leave full screen. Press Esc again to cancel a command. Dialogs Key What it does Esc Closes the dialog (Plot to PDF, or a question such as \"Unsaved changes\", where it means Cancel) Tab Moves between the dialog's settings and buttons While a dialog is open, the drawing's keys are off. Panel fields In a box in the right or left panel: Key What it does Enter Applies the value and leaves the box Esc Puts the old value back and leaves the box Tab Applies and moves to the next box While a box has the cursor, letters type into it, not into the command line. Mouse Action What it does Click Picks a point when a command asks for one; picks an object when it asks for objects; otherwise selects an object, or a grip Shift+click Removes a selected object from the selection (with Clicking objects set to Replaces the selection in Settings: adds it). While picking a point: ignores object snaps for that click Click on empty space, then click again A selection box: left to right is a window, right to left a crossing Drag on empty space A lasso: moving right first is a window, left first a crossing; Space while dragging cycles window, crossing and fence; Esc drops it (LASSOMODE 0, or Settings, makes it the rectangle again) Drag through pieces in TRIM or EXTEND Trims (or extends) every piece the path crosses; hold Shift for the other one Drag a grip Moves the grip (or click the grip, then click where it goes) Double-click text Selects it and puts the cursor in its Text box in the right panel Right-click Enter. During a grip move or a selection box: cancels it Drag with the right button Pans Wheel Zooms in and out about the crosshair Drag with the wheel (middle button) Pans Double-click the wheel Zooms to the whole drawing (extents) Drop a DWG or DXF on the drawing Opens it On a trackpad or a touch screen, see Mouse, trackpad and touch. Keys the browser keeps Ctrl+N, Ctrl+W and Ctrl+T always belong to the browser. CadCraft does not use them: use New or type NEW for a new drawing. CadCraft's clipboard is its own: Ctrl+C and Ctrl+V copy objects between CadCraft drawings and tabs, not to or from other programs. If Ctrl+Y opens your browser's history instead of redoing, use Ctrl+Shift+Z or the Redo button."},{"slug":"commands","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Command reference","summary":"Every command in the CadCraft drafting app, with its aliases, the questions it asks, its options and defaults.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["commands","aliases","command list","area","perimeter","measuregeom","line","pline","circle","arc","trim","offset","dimlinear","block","insert","layout","plot","zoom","help","copyclip","cutclip","pasteclip","pasteorig","copybase","duplicate","selectall"],"headings":[{"id":"draw","text":"Draw","level":2},{"id":"line","text":"LINE","level":3},{"id":"pline","text":"PLINE","level":3},{"id":"circle","text":"CIRCLE","level":3},{"id":"arc","text":"ARC","level":3},{"id":"rectang","text":"RECTANG","level":3},{"id":"polygon","text":"POLYGON","level":3},{"id":"ellipse","text":"ELLIPSE","level":3},{"id":"hatch","text":"HATCH","level":3},{"id":"point","text":"POINT","level":3},{"id":"text","text":"TEXT","level":3},{"id":"modify","text":"Modify","level":2},{"id":"textedit","text":"TEXTEDIT","level":3},{"id":"erase","text":"ERASE","level":3},{"id":"move","text":"MOVE","level":3},{"id":"copy","text":"COPY","level":3},{"id":"rotate","text":"ROTATE","level":3},{"id":"scale","text":"SCALE","level":3},{"id":"mirror","text":"MIRROR","level":3},{"id":"stretch","text":"STRETCH","level":3},{"id":"array-arrayrect-arraypolar","text":"ARRAY, ARRAYRECT, ARRAYPOLAR","level":3},{"id":"offset","text":"OFFSET","level":3},{"id":"trim","text":"TRIM","level":3},{"id":"extend","text":"EXTEND","level":3},{"id":"linkviews","text":"LINKVIEWS","level":3},{"id":"openin3d","text":"OPENIN3D","level":3},{"id":"lassomode","text":"LASSOMODE","level":3},{"id":"fillet","text":"FILLET","level":3},{"id":"chamfer","text":"CHAMFER","level":3},{"id":"break-and-breakatpoint","text":"BREAK and BREAKATPOINT","level":3},{"id":"lengthen","text":"LENGTHEN","level":3},{"id":"join","text":"JOIN","level":3},{"id":"matchprop","text":"MATCHPROP","level":3},{"id":"explode","text":"EXPLODE","level":3},{"id":"clipboard","text":"Clipboard","level":2},{"id":"copyclip","text":"COPYCLIP","level":3},{"id":"cutclip","text":"CUTCLIP","level":3},{"id":"copybase","text":"COPYBASE","level":3},{"id":"pasteclip","text":"PASTECLIP","level":3},{"id":"pasteorig","text":"PASTEORIG","level":3},{"id":"duplicate","text":"DUPLICATE","level":3},{"id":"select","text":"SELECT","level":3},{"id":"qselect","text":"QSELECT","level":3},{"id":"selectsimilar","text":"SELECTSIMILAR","level":3},{"id":"find","text":"FIND","level":3},{"id":"selectall-and-deselect","text":"SELECTALL and DESELECT","level":3},{"id":"dimensions","text":"Dimensions","level":2},{"id":"dimlinear","text":"DIMLINEAR","level":3},{"id":"dimaligned","text":"DIMALIGNED","level":3},{"id":"dimangular","text":"DIMANGULAR","level":3},{"id":"dimradius-and-dimdiameter","text":"DIMRADIUS and DIMDIAMETER","level":3},{"id":"dimcontinue","text":"DIMCONTINUE","level":3},{"id":"dimbaseline","text":"DIMBASELINE","level":3},{"id":"dimscale-dimtxt-dimasz-dimdec-dimgap-dimexe-dimexo","text":"DIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO","level":3},{"id":"dimlunit","text":"DIMLUNIT","level":3},{"id":"revcloud","text":"REVCLOUD","level":3},{"id":"mleader","text":"MLEADER","level":3},{"id":"parametric","text":"Parametric","level":2},{"id":"geomconstraint-and-the-gc-commands","text":"GEOMCONSTRAINT and the GC commands","level":3},{"id":"dimconstraint-and-the-dc-commands","text":"DIMCONSTRAINT and the DC commands","level":3},{"id":"autoconstrain-delconstraint-constraintbar-dcdisplay","text":"AUTOCONSTRAIN, DELCONSTRAINT, CONSTRAINTBAR, DCDISPLAY","level":3},{"id":"parameters-and-parameters","text":"PARAMETERS and -PARAMETERS","level":3},{"id":"swbout","text":"SWBOUT","level":3},{"id":"blocks","text":"Blocks","level":2},{"id":"block","text":"BLOCK","level":3},{"id":"attdef","text":"ATTDEF","level":3},{"id":"bedit-and-bclose","text":"BEDIT and BCLOSE","level":3},{"id":"viewbase","text":"VIEWBASE","level":3},{"id":"publish","text":"PUBLISH","level":3},{"id":"sheetset","text":"SHEETSET","level":3},{"id":"xattach-xref","text":"XATTACH, XREF","level":3},{"id":"count","text":"COUNT","level":3},{"id":"insert","text":"INSERT","level":3},{"id":"library","text":"LIBRARY","level":3},{"id":"purge","text":"PURGE","level":3},{"id":"delblocks","text":"DELBLOCKS","level":3},{"id":"sheets-and-plotting","text":"Sheets and plotting","level":2},{"id":"layout","text":"LAYOUT","level":3},{"id":"mview","text":"MVIEW","level":3},{"id":"vports","text":"VPORTS","level":3},{"id":"vpview","text":"VPVIEW","level":3},{"id":"plot","text":"PLOT","level":3},{"id":"inquiry","text":"Inquiry","level":2},{"id":"dist","text":"DIST","level":3},{"id":"area","text":"AREA","level":3},{"id":"measuregeom","text":"MEASUREGEOM","level":3},{"id":"id","text":"ID","level":3},{"id":"list","text":"LIST","level":3},{"id":"view-file-and-other","text":"View, file and other","level":2},{"id":"zoom","text":"ZOOM","level":3},{"id":"namedview","text":"NAMEDVIEW","level":3},{"id":"undo-and-redo","text":"UNDO and REDO","level":3},{"id":"layer","text":"LAYER","level":3},{"id":"linetype-lweight-ltscale","text":"LINETYPE, LWEIGHT, LTSCALE","level":3},{"id":"rename-laymrg-laydel","text":"RENAME, LAYMRG, LAYDEL","level":3},{"id":"layiso-layuniso-layoff-layfrz-laymcur-layon-laythw","text":"LAYISO, LAYUNISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ, LAYMCUR, LAYON, LAYTHW","level":3},{"id":"new-open-save-saveas-dxfout","text":"NEW, OPEN, SAVE, SAVEAS, DXFOUT","level":3},{"id":"polarang","text":"POLARANG","level":3},{"id":"units","text":"UNITS","level":3},{"id":"appload","text":"APPLOAD","level":3},{"id":"aliasedit-reinit","text":"ALIASEDIT, REINIT","level":3},{"id":"help","text":"HELP","level":3},{"id":"select-objects-prompt","text":"Select objects prompt","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EType a command name or an alias and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Case does not matter, and a leading \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E.\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E is ignored. Type \u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E) to print a short list of every command in the command line history; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for this help.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow to read the prompts: options are in square brackets, and you choose one by typing its capital letters (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E for Diameter). A default is in angle brackets; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to accept it. Where a point is asked for, you can click or type any of the formats in \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E. Where a distance is asked for, you can type a number or click two points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E where a point or an object is required, and there is no default, ends the command. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E cancels it. Whatever a command did is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"draw\"\u003EDraw\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"line\"\u003ELINE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E. Straight segments, each its own object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point\u003C/code\u003E (Enter: start from the last point you gave)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated. After two segments: \u003Ccode\u003E[Undo/Close]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last segment, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E closes to the first point and ends. Enter ends.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pline\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPL\u003C/code\u003E. One polyline of line and arc segments.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point [Arc/Close/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E (Close once there are two segments)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E switches to arcs: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify endpoint of arc [Line/CLose/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E. Each arc continues tangent to the segment before. \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E goes back to lines, \u003Ccode\u003ECL\u003C/code\u003E closes with an arc.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEnter ends; the polyline stays open.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"circle\"\u003ECIRCLE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point for circle [3P/2P]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify radius of circle [Diameter] &lt;last radius&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify diameter of circle\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2P\u003C/code\u003E: two ends of a diameter. \u003Ccode\u003E3P\u003C/code\u003E: three points on the circle.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"arc\"\u003EARC\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of arc [Center]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point of arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify end point of arc\u003C/code\u003E: an arc through three points.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point of arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify end point of arc\u003C/code\u003E. The arc runs counterclockwise from start to end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rectang\"\u003ERECTANG\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EREC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ERECTANGLE\u003C/code\u003E. A closed polyline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify other corner point\u003C/code\u003E. Type \u003Ccode\u003E@width,height\u003C/code\u003E for the second corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"polygon\"\u003EPOLYGON\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPOL\u003C/code\u003E. A regular polygon as a closed polyline.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter number of sides &lt;4&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (3 to 1024)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center of polygon\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Inscribed in circle/Circumscribed about circle] &lt;I&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify radius of circle\u003C/code\u003E: click or type a point; its distance sets the size and its direction turns the polygon.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"ellipse\"\u003EELLIPSE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EEL\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify axis endpoint of ellipse [Center]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify other endpoint of axis\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify distance to other axis\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center of ellipse\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify endpoint of axis\u003C/code\u003E, then the distance to the other axis.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"hatch\"\u003EHATCH\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBHATCH\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHatch\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. Prints the current pattern, scale and angle first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EPick internal point [Select objects/Pattern/SCale/Angle]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated (\u003Ccode\u003EUndo\u003C/code\u003E once there is an area): each click adds the closed area round it, with the closed shapes inside it as islands. Enter makes one hatch of all of them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect closed objects\u003C/code\u003E (closed polylines, circles, closed ellipses).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter a pattern name, SOLID, or ? for the list &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify pattern scale\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify pattern angle\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/hatch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/hatch\"\u003EHatch and fill areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"point\"\u003EPOINT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPO\u003C/code\u003E. Point objects, one per click, until Enter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify a point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"text\"\u003ETEXT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDTEXT\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EText\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. Single-line text; each line is its own object.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of text [Justify]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] &lt;L&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point of text\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify height &lt;current text height&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle of text &lt;0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter text\u003C/code\u003E, repeated: each Enter starts the next line below. Enter on an empty line ends. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E types a space here.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"modify\"\u003EModify\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParagraph\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar. Paragraph text that wraps to a width, typed in place.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width]\u003C/code\u003E: the width across the two corners is where the text wraps. \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify height\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EEnter justification [TL/TC/TR/ML/MC/MR/BL/BC/BR] &lt;TL&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EEnter line spacing factor (0.25 to 4)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify width (0 for no wrapping)\u003C/code\u003E and skips the second corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThen a box opens on the drawing: type, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E for a new paragraph, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Return\"\u003E⌘ Return\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Enter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or a click outside to finish, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to cancel. Its bar sets height, line spacing, width and justification. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text#write-a-note-that-wraps\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\" data-hash=\"write-a-note-that-wraps\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"textedit\"\u003ETEXTEDIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EED\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDEDIT\u003C/code\u003E; or double-click a text. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect a text to edit\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: each opens in the same box. Started on a selected or double-clicked text, it edits that one and ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"erase\"\u003EERASE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDELETE\u003C/code\u003E; or press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E with objects selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"move\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point [Displacement]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify displacement\u003C/code\u003E, typed as \u003Ccode\u003Edx,dy\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copy\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ECO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECP\u003C/code\u003E. Copies as many times as you give points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point [Displacement]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until Enter. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rotate\"\u003EROTATE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ERO\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle &lt;last angle&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: degrees, counterclockwise positive; or click a point to rotate toward it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"scale\"\u003ESCALE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESC\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify scale factor\u003C/code\u003E: type a factor such as \u003Ccode\u003E0.5\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E. A clicked point gives its distance from the base point as the factor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mirror\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMI\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point of mirror line\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EErase source objects? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"stretch\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStretch\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to stretch\u003C/code\u003E: drag crossing windows (or type \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E) over the ends to move. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point [Displacement]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E. Points inside the windows move: line ends, polyline vertices (arc segments keep their height), dimension points; circles, ellipses, text, points and blocks move whole when their center or insertion point is inside. Without a window, the objects move whole.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"array-arrayrect-arraypolar\"\u003EARRAY, ARRAYRECT, ARRAYPOLAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArray\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EEnter array type [Rectangular/POlar] &lt;Rectangular&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (ARRAY only).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERectangular starts at 4 columns × 3 rows, spaced 1.5 times the objects&#039; size, shown as a preview. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [COUnt/Spacing/COLumns/Rows/eXit] &lt;X&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ECOU\u003C/code\u003E asks columns and rows, \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E the distances between columns and between rows (negative goes left or down), \u003Ccode\u003ECOL\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E one direction each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPolar asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point of array\u003C/code\u003E, starts at 6 items all the way round, rotated. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Items/Fill angle/ROTate items/eXit] &lt;X&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E items, \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E the angle to fill (+ counterclockwise, − clockwise), \u003Ccode\u003EROT\u003C/code\u003E Yes or No.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (eXit) makes the copies, as plain objects. One undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"offset\"\u003EOFFSET\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify offset distance [Through] &lt;last distance, or Through&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to offset\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point on side to offset\u003C/code\u003E (or, with Through, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify through point\u003C/code\u003E), repeated until Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"trim\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ETR\u003C/code\u003E. Prints \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend\u003C/code\u003E first. In Quick mode every visible object is a cutting edge, and an object nothing crosses is erased; in Standard mode it asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect cutting edges (Enter = every object)\u003C/code\u003E first and cuts only at those.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to trim or shift-select to extend [cuTting edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/eRase/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated: click the piece to remove (it shows grey and dashed while the pointer rests on it), drag a path through several pieces (all go in one step), or click two empty spots for a fence between them. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E extends instead. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E chooses cutting edges (Standard mode), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E asks fence points, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E two window corners (pieces partly inside go), \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter a trim mode option [Quick/Standard]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an implied edge extension mode [Extend/No extend]\u003C/code\u003E (EDGEMODE), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E erases objects you select, \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E undoes the last pick or drag. Enter ends. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/trim-and-extend\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/trim-and-extend\"\u003ETrim and extend\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"extend\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EEX\u003C/code\u003E. The same as TRIM the other way round: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to extend or shift-select to trim [Boundary edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/Undo]\u003C/code\u003E. A click lengthens the end nearest it to the next boundary; a dragged path or fence lengthens every object it crosses; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E trims. \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E chooses boundary edges (Standard mode). Mode and Edge are shared with TRIM.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"linkviews\"\u003ELINKVIEWS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESYNCVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELink the panes&#039; views\u003C/span\u003E, or the link icon on the drawing tabs. \u003Ccode\u003ELink the panes&#039; views [ON/OFF]\u003C/code\u003E (Enter takes the other state): while on, a pan or zoom in the active pane moves every pane showing the same kind of space. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"openin3d\"\u003EOPENIN3D\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen in ModelCraft\u003C/span\u003E where the 3D modeling app takes a handed-over drawing. Opens it there in a new tab, as a DWG (nothing is uploaded). If the browser blocks the tab, it says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"lassomode\"\u003ELASSOMODE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter new value for LASSOMODE (1 = lasso, 0 = rectangle)\u003C/code\u003E: what pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws. The same as Settings › CadCraft › Dragging to select. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"fillet\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Prints \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent settings: Mode = TRIM, Radius = 0\u003C/code\u003E first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect first object [Undo/Polyline/Radius/Trim/Multiple]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify fillet radius &lt;last&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter Trim mode option [Trim/No trim]\u003C/code\u003E: with No trim the objects stay as they are and only the arc is added.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E keeps the command going for more corners until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last corner without leaving the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect 2D polyline [Radius]\u003C/code\u003E and rounds every corner where two straight segments meet. Fillets it already has are replaced by the new radius; radius 0 takes them out. Corners whose sides are too short for the radius are left and counted.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect second object or shift-select to apply corner [Radius]\u003C/code\u003E. Hold \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E as you click for a sharp corner, whatever the radius.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClick each object on the part you want to keep. Two lines that do not meet are extended. Two parallel lines get a half circle across the gap, at the end of the first line nearest your click. A circle is never cut. Two segments of one polyline next to each other get the arc inside the polyline; a polyline and another object work when the cut lands on the polyline&#039;s first or last segment.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"chamfer\"\u003ECHAMFER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ECHA\u003C/code\u003E. Lines and the straight segments of polylines. Prints the current distances (or length and angle) first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect first line [Undo/Polyline/Distance/Angle/Trim/mEthod/Multiple]\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first chamfer distance &lt;last&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second chamfer distance &lt;the first&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: the first is cut from the first line you pick, the second from the other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify chamfer length on the first line\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify chamfer angle from the first line\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E (mEthod) chooses which of the two the next chamfer uses. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E work as in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EFILLET\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line or shift-select to apply corner [Distance/Angle/mEthod]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click makes a sharp corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA distance longer than the line is refused (\u003Ccode\u003EDistance is too large.\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"break-and-breakatpoint\"\u003EBREAK and BREAKATPOINT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EBR\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object\u003C/code\u003E (where you click is the first point), \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second break point [First point]\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first break point\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second break point\u003C/code\u003E. The part between goes; on a circle, counter-clockwise from the first point. BREAKATPOINT asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify break point\u003C/code\u003E and splits the object in two there (not a circle).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"lengthen\"\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELEN\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object to measure [DElta/Percent/Total/DYnamic]\u003C/code\u003E: a pick prints \u003Ccode\u003ECurrent length\u003C/code\u003E (and an arc&#039;s included angle) and asks again. \u003Ccode\u003EDE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter delta length (negative shortens) [Angle]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EEnter percentage length\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify total length [Angle]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDY\u003C/code\u003E drags. Then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object to change [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated: the end nearest the click moves. Lines, arcs and open polylines.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"join\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EJ\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to join\u003C/code\u003E. Lines, arcs and open polylines that meet end to end become polylines (closed when the chain closes); lines on one line become one line; arcs of one circle become one arc or the circle. The history says how many objects joined and how many did not.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"matchprop\"\u003EMATCHPROP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPAINTER\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect source object\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect destination objects [Settings]\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Copies layer and color, and between objects of the same kind the text height and width, the dimension sizes, or the hatch pattern. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ETurn a property on or off [Color/LAyer/Text/Dimension/Hatch]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; the choice is remembered for the session.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"explode\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E. Polylines become lines and arcs, block references become their objects, dimensions become lines, arrowheads and text.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"clipboard\"\u003EClipboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft&#039;s own clipboard: it works within a drawing, between drawings and between CadCraft tabs in this browser. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copyclip\"\u003ECOPYCLIP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command C\"\u003E⌘C\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+C\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECopy\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group. Copies objects to the clipboard, with the blocks and layers they use. The objects stay selected.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to copy\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"cutclip\"\u003ECUTCLIP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command X\"\u003E⌘X\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+X\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECut\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. Copies objects to the clipboard, then erases them (one undo step).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to cut\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"copybase\"\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECopies objects to the clipboard with a base point you pick; pasting places that point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to copy\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pasteclip\"\u003EPASTECLIP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command V\"\u003E⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E group. The copy follows the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of its extents (or its center, or the \u003Ccode\u003ECOPYBASE\u003C/code\u003E point; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/settings\"\u003ECadCraft settings\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe pasted objects are selected. A block the drawing already has under the same name keeps this drawing&#039;s definition.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"pasteorig\"\u003EPASTEORIG\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command V\"\u003E⇧⌘V\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+V\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPaste in place\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. Pastes at the coordinates the objects were copied from. Asks nothing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"duplicate\"\u003EDUPLICATE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDUP\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command D\"\u003E⌘D\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+D\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDuplicate and move\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E menu. A copy of the objects moves with the pointer from where it is; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E leaves nothing. Does not use the clipboard.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects to duplicate\u003C/code\u003E (skipped when objects are already selected)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify where the copy goes (Esc leaves nothing)\u003C/code\u003E: click, type a point, \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E (from where the pointer was), or a distance toward the cursor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"select\"\u003ESELECT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAsks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E (with every option below) and leaves what you chose selected, for the next command or for \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E later.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"qselect\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EQS\u003C/code\u003E. In the browser, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EQuick select\u003C/span\u003E dialog (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/select-objects#select-by-a-rule-quick-select\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/select-objects\" data-hash=\"select-by-a-rule-quick-select\"\u003ESelect objects\u003C/a\u003E). Without it, the command line asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter object type\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter property\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter operator [=/&lt;&gt;/&gt;/&lt;/*/Select all] &lt;=&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEnter value\u003C/code\u003E. The matches become the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"selectsimilar\"\u003ESELECTSIMILAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESIM\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects [SEttings]\u003C/code\u003E (or the current selection): selects every object of the same type with the same layer and color (and block name, hatch pattern). \u003Ccode\u003ESE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ETurn a property on or off [LAyer/COlor/NAme/PAttern/HEight]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"find\"\u003EFIND\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E. In the browser, the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind and replace\u003C/span\u003E dialog (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/text#find-and-replace-words\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/text\" data-hash=\"find-and-replace-words\"\u003EAdd text and labels\u003C/a\u003E). Without it: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text to find\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EEnter replacement text (Enter: select what is found)\u003C/code\u003E. Looks in text, attribute values and dimension text, on the open tab or in the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"selectall-and-deselect\"\u003ESELECTALL and DESELECT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESELECTALL\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003EAI_SELALL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) selects every object you can edit on the open tab. \u003Ccode\u003EDESELECT\u003C/code\u003E (aliases \u003Ccode\u003EAI_DESELECT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESELECTNONE\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) clears the selection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions\"\u003EDimensions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimlinear\"\u003EDIMLINEAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDLI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMLIN\u003C/code\u003E. Horizontal or vertical distance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first extension line origin [Select object]\u003C/code\u003E (Enter or \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object to dimension\u003C/code\u003E, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment)\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second extension line origin\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text/Horizontal/Vertical/Rotated]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ET\u003C/code\u003E asks for text (\u003Ccode\u003E&lt;&gt;\u003C/code\u003E is the measured value), \u003Ccode\u003EH\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EV\u003C/code\u003E fix the direction, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle of dimension line &lt;0&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimaligned\"\u003EDIMALIGNED\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMALI\u003C/code\u003E. The true distance between two points.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOrigins as in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimangular\"\u003EDIMANGULAR\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDAN\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMANG\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArc: its angle. Circle: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E. Line: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line\u003C/code\u003E. Enter: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify angle vertex\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second angle endpoint\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text]\u003C/code\u003E: click inside the angle to measure.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimradius-and-dimdiameter\"\u003EDIMRADIUS and DIMDIAMETER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDRA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMRAD\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EDDI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMDIA\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc or circle\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location [Text]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimcontinue\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDCO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMCONT\u003C/code\u003E. Continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made (or asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect linear or aligned dimension\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second extension line origin [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E, repeated until Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimbaseline\"\u003EDIMBASELINE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDBA\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDIMBASE\u003C/code\u003E. Like \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/code\u003E, but every dimension starts from the first origin and is stacked further out.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimscale-dimtxt-dimasz-dimdec-dimgap-dimexe-dimexo\"\u003EDIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. Each asks \u003Ccode\u003EEnter new value for … &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and sets that size for \u003Cstrong\u003Enew\u003C/strong\u003E dimensions: overall scale, text height, arrow size, decimal places (up to 8), gap round the text, extension beyond the dimension line, offset from the origin. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimlunit\"\u003EDIMLUNIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDimension setting: the format of new dimensions only (1 scientific, 2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"revcloud\"\u003EREVCLOUD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ERC\u003C/code\u003E. Prints \u003Ccode\u003EArc length: … Type: …\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point [Arc length/Object/Rectangular/Polygonal/Freehand]\u003C/code\u003E. Rectangular asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner\u003C/code\u003E; Polygonal \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point (Enter closes the cloud) [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E; Freehand follows the pointer until a click or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EReverse direction? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E and turns the object into the cloud. \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify the length of each arc\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E choose the type for the next cloud. The result is a closed polyline of arcs.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mleader\"\u003EMLEADER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMLD\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELeader\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader arrowhead location [Arrowhead size/Landing distance]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify leader landing location\u003C/code\u003E, then the text in the in-place box (or, without it, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter text (an empty line ends)\u003C/code\u003E). Saved as a real multileader in DWG; DXF keeps the lines, arrowhead and text it draws.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"parametric\"\u003EParametric\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/constraints\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/constraints\"\u003EConstrain a drawing\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/parameters\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/parameters\"\u003EUse parameters and equations\u003C/a\u003E. Constraints work on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"geomconstraint-and-the-gc-commands\"\u003EGEOMCONSTRAINT and the GC commands\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias \u003Ccode\u003EGCON\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter constraint type [Horizontal/Vertical/Perpendicular/PArallel/Tangent/Coincident/CONcentric/COLlinear/Symmetric/Equal/Fix] &lt;Horizontal&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then that command&#039;s prompts.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object [2Points] &lt;2Points&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPARALLEL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCPERPENDICULAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOLLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCONCENTRIC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCEQUAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCTANGENT\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first object\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second object\u003C/code\u003E (the second one moves).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCCOINCIDENT\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first point [Object/Autoconstrain] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second point [Object] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCFIX\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect point [Object] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EGCSYMMETRIC\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first object [2Points] &lt;2Points&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second object\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect symmetry line\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dimconstraint-and-the-dc-commands\"\u003EDIMCONSTRAINT and the DC commands\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias \u003Ccode\u003EDCON\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect associative dimension to convert [LInear/Horizontal/Vertical/ALigned/ANgular/Radial/Diameter/Convert] &lt;ALigned&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCLINEAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCHORIZONTAL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCVERTICAL\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first constraint point [Object] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second constraint point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify dimension line location\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDimension text &lt;d1=40&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (a number, an expression, or \u003Ccode\u003Ename=expression\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCALIGNED\u003C/code\u003E: as above, with \u003Ccode\u003E[Object/Point &amp; line/2Lines]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCRADIUS\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDIAMETER\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc or circle\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect first line or arc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESelect second line\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCCONVERT\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect dimensions to convert\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"autoconstrain-delconstraint-constraintbar-dcdisplay\"\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN, DELCONSTRAINT, CONSTRAINTBAR, DCDISPLAY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAUTOCONSTRAIN\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects [Settings]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks the distance and angle tolerances.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDELCONSTRAINT\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003EDELCON\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E; their constraints go.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECONSTRAINTBAR\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003ECBAR\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects [Showall/Hideall]\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003E[Show/Hide]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Show/Hide]\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"parameters-and-parameters\"\u003EPARAMETERS and -PARAMETERS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E (aliases \u003Ccode\u003EPAR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPARAM\u003C/code\u003E) opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParameters\u003C/span\u003E panel. \u003Ccode\u003E-PARAMETERS\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [New/Edit/Rename/Delete/?] &lt;?&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"swbout\"\u003ESWBOUT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias \u003Ccode\u003ESWMACRO\u003C/code\u003E. Downloads a macro (.swb) that rebuilds the model as one constrained sketch in SolidWorks. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/solidworks-sketches\"\u003EMove sketches to and from other part modelers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"blocks\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"block\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EB\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EBMAKE\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name\u003C/code\u003E. If it exists: \u003Ccode\u003EBlock &quot;…&quot; already exists. Redefine it? [Yes/No] &lt;N&gt;\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion base point\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E. They are replaced by one reference to the new block.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"attdef\"\u003EATTDEF\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EATT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-ATTDEF\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefine attribute\u003C/span\u003E. Prints the modes, then \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option to change (Enter when done) [Invisible/Constant/Preset]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter attribute tag name\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter attribute prompt\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter default attribute value\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EEnter attribute value\u003C/code\u003E when constant), \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify start point of text\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify text height\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle of text\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E takes attribute definitions into the block.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"bedit-and-bclose\"\u003EBEDIT and BCLOSE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EBE\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit block\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name to edit &lt;the selected reference&#039;s block&gt;\u003C/code\u003E opens the block editor. \u003Ccode\u003EBCLOSE\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003EClose the block editor on &quot;…&quot; [Save/Discard] &lt;Save&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; the banner&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave block\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard changes\u003C/span\u003E do the same. Everything done in the editor is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"viewbase\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAlias: \u003Ccode\u003EFLATSHOT\u003C/code\u003E. A flat 2D view of the solids on the 3D tab: \u003Ccode\u003EView [Front/Top/Right/Left/BAck/BOttom/Iso]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EHidden lines [Show/Hide]\u003C/code\u003E, a scale (\u003Ccode\u003E1:20\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2:1\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E takes the one that fits the sheet), then the view&#039;s center. One block reference, visible edges on VIEW-VISIBLE, hidden ones dashed on VIEW-HIDDEN. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/3d-solids#flat-views-on-a-sheet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/3d-solids\" data-hash=\"flat-views-on-a-sheet\"\u003EFlat views on a sheet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"publish\"\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003E-PUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAll sheets in one PDF\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog. \u003Ccode\u003ESheets to plot [All/Some]\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E plots every sheet, \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks for names separated by commas. One PDF, a page and a bookmark per sheet. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf#every-sheet-in-one-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-hash=\"every-sheet-in-one-pdf\"\u003EEvery sheet in one PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"sheetset\"\u003ESHEETSET\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ESSM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESHEETSETMANAGER\u003C/code\u003E. Brings you to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESheet set\u003C/span\u003E section of the left panel: sheets from many drawings, numbered and titled, their title blocks filled from the set, published as one PDF. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/sheet-sets\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/sheet-sets\"\u003ESheet sets\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"xattach-xref\"\u003EXATTACH, XREF\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXATTACH\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXA\u003C/code\u003E): pick a DWG or DXF, then its insertion point. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EXREF\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EXR\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-XREF\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003ELinked drawings [?/Attach/Reload/Bind/Detach] (Enter: ?)\u003C/code\u003E; with more than one link, pick one of its references or type \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E and its name. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#linked-drawings-external-references\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"linked-drawings-external-references\"\u003ELinked drawings\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"count\"\u003ECOUNT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EBCOUNT\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECount blocks\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect a block reference to count, or [All/Export] (Enter: All)\u003C/code\u003E: a pick selects and counts every reference of that block in this space; \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E lists them all; \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E then \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (counts) or \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (attributes) downloads a CSV. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/blocks#count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/blocks\" data-hash=\"count-blocks-and-export-a-schedule\"\u003ECount blocks and export a schedule\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"insert\"\u003EINSERT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDDINSERT\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter block name or [File/Library] &lt;last block&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (case does not matter). \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E asks for a DWG or DXF, then the whole drawing or one of its blocks; a name already used asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep existing\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERename\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E opens the library panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify insertion point [Scale/Rotate]\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify scale factor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify rotation angle\u003C/code\u003E; both are remembered.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThen \u003Ccode\u003EEnter &lt;prompt&gt; (&lt;TAG&gt;) &lt;default&gt;\u003C/code\u003E for each attribute the block asks for. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/attributes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/attributes\"\u003EBlock attributes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"library\"\u003ELIBRARY\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELIB\u003C/code\u003E. Opens the library panel: place items, check for updates, library credits. No questions. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/insert-from-file-and-library\"\u003EInsert from a file or the library\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"purge\"\u003EPURGE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPU\u003C/code\u003E. Removes block definitions that nothing uses. No questions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"delblocks\"\u003EDELBLOCKS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocks\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDelete blocks\u003C/span\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect block to delete [Name/eXit]\u003C/code\u003E: pick a block reference, or \u003Ccode\u003EN\u003C/code\u003E for the list of blocks with a filter (choose several), or \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end. Every reference goes — in the model, on every sheet and inside other blocks — and then the definition, and the command line says what was deleted and what couldn&#039;t be. Unnamed blocks and linked drawings are not offered. The prompt comes back for the next block; the whole command is one undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-and-plotting\"\u003ESheets and plotting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layout\"\u003ELAYOUT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELO\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ENEWLAYOUT\u003C/code\u003E; or the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E+\u003C/span\u003E button under the drawing. A new sheet with a border, a title block and one viewport, on ISO A3 (or Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet). No questions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"mview\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMV\u003C/code\u003E. On a sheet only (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/code\u003E on a sheet does the same).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify corner of viewport\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner\u003C/code\u003E. The viewport shows the whole model at a standard scale.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"vports\"\u003EVPORTS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EVIEWPORTS\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESPLIT\u003C/code\u003E is the same from a sheet too, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit side by side\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESplit stacked\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFour panes\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOne pane\u003C/span\u003E run it. On the model: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter number of panes [SIngle/2/3/4] &lt;3&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003E[Horizontal/Vertical] &lt;Vertical&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (Vertical = side by side), \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003E[Left/Right] &lt;Right&gt;\u003C/code\u003E (where the big pane goes). Each pane keeps its own view and space; a click makes a pane active. On a sheet it is \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"vpview\"\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. On a sheet only.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESelect viewport\u003C/code\u003E (skipped if one is selected), then on the model: \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner of the window to show\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify opposite corner\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"plot\"\u003EPLOT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EPRINT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EPUBLISH\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEXPORTPDF\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command P\"\u003E⌘P\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+P\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/plot-pdf\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/plot-pdf\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"inquiry\"\u003EInquiry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"dist\"\u003EDIST\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EDI\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMeasure distance\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first point\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E. Prints distance, angle, Delta X and Delta Y.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"area\"\u003EAREA\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EAA\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArea\u003C/span\u003E. Prints the area and perimeter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify first corner point [Object/Add area/Subtract area] &lt;Object&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClick or type corners; at \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify next point (Enter closes) [Undo]\u003C/code\u003E press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to close the outline. \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last corner.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object\u003C/code\u003E: a circle (prints its circumference), an ellipse, a polyline (an open one is closed by a straight line for the area, and its length is printed) or a hatch (holes are taken off).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (Add area) and \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E (Subtract area) keep a running total: the prompt starts with \u003Ccode\u003E(ADD mode)\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E(SUBTRACT mode)\u003C/code\u003E, each area is added or taken off, and \u003Ccode\u003ETotal area = …\u003C/code\u003E is printed after each. In these modes \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E picks objects until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the first prompt ends the command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"measuregeom\"\u003EMEASUREGEOM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EMEA\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [Distance/Radius/Angle/ARea] &lt;Distance&gt;\u003C/code\u003E; after each measurement it asks again, with \u003Ccode\u003EeXit\u003C/code\u003E the default.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E: two points, as \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect arc or circle\u003C/code\u003E; prints the radius and diameter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E: an arc (its included angle), a line and then a second line (the angle between them, toward their far ends), or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and three points (vertex, then the two ends).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EAR\u003C/code\u003E: as \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E, one area.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"id\"\u003EID\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENo aliases. \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify point\u003C/code\u003E; prints its X and Y.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"list\"\u003ELIST\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELI\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ELS\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E; prints each object&#039;s type, layer and main measurements.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"view-file-and-other\"\u003EView, file and other\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"zoom\"\u003EZOOM\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003ESpecify corner of window, enter a scale factor (nX) [Extents/All/Window/Previous/Scale/Center]\u003C/code\u003E. Click two corners for a window. \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E or Enter: the whole drawing.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Previous, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E in the toolbar) goes back to the view before, up to 10 views per tab; a run of wheel steps or one pan counts as one view.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA number such as \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2x\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E0.5x\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E and then the number) zooms in or out by that factor round the middle of the screen.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E asks \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify center point\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EEnter height or magnification (nX)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"namedview\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ENV\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EVIEWS\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003EEnter an option [?/Restore/Save/Window/Delete] &lt;Restore&gt;\u003C/code\u003E, then the view&#039;s name. \u003Ccode\u003ES\u003C/code\u003E saves the view on screen, \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E a window you pick, \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E shows a saved view, \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E deletes one, \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists the views of this tab. Saving under an existing name replaces it; each change is one undo step. Views belong to the tab they were saved on. DWG and DXF files keep them (the VIEW table); a sheet&#039;s views come back on the first sheet, since the file does not say which.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"undo-and-redo\"\u003EUNDO and REDO\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREDO\u003C/code\u003E has none. Also \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Y\"\u003E⌘Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Y\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layer\"\u003ELAYER\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003ELA\u003C/code\u003E. Jumps to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"linetype-lweight-ltscale\"\u003ELINETYPE, LWEIGHT, LTSCALE\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINETYPE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELT\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EEnter linetype for new objects\u003C/code\u003E — \u003Ccode\u003EByLayer\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EByBlock\u003C/code\u003E or a name (\u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists them: Continuous, the drawing&#039;s own, and DASHED, HIDDEN, CENTER, PHANTOM, DOT, DASHDOT, BORDER, DIVIDE with their \u003Ccode\u003E2\u003C/code\u003E (half) and \u003Ccode\u003EX2\u003C/code\u003E (double) sizes). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWEIGHT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELW\u003C/code\u003E): the lineweight for new objects in mm, or \u003Ccode\u003EByLayer\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EByBlock\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDefault\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELTSCALE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELTS\u003C/code\u003E): the drawing&#039;s linetype scale (undoable). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELWT\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EShow lineweights [ON/OFF]\u003C/code\u003E — the same as the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E button in the status bar. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#line-weights-and-linetypes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"rename-laymrg-laydel\"\u003ERENAME, LAYMRG, LAYDEL\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ERENAME\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E-RENAME\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EREN\u003C/code\u003E): \u003Ccode\u003EEnter object type to rename [LAyer/Block]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EEnter old name\u003C/code\u003E, then the new name; objects or references follow. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMRG\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object on layer to merge or [Name]\u003C/code\u003E until \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object on target layer or [Name]\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYDEL\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object on layer to delete or [Name]\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ccode\u003EY\u003C/code\u003E: the layer and every object on it go (not layer 0, DEFPOINTS or the current layer). One undo step each. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers#rename-merge-and-delete-layers\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\" data-hash=\"rename-merge-and-delete-layers\"\u003EOrganize a drawing with layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"layiso-layuniso-layoff-layfrz-laymcur-layon-laythw\"\u003ELAYISO, LAYUNISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ, LAYMCUR, LAYON, LAYTHW\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELayers by picking objects; also in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETools\u003C/span\u003E menu. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects on the layer(s) to be isolated\u003C/code\u003E, then \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: every other layer goes off. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E turns them back on. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYOFF\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYFRZ\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect an object on the layer to be turned off\u003C/code\u003E (or frozen), over and over; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back the last one, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E ends. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYMCUR\u003C/code\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003ESelect object whose layer will become current\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYON\u003C/code\u003E turns every layer on, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYTHW\u003C/code\u003E thaws every layer. Each command is one undo step. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/layers#work-on-layers-by-picking-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/layers\" data-hash=\"work-on-layers-by-picking-objects\"\u003EOrganize a drawing with layers\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"new-open-save-saveas-dxfout\"\u003ENEW, OPEN, SAVE, SAVEAS, DXFOUT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E starts an empty drawing and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EOPEN\u003C/code\u003E opens a DWG or DXF, each in its own tab over the drawing (an untouched new drawing is used instead of a new tab). Closing a tab with unsaved changes asks first. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/split-screen\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/split-screen\"\u003ESplit the screen\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E (alias \u003Ccode\u003EQSAVE\u003C/code\u003E, also \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) downloads a copy in the drawing&#039;s format: DWG if it was opened from a DWG, DXF if from a DXF. A new drawing uses the format last chosen in Save as in this browser, else DWG.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVEAS\u003C/code\u003E (also \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E) opens the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy as\u003C/span\u003E dialog: choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDWG\u003C/span\u003E (and its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVersion\u003C/span\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF 2000\u003C/span\u003E, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E. The choice sticks for this drawing and for new drawings in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDXFOUT\u003C/code\u003E always downloads a DXF R12 copy, without changing the format \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESAVE\u003C/code\u003E uses.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESee \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#saving\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"saving\"\u003EFiles: what opens, what saves\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"polarang\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter polar angle step in degrees &lt;90&gt;\u003C/code\u003E: the step \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPolar\u003C/span\u003E tracking lines up at (more than 0, up to 180). Kept in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"units\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EUN\u003C/code\u003E. How lengths are written and typed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEnter length format [Scientific/Decimal/Engineering/Architectural/Fractional] &lt;current&gt;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EArchitectural and Fractional: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter denominator of smallest fraction to display (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 or 256)\u003C/code\u003E. The others: \u003Ccode\u003EEnter number of decimal places (0 to 8)\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew dimensions follow it. One undo step. Same as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrecision\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"appload\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003EAP\u003C/code\u003E; or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad application…\u003C/span\u003E. The \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/span\u003E dialog: load \u003Ccode\u003E.lsp\u003C/code\u003E routines and \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E dialog files, unload them, load them again from the history, and the Startup Suite. A routine&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003E(defun c:NAME …)\u003C/code\u003E commands then work like any other. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/load-applications\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/load-applications\"\u003ELoad applications\u003C/a\u003E and \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/run-lisp\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/run-lisp\"\u003ERun LISP routines\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"aliasedit-reinit\"\u003EALIASEDIT, REINIT\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EALIASEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EView\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMore\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEdit aliases…\u003C/span\u003E) opens your aliases file (\u003Ccode\u003EALIAS, *COMMAND\u003C/code\u003E lines); \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E applies it. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EREINIT\u003C/code\u003E applies it again. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/command-aliases\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/command-aliases\"\u003EEdit command aliases\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3 id=\"help\"\u003EHELP\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAliases: \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E. Prints every command, its aliases and a one-line description in the command line history. It does not open this help: press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E for that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"select-objects-prompt\"\u003ESelect objects prompt\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt any \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E prompt: click objects, draw windows (left to right) and crossings (right to left) with two clicks, or press and drag a lasso (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while dragging: window, crossing, fence), \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift\"\u003E⇧\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EShift\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E+click to remove, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to finish. You can also type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E (everything), \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E (the last object), \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E (Previous: what the last command used), \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E (Fence: objects a line of points crosses; \u003Ccode\u003EU\u003C/code\u003E takes back a point), \u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E (Window), \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E (Crossing), \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E (Remove mode: picks take objects out; the prompt reads \u003Ccode\u003ERemove objects\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode\u003EA\u003C/code\u003E (Add again). \u003Ccode\u003E?\u003C/code\u003E lists them. They are not shown in the prompt. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E over overlapping objects highlights the next one; click to pick it. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/select-objects\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/select-objects\"\u003ESelect objects\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Type a command name or an alias and press Enter or Space. Case does not matter, and a leading _, -, . or ' is ignored. Type HELP (or ?) to print a short list of every command in the command line history; press F1 for this help. How to read the prompts: options are in square brackets, and you choose one by typing its capital letters (D for Diameter). A default is in angle brackets; press Enter to accept it. Where a point is asked for, you can click or type any of the formats in Coordinate and distance entry. Where a distance is asked for, you can type a number or click two points. Pressing Enter where a point or an object is required, and there is no default, ends the command. Esc cancels it. Whatever a command did is one undo step. Draw LINE Aliases: L. Straight segments, each its own object. Specify first point (Enter: start from the last point you gave) Specify next point [Undo], repeated. After two segments: [Undo/Close]. U takes back the last segment, C closes to the first point and ends. Enter ends. PLINE Aliases: PL. One polyline of line and arc segments. Specify start point Specify next point [Arc/Close/Undo] (Close once there are two segments) A switches to arcs: Specify endpoint of arc [Line/CLose/Undo]. Each arc continues tangent to the segment before. L goes back to lines, CL closes with an arc. Enter ends; the polyline stays open. CIRCLE Aliases: C. Specify center point for circle [3P/2P] Specify radius of circle [Diameter] \u003Clast radius\u003E; D asks Specify diameter of circle. 2P: two ends of a diameter. 3P: three points on the circle. ARC Aliases: A. Specify start point of arc [Center], Specify second point of arc, Specify end point of arc: an arc through three points. C: Specify center point of arc, Specify start point of arc, Specify end point of arc. The arc runs counterclockwise from start to end. RECTANG Aliases: REC, RECTANGLE. A closed polyline. Specify first corner point, Specify other corner point. Type @width,height for the second corner. POLYGON Aliases: POL. A regular polygon as a closed polyline. Enter number of sides \u003C4\u003E (3 to 1024) Specify center of polygon Enter an option [Inscribed in circle/Circumscribed about circle] \u003CI\u003E Specify radius of circle: click or type a point; its distance sets the size and its direction turns the polygon. ELLIPSE Aliases: EL. Specify axis endpoint of ellipse [Center], Specify other endpoint of axis, Specify distance to other axis C: Specify center of ellipse, Specify endpoint of axis, then the distance to the other axis. HATCH Aliases: H, BH, BHATCH; or Hatch in the toolbar. Prints the current pattern, scale and angle first. Pick internal point [Select objects/Pattern/SCale/Angle], repeated (Undo once there is an area): each click adds the closed area round it, with the closed shapes inside it as islands. Enter makes one hatch of all of them. S: Select closed objects (closed polylines, circles, closed ellipses). P: Enter a pattern name, SOLID, or ? for the list \u003Ccurrent\u003E. SC: Specify pattern scale. A: Specify pattern angle. See Hatch and fill areas. POINT Aliases: PO. Point objects, one per click, until Enter. Specify a point TEXT Aliases: DT, DTEXT; or Text in the toolbar. Single-line text; each line is its own object. Specify start point of text [Justify] J: Enter an option [Left/Center/Right/Middle/TL/MC] \u003CL\u003E, then Specify insertion point of text Specify height \u003Ccurrent text height\u003E Specify rotation angle of text \u003C0\u003E Enter text, repeated: each Enter starts the next line below. Enter on an empty line ends. Space types a space here. Modify Aliases: MT, T; or Paragraph in the toolbar. Paragraph text that wraps to a width, typed in place. Specify first corner Specify opposite corner [Height/Justify/Line spacing/Rotation/Width]: the width across the two corners is where the text wraps. H asks Specify height, J Enter justification [TL/TC/TR/ML/MC/MR/BL/BC/BR] \u003CTL\u003E, L Enter line spacing factor (0.25 to 4), R Specify rotation angle, W Specify width (0 for no wrapping) and skips the second corner. Then a box opens on the drawing: type, Enter for a new paragraph, Ctrl+Enter or a click outside to finish, Esc to cancel. Its bar sets height, line spacing, width and justification. See Add text and labels. TEXTEDIT Aliases: ED, DDEDIT; or double-click a text. Select a text to edit, repeated until Enter: each opens in the same box. Started on a selected or double-clicked text, it edits that one and ends. ERASE Aliases: E, DELETE; or press Delete with objects selected. Select objects (skipped when objects are already selected) MOVE Aliases: M. Select objects Specify base point [Displacement], Specify second point D: Specify displacement, typed as dx,dy. COPY Aliases: CO, CP. Copies as many times as you give points. Select objects Specify base point [Displacement] Specify second point [Undo], repeated until Enter. U takes back the last copy. ROTATE Aliases: RO. Select objects, Specify base point Specify rotation angle \u003Clast angle\u003E: degrees, counterclockwise positive; or click a point to rotate toward it. SCALE Aliases: SC. Select objects, Specify base point Specify scale factor: type a factor such as 0.5 or 2. A clicked point gives its distance from the base point as the factor. MIRROR Aliases: MI. Select objects Specify first point of mirror line, Specify second point of mirror line Erase source objects? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E STRETCH Aliases: S; or Stretch. Select objects to stretch: drag crossing windows (or type C) over the ends to move. Specify base point [Displacement], Specify second point. Points inside the windows move: line ends, polyline vertices (arc segments keep their height), dimension points; circles, ellipses, text, points and blocks move whole when their center or insertion point is inside. Without a window, the objects move whole. ARRAY, ARRAYRECT, ARRAYPOLAR Aliases: AR; or Array. Select objects, then Enter array type [Rectangular/POlar] \u003CRectangular\u003E (ARRAY only). Rectangular starts at 4 columns × 3 rows, spaced 1.5 times the objects' size, shown as a preview. Enter an option [COUnt/Spacing/COLumns/Rows/eXit] \u003CX\u003E: COU asks columns and rows, S the distances between columns and between rows (negative goes left or down), COL and R one direction each. Polar asks Specify center point of array, starts at 6 items all the way round, rotated. Enter an option [Items/Fill angle/ROTate items/eXit] \u003CX\u003E: I items, F the angle to fill (+ counterclockwise, − clockwise), ROT Yes or No. Enter (eXit) makes the copies, as plain objects. One undo step. OFFSET Aliases: O. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Specify offset distance [Through] \u003Clast distance, or Through\u003E Select object to offset, then Specify point on side to offset (or, with Through, Specify through point), repeated until Enter. TRIM Aliases: TR. Prints Current settings: Quick mode, edges: No extend first. In Quick mode every visible object is a cutting edge, and an object nothing crosses is erased; in Standard mode it asks Select cutting edges (Enter = every object) first and cuts only at those. Select object to trim or shift-select to extend [cuTting edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/eRase/Undo], repeated: click the piece to remove (it shows grey and dashed while the pointer rests on it), drag a path through several pieces (all go in one step), or click two empty spots for a fence between them. Shift extends instead. T chooses cutting edges (Standard mode), F asks fence points, C two window corners (pieces partly inside go), O asks Enter a trim mode option [Quick/Standard], E asks Enter an implied edge extension mode [Extend/No extend] (EDGEMODE), R erases objects you select, U undoes the last pick or drag. Enter ends. See Trim and extend. EXTEND Aliases: EX. The same as TRIM the other way round: Select object to extend or shift-select to trim [Boundary edges/Fence/Crossing/mOde/Edge/Undo]. A click lengthens the end nearest it to the next boundary; a dragged path or fence lengthens every object it crosses; Shift trims. B chooses boundary edges (Standard mode). Mode and Edge are shared with TRIM. LINKVIEWS Aliases: SYNCVIEWS; or View › More › Link the panes' views, or the link icon on the drawing tabs. Link the panes' views [ON/OFF] (Enter takes the other state): while on, a pan or zoom in the active pane moves every pane showing the same kind of space. See Split the screen. OPENIN3D No aliases; or View › More › Open in ModelCraft where the 3D modeling app takes a handed-over drawing. Opens it there in a new tab, as a DWG (nothing is uploaded). If the browser blocks the tab, it says so. LASSOMODE Enter new value for LASSOMODE (1 = lasso, 0 = rectangle): what pressing and dragging on an empty spot draws. The same as Settings › CadCraft › Dragging to select. See Lasso selection. FILLET Aliases: F. Lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Prints Current settings: Mode = TRIM, Radius = 0 first. Select first object [Undo/Polyline/Radius/Trim/Multiple] R asks Specify fillet radius \u003Clast\u003E. T asks Enter Trim mode option [Trim/No trim]: with No trim the objects stay as they are and only the arc is added. M keeps the command going for more corners until Enter; U takes back the last corner without leaving the command. P asks Select 2D polyline [Radius] and rounds every corner where two straight segments meet. Fillets it already has are replaced by the new radius; radius 0 takes them out. Corners whose sides are too short for the radius are left and counted. Select second object or shift-select to apply corner [Radius]. Hold Shift as you click for a sharp corner, whatever the radius. Click each object on the part you want to keep. Two lines that do not meet are extended. Two parallel lines get a half circle across the gap, at the end of the first line nearest your click. A circle is never cut. Two segments of one polyline next to each other get the arc inside the polyline; a polyline and another object work when the cut lands on the polyline's first or last segment. CHAMFER Aliases: CHA. Lines and the straight segments of polylines. Prints the current distances (or length and angle) first. Select first line [Undo/Polyline/Distance/Angle/Trim/mEthod/Multiple] D asks Specify first chamfer distance \u003Clast\u003E and Specify second chamfer distance \u003Cthe first\u003E: the first is cut from the first line you pick, the second from the other. A asks Specify chamfer length on the first line and Specify chamfer angle from the first line. E (mEthod) chooses which of the two the next chamfer uses. T, M, U and P work as in FILLET. Select second line or shift-select to apply corner [Distance/Angle/mEthod]. Shift+click makes a sharp corner. A distance longer than the line is refused (Distance is too large.). BREAK and BREAKATPOINT Aliases: BR. Select object (where you click is the first point), Specify second break point [First point]; F asks Specify first break point and Specify second break point. The part between goes; on a circle, counter-clockwise from the first point. BREAKATPOINT asks Specify break point and splits the object in two there (not a circle). LENGTHEN Aliases: LEN. Select an object to measure [DElta/Percent/Total/DYnamic]: a pick prints Current length (and an arc's included angle) and asks again. DE asks Enter delta length (negative shortens) [Angle], P Enter percentage length, T Specify total length [Angle], DY drags. Then Select an object to change [Undo], repeated: the end nearest the click moves. Lines, arcs and open polylines. JOIN Aliases: J. Select objects to join. Lines, arcs and open polylines that meet end to end become polylines (closed when the chain closes); lines on one line become one line; arcs of one circle become one arc or the circle. The history says how many objects joined and how many did not. MATCHPROP Aliases: MA, PAINTER. Select source object, then Select destination objects [Settings] and Enter. Copies layer and color, and between objects of the same kind the text height and width, the dimension sizes, or the hatch pattern. S asks Turn a property on or off [Color/LAyer/Text/Dimension/Hatch], repeated until Enter; the choice is remembered for the session. EXPLODE Aliases: X. Polylines become lines and arcs, block references become their objects, dimensions become lines, arrowheads and text. Select objects Clipboard CadCraft's own clipboard: it works within a drawing, between drawings and between CadCraft tabs in this browser. See Copy, paste and duplicate. COPYCLIP Ctrl+C, or Copy in the Edit group. Copies objects to the clipboard, with the blocks and layers they use. The objects stay selected. Select objects to copy (skipped when objects are already selected) CUTCLIP Ctrl+X, or Cut in the More menu. Copies objects to the clipboard, then erases them (one undo step). Select objects to cut (skipped when objects are already selected) COPYBASE Copies objects to the clipboard with a base point you pick; pasting places that point. Specify base point Select objects to copy PASTECLIP Ctrl+V, or Paste in the Edit group. The copy follows the cursor, hanging from the lower-left corner of its extents (or its center, or the COPYBASE point; see CadCraft settings). Specify insertion point The pasted objects are selected. A block the drawing already has under the same name keeps this drawing's definition. PASTEORIG Ctrl+Shift+V, or Paste in place in the More menu. Pastes at the coordinates the objects were copied from. Asks nothing. DUPLICATE Aliases: DUP. Ctrl+D, or Duplicate and move in the More menu. A copy of the objects moves with the pointer from where it is; Esc leaves nothing. Does not use the clipboard. Select objects to duplicate (skipped when objects are already selected) Specify where the copy goes (Esc leaves nothing): click, type a point, @dx,dy (from where the pointer was), or a distance toward the cursor. SELECT Asks Select objects (with every option below) and leaves what you chose selected, for the next command or for P later. QSELECT Aliases: QS. In the browser, the Quick select dialog (see Select objects). Without it, the command line asks Enter object type, Enter property, Enter operator [=/\u003C\u003E/\u003E/\u003C/*/Select all] \u003C=\u003E and Enter value. The matches become the selection. SELECTSIMILAR Aliases: SIM. Select objects [SEttings] (or the current selection): selects every object of the same type with the same layer and color (and block name, hatch pattern). SE asks Turn a property on or off [LAyer/COlor/NAme/PAttern/HEight], repeated until Enter. FIND No aliases; or Edit › More › Find and replace. In the browser, the Find and replace dialog (see Add text and labels). Without it: Enter text to find, then Enter replacement text (Enter: select what is found). Looks in text, attribute values and dimension text, on the open tab or in the selection. SELECTALL and DESELECT SELECTALL (alias AI_SELALL, Ctrl+A) selects every object you can edit on the open tab. DESELECT (aliases AI_DESELECT, SELECTNONE; or Esc) clears the selection. Dimensions DIMLINEAR Aliases: DLI, DIMLIN. Horizontal or vertical distance. Specify first extension line origin [Select object] (Enter or S: Select object to dimension, then click a line, arc, circle or polyline segment) Specify second extension line origin Specify dimension line location [Text/Horizontal/Vertical/Rotated]. T asks for text (\u003C\u003E is the measured value), H and V fix the direction, R asks Specify angle of dimension line \u003C0\u003E. DIMALIGNED Aliases: DAL, DIMALI. The true distance between two points. Origins as in DIMLINEAR, then Specify dimension line location [Text]. DIMANGULAR Aliases: DAN, DIMANG. Select arc, circle or line, or Enter for a vertex Arc: its angle. Circle: Specify second angle endpoint. Line: Select second line. Enter: Specify angle vertex, Specify first angle endpoint, Specify second angle endpoint. Specify dimension line location [Text]: click inside the angle to measure. DIMRADIUS and DIMDIAMETER Aliases: DRA, DIMRAD; DDI, DIMDIA. Select arc or circle, then Specify dimension line location [Text]. DIMCONTINUE Aliases: DCO, DIMCONT. Continues from the last linear or aligned dimension made (or asks Select linear or aligned dimension). Specify second extension line origin [Undo], repeated until Enter. DIMBASELINE Aliases: DBA, DIMBASE. Like DIMCONTINUE, but every dimension starts from the first origin and is stacked further out. DIMSCALE, DIMTXT, DIMASZ, DIMDEC, DIMGAP, DIMEXE, DIMEXO No aliases. Each asks Enter new value for … \u003Ccurrent\u003E and sets that size for new dimensions: overall scale, text height, arrow size, decimal places (up to 8), gap round the text, extension beyond the dimension line, offset from the origin. See Set dimension sizes. DIMLUNIT Dimension setting: the format of new dimensions only (1 scientific, 2 decimal, 3 engineering, 4 architectural, 5 fractional). REVCLOUD Aliases: RC. Prints Arc length: … Type: …, then Specify first point [Arc length/Object/Rectangular/Polygonal/Freehand]. Rectangular asks Specify opposite corner; Polygonal Specify next point (Enter closes the cloud) [Undo]; Freehand follows the pointer until a click or Enter; O asks Select object and Reverse direction? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E and turns the object into the cloud. A asks Specify the length of each arc. R, P and F choose the type for the next cloud. The result is a closed polyline of arcs. MLEADER Aliases: MLD; or Leader. Specify leader arrowhead location [Arrowhead size/Landing distance], Specify leader landing location, then the text in the in-place box (or, without it, Enter text (an empty line ends)). Saved as a real multileader in DWG; DXF keeps the lines, arrowhead and text it draws. Parametric See Constrain a drawing and Use parameters and equations. Constraints work on the Model tab. GEOMCONSTRAINT and the GC commands Alias GCON: Enter constraint type [Horizontal/Vertical/Perpendicular/PArallel/Tangent/Coincident/CONcentric/COLlinear/Symmetric/Equal/Fix] \u003CHorizontal\u003E, then that command's prompts. GCHORIZONTAL, GCVERTICAL: Select an object [2Points] \u003C2Points\u003E. GCPARALLEL, GCPERPENDICULAR, GCCOLLINEAR, GCCONCENTRIC, GCEQUAL, GCTANGENT: Select first object, Select second object (the second one moves). GCCOINCIDENT: Select first point [Object/Autoconstrain] \u003CObject\u003E, Select second point [Object] \u003CObject\u003E. GCFIX: Select point [Object] \u003CObject\u003E. GCSYMMETRIC: Select first object [2Points] \u003C2Points\u003E, Select second object, Select symmetry line. DIMCONSTRAINT and the DC commands Alias DCON: Select associative dimension to convert [LInear/Horizontal/Vertical/ALigned/ANgular/Radial/Diameter/Convert] \u003CALigned\u003E. DCLINEAR, DCHORIZONTAL, DCVERTICAL: Specify first constraint point [Object] \u003CObject\u003E, Specify second constraint point, Specify dimension line location, Dimension text \u003Cd1=40\u003E (a number, an expression, or name=expression). DCALIGNED: as above, with [Object/Point & line/2Lines]. DCRADIUS, DCDIAMETER: Select arc or circle. DCANGULAR: Select first line or arc, Select second line. DCCONVERT: Select dimensions to convert. AUTOCONSTRAIN, DELCONSTRAINT, CONSTRAINTBAR, DCDISPLAY AUTOCONSTRAIN: Select objects [Settings]. S asks the distance and angle tolerances. DELCONSTRAINT (alias DELCON): Select objects; their constraints go. CONSTRAINTBAR (alias CBAR): Select objects [Showall/Hideall], then [Show/Hide]. DCDISPLAY: Enter an option [Show/Hide]. PARAMETERS and -PARAMETERS PARAMETERS (aliases PAR, PARAM) opens the Parameters panel. -PARAMETERS: Enter an option [New/Edit/Rename/Delete/?] \u003C?\u003E. SWBOUT Alias SWMACRO. Downloads a macro (.swb) that rebuilds the model as one constrained sketch in SolidWorks. See Move sketches to and from other part modelers. Blocks BLOCK Aliases: B, BMAKE. Enter block name. If it exists: Block \"…\" already exists. Redefine it? [Yes/No] \u003CN\u003E. Specify insertion base point Select objects. They are replaced by one reference to the new block. ATTDEF Aliases: ATT, DDATTDEF, -ATTDEF; or Blocks › More › Define attribute. Prints the modes, then Enter an option to change (Enter when done) [Invisible/Constant/Preset], Enter attribute tag name, Enter attribute prompt, Enter default attribute value (Enter attribute value when constant), Specify start point of text, Specify text height, Specify rotation angle of text. BLOCK takes attribute definitions into the block. BEDIT and BCLOSE BE; or Blocks › More › Edit block. Enter block name to edit \u003Cthe selected reference's block\u003E opens the block editor. BCLOSE asks Close the block editor on \"…\" [Save/Discard] \u003CSave\u003E; the banner's Save block and Discard changes do the same. Everything done in the editor is one undo step. VIEWBASE Alias: FLATSHOT. A flat 2D view of the solids on the 3D tab: View [Front/Top/Right/Left/BAck/BOttom/Iso], Hidden lines [Show/Hide], a scale (1:20, 2:1; Enter takes the one that fits the sheet), then the view's center. One block reference, visible edges on VIEW-VISIBLE, hidden ones dashed on VIEW-HIDDEN. See Flat views on a sheet. PUBLISH Aliases: -PUBLISH; or All sheets in one PDF in the Plot to PDF dialog. Sheets to plot [All/Some]: Enter plots every sheet, S asks for names separated by commas. One PDF, a page and a bookmark per sheet. See Every sheet in one PDF. SHEETSET Aliases: SSM, SHEETSETMANAGER. Brings you to the Sheet set section of the left panel: sheets from many drawings, numbered and titled, their title blocks filled from the set, published as one PDF. See Sheet sets. XATTACH, XREF XATTACH (XA): pick a DWG or DXF, then its insertion point. XREF (XR, -XREF): Linked drawings [?/Attach/Reload/Bind/Detach] (Enter: ?); with more than one link, pick one of its references or type N and its name. See Linked drawings. COUNT Aliases: BCOUNT; or Blocks › More › Count blocks. Select a block reference to count, or [All/Export] (Enter: All): a pick selects and counts every reference of that block in this space; Enter or A lists them all; E then C (counts) or A (attributes) downloads a CSV. See Count blocks and export a schedule. INSERT Aliases: I, DDINSERT. Enter block name or [File/Library] \u003Clast block\u003E (case does not matter). F asks for a DWG or DXF, then the whole drawing or one of its blocks; a name already used asks Keep existing or Rename. L opens the library panel. Specify insertion point [Scale/Rotate]. S asks Specify scale factor, R asks Specify rotation angle; both are remembered. Then Enter \u003Cprompt\u003E (\u003CTAG\u003E) \u003Cdefault\u003E for each attribute the block asks for. See Block attributes. LIBRARY Aliases: LIB. Opens the library panel: place items, check for updates, library credits. No questions. See Insert from a file or the library. PURGE Aliases: PU. Removes block definitions that nothing uses. No questions. DELBLOCKS Or Blocks › More › Delete blocks. Select block to delete [Name/eXit]: pick a block reference, or N for the list of blocks with a filter (choose several), or X / Enter to end. Every reference goes — in the model, on every sheet and inside other blocks — and then the definition, and the command line says what was deleted and what couldn't be. Unnamed blocks and linked drawings are not offered. The prompt comes back for the next block; the whole command is one undo step. Sheets and plotting LAYOUT Aliases: LO, NEWLAYOUT; or the + button under the drawing. A new sheet with a border, a title block and one viewport, on ISO A3 (or Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet). No questions. MVIEW Aliases: MV. On a sheet only (VPORTS on a sheet does the same). Specify corner of viewport, Specify opposite corner. The viewport shows the whole model at a standard scale. VPORTS Aliases: VIEWPORTS. SPLIT is the same from a sheet too, and View › More › Split side by side, Split stacked, Four panes, One pane run it. On the model: Enter number of panes [SIngle/2/3/4] \u003C3\u003E; 2 asks [Horizontal/Vertical] \u003CVertical\u003E (Vertical = side by side), 3 asks [Left/Right] \u003CRight\u003E (where the big pane goes). Each pane keeps its own view and space; a click makes a pane active. On a sheet it is MVIEW. See Split the screen. VPVIEW No aliases. On a sheet only. Select viewport (skipped if one is selected), then on the model: Specify first corner of the window to show, Specify opposite corner. PLOT Aliases: PRINT, PUBLISH, EXPORTPDF; or Ctrl+P. Opens the Plot to PDF dialog. See Plot to PDF. Inquiry DIST Aliases: DI; or View › More › Measure distance. Specify first point, Specify second point. Prints distance, angle, Delta X and Delta Y. AREA Aliases: AA; or View › More › Area. Prints the area and perimeter. Specify first corner point [Object/Add area/Subtract area] \u003CObject\u003E Click or type corners; at Specify next point (Enter closes) [Undo] press Enter to close the outline. U takes back the last corner. Enter or O, then Select object: a circle (prints its circumference), an ellipse, a polyline (an open one is closed by a straight line for the area, and its length is printed) or a hatch (holes are taken off). A (Add area) and S (Subtract area) keep a running total: the prompt starts with (ADD mode) or (SUBTRACT mode), each area is added or taken off, and Total area = … is printed after each. In these modes O picks objects until Enter, and Enter at the first prompt ends the command. MEASUREGEOM Aliases: MEA. Enter an option [Distance/Radius/Angle/ARea] \u003CDistance\u003E; after each measurement it asks again, with eXit the default. D: two points, as DIST. R: Select arc or circle; prints the radius and diameter. A: an arc (its included angle), a line and then a second line (the angle between them, toward their far ends), or Enter and three points (vertex, then the two ends). AR: as AREA, one area. ID No aliases. Specify point; prints its X and Y. LIST Aliases: LI, LS. Select objects; prints each object's type, layer and main measurements. View, file and other ZOOM Aliases: Z. Specify corner of window, enter a scale factor (nX) [Extents/All/Window/Previous/Scale/Center]. Click two corners for a window. E, A or Enter: the whole drawing. P (Previous, or Previous in the toolbar) goes back to the view before, up to 10 views per tab; a run of wheel steps or one pan counts as one view. A number such as 2, 2x or 0.5x (or S and then the number) zooms in or out by that factor round the middle of the screen. C asks Specify center point and Enter height or magnification (nX). NAMEDVIEW Aliases: NV, VIEWS. Enter an option [?/Restore/Save/Window/Delete] \u003CRestore\u003E, then the view's name. S saves the view on screen, W a window you pick, R shows a saved view, D deletes one, ? lists the views of this tab. Saving under an existing name replaces it; each change is one undo step. Views belong to the tab they were saved on. DWG and DXF files keep them (the VIEW table); a sheet's views come back on the first sheet, since the file does not say which. UNDO and REDO Aliases: U for UNDO; REDO has none. Also Ctrl+Z and Ctrl+Y. LAYER Aliases: LA. Jumps to the Layers panel. LINETYPE, LWEIGHT, LTSCALE LINETYPE (LT): Enter linetype for new objects — ByLayer, ByBlock or a name (? lists them: Continuous, the drawing's own, and DASHED, HIDDEN, CENTER, PHANTOM, DOT, DASHDOT, BORDER, DIVIDE with their 2 (half) and X2 (double) sizes). LWEIGHT (LW): the lineweight for new objects in mm, or ByLayer, ByBlock, Default. LTSCALE (LTS): the drawing's linetype scale (undoable). LWDISPLAY (LWT): Show lineweights [ON/OFF] — the same as the LWT button in the status bar. See Line weights and linetypes. RENAME, LAYMRG, LAYDEL RENAME (-RENAME, REN): Enter object type to rename [LAyer/Block], Enter old name, then the new name; objects or references follow. LAYMRG: Select object on layer to merge or [Name] until Enter, then Select object on target layer or [Name] and Y. LAYDEL: Select object on layer to delete or [Name], then Y: the layer and every object on it go (not layer 0, DEFPOINTS or the current layer). One undo step each. See Organize a drawing with layers. LAYISO, LAYUNISO, LAYOFF, LAYFRZ, LAYMCUR, LAYON, LAYTHW Layers by picking objects; also in the Layers panel's Tools menu. LAYISO: Select objects on the layer(s) to be isolated, then Enter: every other layer goes off. LAYUNISO turns them back on. LAYOFF and LAYFRZ: Select an object on the layer to be turned off (or frozen), over and over; U takes back the last one, Enter ends. LAYMCUR: Select object whose layer will become current. LAYON turns every layer on, LAYTHW thaws every layer. Each command is one undo step. See Organize a drawing with layers. NEW, OPEN, SAVE, SAVEAS, DXFOUT NEW starts an empty drawing and OPEN opens a DWG or DXF, each in its own tab over the drawing (an untouched new drawing is used instead of a new tab). Closing a tab with unsaved changes asks first. See Split the screen. SAVE (alias QSAVE, also Ctrl+S) downloads a copy in the drawing's format: DWG if it was opened from a DWG, DXF if from a DXF. A new drawing uses the format last chosen in Save as in this browser, else DWG. SAVEAS (also Ctrl+Shift+S) opens the Save a copy as dialog: choose DWG (and its Version), DXF R12 or DXF 2000, then Save. The choice sticks for this drawing and for new drawings in this browser. DXFOUT always downloads a DXF R12 copy, without changing the format SAVE uses. See Files: what opens, what saves. POLARANG Enter polar angle step in degrees \u003C90\u003E: the step Polar tracking lines up at (more than 0, up to 180). Kept in this browser. UNITS Aliases: UN. How lengths are written and typed. Enter length format [Scientific/Decimal/Engineering/Architectural/Fractional] \u003Ccurrent\u003E Architectural and Fractional: Enter denominator of smallest fraction to display (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128 or 256). The others: Enter number of decimal places (0 to 8). New dimensions follow it. One undo step. Same as Length format and Precision in Current settings; see Feet and inches. APPLOAD Aliases: AP; or View › More › Load application…. The Load applications dialog: load .lsp routines and .dcl dialog files, unload them, load them again from the history, and the Startup Suite. A routine's (defun c:NAME …) commands then work like any other. See Load applications and Run LISP routines. ALIASEDIT, REINIT ALIASEDIT (or View › More › Edit aliases…) opens your aliases file (ALIAS, *COMMAND lines); Save applies it. REINIT applies it again. See Edit command aliases. HELP Aliases: ?. Prints every command, its aliases and a one-line description in the command line history. It does not open this help: press F1 or click Help for that. Select objects prompt At any Select objects prompt: click objects, draw windows (left to right) and crossings (right to left) with two clicks, or press and drag a lasso (Space while dragging: window, crossing, fence), Shift+click to remove, and press Enter to finish. You can also type ALL (everything), L (the last object), P (Previous: what the last command used), F (Fence: objects a line of points crosses; U takes back a point), W (Window), C (Crossing), R (Remove mode: picks take objects out; the prompt reads Remove objects) and A (Add again). ? lists them. They are not shown in the prompt. Tab over overlapping objects highlights the next one; click to pick it. See Select objects."},{"slug":"coordinate-entry","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Coordinate and distance entry","summary":"Every way to type a point, a distance, an angle or a number at the CadCraft command line, with examples.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["dynamic input","dyn","f12","tab","coordinates","absolute","relative","polar","@","direct distance","angle","degrees","last point","lastpoint","typing points","input"],"headings":[{"id":"points","text":"Points","level":2},{"id":"the-last-point","text":"The last point","level":2},{"id":"direct-distance-entry","text":"Direct distance entry","level":2},{"id":"angles","text":"Angles","level":2},{"id":"distances","text":"Distances","level":2},{"id":"dynamic-input","text":"Dynamic input","level":2},{"id":"options-and-defaults","text":"Options and defaults","level":2},{"id":"the-coordinate-display","text":"The coordinate display","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"points\"\u003EPoints\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the prompt asks for a point, type one of these and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeaning\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EExample\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbsolute: measured from the origin 0,0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E2500,1200\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E#x,y\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlso absolute (accepted for habit)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E#2500,1200\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERelative: across and up from the last point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@d&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERelative polar: distance and angle from the last point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@1500&lt;45\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ed&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAbsolute polar: distance and angle from the origin\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E3000&lt;90\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea single number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDirect distance: that far from the base point, toward the crosshair\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1200\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr click in the drawing; object snaps and Ortho apply to clicks.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERules:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA comma separates X from Y. Decimals use a period: \u003Ccode\u003E1250.75,300\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENegative values go left and down: \u003Ccode\u003E@-600,0\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDo not type spaces: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E sends the line, like \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENumbers may use exponents: \u003Ccode\u003E1e3\u003C/code\u003E is 1000.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENo units are typed, and no feet-and-inch notation. Type plain numbers in drawing units.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-last-point\"\u003EThe last point\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E counts from the \u003Cstrong\u003Elast point you gave at any point prompt\u003C/strong\u003E, in any command: a click, a snap or a typed point. It is remembered between commands, so you can start a new line \u003Ccode\u003E@0,500\u003C/code\u003E away from where the last one ended. Before you have given any point, \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E counts from 0,0.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome places where this matters:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E, after the first copy the last point is that copy&#039;s point. \u003Ccode\u003E@600,0\u003C/code\u003E twice places copies 600 and 1200 from the original.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter you place a dimension, the last point is where you placed the dimension line, not the dimension&#039;s end.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen in doubt, use an absolute point, a snap, or a direct distance.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"direct-distance-entry\"\u003EDirect distance entry\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA bare number is a distance from the command&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003Ebase point\u003C/strong\u003E (the point the rubber band starts from) toward the crosshair:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMove the crosshair in the direction you want.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the distance and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E on, the direction snaps to straight across or straight up and down, which makes this the fastest way to draw square walls. If there is no base point, or the crosshair sits exactly on it, the number is refused with &quot;Point or option keyword required.&quot;\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"angles\"\u003EAngles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn degrees, as a plain number: \u003Ccode\u003E30\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-45\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E112.5\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EMeasured counterclockwise from the positive X direction (pointing right): 0 is right, 90 up, 180 left, 270 or -90 down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhere an angle is asked for (rotation, text angle), you can also click: the angle is measured from the base point to the click. Before a base point exists, the first click sets it and the prompt asks for a second point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"distances\"\u003EDistances\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhere a distance is asked for (radius, offset, text height, scale factor):\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Etype a positive number (in a feet-and-inch drawing also \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/units-papers-scales#feet-and-inches-and-fractions\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/units-papers-scales\" data-hash=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches\u003C/a\u003E), or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eclick a point: the distance from the command&#039;s base point to it, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eclick two points when there is no base point: the prompt changes to \u003Ccode\u003ESpecify second point\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E&quot;Requires a positive number or two points.&quot; means the value was not a number, or was negative.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dynamic-input\"\u003EDynamic input\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDyn\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F12\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF12\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, in the status bar) puts the values by the crosshair while a command asks for a point.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith no base point yet (a line&#039;s first point), the two fields are \u003Cstrong\u003EX\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EY\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAfter that, they are the \u003Cstrong\u003Edistance\u003C/strong\u003E and the \u003Cstrong\u003Eangle\u003C/strong\u003E from the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ETyping still goes to the command line; the field by the crosshair shows it, and the rubber band follows it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a value: it goes into the highlighted field.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E (or a comma after X). The value is locked (shown bold) and the next field is highlighted. A locked distance keeps the rubber band that long while you aim with the mouse.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the second value, or just aim, and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E50\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E90\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E draws 50 straight up from the last point.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressing \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E after one value without \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Tab\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ETab\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works as direct distance entry. Feet and inches work in the fields too (\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E). Distance and angle prompts (a circle&#039;s radius, a rotation) show their value by the crosshair as well.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"options-and-defaults\"\u003EOptions and defaults\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOptions appear in square brackets. Type the capital letters of an option (\u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode\u003E[Diameter]\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECL\u003C/code\u003E for \u003Ccode\u003ECLose\u003C/code\u003E) and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. Longer beginnings of the word also work (\u003Ccode\u003EDIA\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA default appears in angle brackets, such as \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;500&gt;\u003C/code\u003E. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or right-click to use it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-coordinate-display\"\u003EThe coordinate display\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe status bar shows the crosshair&#039;s X and Y, after snaps and Ortho. While a command has a base point it adds \u003Ccode\u003EΔ\u003C/code\u003E with the distance and angle from that point, for example \u003Ccode\u003EΔ 1500 &lt; 30°\u003C/code\u003E: the numbers you would type as \u003Ccode\u003E@1500&lt;30\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E to print a point&#039;s coordinates and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E to measure between two points.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Points When the prompt asks for a point, type one of these and press Enter: You type Meaning Example x,y Absolute: measured from the origin 0,0 2500,1200 #x,y Also absolute (accepted for habit) #2500,1200 @dx,dy Relative: across and up from the last point @600,0 @d\u003Ca Relative polar: distance and angle from the last point @1500\u003C45 d\u003Ca Absolute polar: distance and angle from the origin 3000\u003C90 a single number Direct distance: that far from the base point, toward the crosshair 1200 Or click in the drawing; object snaps and Ortho apply to clicks. Rules: A comma separates X from Y. Decimals use a period: 1250.75,300. Negative values go left and down: @-600,0. Do not type spaces: Space sends the line, like Enter. Numbers may use exponents: 1e3 is 1000. No units are typed, and no feet-and-inch notation. Type plain numbers in drawing units. The last point @ counts from the last point you gave at any point prompt, in any command: a click, a snap or a typed point. It is remembered between commands, so you can start a new line @0,500 away from where the last one ended. Before you have given any point, @ counts from 0,0. Some places where this matters: In COPY, after the first copy the last point is that copy's point. @600,0 twice places copies 600 and 1200 from the original. After you place a dimension, the last point is where you placed the dimension line, not the dimension's end. When in doubt, use an absolute point, a snap, or a direct distance. Direct distance entry A bare number is a distance from the command's base point (the point the rubber band starts from) toward the crosshair: Move the crosshair in the direction you want. Type the distance and press Enter. With Ortho on, the direction snaps to straight across or straight up and down, which makes this the fastest way to draw square walls. If there is no base point, or the crosshair sits exactly on it, the number is refused with \"Point or option keyword required.\" Angles In degrees, as a plain number: 30, -45, 112.5. Measured counterclockwise from the positive X direction (pointing right): 0 is right, 90 up, 180 left, 270 or -90 down. Where an angle is asked for (rotation, text angle), you can also click: the angle is measured from the base point to the click. Before a base point exists, the first click sets it and the prompt asks for a second point. Distances Where a distance is asked for (radius, offset, text height, scale factor): type a positive number (in a feet-and-inch drawing also 12'6\" or 6-1/2: see Feet and inches), or click a point: the distance from the command's base point to it, or click two points when there is no base point: the prompt changes to Specify second point. \"Requires a positive number or two points.\" means the value was not a number, or was negative. Dynamic input Dyn (F12, in the status bar) puts the values by the crosshair while a command asks for a point. With no base point yet (a line's first point), the two fields are X and Y. After that, they are the distance and the angle from the last point. Typing still goes to the command line; the field by the crosshair shows it, and the rubber band follows it. Type a value: it goes into the highlighted field. Press Tab (or a comma after X). The value is locked (shown bold) and the next field is highlighted. A locked distance keeps the rubber band that long while you aim with the mouse. Type the second value, or just aim, and press Enter. 50, Tab, 90, Enter draws 50 straight up from the last point. Pressing Enter after one value without Tab works as direct distance entry. Feet and inches work in the fields too (12'6\"). Distance and angle prompts (a circle's radius, a rotation) show their value by the crosshair as well. Options and defaults Options appear in square brackets. Type the capital letters of an option (D for [Diameter], CL for CLose) and press Enter. Longer beginnings of the word also work (DIA). A default appears in angle brackets, such as \u003C500\u003E. Press Enter or right-click to use it. The coordinate display The status bar shows the crosshair's X and Y, after snaps and Ortho. While a command has a base point it adds Δ with the distance and angle from that point, for example Δ 1500 \u003C 30°: the numbers you would type as @1500\u003C30. Use ID to print a point's coordinates and DIST to measure between two points."},{"slug":"units-papers-scales","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Units, paper sizes and scales","summary":"What drawing units mean in CadCraft, the paper sizes for sheets and plots, every standard scale, and how text and dimension sizes relate to the plot scale.","section":"reference","order":40,"keywords":["units","millimeters","inches","feet","feet and inches","architectural","engineering","fractional","fractions","lunits","length format","precision","meters","paper size","a3","a1","arch d","ansi","tabloid","letter","scale","1:100","1/4 inch","architectural scale","engineering scale","insunits"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-units","text":"Drawing units","level":2},{"id":"feet-and-inches-and-fractions","text":"Feet and inches, and fractions","level":2},{"id":"paper-sizes","text":"Paper sizes","level":2},{"id":"scales","text":"Scales","level":2},{"id":"sizes-on-paper-and-in-the-model","text":"Sizes on paper and in the model","level":2},{"id":"line-weights-and-linetypes","text":"Line weights and linetypes","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-units\"\u003EDrawing units\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou draw at full size, in drawing units. What one unit stands for is the drawing&#039;s \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E setting:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected, on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMillimetres\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECentimetres\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMetres\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFeet\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChanging the units resizes nothing. It changes how scales are read on sheets and in plots, and which scales are offered. A drawing opened from a DWG or DXF keeps the units stored in the file; the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDrawing\u003C/span\u003E section of the left panel shows them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnitless\u003C/span\u003E drawing is treated as being in the paper&#039;s unit: millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"feet-and-inches-and-fractions\"\u003EFeet and inches, and fractions\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHow lengths are \u003Cstrong\u003Ewritten and typed\u003C/strong\u003E is a separate setting, the length format. It changes no geometry, only how numbers look and what you can type.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E so nothing is selected, on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength format\u003C/span\u003E: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDecimal\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E150.5\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6 1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6.5&quot;\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFractional\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E150 1/2\u003C/code\u003E) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScientific\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E1.505E+02\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrecision\u003C/span\u003E: decimal places, or the smallest fraction (1/16 is the usual choice for architectural).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOr type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNITS\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EUN\u003C/code\u003E) and answer its two questions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EArchitectural\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEngineering\u003C/span\u003E, one drawing unit is one inch, as in AutoCAD: set \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E to \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EInches\u003C/span\u003E too. You can then type lengths in feet and inches anywhere a length is asked for, and in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E boxes:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou type\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;-6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6 inches (150)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;6-1/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet 6½ inches. Use a hyphen before the fraction: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E at the command line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E12&#039;\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E12 feet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E6&quot;\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E6\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E6 inches: a plain number is inches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E1/2\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E6-1/2\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efractions work in any format\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPoints take the same lengths: \u003Ccode\u003E@12&#039;6&quot;,0\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@10&#039;&lt;90\u003C/code\u003E. Angles stay in degrees.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe format shows in the history (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIST\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EID\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELIST\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E: areas as square inches and square feet), in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, in the coordinates on the status bar, and in \u003Cstrong\u003Enew dimensions\u003C/strong\u003E. Sheets keep their paper units (millimeters or inches) as decimals.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe length format is saved in DWG and DXF files, and each dimension keeps its own: a saved drawing opens again as you left it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"paper-sizes\"\u003EPaper sizes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESheets and model plots use these sizes, in either orientation:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EPaper\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESize\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EUnit on the sheet\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A4\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E297 × 210 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A3\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E420 × 297 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E594 × 420 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A1\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E841 × 594 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO A0\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E1189 × 841 mm\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emillimeters\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELetter\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E11 × 8.5 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELegal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E14 × 8.5 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETabloid / ANSI B\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E17 × 11 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EANSI C\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E22 × 17 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EANSI D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E34 × 22 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EARCH C\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E24 × 18 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EARCH D\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E36 × 24 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EARCH E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E48 × 36 in\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENew sheets start on ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, and on Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet. Model plots start on ISO A4 or Letter the same way, then remember your last choice. Sheets from opened files keep their own size, shown as &quot;Custom&quot; when it is not one of these.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scales\"\u003EScales\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA scale \u003Ccode\u003E1:n\u003C/code\u003E means one unit on paper shows n of the same unit in the model, so 1:100 shows 1 m of building as 10 mm of paper. CadCraft works this out from the drawing units and the paper&#039;s unit.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMetric scales\u003C/strong\u003E, offered for every drawing: 10:1, 5:1, 2:1, 1:1, 1:2, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000, 1:1250, 1:2000, 1:2500, 1:5000, 1:10000.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EArchitectural scales\u003C/strong\u003E, offered when the units are inches or feet: 3&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1-1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/2&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/8&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 3/32&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;, 1/16&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEngineering scales\u003C/strong\u003E, offered when the units are inches or feet: 1&quot; = 10&#039;, 1&quot; = 20&#039;, 1&quot; = 30&#039;, 1&quot; = 40&#039;, 1&quot; = 50&#039;, 1&quot; = 100&#039;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a viewport is aimed with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow the whole model\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick a window on the model…\u003C/span\u003E, CadCraft picks the largest standard scale that fits. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit the whole model\u003C/span\u003E sets an exact scale instead, which reads as a plain ratio.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sizes-on-paper-and-in-the-model\"\u003ESizes on paper and in the model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EText and dimension sizes are in the units of the space you draw in.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EYou want on paper\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrawn on a sheet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrawn in the model at 1:50 (mm drawing)\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDrawn in the model at 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot; (inch drawing)\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5 mm text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E2.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E125\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E3/32&quot; text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E0.09375\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E4.5\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFor dimensions, keep the sizes as paper sizes and set the overall scale (\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E) to the plot scale: 50 for 1:50, 48 for 1/4&quot; = 1&#039;-0&quot;. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"line-weights-and-linetypes\"\u003ELine weights and linetypes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA layer, or an object, can have a \u003Cstrong\u003Elineweight\u003C/strong\u003E (0.05 to 2.11 mm) and a \u003Cstrong\u003Elinetype\u003C/strong\u003E (dashed, hidden, center, phantom, dot…). Set them in the right panel: with nothing selected, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E are what new objects get (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy layer\u003C/span\u003E to follow their layer), and the two under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayer …\u003C/span\u003E are the current layer&#039;s own. With objects selected, the same two rows and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype scale\u003C/span\u003E change those objects. The commands are \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELINETYPE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELT\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWEIGHT\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELW\u003C/code\u003E) and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELTSCALE\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELTS\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELinetypes show on screen and in plots. Their dashes are in drawing units: a metric drawing uses the ISO sizes (DASHED: 12.7 mm dashes), an inch drawing the inch ones (0.5&quot;). If the dashes look too small or too large, change the drawing&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELinetype scale\u003C/span\u003E (LTSCALE): every dash and gap is multiplied by it; an object&#039;s own scale multiplies it again. A pattern too fine to see at all is drawn as a solid line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELineweights print in plots, in mm on the paper whatever the scale. Lines with the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E lineweight use the pen width chosen in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog (0.13 to 0.70 mm; 0.25 mm to start with); a lineweight of 0 prints as the thinnest line. On screen every line is one pixel wide until you press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar (or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELWDISPLAY\u003C/code\u003E): then a lineweight above 0.25 mm draws wider (0.5 mm = 2 pixels) in the model, and at its true width on the paper on a sheet. The choice is kept in this browser.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Drawing units You draw at full size, in drawing units. What one unit stands for is the drawing's Units setting: Press Esc so nothing is selected, on the Model tab. In Current settings, choose Units: Unitless, Millimetres, Centimetres, Metres, Inches or Feet. Changing the units resizes nothing. It changes how scales are read on sheets and in plots, and which scales are offered. A drawing opened from a DWG or DXF keeps the units stored in the file; the Drawing section of the left panel shows them. A Unitless drawing is treated as being in the paper's unit: millimeters on ISO paper, inches on US paper. Feet and inches, and fractions How lengths are written and typed is a separate setting, the length format. It changes no geometry, only how numbers look and what you can type. Press Esc so nothing is selected, on the Model tab. In Current settings, choose Length format: Decimal (150.5), Architectural (12'-6 1/2\"), Engineering (12'-6.5\"), Fractional (150 1/2) or Scientific (1.505E+02). Choose Precision: decimal places, or the smallest fraction (1/16 is the usual choice for architectural). Or type UNITS (UN) and answer its two questions. With Architectural or Engineering, one drawing unit is one inch, as in AutoCAD: set Units to Inches too. You can then type lengths in feet and inches anywhere a length is asked for, and in the Properties boxes: You type Means 12'6\" or 12'-6\" 12 feet 6 inches (150) 12'6-1/2\" 12 feet 6½ inches. Use a hyphen before the fraction: Space is Enter at the command line. 12' 12 feet 6\" or 6 6 inches: a plain number is inches 1/2, 6-1/2 fractions work in any format Points take the same lengths: @12'6\",0 or @10'\u003C90. Angles stay in degrees. The format shows in the history (DIST, ID, LIST, AREA: areas as square inches and square feet), in Properties, in the coordinates on the status bar, and in new dimensions. Sheets keep their paper units (millimeters or inches) as decimals. The length format is saved in DWG and DXF files, and each dimension keeps its own: a saved drawing opens again as you left it. Paper sizes Sheets and model plots use these sizes, in either orientation: Paper Size Unit on the sheet ISO A4 297 × 210 mm millimeters ISO A3 420 × 297 mm millimeters ISO A2 594 × 420 mm millimeters ISO A1 841 × 594 mm millimeters ISO A0 1189 × 841 mm millimeters Letter 11 × 8.5 in inches Legal 14 × 8.5 in inches Tabloid / ANSI B 17 × 11 in inches ANSI C 22 × 17 in inches ANSI D 34 × 22 in inches ARCH C 24 × 18 in inches ARCH D 36 × 24 in inches ARCH E 48 × 36 in inches New sheets start on ISO A3 for metric and unitless drawings, and on Tabloid for drawings in inches or feet. Model plots start on ISO A4 or Letter the same way, then remember your last choice. Sheets from opened files keep their own size, shown as \"Custom\" when it is not one of these. Scales A scale 1:n means one unit on paper shows n of the same unit in the model, so 1:100 shows 1 m of building as 10 mm of paper. CadCraft works this out from the drawing units and the paper's unit. Metric scales, offered for every drawing: 10:1, 5:1, 2:1, 1:1, 1:2, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:25, 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:250, 1:500, 1:1000, 1:1250, 1:2000, 1:2500, 1:5000, 1:10000. Architectural scales, offered when the units are inches or feet: 3\" = 1'-0\", 1-1/2\" = 1'-0\", 1\" = 1'-0\", 3/4\" = 1'-0\", 1/2\" = 1'-0\", 3/8\" = 1'-0\", 1/4\" = 1'-0\", 3/16\" = 1'-0\", 1/8\" = 1'-0\", 3/32\" = 1'-0\", 1/16\" = 1'-0\". Engineering scales, offered when the units are inches or feet: 1\" = 10', 1\" = 20', 1\" = 30', 1\" = 40', 1\" = 50', 1\" = 100'. When a viewport is aimed with Show the whole model or Pick a window on the model…, CadCraft picks the largest standard scale that fits. Fit the whole model sets an exact scale instead, which reads as a plain ratio. Sizes on paper and in the model Text and dimension sizes are in the units of the space you draw in. You want on paper Drawn on a sheet Drawn in the model at 1:50 (mm drawing) Drawn in the model at 1/4\" = 1'-0\" (inch drawing) 2.5 mm text 2.5 125 — 3/32\" text 0.09375 — 4.5 For dimensions, keep the sizes as paper sizes and set the overall scale (DIMSCALE) to the plot scale: 50 for 1:50, 48 for 1/4\" = 1'-0\". See Set dimension sizes. Line weights and linetypes A layer, or an object, can have a lineweight (0.05 to 2.11 mm) and a linetype (dashed, hidden, center, phantom, dot…). Set them in the right panel: with nothing selected, Linetype and Lineweight under Current settings are what new objects get (By layer to follow their layer), and the two under Layer … are the current layer's own. With objects selected, the same two rows and Linetype scale change those objects. The commands are LINETYPE (LT), LWEIGHT (LW) and LTSCALE (LTS). Linetypes show on screen and in plots. Their dashes are in drawing units: a metric drawing uses the ISO sizes (DASHED: 12.7 mm dashes), an inch drawing the inch ones (0.5\"). If the dashes look too small or too large, change the drawing's Linetype scale (LTSCALE): every dash and gap is multiplied by it; an object's own scale multiplies it again. A pattern too fine to see at all is drawn as a solid line. Lineweights print in plots, in mm on the paper whatever the scale. Lines with the Default lineweight use the pen width chosen in the Plot to PDF dialog (0.13 to 0.70 mm; 0.25 mm to start with); a lineweight of 0 prints as the thinnest line. On screen every line is one pixel wide until you press LWT in the status bar (or type LWDISPLAY): then a lineweight above 0.25 mm draws wider (0.5 mm = 2 pixels) in the model, and at its true width on the paper on a sheet. The choice is kept in this browser."},{"slug":"run-lisp","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Run LISP routines","summary":"What CadCraft's LISP runs (functions, prompts, commands, objects, dialogs, vla objects), how to type LISP on the command line, and what it does not have yet.","section":"reference","order":48,"keywords":["lisp","routine","lsp","dcl","dialog","command","defun","entget","ssget","vla","vlax","vl","getpoint","error","compatibility","what works"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn","level":2},{"id":"type-lisp-on-the-command-line","text":"Type LISP on the command line","level":2},{"id":"what-runs","text":"What runs","level":2},{"id":"routines-tested","text":"Routines tested","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2},{"id":"mistakes-to-avoid","text":"Mistakes to avoid","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\"\u003EWhat you will learn\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhat to expect when you load a LISP routine written for another CAD program: which parts run in CadCraft, and what it does not have yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft has its own LISP. It was written from the public AutoLISP reference, not from anyone else&#039;s program, so most routines written for AutoCAD run unchanged. Where something is missing, the command line names the function, so you can tell a gap in CadCraft from a mistake in the routine.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"type-lisp-on-the-command-line\"\u003EType LISP on the command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType an expression that starts with \u003Ccode\u003E(\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E: \u003Ccode\u003E(+ 1 2)\u003C/code\u003E prints \u003Ccode\u003E3\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Ccode\u003E!name\u003C/code\u003E prints the value of a variable. An expression that doesn&#039;t close yet waits for the next line.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAt a prompt, an expression answers it: at &quot;Specify radius&quot; type \u003Ccode\u003E(* 2 12.5)\u003C/code\u003E for 25.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-runs\"\u003EWhat runs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe language:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Edefun\u003C/code\u003E (with locals after \u003Ccode\u003E/\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode\u003Esetq\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elambda\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eif\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Econd\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewhile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Erepeat\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eforeach\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eprogn\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eand\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eor\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Equote\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eapply\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emapcar\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eeval\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eread\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eload\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Ccode\u003E*error*\u003C/code\u003E (a local \u003Ccode\u003E*error*\u003C/code\u003E works). Names are not case-sensitive; integers and reals differ, as you expect (\u003Ccode\u003E(/ 7 2)\u003C/code\u003E is 3).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBuilt-in functions:\u003C/strong\u003E maths, lists (\u003Ccode\u003Ecar\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecdr\u003C/code\u003E and their family, \u003Ccode\u003Eassoc\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enth\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Emember\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esubst\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eappend\u003C/code\u003E…), strings (\u003Ccode\u003Estrcat\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Esubstr\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estrcase\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewcmatch\u003C/code\u003E…), conversions (\u003Ccode\u003Ertos\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eatof\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eitoa\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eangtos\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edistof\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecvunit\u003C/code\u003E), geometry (\u003Ccode\u003Epolar\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edistance\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eangle\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Einters\u003C/code\u003E) and files (\u003Ccode\u003Eopen\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eread-line\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ewrite-line\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eclose\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efindfile\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECommands:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E(defun c:NAME …)\u003C/code\u003E makes a command. \u003Ccode\u003E(command …)\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E(command-s …)\u003C/code\u003E drive CadCraft&#039;s own commands, with \u003Ccode\u003Epause\u003C/code\u003E for your answer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPrompts:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Egetpoint\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetcorner\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetdist\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetangle\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetreal\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetint\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetstring\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetkword\u003C/code\u003E with \u003Ccode\u003Einitget\u003C/code\u003E keywords, \u003Ccode\u003Eentsel\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enentsel\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Essget\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EC\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EWP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ECP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EX\u003C/code\u003E, with filter lists and \u003Ccode\u003E-4\u003C/code\u003E tests).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects as lists:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Eentget\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentmod\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentmake\u003C/code\u003E (blocks too), \u003Ccode\u003Eentdel\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentlast\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eentnext\u003C/code\u003E (a block reference&#039;s attributes come after it as ATTRIBs, then a SEQEND, so attribute routines work), \u003Ccode\u003Ehandent\u003C/code\u003E, selection sets, \u003Ccode\u003Etblsearch\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Etblnext\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Elayoutlist\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eregapp\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Egetvar\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Esetvar\u003C/code\u003E for the settings CadCraft has.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDialogs:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E.dcl\u003C/code\u003E files with \u003Ccode\u003Eload_dialog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Enew_dialog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eaction_tile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eset_tile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eget_tile\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estart_list\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Estart_dialog\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edone_dialog\u003C/code\u003E. Buttons, edit boxes, list boxes, popup lists, toggles, radio buttons, rows, columns and boxes all show. A routine that writes its dialog to a temporary file works too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EObjects:\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode\u003Evl-load-com\u003C/code\u003E (it does nothing; nothing needs loading), the \u003Ccode\u003Evl-\u003C/code\u003E list and string functions, \u003Ccode\u003Evl-catch-all-apply\u003C/code\u003E, and \u003Ccode\u003Evla-get-…\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode\u003Evla-put-…\u003C/code\u003E properties, \u003Ccode\u003Evla-…\u003C/code\u003E methods and \u003Ccode\u003Evlax-for\u003C/code\u003E over the Blocks, Layers and Layouts collections, model space and block definitions, with variants and safe arrays.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"routines-tested\"\u003ERoutines tested\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft&#039;s tests run a set of routines written for these checks (selection filters, prompts, commands, objects, error handlers), and one popular routine people asked for by name: Lee Mac&#039;s DelBlocks, by name through its dialog and by picking a block. CadCraft also has its own \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDELBLOCKS\u003C/code\u003E, so you don&#039;t need a routine for that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Egrread\u003C/code\u003E (tracking the mouse yourself), reactors (\u003Ccode\u003Evlr-…\u003C/code\u003E), curve functions (\u003Ccode\u003Evlax-curve-…\u003C/code\u003E), dictionaries and extended data (\u003Ccode\u003Edictsearch\u003C/code\u003E, xdata in \u003Ccode\u003Eentget\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Eentmake\u003C/code\u003E), and the Express Tools&#039; \u003Ccode\u003Eacet-…\u003C/code\u003E functions.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EImages and slides in dialogs show as blank frames.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERunning another program, the Windows registry, and files outside the ones you load: a routine can write and read its own temporary files, which last until you close the page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen a routine reaches one of these, the command line shows &quot;no function definition&quot; and the name, and says CadCraft does not have it yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mistakes-to-avoid\"\u003EMistakes to avoid\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA command with the same name as one of CadCraft&#039;s.\u003C/strong\u003E CadCraft&#039;s own command stays. Run the routine&#039;s as \u003Ccode\u003E(c:NAME)\u003C/code\u003E on the command line.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERunning LISP you don&#039;t trust.\u003C/strong\u003E A routine can change anything in the drawing, but nothing outside it: it can&#039;t reach your files, other sites or other apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"What you will learn What to expect when you load a LISP routine written for another CAD program: which parts run in CadCraft, and what it does not have yet. CadCraft has its own LISP. It was written from the public AutoLISP reference, not from anyone else's program, so most routines written for AutoCAD run unchanged. Where something is missing, the command line names the function, so you can tell a gap in CadCraft from a mistake in the routine. Type LISP on the command line Type an expression that starts with ( and press Enter: (+ 1 2) prints 3. !name prints the value of a variable. An expression that doesn't close yet waits for the next line. At a prompt, an expression answers it: at \"Specify radius\" type (* 2 12.5) for 25. What runs The language: defun (with locals after /), setq, lambda, if, cond, while, repeat, foreach, progn, and, or, quote, apply, mapcar, eval, read, load, and *error* (a local *error* works). Names are not case-sensitive; integers and reals differ, as you expect ((/ 7 2) is 3). Built-in functions: maths, lists (car, cdr and their family, assoc, nth, member, subst, append…), strings (strcat, substr, strcase, wcmatch…), conversions (rtos, atof, itoa, angtos, distof, cvunit), geometry (polar, distance, angle, inters) and files (open, read-line, write-line, close, findfile). Commands: (defun c:NAME …) makes a command. (command …) and (command-s …) drive CadCraft's own commands, with pause for your answer. Prompts: getpoint, getcorner, getdist, getangle, getreal, getint, getstring, getkword with initget keywords, entsel, nentsel and ssget (W, C, F, WP, CP, P, L, X, with filter lists and -4 tests). Objects as lists: entget, entmod, entmake (blocks too), entdel, entlast, entnext (a block reference's attributes come after it as ATTRIBs, then a SEQEND, so attribute routines work), handent, selection sets, tblsearch and tblnext, layoutlist, regapp, getvar and setvar for the settings CadCraft has. Dialogs: .dcl files with load_dialog, new_dialog, action_tile, set_tile, get_tile, start_list, start_dialog, done_dialog. Buttons, edit boxes, list boxes, popup lists, toggles, radio buttons, rows, columns and boxes all show. A routine that writes its dialog to a temporary file works too. Objects: vl-load-com (it does nothing; nothing needs loading), the vl- list and string functions, vl-catch-all-apply, and vla-get-… / vla-put-… properties, vla-… methods and vlax-for over the Blocks, Layers and Layouts collections, model space and block definitions, with variants and safe arrays. Routines tested CadCraft's tests run a set of routines written for these checks (selection filters, prompts, commands, objects, error handlers), and one popular routine people asked for by name: Lee Mac's DelBlocks, by name through its dialog and by picking a block. CadCraft also has its own DELBLOCKS, so you don't need a routine for that. Not built yet grread (tracking the mouse yourself), reactors (vlr-…), curve functions (vlax-curve-…), dictionaries and extended data (dictsearch, xdata in entget and entmake), and the Express Tools' acet-… functions. Images and slides in dialogs show as blank frames. Running another program, the Windows registry, and files outside the ones you load: a routine can write and read its own temporary files, which last until you close the page. When a routine reaches one of these, the command line shows \"no function definition\" and the name, and says CadCraft does not have it yet. Mistakes to avoid A command with the same name as one of CadCraft's. CadCraft's own command stays. Run the routine's as (c:NAME) on the command line. Running LISP you don't trust. A routine can change anything in the drawing, but nothing outside it: it can't reach your files, other sites or other apps."},{"slug":"mouse-trackpad-touch","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Mouse, trackpad and touch","summary":"How to zoom, pan, right-click and pick in CadCraft with a wheel mouse, a Mac or PC trackpad, or a touch screen, and what differs between browsers.","section":"reference","order":50,"keywords":["trackpad","touchpad","magic mouse","touch","tablet","ipad","pan","zoom","pinch","right click","two-finger","middle button","browser","chrome","firefox","safari","edge"],"headings":[{"id":"wheel-mouse","text":"Wheel mouse","level":2},{"id":"trackpad","text":"Trackpad","level":2},{"id":"touch-screens-and-tablets","text":"Touch screens and tablets","level":2},{"id":"browsers","text":"Browsers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft is designed for a mouse with a wheel. Everything also works with a trackpad, with a few habits to learn. Touch screens work for picking, with limits.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"wheel-mouse\"\u003EWheel mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E roll the wheel. The drawing zooms about the crosshair, so point at what you want to see.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E hold the wheel down and drag, or drag with the right button.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom to everything:\u003C/strong\u003E double-click the wheel, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBack to the view before:\u003C/strong\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E in the View group, or type \u003Ccode\u003EZ\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. A run of wheel steps counts as one view, so one \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPrevious\u003C/span\u003E undoes it. Save views you come back to with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ENV\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEnter:\u003C/strong\u003E right-click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"trackpad\"\u003ETrackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E scroll with two fingers, up and down. Pinching also zooms in Chrome, Edge and Firefox.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click (Enter):\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if it does nothing. Control-click may count as an ordinary click in CadCraft rather than a right-click, so use the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E there is no middle button. Zoom out with a two-finger scroll, point at the area you want, and zoom back in: zooming follows the crosshair, so this moves you across the drawing. You can also drag with a two-finger click held down (a right-button drag).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom to an area:\u003C/strong\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E in the View group and click two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom:\u003C/strong\u003E scroll with two fingers, up and down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click (Enter):\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad, depending on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPan:\u003C/strong\u003E there is usually no middle button. Zoom out with a two-finger scroll, point at the area you want, and zoom back in: zooming follows the crosshair, so this moves you across the drawing. You can also drag with the right button held down.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EZoom to an area:\u003C/strong\u003E click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E in the View group and click two corners.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EHolding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and dragging does \u003Cstrong\u003Enot\u003C/strong\u003E pan in CadCraft: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E is Enter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESideways two-finger scrolling does nothing; only up and down zooms.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"touch-screens-and-tablets\"\u003ETouch screens and tablets\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft reads a finger as a mouse click:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA tap picks a point or an object, or selects.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging a finger on empty space draws a lasso (moving right first a window, left first a crossing; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/lasso-selection\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/lasso-selection\"\u003ELasso selection\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E, dragging a finger through pieces trims or extends them all.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDragging a selected object&#039;s grip moves the grip (grips and the pick box are bigger for a finger).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPinch\u003C/strong\u003E with two fingers to zoom about the point between them; move two fingers together to \u003Cstrong\u003Epan\u003C/strong\u003E. A second finger cancels whatever the first one began, so a pinch never leaves a mark.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Elong press\u003C/strong\u003E (hold still for about half a second) is a right click: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E. It ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder the command line a \u003Cstrong\u003Ekey row\u003C/strong\u003E appears: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E, Undo, Redo, the characters a tablet keyboard hides (\u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E,\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&lt;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&#039;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOrtho\u003C/span\u003E, a keyboard button, and a big \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E. Tap the keyboard button (or the command line) to type a command or a number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThere is no hover, so snap markers only show where your finger is.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are using a touch screen. For anything more than a quick look, an attached keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make CadCraft much easier to use.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"browsers\"\u003EBrowsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft works the same in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The differences are small:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ETopic\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat to know\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpening a DWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELarge drawings take a few seconds to read in every browser; the status line shows &quot;Reading …&quot; meanwhile\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDownloads\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEach browser decides where downloads go and whether to ask first; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen to print\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpens the PDF in a new tab. If the browser blocks pop-ups, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF7\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox may offer caret browsing; choose No\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeaving with unsaved changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery browser shows its own &quot;Leave site?&quot; question, which CadCraft cannot change\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, a PDF opened with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen to print\u003C/span\u003E may show in the tab without printing: use \u003Cstrong\u003EFile\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EPrint\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFirefox may ask what to do with each download. Choose to save the file, or change this once in Firefox&#039;s settings under Downloads.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E","text":"CadCraft is designed for a mouse with a wheel. Everything also works with a trackpad, with a few habits to learn. Touch screens work for picking, with limits. Wheel mouse Zoom: roll the wheel. The drawing zooms about the crosshair, so point at what you want to see. Pan: hold the wheel down and drag, or drag with the right button. Zoom to everything: double-click the wheel, or click Extents. Back to the view before: click Previous in the View group, or type Z, Enter, P, Enter. A run of wheel steps counts as one view, so one Previous undoes it. Save views you come back to with NAMEDVIEW (NV). Enter: right-click. Trackpad Zoom: scroll with two fingers, up and down. Pinching also zooms in Chrome, Edge and Firefox. Right-click (Enter): click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if it does nothing. Control-click may count as an ordinary click in CadCraft rather than a right-click, so use the two-finger click. Pan: there is no middle button. Zoom out with a two-finger scroll, point at the area you want, and zoom back in: zooming follows the crosshair, so this moves you across the drawing. You can also drag with a two-finger click held down (a right-button drag). Zoom to an area: click Window in the View group and click two corners. Zoom: scroll with two fingers, up and down. Right-click (Enter): click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad, depending on your touchpad settings. Pan: there is usually no middle button. Zoom out with a two-finger scroll, point at the area you want, and zoom back in: zooming follows the crosshair, so this moves you across the drawing. You can also drag with the right button held down. Zoom to an area: click Window in the View group and click two corners. Holding Space and dragging does not pan in CadCraft: Space is Enter. Sideways two-finger scrolling does nothing; only up and down zooms. Touch screens and tablets CadCraft reads a finger as a mouse click: A tap picks a point or an object, or selects. Dragging a finger on empty space draws a lasso (moving right first a window, left first a crossing; see Lasso selection). In TRIM and EXTEND, dragging a finger through pieces trims or extends them all. Dragging a selected object's grip moves the grip (grips and the pick box are bigger for a finger). Pinch with two fingers to zoom about the point between them; move two fingers together to pan. A second finger cancels whatever the first one began, so a pinch never leaves a mark. A long press (hold still for about half a second) is a right click: Enter. It ends a command, finishes a selection, or repeats the last command. Under the command line a key row appears: Esc, Undo, Redo, the characters a tablet keyboard hides (@ , \u003C ' \" -), Snap, Ortho, a keyboard button, and a big Enter. Tap the keyboard button (or the command line) to type a command or a number. There is no hover, so snap markers only show where your finger is. You are using a touch screen. For anything more than a quick look, an attached keyboard and a mouse or trackpad make CadCraft much easier to use. Browsers CadCraft works the same in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari, on Mac, Windows and Linux. The differences are small: Topic What to know Opening a DWG Large drawings take a few seconds to read in every browser; the status line shows \"Reading …\" meanwhile Downloads Each browser decides where downloads go and whether to ask first; see Browsers and computers Open to print Opens the PDF in a new tab. If the browser blocks pop-ups, CadCraft downloads the PDF instead F7 Firefox may offer caret browsing; choose No Leaving with unsaved changes Every browser shows its own \"Leave site?\" question, which CadCraft cannot change In Safari, a PDF opened with Open to print may show in the tab without printing: use File, then Print. Firefox may ask what to do with each download. Choose to save the file, or change this once in Firefox's settings under Downloads."},{"slug":"troubleshooting","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Troubleshooting","summary":"Messages CadCraft shows and problems you may meet, each with the cause and the fix.","section":"trouble","order":10,"keywords":["problem","error","not working","message","cannot open","unknown command","nothing happens","stuck","keys not working","blank","missing","fix"],"headings":[{"id":"opening-files","text":"Opening files","level":2},{"id":"the-command-line","text":"The command line","level":2},{"id":"editing","text":"Editing","level":2},{"id":"dimensions-and-blocks","text":"Dimensions and blocks","level":2},{"id":"sheets-and-plotting","text":"Sheets and plotting","level":2},{"id":"keys-and-the-screen","text":"Keys and the screen","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"opening-files\"\u003EOpening files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings. &quot;…&quot; is neither.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe file is not a \u003Ccode\u003E.dwg\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.dxf\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the drawing file itself. PDFs and images cannot be opened.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF (or open the DWG) and try again.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBinary DXF is not read.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for a text (ASCII) DXF, or the DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This file is not a readable DXF (bad group code on line …).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe DXF is damaged or not really a DXF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for the file again, or for a DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a version the reader does not handle yet (…).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe DWG reader could not read it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAsk for the DWG saved in an older version, or a DXF.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Could not open …&quot; followed by a network message, on the first DWG\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe DWG reader could not be downloaded: you are offline and CadCraft is not saved for offline use, or the connection dropped.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGo online and try again, or open a DXF. Click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for &quot;Offline ready&quot; so DWG files open offline next time. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/offline\" data-topic=\"basics/offline\"\u003EWorking offline\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline button says &quot;Could not save&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESome files did not download.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the connection and click the button to try again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offline button says &quot;Offline not available&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPrivate windows and some managed computers cannot keep an offline copy.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a normal window, or stay online.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft does not open with no connection\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIt was not saved for offline use in this browser, or the browser cleared it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENext time you are online, click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E and wait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing opens but parts are missing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EObjects CadCraft cannot draw yet, or layers that are off or frozen in the file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERead &quot;About this file&quot; in the left panel. Check the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELayers\u003C/span\u003E panel.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA viewport shows the model turned differently from the original\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwisted viewports are shown square to the sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText wraps onto two lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft draws text in a Helvetica-style font, wider than some CAD fonts.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShorten the text in Properties, or live with the extra line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-command-line\"\u003EThe command line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Unknown command &quot;…&quot;. Type HELP for the list.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo command or alias by that name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EHELP\u003C/code\u003E, or see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/commands\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/commands\"\u003ECommand reference\u003C/a\u003E. Some familiar commands are \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/not-built-yet\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/not-built-yet\"\u003Enot built yet\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No such command&quot; next to the command line while typing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you typed matches nothing yet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeep typing, or check the spelling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Point or option keyword required.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat you typed is not a point or an option.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the format: \u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E@d&lt;a\u003C/code\u003E, or a single number. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/coordinate-entry\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/coordinate-entry\"\u003ECoordinate and distance entry\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Requires a positive number or two points.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA distance was negative or not a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType a positive number, or click two points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Requires an angle in degrees or two points.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angle was not a number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType degrees as a plain number.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Requires a number.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA number was expected.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType digits only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Invalid option keyword.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe letters do not match an option.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType the capital letters shown in the square brackets.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003E*Cancel*\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E where a point was required.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStart the command again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping does not reach the command line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA box in a panel or a dialog has the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or click on the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command uses objects you did not pick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey were selected before you started it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before the command.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA line went somewhere unexpected\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode\u003Ex,y\u003C/code\u003E is absolute.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Ccode\u003E@dx,dy\u003C/code\u003E for relative points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Finish or cancel the current command first (Esc).&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while a command was running.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then save.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Could not save as DWG (AutoCAD 2000): … Try Save as, then DXF.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESomething in the drawing could not be written as DWG.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDXF R12\u003C/span\u003E and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Not saved.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou closed the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave a copy as\u003C/span\u003E dialog with \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E or \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Shift Command S\"\u003E⇧⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again and click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"editing\"\u003EEditing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn object will not select\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIts layer is locked, off or frozen; or you are on a sheet and it is the model inside a viewport.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUnlock the layer, or go to the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing found there.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo object under the click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZoom in and click on the line itself.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing crosses that object there.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E found no object crossing the piece you picked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck that the cutting line really crosses it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Cannot trim that object.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExplode blocks first, or erase and redraw.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No boundary in that direction.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXTEND\u003C/code\u003E found nothing to meet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick nearer the end that should grow.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Cannot offset that object.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOffset works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExplode it first, or draw a parallel line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Cannot offset that far.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe offset would turn the shape inside out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse a smaller distance.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Radius is too large.&quot; or &quot;Distance is too large.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe fillet or chamfer would run past the end of a line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet a smaller radius with \u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E, or smaller distances with \u003Ccode\u003ED\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe corner would cut into the middle of a polyline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or use \u003Ccode\u003EP\u003C/code\u003E for every corner.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick a second, different line.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe second pick was the same line, or not a line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick another line.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Only polylines, block references and dimensions explode.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selection had nothing to explode.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESelect a polyline, block reference or dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou tried to turn off the current layer.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick another layer&#039;s name, then turn this one off.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;There is already a layer called …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer names must be unique.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose another name.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUndo did not undo a layer change\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer settings are not undone.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange the setting back by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Nothing to undo.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is nothing left to undo, or you opened or started a drawing (that clears the history).\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E—\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"dimensions-and-blocks\"\u003EDimensions and blocks\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimension text and arrows are tiny or huge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESizes are in drawing units; the overall scale does not fit the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E to the plot scale; see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe angular dimension&#039;s location sits on a leg.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick inside the angle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Those lines are parallel.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParallel lines have no angle between them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick two lines that meet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick a linear or aligned dimension.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMCONTINUE\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMBASELINE\u003C/code\u003E needs one to start from.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPick a linear or aligned dimension.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library …&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing has no blocks of its own.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EL\u003C/code\u003E, or make a block with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No block called &quot;…&quot;.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name is wrong.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe message lists the blocks there are.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A block name cannot start with * or use …&quot; followed by a list of characters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe name has a character blocks cannot use.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse letters, digits, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003E_\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;A block cannot contain itself.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe selection holds a reference to the block being made.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELeave that reference out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;No objects selected; nothing made.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou pressed \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E before selecting.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBLOCK\u003C/code\u003E again and select the objects.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-and-plotting\"\u003ESheets and plotting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMessage or symptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Viewports go on a layout. Open a layout tab (or make one with LAYOUT) first.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMVIEW\u003C/code\u003E on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick a sheet tab first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Open the layout with the viewport first.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the sheet tab first.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA viewport scale looks ten times wrong\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing units are wrong.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUnits\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECurrent settings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;There is nothing to plot yet.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe model is empty.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw something, or open a drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Pick the window to plot.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA window…\u003C/span\u003E is chosen and no window was picked.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPick\u003C/span\u003E and pick two corners.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;… bigger than the paper: the edges are cut off.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe area does not fit at that scale.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose a smaller scale, a larger paper, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFit to paper\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;The browser blocked the new tab, so the PDF was downloaded instead.&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPop-ups are blocked for the site.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the downloaded PDF, or allow pop-ups for the site.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECharacters print as &quot;?&quot;\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe PDF font has Western European characters only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot built yet for other scripts.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstruction lines show in the PDF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir layer plots.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick the layer&#039;s printer button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAll lines are equally thick\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETheir lineweight is \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDefault\u003C/span\u003E, so they print at the dialog&#039;s pen width.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGive the layers or objects a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELineweight\u003C/span\u003E in the right panel, or choose a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine weight\u003C/span\u003E in the dialog. To see lineweights on screen, press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELWT\u003C/span\u003E in the status bar.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"keys-and-the-screen\"\u003EKeys and the screen\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESymptom\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECause\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFix\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF3, F7 or F8 changes volume or opens Mission Control\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMac function keys.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E, or use the status bar buttons. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF7 asks about caret browsing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox&#039;s own use of F7.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose No; use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGrid\u003C/span\u003E button.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E opened a browser window\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser keeps that key.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENew\u003C/span\u003E or type \u003Ccode\u003ENEW\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command A\"\u003E⌘A\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+A\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E selected the command line&#039;s text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command line had the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick an object first, or type \u003Ccode\u003EALL\u003C/code\u003E at a \u003Ccode\u003ESelect objects\u003C/code\u003E prompt.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does not erase\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command line had the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType \u003Ccode\u003EE\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERight-click does nothing on a Mac trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl-click may count as an ordinary click.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick with two fingers.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENothing pans on a trackpad\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere is no middle button, and Space does not pan.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrag with a right-click held, or zoom out and in at another spot.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe first \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E left full screen\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe browser uses Esc to leave full screen.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again to cancel the command.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E does nothing on a Mac\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EF1 is a brightness key.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E, or click \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETyping goes into the help&#039;s search box\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe help panel has the cursor.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick on the drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing went blank\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EYou zoomed far away.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EClick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E, or double-click the wheel.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E&quot;Leave site?&quot; when closing the tab\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThere are unsaved changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStay, save with \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, then close.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E","text":"Opening files Message or symptom Cause Fix \"CadCraft opens DWG and DXF drawings. \"…\" is neither.\" The file is not a .dwg or .dxf. Open the drawing file itself. PDFs and images cannot be opened. \"This is a binary DXF. Save it as ASCII DXF (or open the DWG) and try again.\" Binary DXF is not read. Ask for a text (ASCII) DXF, or the DWG. \"This file is not a readable DXF (bad group code on line …).\" The DXF is damaged or not really a DXF. Ask for the file again, or for a DWG. \"This drawing could not be read. It may be damaged, or saved in a version the reader does not handle yet (…).\" The DWG reader could not read it. Ask for the DWG saved in an older version, or a DXF. \"Could not open …\" followed by a network message, on the first DWG The DWG reader could not be downloaded: you are offline and CadCraft is not saved for offline use, or the connection dropped. Go online and try again, or open a DXF. Click Save for offline and wait for \"Offline ready\" so DWG files open offline next time. See Working offline. The offline button says \"Could not save\" Some files did not download. Check the connection and click the button to try again. The offline button says \"Offline not available\" Private windows and some managed computers cannot keep an offline copy. Use a normal window, or stay online. CadCraft does not open with no connection It was not saved for offline use in this browser, or the browser cleared it. Next time you are online, click Save for offline and wait for \"Offline ready\". The drawing opens but parts are missing Objects CadCraft cannot draw yet, or layers that are off or frozen in the file. Read \"About this file\" in the left panel. Check the Layers panel. A viewport shows the model turned differently from the original Twisted viewports are shown square to the sheet. Not built yet. Text wraps onto two lines CadCraft draws text in a Helvetica-style font, wider than some CAD fonts. Shorten the text in Properties, or live with the extra line. The command line Message or symptom Cause Fix \"Unknown command \"…\". Type HELP for the list.\" No command or alias by that name. Type HELP, or see Command reference. Some familiar commands are not built yet. \"No such command\" next to the command line while typing What you typed matches nothing yet. Keep typing, or check the spelling. \"Point or option keyword required.\" What you typed is not a point or an option. Check the format: x,y, @dx,dy, @d\u003Ca, or a single number. See Coordinate and distance entry. \"Requires a positive number or two points.\" A distance was negative or not a number. Type a positive number, or click two points. \"Requires an angle in degrees or two points.\" The angle was not a number. Type degrees as a plain number. \"Requires a number.\" A number was expected. Type digits only. \"Invalid option keyword.\" The letters do not match an option. Type the capital letters shown in the square brackets. *Cancel* You pressed Esc, or Enter where a point was required. Start the command again. Typing does not reach the command line A box in a panel or a dialog has the cursor. Press Enter or Esc, or click on the drawing. The command uses objects you did not pick They were selected before you started it. Press Esc before the command. A line went somewhere unexpected x,y is absolute. Use @dx,dy for relative points. \"Finish or cancel the current command first (Esc).\" You pressed Ctrl+S while a command was running. Press Esc, then save. \"Could not save as DWG (AutoCAD 2000): … Try Save as, then DXF.\" Something in the drawing could not be written as DWG. Press Ctrl+Shift+S, choose DXF R12 and click Save. \"Not saved.\" You closed the Save a copy as dialog with Cancel or Esc. Press Ctrl+Shift+S again and click Save. Editing Message or symptom Cause Fix An object will not select Its layer is locked, off or frozen; or you are on a sheet and it is the model inside a viewport. Unlock the layer, or go to the Model tab. \"Nothing found there.\" No object under the click. Zoom in and click on the line itself. \"Nothing crosses that object there.\" TRIM found no object crossing the piece you picked. Check that the cutting line really crosses it. \"Cannot trim that object.\" Trim works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Explode blocks first, or erase and redraw. \"No boundary in that direction.\" EXTEND found nothing to meet. Pick nearer the end that should grow. \"Cannot offset that object.\" Offset works on lines, arcs, circles and polylines. Explode it first, or draw a parallel line. \"Cannot offset that far.\" The offset would turn the shape inside out. Use a smaller distance. \"Radius is too large.\" or \"Distance is too large.\" The fillet or chamfer would run past the end of a line. Set a smaller radius with R, or smaller distances with D. \"Only the first or last segment of an open polyline can be cut back.\" The corner would cut into the middle of a polyline. Pick two segments of the polyline next to each other, or use P for every corner. \"Pick a second, different line.\" The second pick was the same line, or not a line. Pick another line. \"Only polylines, block references and dimensions explode.\" The selection had nothing to explode. Select a polyline, block reference or dimension. \"That is the current layer. Make another layer current first.\" You tried to turn off the current layer. Click another layer's name, then turn this one off. \"There is already a layer called …\" Layer names must be unique. Choose another name. Undo did not undo a layer change Layer settings are not undone. Change the setting back by hand. \"Nothing to undo.\" There is nothing left to undo, or you opened or started a drawing (that clears the history). — Dimensions and blocks Message or symptom Cause Fix Dimension text and arrows are tiny or huge Sizes are in drawing units; the overall scale does not fit the drawing. Set DIMSCALE to the plot scale; see Set dimension sizes. \"Pick a point inside the angle you want to measure.\" The angular dimension's location sits on a leg. Click inside the angle. \"Those lines are parallel.\" Parallel lines have no angle between them. Pick two lines that meet. \"Pick a linear or aligned dimension.\" DIMCONTINUE or DIMBASELINE needs one to start from. Pick a linear or aligned dimension. \"This drawing has no blocks yet: F inserts one from a file, L from the library …\" The drawing has no blocks of its own. Type F or L, or make a block with BLOCK. \"No block called \"…\".\" The name is wrong. The message lists the blocks there are. \"A block name cannot start with * or use …\" followed by a list of characters The name has a character blocks cannot use. Use letters, digits, - and _. \"A block cannot contain itself.\" The selection holds a reference to the block being made. Leave that reference out. \"No objects selected; nothing made.\" You pressed Enter before selecting. Run BLOCK again and select the objects. Sheets and plotting Message or symptom Cause Fix \"Viewports go on a layout. Open a layout tab (or make one with LAYOUT) first.\" MVIEW on the Model tab. Click a sheet tab first. \"Open the layout with the viewport first.\" VPVIEW on the Model tab. Click the sheet tab first. A viewport scale looks ten times wrong The drawing units are wrong. Set Units in Current settings. \"There is nothing to plot yet.\" The model is empty. Draw something, or open a drawing. \"Pick the window to plot.\" A window… is chosen and no window was picked. Click Pick and pick two corners. \"… bigger than the paper: the edges are cut off.\" The area does not fit at that scale. Choose a smaller scale, a larger paper, or Fit to paper. \"The browser blocked the new tab, so the PDF was downloaded instead.\" Pop-ups are blocked for the site. Open the downloaded PDF, or allow pop-ups for the site. Characters print as \"?\" The PDF font has Western European characters only. Not built yet for other scripts. Construction lines show in the PDF Their layer plots. Click the layer's printer button. All lines are equally thick Their lineweight is Default, so they print at the dialog's pen width. Give the layers or objects a Lineweight in the right panel, or choose a Line weight in the dialog. To see lineweights on screen, press LWT in the status bar. Keys and the screen Symptom Cause Fix F3, F7 or F8 changes volume or opens Mission Control Mac function keys. Hold fn, or use the status bar buttons. See Keys, mice and trackpads. F7 asks about caret browsing Firefox's own use of F7. Choose No; use the Grid button. Ctrl+N opened a browser window The browser keeps that key. Click New or type NEW. Ctrl+A selected the command line's text The command line had the cursor. Click an object first, or type ALL at a Select objects prompt. Delete does not erase The command line had the cursor. Type E and press Enter. Right-click does nothing on a Mac trackpad Control-click may count as an ordinary click. Click with two fingers. Nothing pans on a trackpad There is no middle button, and Space does not pan. Drag with a right-click held, or zoom out and in at another spot. The first Esc left full screen The browser uses Esc to leave full screen. Press Esc again to cancel the command. F1 does nothing on a Mac F1 is a brightness key. Hold fn, or click Help. Typing goes into the help's search box The help panel has the cursor. Click on the drawing. The drawing went blank You zoomed far away. Click Extents, or double-click the wheel. \"Leave site?\" when closing the tab There are unsaved changes. Stay, save with Ctrl+S, then close."},{"slug":"not-built-yet","scope":"cadcraft","title":"Not built yet","summary":"What this build of the CadCraft drafting app does not do yet, and what to do instead today.","section":"trouble","order":20,"keywords":["missing","limitations","properties","ucs","annotative","dynamic block","spline","roadmap","not supported","dwg save","hatch","stretch","array","join","matchprop","break","lengthen","linetype","lineweight","xref","cloud","desktop","3d","solids","booleans","multileader","wipeout"],"headings":[{"id":"files-and-saving","text":"Files and saving","level":2},{"id":"drawing-and-editing","text":"Drawing and editing","level":2},{"id":"the-properties-panel","text":"The Properties panel","level":2},{"id":"precision-aids","text":"Precision aids","level":2},{"id":"sheets-dimensions-and-plotting","text":"Sheets, dimensions and plotting","level":2},{"id":"constraints-and-parameters","text":"Constraints and parameters","level":2},{"id":"3d","text":"3D","level":2},{"id":"routines-and-customizing","text":"Routines and customizing","level":2},{"id":"help","text":"Help","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ECadCraft is in development. This page lists what the current build does not do, so you do not hunt for it. Each item says what to do instead today.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-and-saving\"\u003EFiles and saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA CadCraft DWG checked in AutoCAD itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery saved version is read back by three other programs&#039; DWG readers and by CadCraft. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/files#what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/files\" data-hash=\"what-a-dwg-keeps-and-changes\"\u003EWhat a DWG keeps and changes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving DXF newer than 2000\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave as DXF 2000, or as DWG up to AutoCAD 2018.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving over the opened file\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEvery save downloads a new copy. Replace the old file yourself if you want to.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. Keep your DWG and DXF files in your own folders.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA desktop version\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENot available yet. CadCraft runs in the browser.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EKeeping a loaded linked drawing as a link when saving; overlays; clipping a link; editing a link in place\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESaving binds a loaded link into the file (it stays a link in CadCraft until then). Reload after changing the other drawing.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPictures (images) in opened files, and wipeouts that hide what is under them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA picture shows as a frame with its file name; a wipeout as its frame.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing multileaders that have a block, several leader lines or curved lines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are drawn as the other program drew them and move as one. Text multileaders come in editable.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETables in a DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounted in &quot;About this file&quot;, not shown. Tables in a DWG are shown as the other program drew them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBinary DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESave it as text (ASCII) DXF in the program that made it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste with other programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECadCraft&#039;s clipboard works between CadCraft drawings and tabs in the same browser, not with other programs. Save as DWG or DXF and open it there. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/copy-paste\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/copy-paste\"\u003ECopy, paste and duplicate\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMore than one drawing open at a time\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen the other drawing in a second browser tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-and-editing\"\u003EDrawing and editing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatches tied to their boundary (associative), gradient fills, and patterns beyond the standard set\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHatch again after changing the outline. Opened hatches with other patterns show as a tone. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/hatch\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/hatch\"\u003EHatch and fill areas\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELinetypes with shapes or text in them, and lineweights in a saved DXF R12\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShapes and text in a file&#039;s linetype are left out of its dashes; save as DWG to keep lineweights.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPatterned hatches in a saved DXF\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ER12 has no hatch: they are saved as their outlines. Save as DWG to keep the pattern.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAssociative arrays (edited later as one object) and arrays along a path\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EARRAY\u003C/code\u003E makes plain copies: undo and run it again to change it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ESTRETCH\u003C/code\u003E with a crossing polygon\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse one or more crossing windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPEDIT\u003C/code\u003E (editing a polyline&#039;s vertices and widths at the command line)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGrips, and the vertex browser in Properties (every vertex, its widths and bulge); \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EJOIN\u003C/code\u003E makes a polyline from pieces.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EBREAK\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELENGTHEN\u003C/code\u003E on ellipses and splines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw them again, or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E to another object.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillet and chamfer between an arc segment of a polyline and the segment next to it; fillet joining a line into a polyline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPLODE\u003C/code\u003E the polyline, then fillet the pieces. The fillet arc is added as its own object.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim and extend: the Project option (3D) and extending a polyline&#039;s arc end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETrim in the plane of the drawing; for an arc end, explode the polyline or draw the arc again.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EText styles, fonts, columns and formatting inside paragraph text (bold, stacked fractions, colors per word)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne Helvetica-style font; one height and color per text object.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EParagraph text edited at its rotation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe editor opens level; the text keeps its rotation.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMultileaders saved as multileader objects in a DXF; several leader lines on one multileader; block content\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA DWG keeps them as multileaders; an R12 DXF keeps what they draw (lines, arrowhead and text).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETables\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELines and text.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAttribute definitions kept on their own in a saved file (outside a block), multiple-line attributes, dynamic blocks (parameters and actions)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPut attribute definitions into a block before saving: on their own they save as text showing the tag.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELayer states (saving and restoring which layers are on)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUse \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYISO\u003C/code\u003E / \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELAYUNISO\u003C/code\u003E, or turn layers on and off by hand.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWindow polygon and crossing polygon selection, and saved selection filters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFence (\u003Ccode\u003EF\u003C/code\u003E), Window, Crossing, Remove (\u003Ccode\u003ER\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EQSELECT\u003C/code\u003E for a rule.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-properties-panel\"\u003EThe Properties panel\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDrawing the Fit choices, and the horizontal text position and text below the line on dimensions other than linear and aligned\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are kept on the object, shown in Properties and saved; CadCraft places the text by its own rule.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterials that other programs see\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA saved file keeps the material name for CadCraft; other programs show the layer&#039;s material.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETransparency in DWG 2000 and DXF files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOther programs show those objects solid; CadCraft keeps the transparency. Save as DWG 2004 or later for everyone.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn associative hatch in other programs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe hatch is saved; CadCraft keeps the setting, other programs see a hatch that does not follow its boundary.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA user coordinate system (UCS)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECoordinates in Properties are world coordinates.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnnotative scaling\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe annotative setting is kept with text; the annotation scale is always 1:1.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDynamic block parameters\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA dynamic block shows as the shape it was saved in.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESplines with fit and control points\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn opened spline becomes a polyline; edit its vertices in Properties.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA hatch that follows its boundary (associative)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe associative setting is kept and saved; hatch again after changing the outline.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"precision-aids\"\u003EPrecision aids\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETracking from points across commands, and the tracking settings dialog (relative polar angles, extra angles)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPOLARANG\u003C/code\u003E sets the step; acquire the points again in the next command.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESnap to grid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType coordinates; the grid is a visual guide only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EArc and Length options while picking an area&#039;s corners, and MEASUREGEOM Volume\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the outline with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPLINE\u003C/code\u003E (arcs included), then \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAREA\u003C/code\u003E \u003Ccode\u003EO\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDynamic input for grips, and its options list (the down arrow)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe command line shows the options.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAngles in degrees-minutes-seconds, grads or surveyor&#039;s bearings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EType decimal degrees: \u003Ccode\u003E30.5\u003C/code\u003E for 30°30&#039;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA view manager dialog; named views of a sheet that come back on that sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENAMEDVIEW\u003C/code\u003E lists and restores views. A saved file keeps them, but a sheet&#039;s views come back on the first sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETouch gestures (pinch zoom, two-finger pan)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExtents\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWindow\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sheets-dimensions-and-plotting\"\u003ESheets, dimensions and plotting\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEditing the model through a viewport\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab; aim viewports with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVPVIEW\u003C/code\u003E or Properties.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwisted (rotated) viewports\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShown square to the sheet.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA title block that updates itself\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEdit its texts by hand; the SCALE field does not follow viewport changes.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENamed dimension styles\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESettings for new dimensions plus each dimension&#039;s own sizes. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/dimension-settings\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/dimension-settings\"\u003ESet dimension sizes\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOrdinate dimensions, and editable two-line angular dimensions from opened files\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are shown as drawn in the file.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAnnotative scaling\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESet sizes for one plot scale with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDIMSCALE\u003C/code\u003E and text heights.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPlot style tables and page setups\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChoose ink and pen width in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot to PDF\u003C/span\u003E dialog.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECharacters outside the Western European set in PDFs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey print as &quot;?&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"constraints-and-parameters\"\u003EConstraints and parameters\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraints on sheets, in blocks, on ellipses, splines or text\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstrain on the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EModel\u003C/span\u003E tab, on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESmooth (G2), and constraining a polyline&#039;s arc bulge directly\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETangent and equal; an arc segment follows its two ends.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReference (driven) dimensional constraints, and \u003Ccode\u003E3Point\u003C/code\u003E in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EDCANGULAR\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA plain dimension (DIMLINEAR …) shows a value without driving it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDimensional constraints of a fixed size on paper, and plotting them\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show on screen only; add plain dimensions for the plot.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstraints kept through EXPLODE, BREAK, JOIN and other commands that make new objects, and on trimmed polylines\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConstrain them again (AUTOCONSTRAIN helps). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMIRROR\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ETRIM\u003C/code\u003E of lines, arcs and circles keep them.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDesign tables (configurations) in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChange parameters in the panel.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA tested round trip with SolidWorks\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECheck the sketch the macro builds before you rely on it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"3d\"\u003E3D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFillets, chamfers, shells and lofts; sweeps along a path that rises (3D paths)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude outlines with arcs, push and pull faces, revolve and sweep along flat paths.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EExtrude in another direction than up\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDraw the shape on the ground and extrude it up or down (with a taper or along a path if you like); rotate the solid afterwards.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElevation and thickness of 2D objects\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe drawing lies on the ground (height 0) on the 3D tab.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESolids, regions, bodies and meshes from other programs (3DSOLID, ACIS, polyface and polygon meshes)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECounted in &quot;About this file&quot;, not shown. Ask for them as 3D faces in model space.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECopy and paste of solids\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOn the 3D tab, use \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECOPY\u003C/code\u003E. The clipboard holds 2D objects only.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESections, views that follow later changes to the solids, and shaded solids on plotted sheets\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EVIEWBASE\u003C/code\u003E places flat views (a snapshot); run it again after changing the solids.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMaterials, textures and lighting settings\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe visual styles: shaded, edges, hidden line, wireframe, X-ray.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"routines-and-customizing\"\u003ERoutines and customizing\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EInstead, today\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECompiled or protected LISP (.fas, .vlx), native programs (.arx, .crx, .dbx) and VBA (.dvb)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoad a routine&#039;s .lsp source with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EAPPLOAD\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELISP&#039;s \u003Ccode\u003Egrread\u003C/code\u003E, reactors (\u003Ccode\u003Evlr-…\u003C/code\u003E), curve functions (\u003Ccode\u003Evlax-curve-…\u003C/code\u003E), dictionaries and extended data, the Express Tools&#039; \u003Ccode\u003Eacet-…\u003C/code\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe rest of the language runs: see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/cadcraft/run-lisp\" data-topic=\"cadcraft/run-lisp\"\u003ERun LISP routines\u003C/a\u003E. The command line names a missing function when a routine reaches one.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EImages and slides in a routine&#039;s dialog\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey show as blank frames; the rest of the dialog works.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECraftScript (.craft), Lua and Python in CadCraft\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ERun them in PartCraft&#039;s Script workspace.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe Startup Suite and aliases kept on your computer or synced\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThey are kept in this browser; the desktop app and the cloud service will keep them for you.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"help\"\u003EHelp\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help describes this build. When something is added, it moves out of this page and into the walkthroughs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"CadCraft is in development. This page lists what the current build does not do, so you do not hunt for it. Each item says what to do instead today. Files and saving Not built yet Instead, today A CadCraft DWG checked in AutoCAD itself Every saved version is read back by three other programs' DWG readers and by CadCraft. See What a DWG keeps and changes. Saving DXF newer than 2000 Save as DXF 2000, or as DWG up to AutoCAD 2018. Saving over the opened file Every save downloads a new copy. Replace the old file yourself if you want to. Cloud saving and sync between computers Not available yet. Keep your DWG and DXF files in your own folders. A desktop version Not available yet. CadCraft runs in the browser. Keeping a loaded linked drawing as a link when saving; overlays; clipping a link; editing a link in place Saving binds a loaded link into the file (it stays a link in CadCraft until then). Reload after changing the other drawing. Pictures (images) in opened files, and wipeouts that hide what is under them A picture shows as a frame with its file name; a wipeout as its frame. Editing multileaders that have a block, several leader lines or curved lines They are drawn as the other program drew them and move as one. Text multileaders come in editable. Tables in a DXF Counted in \"About this file\", not shown. Tables in a DWG are shown as the other program drew them. Binary DXF Save it as text (ASCII) DXF in the program that made it. Copy and paste with other programs CadCraft's clipboard works between CadCraft drawings and tabs in the same browser, not with other programs. Save as DWG or DXF and open it there. See Copy, paste and duplicate. More than one drawing open at a time Open the other drawing in a second browser tab. Drawing and editing Not built yet Instead, today Hatches tied to their boundary (associative), gradient fills, and patterns beyond the standard set Hatch again after changing the outline. Opened hatches with other patterns show as a tone. See Hatch and fill areas. Linetypes with shapes or text in them, and lineweights in a saved DXF R12 Shapes and text in a file's linetype are left out of its dashes; save as DWG to keep lineweights. Patterned hatches in a saved DXF R12 has no hatch: they are saved as their outlines. Save as DWG to keep the pattern. Associative arrays (edited later as one object) and arrays along a path ARRAY makes plain copies: undo and run it again to change it. STRETCH with a crossing polygon Use one or more crossing windows. PEDIT (editing a polyline's vertices and widths at the command line) Grips, and the vertex browser in Properties (every vertex, its widths and bulge); JOIN makes a polyline from pieces. BREAK and LENGTHEN on ellipses and splines Draw them again, or TRIM to another object. Fillet and chamfer between an arc segment of a polyline and the segment next to it; fillet joining a line into a polyline EXPLODE the polyline, then fillet the pieces. The fillet arc is added as its own object. Trim and extend: the Project option (3D) and extending a polyline's arc end Trim in the plane of the drawing; for an arc end, explode the polyline or draw the arc again. Text styles, fonts, columns and formatting inside paragraph text (bold, stacked fractions, colors per word) One Helvetica-style font; one height and color per text object. Paragraph text edited at its rotation The editor opens level; the text keeps its rotation. Multileaders saved as multileader objects in a DXF; several leader lines on one multileader; block content A DWG keeps them as multileaders; an R12 DXF keeps what they draw (lines, arrowhead and text). Tables Lines and text. Attribute definitions kept on their own in a saved file (outside a block), multiple-line attributes, dynamic blocks (parameters and actions) Put attribute definitions into a block before saving: on their own they save as text showing the tag. Layer states (saving and restoring which layers are on) Use LAYISO / LAYUNISO, or turn layers on and off by hand. Window polygon and crossing polygon selection, and saved selection filters Fence (F), Window, Crossing, Remove (R), QSELECT for a rule. The Properties panel Not built yet Instead, today Drawing the Fit choices, and the horizontal text position and text below the line on dimensions other than linear and aligned They are kept on the object, shown in Properties and saved; CadCraft places the text by its own rule. Materials that other programs see A saved file keeps the material name for CadCraft; other programs show the layer's material. Transparency in DWG 2000 and DXF files Other programs show those objects solid; CadCraft keeps the transparency. Save as DWG 2004 or later for everyone. An associative hatch in other programs The hatch is saved; CadCraft keeps the setting, other programs see a hatch that does not follow its boundary. A user coordinate system (UCS) Coordinates in Properties are world coordinates. Annotative scaling The annotative setting is kept with text; the annotation scale is always 1:1. Dynamic block parameters A dynamic block shows as the shape it was saved in. Splines with fit and control points An opened spline becomes a polyline; edit its vertices in Properties. A hatch that follows its boundary (associative) The associative setting is kept and saved; hatch again after changing the outline. Precision aids Not built yet Instead, today Tracking from points across commands, and the tracking settings dialog (relative polar angles, extra angles) POLARANG sets the step; acquire the points again in the next command. Snap to grid Type coordinates; the grid is a visual guide only. Arc and Length options while picking an area's corners, and MEASUREGEOM Volume Draw the outline with PLINE (arcs included), then AREA O. Dynamic input for grips, and its options list (the down arrow) The command line shows the options. Angles in degrees-minutes-seconds, grads or surveyor's bearings Type decimal degrees: 30.5 for 30°30'. A view manager dialog; named views of a sheet that come back on that sheet NAMEDVIEW lists and restores views. A saved file keeps them, but a sheet's views come back on the first sheet. Touch gestures (pinch zoom, two-finger pan) Extents and Window. Sheets, dimensions and plotting Not built yet Instead, today Editing the model through a viewport Edit on the Model tab; aim viewports with VPVIEW or Properties. Twisted (rotated) viewports Shown square to the sheet. A title block that updates itself Edit its texts by hand; the SCALE field does not follow viewport changes. Named dimension styles Settings for new dimensions plus each dimension's own sizes. See Set dimension sizes. Ordinate dimensions, and editable two-line angular dimensions from opened files They are shown as drawn in the file. Annotative scaling Set sizes for one plot scale with DIMSCALE and text heights. Plot style tables and page setups Choose ink and pen width in the Plot to PDF dialog. Characters outside the Western European set in PDFs They print as \"?\". Constraints and parameters Not built yet Instead, today Constraints on sheets, in blocks, on ellipses, splines or text Constrain on the Model tab, on lines, polylines, circles, arcs and points. Smooth (G2), and constraining a polyline's arc bulge directly Tangent and equal; an arc segment follows its two ends. Reference (driven) dimensional constraints, and 3Point in DCANGULAR A plain dimension (DIMLINEAR …) shows a value without driving it. Dimensional constraints of a fixed size on paper, and plotting them They show on screen only; add plain dimensions for the plot. Constraints kept through EXPLODE, BREAK, JOIN and other commands that make new objects, and on trimmed polylines Constrain them again (AUTOCONSTRAIN helps). MIRROR and TRIM of lines, arcs and circles keep them. Design tables (configurations) in CadCraft Change parameters in the panel. A tested round trip with SolidWorks Check the sketch the macro builds before you rely on it. 3D Not built yet Instead, today Fillets, chamfers, shells and lofts; sweeps along a path that rises (3D paths) Extrude outlines with arcs, push and pull faces, revolve and sweep along flat paths. Extrude in another direction than up Draw the shape on the ground and extrude it up or down (with a taper or along a path if you like); rotate the solid afterwards. Elevation and thickness of 2D objects The drawing lies on the ground (height 0) on the 3D tab. Solids, regions, bodies and meshes from other programs (3DSOLID, ACIS, polyface and polygon meshes) Counted in \"About this file\", not shown. Ask for them as 3D faces in model space. Copy and paste of solids On the 3D tab, use COPY. The clipboard holds 2D objects only. Sections, views that follow later changes to the solids, and shaded solids on plotted sheets VIEWBASE places flat views (a snapshot); run it again after changing the solids. Materials, textures and lighting settings The visual styles: shaded, edges, hidden line, wireframe, X-ray. Routines and customizing Not built yet Instead, today Compiled or protected LISP (.fas, .vlx), native programs (.arx, .crx, .dbx) and VBA (.dvb) Load a routine's .lsp source with APPLOAD. LISP's grread, reactors (vlr-…), curve functions (vlax-curve-…), dictionaries and extended data, the Express Tools' acet-… The rest of the language runs: see Run LISP routines. The command line names a missing function when a routine reaches one. Images and slides in a routine's dialog They show as blank frames; the rest of the dialog works. CraftScript (.craft), Lua and Python in CadCraft Run them in PartCraft's Script workspace. The Startup Suite and aliases kept on your computer or synced They are kept in this browser; the desktop app and the cloud service will keep them for you. Help The help describes this build. When something is added, it moves out of this page and into the walkthroughs."},{"slug":"help","scope":"basics","title":"Using this help","summary":"Open the help, search it, and see the steps for your computer.","section":"start","order":10,"keywords":["help","f1","search","tutorials","manual"],"headings":[{"id":"open-the-help","text":"Open the help","level":2},{"id":"find-a-topic","text":"Find a topic","level":2},{"id":"steps-for-your-computer","text":"Steps for your computer","level":2},{"id":"the-same-help-on-the-website","text":"The same help on the website","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"open-the-help\"\u003EOpen the help\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top of the app.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EClose\u003C/span\u003E to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"F1\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EF1\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E together with the \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E key (or the globe key), or use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHelp\u003C/span\u003E button. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys#function-keys-on-a-mac\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\" data-hash=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EKeys on a Mac\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-a-topic\"\u003EFind a topic\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs&#039; command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETopics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBack\u003C/span\u003E returns to where you were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"steps-for-your-computer\"\u003ESteps for your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example &quot;Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChange\u003C/span\u003E on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow all\u003C/span\u003E to see every version of every step, each labeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EKeyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-same-help-on-the-website\"\u003EThe same help on the website\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform&#039;s steps. In the app, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Every app carries its own help. It is part of the app, so it opens with no connection and always describes the version you are running. Open the help Press F1, or choose Help in the bar at the top of the app. Press Esc or choose Close to put it away. Your drawing stays as it was. On most Mac keyboards the top row controls brightness and sound. Press F1 together with the fn key (or the globe key), or use the Help button. See Keys on a Mac. Find a topic Type in the search box at the top of the help. Results update as you type and include other programs' command names, so typing what you would have typed elsewhere usually finds the right page. Topics are grouped the same way in every app: getting started, walkthroughs, coming from other software, reference, and troubleshooting. Links inside a topic open the next topic in the same panel. Back returns to where you were. Steps for your computer Some steps differ between Mac, Windows and Linux, between browsers, and between being online and offline. The help works out which applies to you and shows those steps. The line at the top of a topic says what it chose, for example \"Showing steps for Mac, Chrome or Edge, online\". Choose Change on that line to pick another computer or browser, for example to help a colleague. Choose Show all to see every version of every step, each labeled. Keyboard shortcuts are written the way your keyboard labels them: Ctrl+S reads as ⌘S on a Mac and Ctrl+S on Windows and Linux. The same help on the website The website shows the same topics at getcadcraft.com/tutorials, with every platform's steps. In the app, Open on the website at the bottom of a topic opens that page when you are online."},{"slug":"browsers","scope":"basics","title":"Browsers and computers","summary":"Which browsers the apps run in, what differs between them, and what your computer needs.","section":"start","order":20,"keywords":["chrome","edge","firefox","safari","brave","browser","mac","windows","linux","ipad","tablet","webgl","requirements","memory"],"headings":[{"id":"which-browser","text":"Which browser","level":2},{"id":"what-differs-between-browsers","text":"What differs between browsers","level":2},{"id":"mac-windows-and-linux","text":"Mac, Windows and Linux","level":2},{"id":"tablets-and-touch-screens","text":"Tablets and touch screens","level":2},{"id":"what-your-computer-needs","text":"What your computer needs","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/your-files\" data-topic=\"basics/your-files\"\u003EWhere your files go\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"which-browser\"\u003EWhich browser\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse an up-to-date version of one of these:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EBrowser\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EEngine\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ENotes\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EChromium\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFirefox\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EGecko\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESafari\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWebKit\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAll three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EWhat differs between browsers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOpening a folder.\u003C/strong\u003E Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESaving.\u003C/strong\u003E The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Chrome or Edge, turn on &quot;Ask where to save each file before downloading&quot; (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose &quot;Always ask you where to save files&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIn Safari, open Settings, then General, and set &quot;File download location&quot; to &quot;Ask for each download&quot; to pick a folder every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mac-windows-and-linux\"\u003EMac, Windows and Linux\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/keys\" data-topic=\"basics/keys\"\u003EKeys, mice and trackpads\u003C/a\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tablets-and-touch-screens\"\u003ETablets and touch screens\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app&#039;s help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-your-computer-needs\"\u003EWhat your computer needs\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThese are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser&#039;s settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EVery large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The apps run inside a web browser. They do the work on your own computer: your files are opened there and never sent to us (see Where your files go). Which browser Use an up-to-date version of one of these: Browser Engine Notes Chrome, Edge, Brave, Opera, Vivaldi Chromium The most complete: folder picking, saving for offline use, file handling. Firefox Gecko Works. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Offline saving may not work in private windows. Safari WebKit Works on Mac and iPad. Opening a whole folder uses a simpler picker. Safari may delete an offline copy you have not used for a few weeks. All three engines are fine for everyday work. Where an app does something differently in one of them, its help shows the steps for your browser. What differs between browsers Opening a folder. Chromium browsers show a real folder picker and can read the folder directly. Firefox and Safari ask you to choose a folder and then read every file in it, which takes longer for big project folders; dragging the folder onto the app works in all three. Saving. The apps save by downloading a file. Your browser decides where downloads go and whether it asks first. In Chrome or Edge, turn on \"Ask where to save each file before downloading\" (in Settings, under Downloads) to choose a folder every time. In Firefox, open Settings, then General, then Downloads, and choose \"Always ask you where to save files\" to pick a folder every time. In Safari, open Settings, then General, and set \"File download location\" to \"Ask for each download\" to pick a folder every time. Mac, Windows and Linux The apps are the same on every computer. What changes is the keyboard: shortcuts are written for your computer automatically. See Keys, mice and trackpads. Tablets and touch screens Some apps accept touch (one finger draws or selects, two fingers pinch to zoom and pan). Each app's help says whether it does. On an iPad, use Safari or Chrome. A keyboard helps with apps that use typed commands. What your computer needs These are planned minimums for the apps, not measured requirements: 8 GB of memory (16 GB is recommended for large federated models). A graphics chip with WebGL 2 for the 3D apps. If a 3D view stays blank, check that hardware acceleration is on in your browser's settings. A screen at least 1280 pixels wide for the full toolbars; smaller screens wrap them. Very large files (big plan sets, big models) use a lot of memory in a browser tab. Close other heavy tabs if an app slows down."},{"slug":"keys","scope":"basics","title":"Keys, mice and trackpads","summary":"How shortcuts are written, function keys on a Mac, right-clicking and panning without a mouse.","section":"start","order":30,"keywords":["keyboard","shortcut","ctrl","command","cmd","option","fn","function keys","trackpad","right click","middle button","magic mouse"],"headings":[{"id":"how-shortcuts-are-written","text":"How shortcuts are written","level":2},{"id":"function-keys-on-a-mac","text":"Function keys on a Mac","level":2},{"id":"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps","text":"Shortcuts the browser keeps","level":2},{"id":"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad","text":"Mouse buttons on a trackpad","level":2},{"id":"typing-in-a-text-box","text":"Typing in a text box","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"how-shortcuts-are-written\"\u003EHow shortcuts are written\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWritten\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn a Mac\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOn Windows and Linux\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command S\"\u003E⌘S\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+S\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECommand (⌘) and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl and S\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EControl (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECtrl, Shift and F\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option ←\"\u003E⌥←\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+←\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOption (⌥) and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAlt and ←\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Delete\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EBackspace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe Delete key\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBackspace\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"fn Delete\"\u003Efn Delete\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EDelete\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efn and Delete (forward delete)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDelete\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EReturn\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEnter\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA plus sign means hold the keys together. &quot;Then&quot; means press one after the other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMost app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Z\"\u003E⌘Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Z\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E works both ways. A few, such as full screen (\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"function-keys-on-a-mac\"\u003EFunction keys on a Mac\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehold \u003Cstrong\u003Efn\u003C/strong\u003E (or the globe key) while you press the key, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Eopen \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EKeyboard Shortcuts\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EFunction Keys\u003C/strong\u003E, and turn on &quot;Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortcuts-the-browser-keeps\"\u003EShortcuts the browser keeps\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command N\"\u003E⌘N\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+N\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command T\"\u003E⌘T\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+T\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command W\"\u003E⌘W\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+W\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command Q\"\u003E⌘Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Q\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on a Mac and \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Option F4\"\u003E⌥ F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EAlt+F4\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;); choose to stay, then save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe browser&#039;s own full screen (\u003Cspan class=\"hp\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:mac\" title=\"Mac\"\u003EControl-Command-F, or the green button on the window\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hp-sep\"\u003E / \u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hpv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\" title=\"Windows or Linux\"\u003EF11\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/span\u003E) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control Shift F\"\u003E⌃⇧F\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+Shift+F\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, which can hide the app&#039;s bars too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"mouse-buttons-on-a-trackpad\"\u003EMouse buttons on a trackpad\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:mac\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EMac\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers. Turn on &quot;Secondary click&quot; in \u003Cstrong\u003ESystem Settings\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003ETrackpad\u003C/strong\u003E, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Control\"\u003E⌃\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you click opens the browser&#039;s menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"os:windows,linux\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EWindows or Linux\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERight-click:\u003C/strong\u003E click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMiddle-button drag (pan):\u003C/strong\u003E each app offers another way to pan, such as holding \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Space\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ESpace\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app&#039;s keyboard page says which.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWheel zoom:\u003C/strong\u003E two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"typing-in-a-text-box\"\u003ETyping in a text box\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhile you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app&#039;s one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing &quot;L&quot; types an L. Press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"How shortcuts are written The help writes each shortcut the way your keyboard labels it. Written On a Mac On Windows and Linux Ctrl+S Command (⌘) and S Ctrl and S Ctrl+Shift+F Control (⌃), Shift (⇧) and F Ctrl, Shift and F Alt+← Option (⌥) and ← Alt and ← Backspace the Delete key Backspace Delete fn and Delete (forward delete) Delete Enter Return Enter A plus sign means hold the keys together. \"Then\" means press one after the other. Most app shortcuts accept either Command or Control on a Mac, so Ctrl+Z works both ways. A few, such as full screen (Ctrl+Shift+F), use the Control key on every computer; the help says so where that applies. Function keys on a Mac On most Mac keyboards the top row changes brightness, volume and media. To send F1 to F12: hold fn (or the globe key) while you press the key, or open System Settings, then Keyboard, then Keyboard Shortcuts, then Function Keys, and turn on \"Use F1, F2, etc. keys as standard function keys\". Some F-keys are also claimed by macOS itself (for example Mission Control). If a key does nothing in the app, try it with fn, or use the toolbar button instead. Shortcuts the browser keeps A web page cannot take some shortcuts from the browser, whatever it does. In every app: Ctrl+N, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+W always open or close browser windows and tabs. The apps use buttons for New and Close instead. Ctrl+Q on a Mac and Alt+F4 on Windows quit the browser. If you have unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"); choose to stay, then save. The browser's own full screen (Control-Command-F, or the green button on the window / F11) still works. The apps also have their own screen modes on Ctrl+Shift+F, which can hide the app's bars too. Mouse buttons on a trackpad The apps use the right button and the middle button. Without a three-button mouse: Right-click: click with two fingers. Turn on \"Secondary click\" in System Settings, then Trackpad, if two-finger click does nothing. Holding Ctrl while you click opens the browser's menu, but inside a drawing some apps read it as an ordinary click (with Control held), so prefer the two-finger click. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the trackpad zooms in most apps. Pinch also zooms where the app supports it. Right-click: click with two fingers, or click the lower-right corner of the touchpad. This depends on your touchpad settings. Middle-button drag (pan): each app offers another way to pan, such as holding Space while you drag or dragging with the right button. The app's keyboard page says which. Wheel zoom: two-finger scroll on the touchpad zooms in most apps. Typing in a text box While you type in a box (a name, a note, a search), the app's one-letter shortcuts are off, so typing \"L\" types an L. Press Esc or click on the drawing to give the keys back to the app."},{"slug":"find-tools","scope":"basics","title":"Find a tool or setting","summary":"Press Ctrl+K and type a few letters to run any tool, change a setting or open a help topic.","section":"start","order":32,"keywords":["find","search","command palette","quick open","run command","ctrl k","cmd k","tool","setting","shortcut"],"headings":[{"id":"find-and-run","text":"Find and run","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EEvery app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don&#039;t need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"find-and-run\"\u003EFind and run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Command K\"\u003E⌘K\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003ECtrl+K\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, or choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType a few letters of what you want: &quot;fillet&quot;, &quot;panel text&quot;, &quot;units&quot;. Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the box.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhat you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETools that can&#039;t run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd-ons&#039; tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Every app has a search box for its tools, its settings and this help. You don't need to know where a tool sits in the toolbar. Find and run Press Ctrl+K, or choose Find in the bar at the top. Type a few letters of what you want: \"fillet\", \"panel text\", \"units\". Every word you type has to match, so add a second word to narrow the list. Press Enter to run the first match, or move with the arrow keys first. Esc closes the box. A tool runs as if you had clicked it in the toolbar. A setting opens Settings at that setting. A help topic opens here, in the help panel. Things to know What you used last comes first the next time, so a tool you use often is one key press away. Tools that can't run right now, for example before a file is open, are listed but greyed out. Add-ons' tools are in the list too, once the add-on is switched on."},{"slug":"arrange-panels","scope":"basics","title":"Arrange the panels and use a second monitor","summary":"Dock the side panels left, right or at the bottom, float them over the drawing, or move them to their own window on another screen.","section":"start","order":35,"keywords":["panels","dock","float","move panel","drag","layout","second monitor","two screens","dual monitor","multiple monitors","window","pop-up","reset layout"],"headings":[{"id":"move-a-panel-in-the-window","text":"Move a panel in the window","level":2},{"id":"use-the-keyboard","text":"Use the keyboard","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor","text":"Put the panels on a second monitor","level":2},{"id":"more-than-one-panels-window","text":"More than one panels window","level":2},{"id":"put-the-panels-back","text":"Put the panels back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThis works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel&#039;s name and a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"move-a-panel-in-the-window\"\u003EMove a panel in the window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag the panel by its name at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhile you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBoth panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"input:touch\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ETouch\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag with one finger on the panel&#039;s name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"use-the-keyboard\"\u003EUse the keyboard\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E button opens a menu: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the left\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock on the right\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDock at the bottom\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFloat over the drawing\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it back\u003C/span\u003E. Tab to the button, press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E, move with the arrow keys and press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E again. \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Esc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEsc\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E closes the menu.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-on-a-second-monitor\"\u003EPut the panels on a second monitor\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E on a panel, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove all panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E. You can also choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove panels to a new window\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EKeep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:chromium\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EChrome or Edge\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut it on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels on my other screen\u003C/span\u003E in a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:firefox,safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EFirefox or Safari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"more-than-one-panels-window\"\u003EMore than one panels window\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn a panel&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003E⋯\u003C/span\u003E menu, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to a new window\u003C/span\u003E opens another window for that panel, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove to panels window 2\u003C/span\u003E (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called &quot;panels 2&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDrag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA panels window that has no panels left closes by itself.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen the next one\u003C/span\u003E once for each window.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"put-the-panels-back\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut the panels back\u003C/span\u003E in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClosing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen it\u003C/span\u003E to bring the window back, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EKeep them here\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReset layout\u003C/span\u003E puts every panel back where it started.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-tip\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ETip\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The panels beside the drawing can move. Put them where they suit you, or give them a window of their own on a second monitor so the drawing gets the whole of the first one. This works in the apps whose panels have a grip: a slim bar with the panel's name and a ⋯ button at the top of each panel. The other apps are getting it next. Move a panel in the window Drag the panel by its name at the top. While you drag, outlined areas appear on the left, the right and the bottom of the drawing. Drop the panel on one to dock it there. The area you are over darkens. Drop it anywhere else over the drawing to make it float. Both panels can share one side: they stack, and the line between them drags to share the space. Drag the inner edge of a docked side to make it wider or narrower; double-click the edge to put it back. A floating panel stays over the drawing. Move it by its name, and resize it by dragging its lower-right corner (or tab to the corner and use the arrow keys). Double-click its name to dock it again where it was. Drag with one finger on the panel's name. Drag the lower-right corner of a floating panel with one finger to resize it, and the edges between docked panels the same way. To float or dock with no dragging, use the ⋯ menu. Use the keyboard Every panel's ⋯ button opens a menu: Dock on the left, Dock on the right, Dock at the bottom, Float over the drawing, Move to a new window and Put it back. Tab to the button, press Enter, move with the arrow keys and press Enter again. Esc closes the menu. Put the panels on a second monitor Choose ⋯ on a panel, then Move all panels to a new window. You can also choose Move panels to a new window in Settings. A small window opens with the panels in it. Drag it to your other monitor and make it as big as you like. Keep working: the panels act on the drawing as before, and the drawing fills the main window. Shortcuts keep working while the panels window is in front: keys you press there, other than typing in a box, go to the drawing. Questions and dialogs the app asks still open in the main window, so look there when one appears. The theme and the panel text size follow the main window. Chrome and Edge can place the window on the other screen for you. With a second screen connected, choose Put it on my other screen in the panels window (or Put the panels on my other screen in a panel's ⋯ menu). The browser asks once whether this site may see your screens; after you allow it, the panels window opens on that screen every time. Drag the panels window to the other monitor yourself. It opens in the same place and at the same size next time. More than one panels window Each panel can have a window of its own: up to four panels windows, for example one per monitor. In a panel's ⋯ menu, Move to a new window opens another window for that panel, and Move to panels window 2 (and so on) moves it into one that is open. The second window is called \"panels 2\". Drag a panel by its name from one window to another: let go over the main window to dock it there (the outlined areas show while you are over it) or to float it, and over another panels window to move it in. Let go anywhere else and it stays where it was. A panels window that has no panels left closes by itself. After a reload the app offers your panels windows again. Browsers open one window per click, so choose Open the next one once for each window. Put the panels back Choose Put the panels back in the panels window, or simply close it. The panels go back where they were. Closing or reloading the main window closes the panels window too. When the app opens again it says your panels were in their own window; choose Open it to bring the window back, or Keep them here. Settings › General › Reset layout puts every panel back where it started. If nothing opens when you move panels to a new window, the browser blocked the pop-up. The app says so and keeps the panels where they were. Allow pop-ups for getcadcraft.com, then try again. The layout is kept for each app in this browser, along with your other settings."},{"slug":"offline","scope":"basics","title":"Working offline","summary":"Save an app for offline use, what works with no connection, and what needs one.","section":"start","order":40,"keywords":["offline","no internet","airplane","site visit","cache","service worker","install","download","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"save-an-app-for-offline-use","text":"Save an app for offline use","level":2},{"id":"what-works-offline","text":"What works offline","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-connection","text":"What needs a connection","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThe apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app&#039;s files saved first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-an-app-for-offline-use\"\u003ESave an app for offline use\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the app while you are online.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E in the bar at the top.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWait for &quot;Offline ready&quot;. The app&#039;s files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDo this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"net:offline\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003EOffline\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EYou are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don&#039;t reload it until you are online again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-works-offline\"\u003EWhat works offline\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESaving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThis help, including search.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-connection\"\u003EWhat needs a connection\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EGetting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave for offline\u003C/span\u003E refreshes the saved copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELinks to the website, such as \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen on the website\u003C/span\u003E and links to other pages.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser&#039;s site data removes it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"hv\" data-v=\"browser:safari\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hv-label\"\u003ESafari\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESafari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDesktop builds, which will not need a browser, are not available yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"The apps do their work on your computer, so most of it works with no connection. The browser only needs to have the app's files saved first. Save an app for offline use Open the app while you are online. Choose Save for offline in the bar at the top. Wait for \"Offline ready\". The app's files, its file readers and this help are now saved in your browser. Apps that read DWG or PDF files save their readers too, so this can take a minute the first time. Do this once per app, in each browser you use. To go back to the app later with no connection, open the same address you used before, or a bookmark of it. You are offline now. If this app was saved for offline use, everything below works. If it was not, you can still use what has already loaded in this tab, but don't reload it until you are online again. What works offline Opening files from your computer, drawing, editing, measuring and checking. Saving and exporting: every save is a download to your computer. This help, including search. What needs a connection Opening the app for the first time, and saving it for offline use. Getting a newer version of the app. When you are online, the app picks up the newest version the next time it opens, and Save for offline refreshes the saved copy. Links to the website, such as Open on the website and links to other pages. Cloud saving and sync between computers, which are not available yet. Things to know A saved copy belongs to one browser on one computer. Clearing your browser's site data removes it. Private windows in some browsers, and some locked-down work computers, do not allow offline saving. The button tells you when it cannot save. Safari removes saved site data from sites you have not visited for several weeks. Open the app now and then while online, or save it again before a trip. Desktop builds, which will not need a browser, are not available yet."},{"slug":"your-files","scope":"basics","title":"Where your files go","summary":"Files stay on your computer, how saving works, and what the browser remembers.","section":"start","order":50,"keywords":["privacy","upload","cloud","save","download","storage","local","security","confidential"],"headings":[{"id":"your-files-stay-on-your-computer","text":"Your files stay on your computer","level":2},{"id":"how-saving-works","text":"How saving works","level":2},{"id":"what-the-browser-remembers","text":"What the browser remembers","level":2},{"id":"cloud-saving","text":"Cloud saving","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"your-files-stay-on-your-computer\"\u003EYour files stay on your computer\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe file is not uploaded.\u003C/strong\u003E We never see it, and it works the same with no connection.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-saving-works\"\u003EHow saving works\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by \u003Cstrong\u003Edownloading\u003C/strong\u003E a new file:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see \u003Ca href=\"/tutorials/basics/browsers#what-differs-between-browsers\" data-topic=\"basics/browsers\" data-hash=\"what-differs-between-browsers\"\u003EBrowsers and computers\u003C/a\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EApps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as &quot;(edited)&quot; or &quot;(marked up)&quot;, so your original stays untouched.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (&quot;Leave site?&quot;).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-browser-remembers\"\u003EWhat the browser remembers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESome apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app&#039;s help says what it keeps.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"cloud-saving\"\u003ECloud saving\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Your files stay on your computer When you open a drawing, model or PDF, the app reads it inside your browser. The file is not uploaded. We never see it, and it works the same with no connection. How saving works A browser page cannot overwrite files on your computer by itself, so the apps save by downloading a new file: The download goes where your browser puts downloads, usually your Downloads folder, unless you have set the browser to ask each time (see Browsers and computers). Apps that change a file save a copy with a new name, such as \"(edited)\" or \"(marked up)\", so your original stays untouched. If you close the tab with unsaved work, the browser asks first (\"Leave site?\"). What the browser remembers Some apps remember small settings in your browser, such as your author name or tool styles. They stay in that browser on that computer, and clearing site data removes them. Each app's help says what it keeps. Cloud saving Cloud saving and sync between computers are part of the commercial license. They are not available yet. Until then, keep your files where you keep your other project files and back them up the usual way."},{"slug":"recover","scope":"basics","title":"Recover unsaved work","summary":"How the apps keep a recovery copy while you work, and how to get your work back after a crash or a closed tab.","section":"trouble","order":55,"keywords":["autosave","recover","recovery","crash","lost work","closed tab","unsaved","restore","backup"],"headings":[{"id":"when-the-copy-is-kept","text":"When the copy is kept","level":2},{"id":"get-your-work-back","text":"Get your work back","level":2},{"id":"things-to-know","text":"Things to know","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EWhile you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"when-the-copy-is-kept\"\u003EWhen the copy is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you switch to another tab or window.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"get-your-work-back\"\u003EGet your work back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen the same app again, in the same browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf it closed before your work was saved, it asks \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover your work?\u003C/span\u003E and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERecover\u003C/span\u003E to open it, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDiscard\u003C/span\u003E to throw it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESave it to a file as soon as it opens.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENot now\u003C/span\u003E keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"things-to-know\"\u003EThings to know\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWhen you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnother tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPrivate windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EClearing your browser&#039;s site data removes the copies.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENot every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app&#039;s own help says whether it does.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"While you work, each app keeps a recovery copy of what you have not saved yet. It is not a save: your file on your computer only changes when you save it. The copy is there so a crash, a closed tab or a computer that turns off does not cost you your work. When the copy is kept A few seconds after you stop editing, and at least every 30 seconds while you keep going. When you switch to another tab or window. The copy stays in this browser, on this computer. Nothing is uploaded. Get your work back Open the same app again, in the same browser. If it closed before your work was saved, it asks Recover your work? and lists what it kept, with the time of the last copy. Choose Recover to open it, or Discard to throw it away. Save it to a file as soon as it opens. Not now keeps the copies for 30 days, and the app asks again the next time it opens. A reload counts too: reload a tab with unsaved work and the app offers it back. Things to know When you save, or close a file on purpose, its recovery copy is removed. Another tab of the same app that is still open never offers your copies. It only offers work from a tab that has gone. Private windows in some browsers, and a browser with no space left, cannot keep a copy. The app tells you when that happens, so save to a file more often. Clearing your browser's site data removes the copies. Not every app keeps a recovery copy yet. Each app's own help says whether it does."}]};
